/* The trainer's scripts: what the trainer says on each Case Flow slide (Presenter view's "Script — read aloud",
   and the day's Trainer Speaker Notes), assembled from build/case-flow/scripts/dN.json by
   build/case-flow/scripts/assemble.cjs. Written to build/case-flow/scripts/SCRIPTSPEC.md.
   Do not edit here: edit the JSON and assemble again. */
window.CM_CF_SCRIPTS = {
 "d1m1-role": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "So, what is Case Management? Simply put, you're the person who keeps the case moving from the first phone call to the final check.\n\nYou track it, you handle it, and you push it forward, one phase at a time.\n\nEvery task you touch has to pass three tests. Is it on time? Is it written down properly in the file? Does everyone who needs to know actually know?\n\nYou'll hear the phrase case health analysis a lot. Honestly, it just means stepping back and reading the whole file for trouble, instead of looking at one task at a time.\n\nOne kind of Case Manager ticks off today's to-do list and goes home. The other notices the client hasn't been to therapy in twelve days and flags it before the adjuster does.\n\n{name}, which of those two do you think the insurance company is hoping we hire?\n\nOn time, written down, everyone knows. Every task, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these three tests do you think new Case Managers fail most often?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here's the whole process, laid end to end. Before I walk you through it, a little guessing game.\n\n{name}, out of these six stages, which one do you think most cases get stuck in? Hold that answer.\n\nIt starts with intake and case opening. You check the facts, run the Conflict Check, which just means making sure the firm doesn't already represent the other side, and open the file in the CMS.\n\nThen planning, where you draft the Case Plan and Atty. Thorne sets strategy. Documentation runs the whole way through.\n\nCommunication means keeping the client, the insurance companies and the doctors updated, and logging every contact the same day. Monitoring means every deadline is on the calendar. Then billing and resolution, and you close a complete file.\n\nSo, the answer? It's documentation, almost every time. Missing records are the number one reason a demand goes out late, and a late demand is money left on the table.\n\nDocumentation is where cases get stuck, so it's where you shine.",
   "ask": "{name}, looking at John Doe's file today, which stage is he sitting in right now?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Okay, quick game, and nobody gets to sit this one out. If your palms are a little sweaty, good. It means you care. On my first day mine were so sweaty I couldn't unlock my own phone.\n\nHere's how it works. I describe a job, you shout who owns it: the client, the attorney, or the Case Manager. Ready?\n\nDeciding whether to settle. Calling the body shop for the estimate. Telling a judge what the case is worth. Making sure the client's MRI report actually lands in the file. {name}, you get the tiebreaker on that last one.\n\nSo why do I play this on day one? Because most new Case Managers think the job is answering phones and moving paper.\n\nBy the end of this block you'll see it's the job that decides whether a good case gets a great settlement or a sad one. It's the client's case, it's the attorney's judgment, and it's your movement.\n\nAnd there's one line in this role you must never cross. Hang on, I'll show you exactly where it is.\n\nThe client owns the case, the attorney owns the judgment, you own the movement.",
   "ask": "{name}, before I tell you: whose file is it really, the client's, Atty. Thorne's, or yours?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules, and that line I promised you.\n\nYou own the movement: deadlines, documents, insurance companies, doctors, and keeping the client updated. Atty. Thorne owns every legal judgment: what the case is worth, whether it's strong, the strategy, and whether to settle.\n\nEvery contact, document and deadline goes in the CMS the same day, because if it isn't in the CMS, it didn't happen.\n\nMy first boss said that so often that when I told her I'd gotten engaged, she asked if I'd logged it.\n\nOkay, here's the line. You can explain the process and confirm facts. You can never tell a client what their case is worth or whether to take an offer.\n\nA brand-new Case Manager once told a client, just trying to be nice, \"You'll get at least fifty grand.\" The client held onto that number for eight months.\n\nEvery offer after that felt like an insult, and the case nearly blew up. One friendly sentence. That's the line.\n\nFacts and process, yes. Value and advice, never.",
   "ask": "{name}, the client asks you, “Should I take what they're offering?” What do you say, word for word?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This is the picture I want you carrying around all week, and there's a little secret hiding in it. I'll get to that.\n\nAcross the slide is the case lifecycle, eight stations left to right: Intake, Claim Set-Up, Treatment, Pre-Demand, Demand, Settlement and Liens, Disbursement, and Litigation.\n\nLiens, by the way, are just bills that get paid out of the settlement before the client gets their share. Litigation is drawn dashed because it's the fork, the road a case only takes if it doesn't settle.\n\nAbove the track, the navy band says the Case Manager owns the movement. Below it, the orange band says Atty. Thorne owns every legal judgment.\n\nNow the secret. See that little CMS monitor under the first station? That's the most important thing on the slide.\n\n{name}, why do you think the CMS sits under station one and not station eight?\n\nThe CMS sits under station one because the whole track runs on it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which station on this track do you think is the easiest place to lose a client's trust?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So here's how it really runs on the job. A new case gets assigned to you.\n\nThe tempting move is to grab the phone and call the client right away to say hi. The better move is to read the raw file end to end in the CMS first, so every party, date and insurance company is noted before you dial.\n\nTrust me on this. On my first day I was so nervous I called a client and introduced myself as him.\n\nThen, a week in, the client asks you a legal question. You log it in the Communications Log, create a task, and send it to Atty. Thorne that same day, with the facts and no opinion from you.\n\nDeadlines and risks go on the Calendar and under Risk Flags, with a next step.\n\nWhy bother? Because the adjuster reads our file history like a report card. A clean, logged file says we're organized and ready to go to court. A messy one says we'll take a lowball.\n\nSo here's your mystery for the next block. John Doe's file already has one detail in it that could cost him money if nobody catches it. {name}, your job is to find it before I do.\n\nRead first, dial second, log everything.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you'd check on a brand-new file, and why that one?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-case": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four ideas, nice and simple.\n\nFirst, the Case Snapshot. Think of it as the file's ID card: the parties, the date of loss, the statute of limitations, which is just the legal deadline to sue, the client's position and the insurance, all on one card.\n\nSecond, the Facts of Loss: how, where and when the crash happened, backed up by the Police Report and witnesses.\n\nThird, the Critical Case Event, the one fact the other side will build their whole defense on. You spot it early so Atty. Thorne can plan around it.\n\nFourth, the source documents: the intake sheet, the supplement and the Police Report.\n\nSomebody new says, \"I'm pretty sure the crash was on a Tuesday.\" Somebody good says, \"Police Report, page one, 02/06/2026.\"\n\nI once heard a Case Manager argue a date with an adjuster for ten minutes, from memory. She lost. You quote the document, never your memory.\n\nAnd never a sticky note. I once found a client's date of loss on a sticky note glued to the bottom of somebody's coffee mug.\n\nQuote the document, never your memory.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think the defense cares so much about the Critical Case Event?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Time to run the five questions on John Doe, and this time the answers come from the documents, not from me.\n\n{name}, you're Who. {name}, you're When. Everybody else, you're the fact-checkers.\n\nWho? John Doe is our client, and Robert Smith is the defendant, meaning the person we're making the claim against. Who else was in the car, and where John Doe was sitting, is in the Police Report. Hmm, funny how I keep saying that, isn't it?\n\nWhat? A collision, and the Run Report, that's the ambulance crew's report, says what they found.\n\nWhen? 02/06/2026, about 7:10 in the morning, and the legal deadline counts from that exact date. Where? The all-way stop at Maple Hollow Rd and Birch Ave.\n\nAnd how? Police Report again, including whether anyone got a ticket.\n\nFive questions, five documents. Get one wrong here and it rides all the way into the demand letter.\n\nFive questions, five documents, zero guesses.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these five answers will you check first in the Police Report, and why?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play Five-Minute Detective. A brand-new file just landed on your desk, and you get five questions before the phone rings. What do you ask?\n\nShout them out, I'm keeping score. {name}, you go first. {name}, give me one nobody's said yet.\n\nNice. Who got hurt, when, who's insured and for how much, and what could go wrong. That's the list every great Case Manager runs in their head.\n\nAnd here's the thing. Every lesson, activity and Learning Check this week works one single file, John Doe v. Robert Smith. Know its facts cold and every click in the CMS will make sense.\n\nNow, remember the mystery I left you with? There's one detail on this file that can change how much money John Doe can reach.\n\nI'm not telling you what it is. By the end of this block, you'll find it yourselves.\n\nOne file, all week. Know it cold.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you only got one question before the phone rang, which one would you ask, and why that one?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, here's what I'll tell you, and what I won't.\n\nThe crash happened on 02/06/2026, around 7:10 a.m., at the all-way stop at Maple Hollow Rd and Birch Ave. Our client is John Doe, 34, a warehouse shift lead at Northgate Distribution.\n\nAtty. Elijah Thorne is Lead Counsel, and you're the Case Manager.\n\nNow the money. The Recovery Stack, which just means every insurance policy that could pay, stacked in the order they pay, is $40,000 across three layers: BI, Host UM, Relative UM.\n\nSay it with me. Again. Love it.\n\nNow the teaser. Which of those layers can he actually reach? That depends on where he was sitting, and I'm not telling you. It's in the Police Report.\n\nSame with the gap between the ambulance date and his first doctor visit. Find it yourself.\n\nThe bare-minimum Case Manager waits for someone to hand them the answer. The really good one goes and reads.\n\nWhere he sat decides what he can reach.",
   "ask": "{name}, no notes: what are the three layers of the stack, in order?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This is John Doe's file at a glance, laid out like a folder.\n\nTop left, the name of the case, John Doe v. Robert Smith, with matter number LSH-2026-MVA-770412 underneath. On the left, the all-way stop at Maple Hollow Rd and Birch Ave.\n\nOn the right, three stacked bars for the $40,000: BI $10,000, Host UM $20,000, Relative UM $10,000.\n\nAlong the bottom there's an orange strip from the date of loss to his first treatment. How long it is? That's yours to work out.\n\nNow look at what's missing. The card doesn't say where he sat, or who got the ticket. {name}, why do you think I left those off?\n\nBecause those two blanks are worth real money. If he was in his own car, he may reach far less. If he was a passenger, a whole extra layer can open up.\n\nOne blank field, thousands of dollars.\n\nOne blank field can be worth thousands of dollars.",
   "ask": "{name}, which document will you open first to fill in the blanks on this card?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it plays out for real.\n\nYour first job is to read the Case File profile and the intake documents until you can say the date of loss, the stack and the critical fact from memory.\n\nThen the matter opens in the CMS, and now memory isn't good enough. Every fact you enter has to point back to a document.\n\nAnd when a fact changes later, and trust me, it will, you update Case Notes and tell the team the same day. Not the sticky note on your monitor. Monitors get bumped, and sticky notes lose their sticky right when you need them.\n\nQuick war story. I had a client who told intake he was driving. Turned out he was in the back seat and his cousin was driving.\n\nNobody checked the Police Report for three weeks. Three weeks of letters to the wrong insurance company.\n\nSo why does it matter? Because the adjuster's first offer is built on what's in our file. Wrong facts, wrong stack, smaller check.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a fact on the file changes on Thursday, who needs to know, and by when?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Last slide for this activity, and it's a short one. Two Dos, two Don'ts, all about knowing your case cold.\n\nFirst Do: trace every fact to its document. When a colleague asks John Doe's date of loss, you don't just say 02/06/2026. You say it's on the Police Report, and you show them where.\n\nSecond Do: say the stack in order, every time. Keystone Mutual's BI, then Harbor Point's Host UM, then Cardinal State Mutual's Relative UM. Forty thousand dollars, same lineup, every call.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Repeating facts from memory alone feels fast, right up until your memory is wrong in front of an adjuster.\n\nAnd never treat the gap as the client's fault. People work, they wait, they hope the pain goes away. The lazy note says he waited, so that's on him. The note that helps lists the dates, 02/06/2026 to 02/20/2026, so Atty. Thorne can explain the gap before the adjuster uses it against us.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? And {name}, which one would tempt you most on a busy day?\n\nHere's your mystery for the rest of today. The Police Report tells you where John Doe was sitting. {name}, who finds it first, and what does it unlock?\n\nDocument the gap with dates. Never judge it.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you open the report, make your guess: driver or passenger, and what's your evidence?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-upl": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four ideas, all practical.\n\nFact sharing is telling John Doe what a record says: a date, an amount, what was sent and when. Process explanation is telling him what happens next, what a form is for, and who decides, but never how it ends.\n\nLegal advice is any opinion on what his facts mean for his rights, his money or his choices, and that belongs to Atty. Thorne alone.\n\nAnd the habit that keeps you safe, the sentence I promised you, is hear, log, route. You write his question down word for word and send it to Atty. Thorne the same day. Think of yourself as a mail carrier: you deliver the letter perfectly, you don't write the reply.\n\n\"Uh, I'm not sure, call back later\" leaves a scared client hanging. \"Great question, that one's for Atty. Thorne. I've written it down exactly, and you'll hear from him by Friday\" leaves him cared for.\n\n{name}, in your own words, why does the second one feel so different?\n\nHear it, log it, route it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these four do you think is hardest to hold onto on a busy day?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: Fact or Opinion. I say a sentence a Case Manager might say to a client. Thumbs up if it's a fact, thumbs down if it's an opinion. Ready?\n\n\"Your appointment is Tuesday at ten.\" \"You'll probably get the policy limits.\" \"We sent the letter on Friday.\" \"Don't worry, the gap won't matter.\"\n\n{name}, you were the only one up on that last one. Tell us why.\n\nHere's the thing. That last one sounds kind, doesn't it? That's exactly why it's dangerous.\n\nToday we're drawing the UPL line. UPL is the unauthorized practice of law, which simply means giving legal advice without a law license. It almost never happens on purpose. It happens when a worried client asks a kind Case Manager a question, and the Case Manager tries to help.\n\nAnd half the time, the client thinks we're the insurance company anyway. I once had a client ask me, very politely, to just mail his check to his cousin's house.\n\nSo here's a simple rule, and one sentence that will save you every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, when has someone asked you for an opinion at work that you weren't sure you were allowed to give, and what did you do?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules, nice and simple. Legal advice is any opinion on what John Doe's facts mean.\n\nYou can share facts: dates, documents, what was sent and when. You can explain the process: what happens next, and who decides.\n\nYou never give an opinion on what the case is worth, on strategy, on whether to settle, or on what a fact like a gap in treatment does to the money.\n\nWhen he asks, you hear it, log it word for word, route it to Atty. Thorne the same day, and tell him when to expect an answer.\n\nOne test before you speak: am I telling him what is, or what it means? \"What is\" is yours.\n\nWhy does this matter so much? If you guess a number and you're wrong, the client anchors on it, like a GPS that's locked onto the wrong address. Every honest offer after that feels like an insult, and good settlements get turned down over one sentence you said to be nice.\n\nWhat is, yes. What it means, never.",
   "ask": "{name}, John Doe says, “Just tell me honestly, is my case any good?” What's your exact answer?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Same questions, answered two ways. Before I reveal the right side, {name}, guess which column has the friendliest-sounding answers. Hold that.\n\nOn the left, fact sharing. \"The Police Report lists the date of loss as 02/06/2026.\" \"The next step is a letter to each insurance company saying we represent you.\" \"That's a question for Atty. Thorne; I've logged it, and he'll call you by Friday.\"\n\nOn the right, the versions that cross the line. \"Your case is probably worth the policy limits.\" \"I'd take the offer.\" \"The gap will hurt your case.\" \"Don't worry, it won't matter.\"\n\nAnd there's the reveal. The kind ones are on the right too. Comfort about the outcome is still an opinion on his case.\n\nA Case Manager I trained once told a client, \"Relax, you've basically already won.\" The client quit physical therapy that week. Why go, if he'd already won?\n\nKind words about the outcome are still advice.",
   "ask": "{name}, which answer on the right sounds the most harmless, and why is it still advice?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's a normal day. John Doe calls and asks what his case is worth. That's Atty. Thorne's question. You log his words exactly in Case Notes, open a task, and it's routed before you go home, with no number given.\n\nHe asks what happens next, and that one's yours. You explain the next step in plain words, with no prediction of how it ends.\n\nWhen an adjuster or a doctor's office asks what you think of the case, you decline politely, share only facts the firm has approved, and log the contact in the Communications Log.\n\nAnd when a client calls to file a claim with you guys, kindly explain that we're his law firm, not his insurance company. The insurance company is the one we're politely arguing with.\n\nOnce an adjuster called and said, all friendly, \"Off the record, what do you think this one's worth?\" There's no off the record with an adjuster. Anything you say becomes their opening offer.\n\n{name}, what would you say to that adjuster?\n\nThere's no off the record. Ever.",
   "ask": "{name}, an adjuster says, “Off the record, what's your number?” What do you say back?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Do and Don't list for the UPL line, where helping a client ends and practicing law begins.\n\nThe Dos. Answer from the record and route what it means. If John Doe asks when his crash happened, you read him 02/06/2026 straight from the Police Report. What that date means goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nUse one routing line, every time, so nobody ever hears a guess. And give the client a time frame for the answer. \"Atty. Thorne will call you back by Friday\" calms people better than any guess.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Reassuring him about the outcome, like \"relax, it won't matter.\" Guessing a value off the record, even when he swears nobody will hold you to it. And coaching him on what to tell an adjuster.\n\nHere's the thing. The kind-sounding answers are often the ones that cross the line. Some Case Managers stonewall, others guess. The best give a process and a time frame. That's the safe kindness.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? {name}, which one tempts you most when a client is upset?\n\nNow your challenge for the next block. When we open John Doe's raw file, there's a question he already asked at intake that nobody routed. {name}, find it, and tell me who should have heard about it.\n\nSafe kindness is a clear next step, not a guess.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the one sentence you'll say every time a client asks what their case is worth?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-l1": {
  "map": {
   "say": "Here's where this lesson is going, so nothing surprises you later. Opening a file runs in six steps.\n\nSeven, if you count remembering your CMS password. And no, it's still not Password1. {name}, you're on step one.\n\nFirst, the Conflict Check, which means checking every party's full legal name to make sure the firm isn't already working for the other side. You do it before the file even exists.\n\nSecond, you verify the facts, starting with the date of loss against the Police Report. Third, you put the statute of limitations, the legal deadline to sue, on the calendar on day one.\n\nFourth, you match every person to their role and record where the client was sitting. Fifth, you record the insurance from the Declaration Page, with where you found it. Sixth, you name and file every document.\n\nA rushed file skips step one and gets to step six whenever. A strong file does all six, in order, on day one.\n\nSix steps, in order, on day one.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these six steps do you think gets skipped most often on a busy day?"
  },
  "theory": {
   "say": "Vocabulary time, and I promise to keep it simple.\n\nThe Recovery Stack is every insurance policy that can pay the claim, stacked in the order it pays. You find every layer at intake, not later.\n\nBodily Injury Liability, or BI, is the at-fault driver's insurance for injuries he causes other people. It pays first. That's called a third-party claim, because we're claiming against someone else's policy.\n\nUM and UIM is coverage for when the at-fault driver has no insurance or not enough. It's first-party, meaning it comes from the car or the household the client is connected to.\n\nAnd then exclusions and rejections. A signed paper turning down UM, or a rule that excludes a car, can close a layer completely. When that happens, you write down the exact document that did it.\n\n{name}, quick check: if Robert Smith's policy is too small, where do we look next?\n\nFind every layer at intake, not later.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these terms is new to you, and which one do you think trips people up?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "A few more terms, and these are the ones people mix up constantly.\n\nMedPay and PIP are medical coverage from the client's side that pays no matter who caused the crash. They pay bills early, while the claim's still open.\n\nProperty Damage liability fixes what the at-fault driver damaged, and collision is the owner's own coverage for their car. Neither one joins the injury stack.\n\nNext, limits. A split limit, say twenty-five, fifty, caps each injured person at the first number and the whole accident at the second. So you read both.\n\nI once watched a Case Manager tell the whole team we had fifty thousand available. We had twenty-five. That was an awkward meeting.\n\nAnd the Declaration Page, or Dec Page, is the insurance company's own statement of who's insured, which cars, what coverage and what limits. That's your proof, not someone's summary.\n\nRead both numbers on a split limit, and trust only the Dec Page.",
   "ask": "{name}, on a split limit, which of the two numbers caps one injured person's claim?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "This is the intake workflow, five steps. {name}, you're my narrator. Tell me if I skip one.\n\nThe raw file comes in from On the Records, and you read it end to end before anything else. Then you check the details, the date of loss, the parties and the signatures, against the Police Report, not the intake note.\n\nNext, the Conflict Check, on every party's full legal name, with the date and result written down.\n\nThen a decision. Complete intake moves forward to acceptance. Incomplete intake goes back. If you're not sure the case qualifies, it goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nOnly then is the file created in the CMS, with six roles mapped, the deadline on the calendar, and documents named.\n\nI get it, the boss wants the file today, so it's tempting to jump straight to creating it. Take the ten extra minutes instead and open it right once.\n\nTen extra minutes now saves ten weeks later.",
   "ask": "{name}, on John Doe's file right now, which of these five steps has actually been done?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play Guess the Price. A brand-new Case Manager makes one mistake in the first hour of opening a file. One field, typed wrong.\n\nHow much money do you think that mistake can cost the client? Shout a number. {name}? Okay, {name}, higher or lower?\n\nI'll reveal it at the end of this block, and honestly, you'll be surprised. Here's your hint: the field is the client's position in the vehicle, where he was sitting.\n\nIntake is where you take control of the file. It's not just typing in data. A file opened wrong stays wrong, because every deadline, every demand letter and every lien check reads from the fields you set in that first hour.\n\nA lien, by the way, is just a bill that gets paid out of the settlement before the client sees a dime.\n\nSo a mixed-up party isn't a typo. It can be a whole insurance policy the client never gets to.\n\nA file opened wrong stays wrong.",
   "ask": "{name}, what do you think is the most expensive mistake you could make in the first hour of a new file?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "You're the gatekeeper of case quality, so incomplete intake goes back, not forward.\n\nRun the Conflict Check before the file exists, on the full legal name of every party: Robert Smith, the host driver, the resident relative. Record its date and result.\n\nConfirm the date of loss against the Police Report, because every deadline counts from it, and put the statute of limitations on the calendar on day one: 02/06/2028.\n\nCompare the story with the Police Report and the client's statement, check every signature, and never quietly fix a mismatch.\n\nMap all six roles. An \"unknown\" party is just a layer you haven't found yet. Record where the client was sitting, and get the emergency contact.\n\nRead the insurance off the Declaration Page and note every source. Name documents the firm's way. And you never tell the client what the case is worth.\n\n{name}, which of these would hurt most if you skipped it?\n\nConflict Check first, deadline on day one, never a silent fix.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the Conflict Check have to happen before the file is created, not just soon after?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Look at the contrast here. On the left, a messy grey stack called raw intake. In the middle, three gates: verify, map, structure.\n\nOn the right, a clean CMS matter card with six role rows, each with a green tick. Underneath, the case's four anchors.\n\nOkay, now the reveal I promised you on the first slide. A client driving his own car may only reach his own policy. A passenger in someone else's car can reach that car's coverage too, and sometimes a relative's.\n\nSame crash, same injuries, and the money available can be four times larger, all decided by one field. That's the price of the mistake.\n\n{name}, was your guess high enough?\n\nSo which is John Doe, driver or passenger? Nope, not telling. Read the Police Report and record it yourself.\n\nDriver or passenger: one field, four times the money.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would have happened to John Doe's recovery if someone had left his position as “driver”?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's follow a real day. The raw file arrives from On the Records. You log into the CMS on the first try, which on a Monday is already a win.\n\nBefore the file even exists, you run and record the Conflict Check in CMS New Case. Then you check the date of loss, the parties and the signatures against the documents.\n\nOnce the matter's created, you put the deadline, 02/06/2028, on the Calendar. You log the retainer, that's the signed agreement that hires us, and the HIPAA authorization, the client's signed permission for us to get his medical records, in Documents.\n\nAs people are identified, you map all six roles in Parties, and nobody stays \"unknown\".\n\nAs documents arrive, you name each one the firm's way and file it, so anyone can find it by name.\n\nWar story. I once inherited a file where every document was named \"scan.pdf\". Forty-one of them. It took me a full afternoon to find the Police Report.\n\nName it right, file it right, find it fast.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think naming a document correctly matters to the settlement, not just to us?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Do and Don't list for intake, and every line on it comes straight out of John Doe's file.\n\nThe Dos first. Record the source of every coverage fact, so when Harbor Point says the Host UM limit is $20,000, the Dec Page or the adjuster's letter sits right next to it.\n\nDocket the statute of limitations on day one. John Doe's counts from 02/06/2026, so 02/06/2028 is on the calendar with reminders before lunch. And keep the CMS as the single source of truth, not a pile of side emails.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Don't create the file before the Conflict Check, which just makes sure the firm has no tie to the other side. And don't run it on a nickname or partial name. \"R. Smith\" isn't Robert Smith.\n\nDon't accept a case because intake mostly looks fine. Files from On the Records arrive unverified. The lazy move treats the intake note as gospel. The sharp move treats it as a rumor until a document proves it.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? {name}, which Do saves you the most pain later?\n\nYour mystery for the next activity: a colleague already typed John Doe's draft record, and it has mistakes in it. {name}, how many, and which one costs the most?\n\nThe intake note is a rumor until a document proves it.",
   "ask": "{name}, make a prediction: how many mistakes do you think are hiding in that draft record?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-open": {
  "task": {
   "say": "So here's your job. With the CMS open, start John Doe's matter in New Case, and before anything else, run and record the Conflict Check on every party's full legal name.\n\nThen go through the draft field by field against the documents: the date of loss, the names and roles, John Doe's position, his date of birth, the report number. Fix each error and write down where the right answer came from. Never fix anything silently.\n\nPut the deadline, 02/06/2028, on the Calendar with reminders, map all six roles in Parties, log the retainer and the HIPAA authorization, and file every intake document, named the firm's way.\n\nThen send Atty. Thorne a short note in Case Notes saying what you corrected. Atty. Thorne, not reply-all.\n\nI once watched a Case Manager reply-all \"who even is this guy?\" about a client to forty-two people. Yikes.\n\n{name}, you're reading the Police Report out loud for your group. {name}, you're checking the draft. Getting the file open is the floor. Open and right is the goal.\n\nEvery fix gets a source. No silent changes.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you change the position field, which document proves you're right?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/09/2026, and an email just came in from On the Records. Before I read it, {name}, guess what kind of matter it is.\n\nOkay, the reveal: a new client, John Doe, front-seat passenger, collision on 02/06/2026 at Maple Hollow Rd and Birch Ave.\n\nAttached are the New Client Intake Sheet, the intake supplement, the retainer, his signed agreement hiring the firm, the HIPAA authorization he signed this morning, and the Riverton PD crash report.\n\nAnd there it is, by the way. Front-seat passenger. Remember what that unlocks?\n\nNow the catch. A colleague already typed a draft intake record into the CMS, and Atty. Thorne wants the matter active today. That draft has mistakes in it.\n\nYour job is to open this case properly. Open the CMS from the Practice Lab slide, start John Doe's matter from New Case, and run the Conflict Check before anything else. Then check the draft against the documents.\n\nTen minutes. I'll hold my questions until you're done.\n\nCheck the draft before you trust it.",
   "ask": "{name}, is John Doe's case open in the CMS now, and where didn't the draft match the documents?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, check yourself, and yes, I'm going to pick on a few of you.\n\n{name}, was the Conflict Check done before the matter existed, on full legal names? Is the date of loss, 02/06/2026, confirmed against the Police Report, and the deadline, 02/06/2028, on the calendar with reminders?\n\nJohn Doe should be the front-seat passenger, not the driver, even though \"Driver\" was already filled in.\n\nDid you catch the position and the date of birth in the draft, and the report number with two digits swapped on the intake sheet, each fixed with its source? An unexplained change is just a new error.\n\nAll six roles mapped, the retainer and HIPAA authorization logged with their signing date, 02/09/2026, and documents named before filing.\n\nAnd before you hit send on anything, read the To line twice. Reply-all is the fastest way to become famous at a law firm.\n\nAny file can have the right answers. A great one shows why. Here's your mystery for the Learning Check: one of those corrections changes which insurance companies we write to tomorrow. Which one?\n\nRight answers, plus the proof of why.",
   "ask": "{name}, where in your Case Notes would Atty. Thorne see why the date of birth changed?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-drive": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Open My Backup Drive from this slide and link your own Google Drive to the platform. It asks Google for one narrow permission: it can only see the folders and files it makes.\n\nThen build the firm's hierarchy by hand, exactly to the standard on the slide.\n\n01 Clients first, with John's folder inside it, named Doe dash John, last name first, and its nine numbered folders in order, intake and retainer down to litigation. Then the firm folders: administration, templates, closed files.\n\nSubmit it. If the grade comes back with problems, read each reason, because every one is a firm rule. Then run Check my drive.\n\nLast, write in Case Notes where the signed retainer, the Police Report and the HIPAA authorization belong, and why.\n\nA folder called Misc helps nobody. A drive anyone can read helps everybody.\n\nBuild it exactly to the standard. Nothing invented.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do the medical records and the bills get two separate folders?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/09/2026, half past four. John Doe's matter went active in the CMS this afternoon. Now picture tomorrow: you're out sick, and a colleague has to find his Police Report in seconds.\n\nThat only works if every file is built the same way. So here's the firm's procedure, four steps, on the left.\n\nOne: create the Main Case Folder, named your full name, a dash, then the case. Two: set up the firm's subfolders. Three: connect the root folder to the platform. Four: keep uploading, every day.\n\nOn the right is the sample. Nine subfolders, numbered in the order the case runs, intake to litigation.\n\nYour trainer sends you the link to the folder you build it in. It's on your dashboard, under My Case Folder.\n\n{name}, look at folders 03 and 04. Why would a firm keep the medical records and the bills apart?\n\nSame layout on every file, so anyone can cover it.",
   "ask": "{name}, what makes a folder easy for someone else to find, when they've never seen your file?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you move on, check yourself, and I'm calling on a few of you.\n\nIs your Google Drive linked, with that one narrow permission? Is every folder named and numbered exactly as the standard has it, nothing invented and nothing renamed?\n\nIs Doe dash John under 01 Clients, with all nine folders inside it and in order? Did the grade come back as built, and did Check my drive come back with nothing missing?\n\nAnd in Case Notes, the retainer and the HIPAA authorization in Intake and Retainer, the Police Report in Police, Witness and Video, each with its reason.\n\nOkay, now the real test. {name}, I say HIPAA authorization. Go. Somebody time it.\n\nThat's the job, really. Any document, in seconds, on any file, for anyone covering it.\n\nAny document, in seconds, for anyone.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a folder goes missing from your drive next week, how will you know, and what do you do?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the Learning Check, game-show edition! You answer from John Doe's file, in your own words, typed under each question, and I'll call a few of you up to defend your answers live.\n\nQuestion one, the warm-up: name the six stages of John Doe's file in order, and tell me what the Case Manager owns at Intake and Case Opening that nobody else will.\n\nQuestion two, the tricky one. John Doe asks you two things on one call: what his case is worth, and whether waiting to see a doctor hurt it. Word for word, what do you tell him on each, who answers if you don't, and what do you record and route?\n\nQuestion three, the big-money question. You're opening his matter in the CMS. What has to happen before the matter is created, on whose names, and why does the order matter?\n\n{name}, you're up first when the timer ends. {name}, you're defending question two.\n\nIf your mind goes blank, that's normal. On my first Learning Check, I spelled my own name wrong.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you type: which of the three questions are you most nervous about, and why?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary. Write the principles in your own words, not the clicks.\n\nThen think through this scenario. Robert Smith's insurance company has already called John Doe, all friendly, and he needs a car to get to work. What do you tell him today, in what order, and what has to be on the file before tomorrow's claim set-up?\n\nReal talk. I once had a client give a Recorded Statement, a taped interview the adjuster can quote forever, to the other side's adjuster because they \"sounded really nice.\"\n\nHe said he felt \"pretty much fine\" the day after the crash. That one sentence followed us all the way to settlement.\n\nThe right answer puts things on the record and sends anything about value or fault to Atty. Thorne. {name}, you'll share yours first.\n\nFriendly voice, other side. Every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the very first thing you'd tell John when he mentions the carrier's call, and why first?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last one, the Skill Scale. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each skill, then submit for review. I'll put your self-rating next to your score, and where there's a big gap, we'll talk. Honest beats impressive here.\n\nAnd no, \"I submitted it, the system ate it\" won't work. If it isn't in the CMS, it didn't happen, and that includes your homework.\n\nOne more thing. UPL is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive an ethics breach.\n\nHere's your mystery to sleep on. John Doe's file is open, the stack is $40,000, and he was a passenger. But tomorrow, he might go quiet on us.\n\nIf your client stops answering the phone, what's the first thing you do, and what's the last? {name}, I want your answer first thing next block.",
   "ask": "{name}, where do you honestly sit, 1 to 5, on opening a matter correctly in the CMS, and what would move you up one?"
  }
 },
 "d1m2-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the road, nice and plain. First you look inside the file before you call anyone: the case status, the deadlines, the last time we spoke.\n\nThen you reach out, and you climb a ladder. Two phone calls, then email and text, then the emergency contact, then a certified letter, which is mail where the post office gives us proof it was delivered. After that, you tell Atty. Thorne what the silence puts at risk.\n\nA few words you'll hear. Due diligence just means proof we made a real, reasonable effort, written down. Approved channels are the ways the client agreed at intake we could contact them, and only those count.\n\nAnd the emergency contact is the backup person who can still reach the client. So, guess who that is on John Doe's file. {name}?\n\nIt's Margaret Doe, his mother. Remember that name, okay? She gets very important in about ten minutes.\n\nLook inside first, then climb the ladder, then tell Atty. Thorne.",
   "ask": "{name}, where on John Doe's file would you look to find his approved contact channels?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Before I show you this list, {name}, a quick bet. Do clients go silent out of nowhere, or is there usually a warning sign? Hold that.\n\nHere's the reveal: silence almost always starts with a bottleneck, which is just a spot where the work slows down or gets stuck.\n\nMissing information, like a wrong phone number or no incident details. Slow follow-up, where we asked a question and nobody chased the answer. A Conflict Check, the search to make sure we don't already represent the other side, stuck in a backlog.\n\nThen there are intake forms with wrong dates or missing signatures, facts so unclear the attorney has to review them, and plain old communication gaps between departments.\n\nA weak Case Manager says, “the client went dark.” A great one asks, “what did we do that made it easy for them to drift?”\n\nAnd it matters, because every one of these bottlenecks weakens the eventual demand. A gap on our side looks like a gap in the case.\n\nRemember: before you blame the client, check our own bottlenecks.",
   "ask": "{name}, looking at John Doe's file right now, which of these bottlenecks do you think he's closest to?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Hands up if you've ever ghosted someone. Come on, a friend, a dentist, a gym that kept calling. Keep them up. Now keep them up if you felt a little guilty about it. Yep.\n\nClients ghost us too, and almost never because they're bad people. They're in pain, they're overwhelmed, they changed phones.\n\nAnd when you finally do get a text back, it says “new phone, who dis.” That's not a client, that's a plot twist.\n\nSo here's my teaser. This lesson isn't really about chasing a client. It's about something sneakier: when a client goes quiet, the case keeps running without them. The deadlines don't stop.\n\nThe No Contact Protocol, which is just our step-by-step plan for when a client stops answering, does three jobs. It tracks the silence officially, it proves the firm tried hard before anyone talks about ending the case, and it protects the firm if somebody later asks, what did you do?\n\n{name}, I'll tell you which of those three matters most in a minute. Make your guess now.\n\nHere's the line to keep: silence stops the client, never the clock.",
   "ask": "{name}, if John Doe went quiet tomorrow, who gets hurt if nobody can prove what the firm did to reach him?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules, and the one habit that separates the pros. Step 1 is internal review: check the status, the live deadlines and the last contact date before you dial. Everybody wants to skip it. Don't.\n\nStep 2 is the ladder: at least two phone calls, then email and text, then the emergency contact, then certified mail or a formal letter.\n\nAnd this is where people fail. You climb by channel, not by volume.\n\nCalling the same cell number nine times and writing “tried everything” isn't trying everything. Bonus points if his voicemail box was full the whole time, so you basically left nine messages for a robot that says the mailbox is full.\n\nWhat good looks like is two calls, then an email, then a text, then Margaret. Each one logged with date, time, channel and outcome, and a follow-up reminder set.\n\nStep 3 is telling Atty. Thorne, because deadlines don't pause for silence.\n\nAnd the line you don't cross, {name}: you can tell the client what we need and by when. You can't tell them we're dropping their case.\n\nBurn it in: climb by channel, not by volume.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you pick up the phone, what deadline is already running on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the whole protocol in one picture, and there's a little hidden lesson in how it's drawn.\n\nDown the left is a spine of six stations. The first three are in full colour: internal review, contact attempts and team notification. That's today. Stations four to six are greyed out with a next lesson tag.\n\nOn the right, station two opens into a ladder of channels: phone, then email and text, then the emergency contact, then certified mail.\n\nNow look closely. {name}, what do you notice about the size of the rungs? Yep, each one gets wider.\n\nThat's the hidden lesson. Every step up reaches further and costs more effort, so you don't skip rungs, and you don't stand on one rung forever either.\n\nAnd down in the corner, the red card. The statute of limitations, which is the legal deadline to file the lawsuit, keeps running the whole time.\n\nEvery rung reaches further. Use each one, then step up.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think each rung on that ladder is drawn wider than the one above it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let me tell you how this plays out for real. A Case Manager I trained once logged eleven calls to a silent client in two days. Eleven. She was so proud.\n\nTurns out the client had dropped his phone in a lake while fishing. Every one of those calls went straight to the bottom of the lake. One email would've reached him in an hour.\n\nSo here's the job. John Doe misses an appointment. That's your trigger. You open the Case Summary in the CMS and do Step 1, so you know the live deadlines.\n\nThen you work the ladder in the Communications Log, the file's diary of every contact, logging each attempt with date, time, channel and outcome.\n\nWhen the ladder's done, you escalate to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, with the impact spelled out: the SOL of 02/06/2028 and any hearing dates.\n\nWhy bother? Because an adjuster who sees a client vanish assumes the injuries weren't that bad. {name}, our log is how we prove otherwise.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would Atty. Thorne need to see in that escalation before acting on it?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two lists up here, two habits and two traps, and every one of them is about what happens while a client goes quiet.\n\nThe Dos first. Set a follow-up reminder the minute each request goes out, not ten days later when you remember, and give the client an easy way to answer.\n\nJohn Doe's a shift lead at Northgate Distribution; he can't take calls on the floor, but a text, a call window or an upload link reaches him.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Letting a file sit with nothing logged because “we're waiting on the client.” And blaming the client for what's really our own process gap.\n\nHere's the contrast. The lazy Case Manager calls twice, shrugs, and writes nothing down. The high-value one logs every attempt with the date, time, channel and outcome, then moves to a new channel instead of a third call to the same phone.\n\n{name}, which of those Don'ts have you seen happen on a real file?\n\nNow, your mystery for the next activity. John Doe's file shows two calls to his mobile on the phone system and zero entries in the log. {name}, figure out what that missing log costs us.\n\nIf it isn't logged, it didn't happen.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does calling Margaret count as a new channel when a third call to John's mobile doesn't?"
  }
 },
 "d1m2-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Okay, your turn to run the No Contact Protocol on John Doe's file. Start with Step 1, the internal review in the CMS Case Summary, and name the deadlines that are live right now. No dialling first.\n\nThen rebuild the attempts already made, those two missing voicemails, and carry the Step 2 ladder forward in the Communications Log. Every attempt gets date, time, channel and outcome, plus the next action and its due date.\n\nThen the fun part: a live call with Margaret Doe. {name}, you might be the one on the phone.\n\nSay who you are, confirm she's the emergency contact on file, and ask her to have John call the firm by a set date. Confirm his best number and current address, all without sharing any case details.\n\nThat last part trips people up, because Margaret will ask, “Is he okay? Is the case okay?” Your answer is friendly and gives her nothing about the case.\n\nFair warning, emergency contacts aren't always thrilled to hear from us. I once had a mom pick up and say, “He's thirty-four, why am I still his answering service?”\n\nYou hand in a full Step 1 review, an unbroken Step 2 log, and your call on Step 3.\n\nKeep this one: review first, dial second, log everything.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you log anything, which deadline is running on this file right now?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Something just landed on the file. Before I reveal it, {name}, guess how many days a client can go quiet before it's a problem. Shout a number.\n\nHere it is, dated 02/16/2026, file LSH-2026-MVA-770412. Six days with no contact from John Doe. His last logged contact was 02/10/2026, and he missed his check-in call on 02/13/2026.\n\nThe phone system shows two calls to his mobile, both voicemail. And here's the twist: neither one is in the Communications Log, the file's diary of every contact.\n\nNo treatment start recorded. His emergency contact is Margaret Doe, at (555) 010-2291, and Step 1 of the No Contact Protocol is due today.\n\nCheck that number before you climb the ladder, by the way. I once called a client's number on file three times and got the same pizza place every time. By call three, they had my order memorized.\n\nNow, {name}, read the bad log entry out loud for us. “Called client, no answer.” Be honest, most of us have written that.\n\nThe trouble is, six months from now nobody can tell when, which number, or what happened next. A note that vague is basically a sticky note in the rain.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's missing from “called client, no answer” that a supervisor would need six months from now?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work against these, and let's talk about the difference between okay and excellent. Okay says, “I called, I emailed.”\n\nExcellent shows Step 1 done first, with status, live deadlines and last contact reviewed. Both earlier voicemails are logged with date, time and channel.\n\nThe phone rung is cleared with at least two attempts, then email and text, not more calls. And Margaret is used, which is the rung people forget most.\n\nEvery entry has date, time, channel and outcome, plus a next action with a due date. Your Step 3 impact names the SOL of 02/06/2028 and the treatment that hasn't started. And nothing threatens to drop the case.\n\nHere's why this matters down the road. That missing treatment start is the seed of the 14-Day Gap, a stretch with no doctor visits that an insurance company will use to cut the offer.\n\nSo here's your mystery, {name}. If John's silent now, what does the adjuster say about it in six months, and how does your log answer them?",
   "ask": "{name}, if you called his mobile three times, how many channels have you actually used?"
  }
 },
 "d1m2-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the reveal I promised. This letter is one last written notice before the firm searches for the client or the attorney moves to withdraw.\n\nIt goes two ways at once. Certified mail proves it was delivered. Email is what people actually read. Each one covers the other's blind spot.\n\nBecause certified mail has one secret talent: nobody is ever home to sign for it. That little green card comes back unclaimed more reliably than anything else in this business.\n\nThen there's the three-criteria gate. Think of it like a door with three locks: the ladder is done, the letter is sent, and the letter's deadline has passed. All three, not one.\n\nOnly then do we skip-trace, which is just a paid search for someone's current address and phone number. It costs the firm money, so the attorney has to approve it.\n\nAnd withdrawal, meaning the firm officially stops representing the client, is decided only by the attorney, under firm and court rules. {name}, which of those three locks do you think people forget to check?\n\nThree locks, all open, or the door stays shut.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these terms have you met before, and where would it show up on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Before I show you the next three steps, {name}, give me a prediction. Of Steps 4, 5 and 6, how many does the Case Manager actually drive? Hold your number.\n\nStep 4 is the Final Warning Letter. It lists every earlier attempt, gives the client 7 to 14 days to respond, and warns that the firm may withdraw. It goes by certified mail and email.\n\nStep 5 is the decision point. If there's still no answer, the attorney decides whether to file a Notice or a Motion to Withdraw, which is the paperwork telling the court the firm is stepping off the case.\n\nStep 6 is closing the file, marked “No response, withdrew per protocol,” with every proof kept.\n\nAnd the reveal: you really drive just one of them, the letter. But you build the record that makes the other two possible.\n\nA sloppy letter turns Step 5 into a gamble. A clean one makes it routine.",
   "ask": "{name}, if John Doe went silent today, how far would his file be from Step 4?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game. Would you rather spend five hundred dollars hiring someone to find a missing person, or spend five dollars on a letter that might make them call you back?\n\nHands up for the letter. Everybody? Good. Then you already get this lesson.\n\nThe Final Warning Letter is the last cheap step before the firm has to pick between two expensive ones: paying to find the client, or the attorney moving to end the case.\n\nSo the letter has two jobs. It has to be specific enough that the client actually answers, and documented enough to back up whatever comes next.\n\nNow here's the teaser. There's one mistake that wastes the firm's money and weakens the whole record, and honestly, new Case Managers make it all the time. It's about three things being true at the same time.\n\n{name}, I'll reveal it on the next slide. Take a guess at what the three things are.\n\nThe takeaway: the cheap letter always comes before the expensive search.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the risk of spending the firm's money on a search before the letter's deadline has even passed?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules. Step 4 goes by certified mail and email. Both, not either. The letter lists the earlier attempts, sets a deadline of 7 to 14 days and warns that withdrawal is possible.\n\nAnd confirm the address first. I once saw a perfect warning letter, beautifully written, mailed to the client's old apartment.\n\nThe new tenant signed for it, read it, and called us asking if he could take over the case. True story, more or less. A letter to a stale address proves nothing.\n\nAt Step 5, the attorney may file to withdraw. That's counsel's call. Skip-tracing starts only when all three locks are open and counsel approves it with the full log attached.\n\n“We've tried several times” tells a judge nothing. “Called 02/10, voicemail 02/13,” and so on, with real dates, tells them everything.\n\nThe letter can say withdrawal is possible. {name}, what it can never do is judge the claim or announce the case is over.",
   "ask": "{name}, why both certified mail and email, when either one would technically get the letter there?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Two panels here, and the most important thing on this slide is one tiny word. See if you can spot it.\n\nOn the left is the letter on letterhead, with four orange badges marking what it has to contain: the earlier attempts, listed; a response deadline of 7 to 14 days; notice that withdrawal is possible; and how to respond.\n\nAbove it, certified mail and email, joined by a bold AND. That's the tiny word, {name}. Not or. And.\n\nOn the right is the Step 5 fork. It starts with one question: did a response come in by the deadline? If yes, the file picks right back up.\n\nIf no, you hit a gate with three chips: ladder done, letter sent, deadline passed. Only then do you reach the skip-trace and the attorney's decision.\n\nIt's drawn as a gate on purpose, honestly, because people love to sneak around it.",
   "ask": "{name}, what happens on this picture if only two of those three chips are true?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the job. Your Step 3 escalation is done and there's still nothing.\n\nSo you draft the Final Warning Letter in the Action Plan Editor, the screen where we build letters and plans from the firm's templates. Every earlier attempt listed, deadline and withdrawal notice in place.\n\nYou confirm the current address and send it from CMS Documents, Outgoing, by certified mail and email. Both logged, certified receipt kept.\n\nThen the deadline passes. And of course, the day after, the client strolls back in with, “Hey, did you guys need something?” Like he just went out for milk.\n\nThat's fine, because the log still proves we did everything right. Now you check all three locks in the log, and only then do you escalate to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, asking for skip-trace approval and the Step 5 decision.\n\nWhy all this paperwork? Because if this case ever goes sideways, a judge or a bar complaint looks at exactly this record. A clean letter protects the client's claim, the firm and you.\n\n{name}, which piece of this would you be most nervous about getting wrong?\n\nRemember: the record you build today is the defense you'll need tomorrow.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you'd check in the log before you escalate to Atty. Thorne?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two lists again, and this time they're about the Final Warning Letter and what comes after it.\n\nOn the Do side, itemize every attempt with its date, because “we have tried several times” isn't evidence. And ask in-house help to confirm the address first. A letter to 2214 Maple Hollow Rd only proves something if John Doe actually still lives there.\n\nOn the Don't side, launching a skip-trace on the first criterion alone. All three have to be true: the ladder's exhausted, the letter's gone out, and its deadline has passed. And writing “we are withdrawing” in a letter the attorney never approved.\n\nThe lazy letter says “we've been trying to reach you, please call.” The high-value letter lists every call, text and email by date, gives a clear deadline, and says withdrawal is possible, never that it's happening.\n\nThat's the hard line. You build the record; the attorney decides to withdraw. {name}, which of these Don'ts would worry you most if you saw it in a draft?\n\nNow your mystery for the drafting activity: Atty. Thorne wants a letter that's urgent but not threatening. {name}, find the one sentence in a typical draft that quietly turns urgent into a threat.\n\nYou build the record; only the attorney ends representation.",
   "ask": "{name}, who decides that representation is ending, and who tells the client?"
  }
 },
 "d1m2-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll draft the Step 4 Final Warning Letter to John Doe in the Action Plan Editor, using the warning letter template.\n\nList every earlier attempt with its date. Set a response deadline inside the 7 to 14 day window, warn that withdrawal is possible, and tell him exactly how to respond.\n\nEvery sentence stays inside the UPL line. UPL is the unauthorized practice of law, meaning giving legal advice when you're not a lawyer.\n\nSo nothing about how strong his claim is, nothing about what silence does to it legally, and nothing saying the case is being dropped.\n\nThen write down what has to be true before a skip-trace can start, and whose approval Step 5 needs.\n\n{name}, you'll read your opening line to the room when you're done, so make it a good one. You hand in a ready-to-send letter with both deliveries logged and the escalation recorded.\n\nRemember: what we need and by when, never what happens to his case.",
   "ask": "{name}, whose approval does the Step 5 decision need, and where will you record that?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "A note from Atty. Thorne just landed on the file, dated 02/21/2026. Before I read it, {name}, guess how many days it's been since we heard from John. Closer than you'd like?\n\nHere it is. Eleven days, no word, and no treatment we know of.\n\nThe phone rung is cleared, emails and texts are out, Margaret Doe has been tried, and it's all logged.\n\nAtty. Thorne wants the Final Warning Letter drafted for review Monday morning: every attempt with its date, a response date, and how to reach us, by certified mail and email.\n\nWithdrawal is possible, not decided, and the letter says nothing about John's claim.\n\nAnd here's the hard part. It has to be urgent but not threatening.\n\nI've seen first drafts that read like a love letter, “please call when you get a chance, no rush,” and drafts that read like a ransom note. Neither one gets a call back.\n\nThe target: clear, dated, firm, and kind.",
   "ask": "{name}, give me one sentence that would make this letter sound threatening rather than urgent?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Swap drafts with the person next to you and grade each other.\n\nFun fact: a good warning letter is the fastest way to make a missing client reappear. Half the time they call within the hour asking why we're being so dramatic.\n\nFirst, {name}, read us the banned and compliant pairs out loud. It'll save someone a full rewrite.\n\nNow, passable versus excellent. A passable letter says “as soon as possible.” An excellent one has a real date inside the 7 to 14 day window.\n\nLook for the firm's name and file reference, and the date of loss, 02/06/2026. Earlier attempts listed with dates. Withdrawal reads as possible, not decided. At least two ways to respond.\n\nCertified mail and email both recorded, address confirmed first. Nothing about claim value or legal consequence, so no “you will lose your case.” Skip-trace locks written down, Step 5 noted as Atty. Thorne's.\n\nThis one letter can be the difference between a client who comes back to finish treatment and a case that closes for nothing.\n\nAnd your mystery: if John does call back, what's the very first thing you check on his file?",
   "ask": "{name}, does your partner's letter tell John what's needed and by when, without telling him what happens to his case?"
  }
 },
 "d1m2-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the game show portion of the day! Your answers come from this file, in your own words, typed under each question. But I'm going to warm you up out loud first.\n\n{name}, lightning round: name the six steps of the No Contact Protocol, in order. Go. Anyone can steal if they stall.\n\nOn the test you'll set out all six steps with what's required at each. You'll also state the protocol's three purposes and say which one protects the firm rather than the client.\n\nHere's a hint, because people answer this one shallowly. Protection from liability, meaning the firm can show it did its job if someone complains later, is the purpose most people leave out. And it's exactly why the record is built the way it is.\n\nThen there's the case of the lazy colleague, who logged three voicemails on three days and called the ladder done. Tell me what's wrong, and what the log should show.\n\nRemember: if it isn't logged with a date, it didn't happen.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the three purposes do you think is easiest to forget?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary, where you put the principles in your own words.\n\nHere's the scenario. It's 04/08/2026. Riverton Spine & Rehab reports John Doe has missed two visits, he isn't answering anyone, and Dana Whitfield has asked John for a Recorded Statement.\n\nTell me what you do first, second and third, and what each step protects. {name}, I'll want to hear your first move out loud.\n\nThis one pulls it all together: the running SOL, a Treatment Gap that's hardening, and the claims work that has to keep moving anyway.\n\nAnd keep in mind, the gap you're watching build here is the same 14 days defense counsel will ask John Doe about at his deposition on Day 5.\n\nThe habit to keep: for every step you take, know exactly what it protects.",
   "ask": "{name}, of those three problems, which one protects the most if you deal with it first?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step is your Skill Scale. Rate yourself honestly, one to five, on each competency, then submit it.\n\nOne to five isn't a grade. It's just a snapshot of where you are today.\n\nWhen I get these back, I'll compare your rating with your Knowledge Check score. Wherever there's a wide gap, either way, we'll have a short chat. Overrating and underrating both tell me something useful.\n\nFair warning: last batch, someone rated themselves a five on everything and then put Margaret Doe down as the defendant. We still talk about it.\n\nNow, a mystery to take with you. John's transportation fell apart on 04/01, and his visits started slipping right after.\n\n{name}, before tomorrow, dig through the file and tell me what we could've done the day his ride ended, like asking Riverton about transportation help, and who we should've told that same day.",
   "ask": "{name}, which competency here are you least sure how to rate, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the road for this lesson, four stops.\n\nFirst, the standing instructions: no recorded call with the other side's insurance, no posts, and nothing signed until we've looked at it. Second, treatment: keep every appointment, and tell us about any problem getting there before a visit is missed.\n\nThird, receipts, because only what's written down can be claimed. Fourth, the car, which is the one exception.\n\nNow the big idea underneath. Anything a client says, posts, signs or skips can become evidence.\n\nA Recorded Statement, by the way, is just the insurance company taping the client's version of the crash, and they can use every word later. And the Letter of Representation is the letter that tells the other side, from now on, you talk to us, not our client.\n\n{name}, quick guess before I answer: what's the difference between a medical danger and a legal danger?\n\nOkay. A medical danger threatens his health, like a fever after surgery, so the doctor's office hears about it today. A legal danger threatens the claim, like three weeks with no therapy, so Atty. Thorne hears about it today. Same urgency, different phone call.\n\nAnything a client says, posts, signs or skips can become evidence.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, give me an example of a legal danger that isn't a medical one, and who hears about it?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's start with a game called Good Intentions, Bad Outcome. I name something a brand-new client does in week one. Thumbs up if it helps the claim, thumbs down if it hurts it. Ready?\n\nPosting a gym selfie to show they're tough. Chatting with the other driver's insurance to be polite. Skipping physical therapy because they feel a bit better.\n\nWhoa, {name}, you were fast on that last one.\n\nHere's what gets me. Every single one of those came from a nice person trying to do the right thing.\n\nSo here's the teaser. Most of the damage to an injury claim happens in the first two weeks, before anyone has told the client what not to do.\n\nBy the end of this block you'll know the three mistakes clients make most, and I'll show you which one has already started on John Doe's file.\n\nYou give the instructions. What any of it means for the claim is Atty. Thorne's to explain.\n\nMost damage happens in the first two weeks, from good intentions.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's one thing a new client might do in week one, meaning well, that could hurt the claim?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "These are the firm's standing instructions, and I want you to hear the difference between doing them on autopilot and doing them well.\n\nThe client gives no Recorded Statement to the other driver's insurance; all their contact comes through us. They start treatment, keep every appointment, and tell you about any barrier, like rides, money or work, before a visit is missed.\n\nThey post nothing about the crash, the injuries or their activities, and sign nothing until we've reviewed it. They keep every receipt, even mileage.\n\nAutopilot is emailing a PDF and calling it done. Doing it well means going through each rule on the phone, having the client say it back to you, and logging that they understood.\n\nAnd yes, clients will argue that a gym selfie proves they're recovering. To an adjuster, it proves they can deadlift, and the offer drops to match.\n\nIs it worth the extra effort? Absolutely. One recorded call can hand the adjuster a quote like \"I'm fine, really,\" and that one sentence can shave thousands off the offer at the end.\n\nDon't just send the rules. Hear them said back.",
   "ask": "{name}, if John Doe asks why he shouldn't give a Recorded Statement, what can you say without giving legal advice?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the board. Green Do column on the left, red Don't column on the right, and each Don't lines up with the Do that replaces it.\n\nLet's play call and response. I read the Don't, you shout the Do. {name}, you're leading the room.\n\nDon't give the other driver's insurance a Recorded Statement. Good. Don't post about the crash. Right. Don't sign the insurance company's release yourself. Exactly, bring it to the firm first.\n\nNow, quick story. I once had a client who didn't post about the accident, and he was very proud of that.\n\nHis cousin posted a video of him moving a couch the next weekend and tagged him. So yes, we now say, and ask your family not to tag you.\n\nDown at the bottom are three orange tags: no Recorded Statement, keep every receipt, and the car only. Those are the three clients break most in week one.\n\nEvery Don't has a Do that replaces it.",
   "ask": "{name}, here's a Don't: signing a carrier's medical authorization form. What's the Do that replaces it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the job. The retainer's signed, so you go over the standing instructions and log that you did it in CMS Onboarding, with the date and how you delivered them.\n\nA few days later the client mentions he paid for a prescription and a ride to urgent care. You tell him to keep both receipts, and you log the cost in Case Notes.\n\nThen the other side's insurance calls him directly. You tell him to send them to the firm, and you log the contact in the Communications Log. The check is simple: no Recorded Statement, and the contact is on the record.\n\nNow think long game. Every receipt you log now becomes a line in the demand later.\n\nThe Case Manager who logs everything hands Atty. Thorne a full bill. The one who doesn't leaves the client's money in a shoebox somewhere. {name}, which would you rather be?\n\nEvery receipt you log today is a line in the demand later.",
   "ask": "{name}, the client says he tossed his pharmacy receipt. What would you have him do today?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Do and Don't list for client instructions. Short list, big consequences.\n\nThe Dos. Ask about barriers to care at every check-in, because they come up quietly. John Doe owns no car, so if his ride falls through, his treatment falls through with it. You catch that early and help line up another ride.\n\nAnd use active listening. When he calls frustrated about missing shifts at Northgate Distribution, let him finish and say his worry back to him. Clients who feel heard follow instructions.\n\nNow the Don'ts. If Keystone Mutual asks for a Recorded Statement and John Doe asks why that's a problem, the lazy answer explains how it could hurt his claim. That's legal advice. You give the instruction, and Atty. Thorne explains the why.\n\nAnd don't let a carrier's Property Damage call drift into injuries. It starts with repair photos, then suddenly they're asking about his neck. You stop it right there.\n\nThe social media rule covers vacations too. Nothing says \"severe neck pain\" to an adjuster like a beach volleyball video with forty likes.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you watched happen?\n\nHere's your mystery for the next activity. John Doe has already broken one of these rules, or nearly has, before we even sent him anything. {name}, listen closely for which one.\n\nYou give the instruction. Atty. Thorne explains what it means.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of today's rules do you think John Doe is closest to breaking right now?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Now you work the plan, and the order is the lesson.\n\nFirst, an email to Dana Whitfield at Keystone, the other driver's adjuster: the firm represents John Doe, she doesn't contact him directly, and any request for a Recorded Statement, which is the insurer taping his version of the crash, comes through the firm. Not a word about his neck.\n\nThen call John back on his cell. Ask about the stiff neck and whether he's seen anyone, tell him the next steps, and have him say the instruction back to you: no Recorded Statement, and no talking about the case with anyone outside the firm.\n\nThen coordinate his first visit with Riverton Urgent Care. Last, the Direction Packet, all six sections, delivered, and every contact logged.\n\n{name}, you're Dana for a minute. How would you try to get John on the phone anyway? {name}, you're John.\n\nIf you work these in whatever order the phone rings, you lose. Shut the door on Keystone first, in writing, because an email has a timestamp and a phone call has a memory.\n\nClose the door in writing first.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the instruction go to Dana in writing, and not only to John on the phone?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Before I play anything, a quick bet. {name}, how long do you think a client's voicemail usually is? Ten seconds? A minute? {name}, your guess?\n\nThis one's longer than you'd like, and every second matters.\n\nJohn Doe's retainer is signed and his matter is open, but nothing has gone out to him yet. And a voicemail just landed on the file, dated 02/10/2026. Where you'd normally see documents beside it, you've got your Notes and Action Plan instead.\n\nPlay the message and type it out exactly as he says it, start to finish. Not your summary, his words. When you hand it in, you'll see the transcript and every fact you should have caught.\n\nThen you write your plan, in the order you'd work it today. That's graded too, with the answer key straight after.\n\nA distracted listener writes \"client called, wants update.\" A sharp one catches the one sentence in the middle that changes today's to-do list.\n\nI once had a client leave a four-minute voicemail that was mostly about his dog. Buried at minute three was the line \"the other guy's insurance called me.\" Guess which part mattered.\n\nType his words, not your summary.",
   "ask": "{name}, from the voicemail alone, what has already happened on this file that your plan has to deal with first?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Let's check the work before you log it, and I'm calling on a few of you. Your transcript and plan are handed in, and you've read them against the key.\n\n{name}, does Dana have it in writing: the firm represents him, no direct contact, statements through the firm, and nothing about his injuries?\n\nOn the call, did you ask about his neck and his treatment status, give him next steps, and hear him repeat the instruction back? Riverton Urgent Care knows he's coming and where records go.\n\nThe packet has all six sections, the Property Damage section names the Civic's owner, and every contact is logged. And Atty. Thorne heard today that Keystone called a represented client, a client who already has a lawyer.\n\n\"I sent the email\" is a start. \"I sent it, logged it, and told the attorney why it mattered\" is the job.\n\nWhy care? If Keystone gets a statement anyway, the date on your email is the proof we drew the line first.\n\nMystery for later: what would you do if John calls back tomorrow and says \"they called me again\"?\n\nSent, logged, and explained to the attorney.",
   "ask": "{name}, how will you actually know John Doe understood the instruction, not just heard it?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Quick show of hands before I say a word about cars. Hands up if you think Property Damage, which just means the car side of a crash, is part of John Doe's case.\n\nKeep them up. {name}, you look very sure. Hold that thought, because the answer is yes and no, and the difference is the whole lesson.\n\nFour ideas. First, the claim follows the vehicle: it belongs to the owner, and John Doe owns no car. Second, the damage is evidence: photos, estimates and supplements show how hard the hit was.\n\nThird, where the owner's claim reaches the client's care, we coordinate. Fourth, everything else about the owner's claim goes to the attorney.\n\nI once watched a new hire open a rental file for a passenger. The passenger was thrilled. The adjuster, not so much.\n\nThe claim follows the car. The damage follows the evidence.",
   "ask": "{name}, from these four ideas, which piece of the Civic's file do you think helps John Doe the most?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "So why does a dented car still matter to a neck injury, even when the car isn't our client's? Let's go phase by phase.\n\nAt intake, a tow from the scene and crush photos flag a serious injury early, so we keep them. In treatment, structural damage matches the injuries the providers write down.\n\nAt the demand, estimates and supplements answer the insurance company's favorite line, that it was a low-impact fender bender.\n\nIn negotiation, the same photos keep the injury from being called minor, and in litigation they become proof for experts.\n\nNow notice what's missing from this table: rental, Loss of Use, the repair argument. Those never show up in John Doe's column.\n\n{name}, you're my scorekeeper for the next slide.\n\nCrush photos answer the fender-bender argument.",
   "ask": "{name}, which phase do you think the low-impact argument shows up in first on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let me tell you whose car this is, because it's not John's.\n\nJohn Doe was the front-seat passenger in Kevin's Escape, and he owns no car at all. The car that got crushed at the curb is Margaret Doe's 2019 Honda Civic.\n\nIts claim runs with Leah Moran at Keystone, under Keystone's $25,000 Property Damage coverage, which sits outside John's $40,000 stack.\n\nSo is Property Damage our case? Not as a claim. But it touches our case in two ways: as evidence of how hard the impact was, and where its dates reach his care.\n\nEverything else is Margaret's, and whether the firm helps her is Atty. Thorne's call, not ours.\n\nAnd a joke from the job, because you've earned one. Every car claim has two owners: the one on the title and the one who called first.\n\nNot our claim, but our evidence.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Margaret asks you tomorrow whether her repair offer is fair, whose question is that?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules, and let's start with how it goes wrong. A Case Manager hears the word car and starts working the car: rental, repair figure, Loss of Use, all of it, on John's file.\n\nThe Case Manager you want to be opens the Police Report first, reads the unit boxes, sees John rode in the Escape and Margaret owns the Civic, and then sorts.\n\nPhotos, the Repair Estimate, any supplement, the tow from the scene: indexed on John's file as evidence. Margaret's rental, Loss of Use, Diminished Value and the repair negotiation: left on her claim and referred.\n\nKeystone accepted Property Damage liability on 02/17/2026, so you record it as a fact, and what it means is Atty. Thorne's to say.\n\nAnd the carve-out still holds. If a car call turns to John's neck, it stops and comes to us.\n\nRead the unit boxes first, then sort.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone's PD adjuster asks John about his neck during a car call. What happens next?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This table is the sort, six rows, and I want you to call each one before I read the middle column.\n\n{name}, photos with the point of impact: ours or Margaret's? Yep, evidence of force, so they go on John's file.\n\nThe Repair Estimate and any supplement: proof of how hard the hit was, same lane. The Police Report's units tell us who rode and who owns, so they get cited in the Case Note.\n\nOkay, now the tricky one. Margaret's rental is hers, but its last covered day takes away John's ride to treatment. So we calendar that date and plan his rides.\n\nLoss of Use and Diminished Value: Margaret's claim, referred. The repair argument: hers, and we never negotiate it.\n\nA Case Manager I trained once called that rental row the sneaky one, and honestly, she was right.\n\nEvidence comes to John's file. Margaret's claim gets referred.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the rental row end up half in our lane and half in Margaret's?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it's three moves, and they happen fast.\n\nA Property Damage document arrives, say the damage photos or an estimate. You could drop it in a folder called car stuff. Please don't. Index it on John's file that day as severity evidence, because at demand time Atty. Thorne will want it in front of Dana Whitfield.\n\nNext, someone asks about the Civic's rental, its Loss of Use or its repair figure. You log the question in their words and refer it to Atty. Thorne the same day, no advice.\n\nThird, a car date touches John's care, like the rental ending. You calendar it as a treatment risk and plan his rides before it.\n\nThat's it. Sort, index, coordinate, refer.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those three moves would you skip if you were in a hurry, and what would it cost?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Do and Don't list for Property Damage, and it's shorter than you'd think.\n\nFirst Do: ask of every PD fact, does it touch our case? Photos of the Escape's crushed front passenger door aren't just car paperwork. That's the door John Doe was sitting behind, so they show the force his body took.\n\nSecond Do: index photos and estimates the day they arrive. A body shop estimate for that passenger side is severity evidence, so it lands in his file the same day.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Never open a rental claim in John's name. The Civic is Margaret Doe's car, so its PD claim and its rental are hers, never his.\n\nAnd never treat Property Damage as just the car. The lazy version files the repair photos with the tow bill and forgets them. The high-value version keeps them where the demand can use them, so the adjuster sees how hard the hit was.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? {name}, why do you think it keeps happening?\n\n{name}, last one to you: where on this file would you look first for proof of how hard the hit was?\n\nThe car is never just the car.",
   "ask": "{name}, before we start: in one line, does Property Damage touch our case, and how?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Your task is to turn John Doe's onboarding directions into tracked tasks in CMS Tasks. For every one, set the owner, the due date, and how you'll check it happened.\n\nFind the card with the wrong owner, reassign it, and write down why it could never have been the client's job in the first place.\n\nHonestly, a card that says the client will chase the body shop is like a card that says the body shop will call back on time. Technically possible, never seen in the wild.\n\nThen schedule the follow-up that confirms he's actually started treatment.\n\nHere's what weak looks like: every card says \"due Friday, owner me, verify: done.\" Here's strong: \"Confirm first therapy visit, owner Case Manager, due in three days, verify by calling the clinic.\" A task with no way to check it is a hope, not a task.\n\nI knew a Case Manager whose whole task list said \"follow up.\" Follow up on what? With whom? Nobody knew, including her.\n\n{name}, read me your first card out loud.\n\nOwner, due date, and a way to check it. All three.",
   "ask": "{name}, for the treatment card, how will you know it actually happened?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/11/2026, five past eight in the morning, and a CMS notice just came in on LSH-2026-MVA-770412.\n\nJohn Doe's Direction Packet was delivered and logged on 02/10/2026, and its directions have been set up as draft task cards for you to review before they go live.\n\nA task card is just a to-do with a name on it. A card can't go live without an owner, a due date, and a way to check it got done.\n\nNow look at what's missing. No treatment start is recorded, and nothing's on the calendar to follow up.\n\nHere's my teaser. One of those cards is assigned to the wrong person, and if it stays that way, nobody will ever do it. I'm not telling you which one.\n\n{name}, you've got first crack at finding it. Read every card carefully.\n\nA direction nobody owns never gets done.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you open the cards, what's the risk of a direction that nobody owns?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Quick self-check before you log it. Every direction has an owner, every task has a due date, and every task has a way to verify it.\n\nDid you find the wrong-owner card, reassign it to the firm, and write down why? Leave a rental task with the client and you'll find him at the counter upgrading to a luxury SUV on a compact-car budget.\n\nIs the treatment follow-up scheduled, with nothing left undated? {name}, tell the room which card was wrong and how you knew.\n\nThat treatment follow-up matters a lot, by the way. It's the bridge into Day 2. Tomorrow, John Doe's gap in treatment becomes impossible to ignore, and this is the task that would have caught it early.\n\nGaps in care are what adjusters love most, because they say the client must not have been that hurt.\n\nSo here's tomorrow's mystery. When we open his file on Day 2, count the days. Somebody's going to find a number that should scare everyone in this room.\n\nThe follow-up task is what catches the gap.",
   "ask": "{name}, if his first appointment slipped, which of your tasks would tell you, and on what date?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-a3": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job, and the order is the lesson. First, open the Police Report and read the unit boxes before you sort anything.\n\nThen take each item in the tray and put it in one of three lanes: evidence for John's injury claim, something to coordinate because it reaches his care, or Margaret's own Property Damage claim. Mark each one refer, coordinate or handle.\n\nIndex the evidence on John's file in Documents. Refer Margaret's questions, and Leah Moran's claimant question, to Atty. Thorne in Tasks. Set the follow-up for the rental's last day. Take John's call.\n\nAnd end your Case Note with one line: does Property Damage touch our case, and how? {name}, heads up, I'll be reading that line out loud.\n\nPolice Report first, then three lanes.",
   "ask": "{name}, which item in the tray do you expect to argue about the most?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/11/2026, and the Property Damage tray is full. Guess how many items, {name}. Six.\n\nLeah Moran at Keystone emails asking us to confirm who the Civic's claimant is, so a rental can be set up once Keystone decides liability.\n\nThen there are the scene and damage photos, the tow record from the scene, the Police Report, a question from Margaret about Loss of Use, and Margaret asking the firm to push Keystone for a higher repair figure.\n\nAnd while you're looking at all that, John calls. He needs a ride to his first appointment, and he wants to know if the rental can be his.\n\nThe tray is labeled urgent, of course. Which is what every tray is labeled.\n\nSix items, one question: whose is it?",
   "ask": "{name}, before you sort anything, which document tells you whose car the Civic is?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, check yourself, and I'm calling on a few of you.\n\nWho rode and who owns, taken from the Police Report, not from memory? The photos, the tow record and the Police Report indexed on John's file as evidence?\n\nThe rental, Loss of Use and the repair figure left on Margaret's claim and referred, with no advice? A follow-up set so the rental's last day lands on John's calendar as a treatment risk, with his rides planned?\n\nJohn told kindly that the rental isn't his, and his ride solved anyway?\n\nA note that just ends with \"sorted\" doesn't tell anybody much. A good one ends with the answer. {name}, read me your last line.\n\nEnd your note with the answer, not the word sorted.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Margaret calls back tomorrow about her repair figure, what's yours to do, and what isn't?"
  }
 },
 "d1m3-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the game show portion of the day! This is your Knowledge Check for onboarding and Property Damage, and every answer comes from John Doe's file, in your own words, typed right under each question.\n\nFirst, the standing instructions and the harm each one prevents. Second, the sort: two items from the car side that feed John's injury claim, and two that stay on Margaret's claim, with the reason.\n\nThird, John asks if Margaret's rental can be his, so tell me what the records show and what you coordinate.\n\n{name}, you're up first. No phoning a friend, and definitely no phoning Keystone.\n\nEvery answer comes from the file.",
   "ask": "{name}, without looking, which item from the Civic's file helps John Doe's claim the most?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the Self-Summary: the principles in your own words, not mine.\n\nOne prompt is the whole module in a sentence. Does Property Damage touch John Doe's case, and how? Write that line first.\n\nThen tell me what you'll do the next time Margaret calls about her car, and name the thing you're least sure of.\n\nA free hint: the answer has the word evidence in it, and it doesn't have the word rental in John's name. {name}, I'll ask you to read yours.\n\nProperty Damage touches our case as evidence, and where it reaches his care.",
   "ask": "{name}, read me your one line: does Property Damage touch our case, and how?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step, the Skill Scale. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each skill, honestly, then submit for review.\n\nThere's no prize for a high number. This isn't social media, so nobody needs your highlight reel.\n\nI'll put your rating next to your score, and if they disagree, we'll talk about it.\n\n{name}, the sorting skill is the one I'll look at first today, because it's the one that keeps Margaret's claim off John's file.\n\nAn honest rating is the useful one.",
   "ask": "{name}, which skill do you expect your self-rating and your score to disagree on?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Early evaluation really comes down to three simple questions. Is there money? Who's responsible? And how is this case likely to move?\n\nCoverage determination is the first one. It means checking which insurance policies apply, what they pay, how high they go, and what they exclude. Basically, is there money to pay the claim?\n\nLiability assessment is the second: who caused the crash and how much, whether it's clear, shared or disputed, and what evidence backs it.\n\nCase projection is the third. It's an early read on risk and where the case is heading, but the value of the case stays with the attorney, always.\n\nOne more idea. Once a case is in a legal setting, Case Management stops being \"I'll do my best\" and becomes strict compliance. Every deadline in the plan goes on the calendar, no exceptions.\n\n{name}, quick guess before I tell you: which of these three questions do you think gets answered wrong most often on brand-new files?\n\nMoney, fault, then forecast. In that order.",
   "ask": "{name}, why can't a projection come before we understand coverage and liability?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "These three pillars only work together, and there's a reason they're in this order. I'll make you guess it at the end.\n\nCoverage asks, is there money to pay the claim? That means valid policies, what type of coverage, the limits, the exclusions and any gaps.\n\nLiability asks, who's responsible, and how much? Who caused it, is it clear, shared or disputed, what's the evidence, and is there comparative negligence, which just means the client might be blamed for part of the crash too.\n\nProjection asks, how is this case likely to move? It pulls from everything: coverage, liability, how bad the injury is, what treatment we expect, and how good our paperwork is.\n\nSo, {name}, why is projection last? Because it leans on the other two.\n\nA projection without coverage and liability is like guessing the score of a game before you know who's playing.\n\nNo projection before coverage and liability.",
   "ask": "{name}, on John Doe's file today, which of these three questions can you actually answer with a document?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game to start. I say a sentence, and you give me a thumbs up if it belongs in a Case Plan, thumbs down if it doesn't. Ready?\n\nThe client has a 14-day gap in treatment. The client is a really nice guy. The other driver ran a red light, according to the Police Report. This case is worth a fortune.\n\n{name}, I saw that face on the last one, so you're my tiebreaker.\n\nSo why this game? The Case Plan is the file's instruction manual. It's what Atty. Thorne reads to set strategy, and it's what every later day of this course works from.\n\nIf the plan hides a risk, the whole strategy gets built on a fake picture, and nobody finds out until it's expensive.\n\nIn a minute I'll show you the plan's layout. One part of it is drawn bigger than everything else, and in red. {name}, keep your eyes peeled for it, because that's the whole lesson in one picture.\n\nA plan that hides a risk builds the strategy on a fake picture.",
   "ask": "{name}, what happens if Atty. Thorne builds a demand strategy without knowing about the 14-day gap?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules, and I'll show you what so-so looks like next to what excellent looks like.\n\nAcceptance comes first. You decide: Accepted, Escalated for review, or Declined, with a reason someone else can follow.\n\nA so-so Case Manager leaves the file in limbo for a week, still \"gathering info.\" An excellent one makes the call, and escalates anything unclear with a short note saying exactly what.\n\nWrite coverage, liability and projection together, and keep the projection preliminary until coverage is confirmed. Liability isn't final until the Police Report, the driver statements and any video back it up, so never tell a client it's a sure thing.\n\nSame goes for the client who says \"I feel fine now\" two weeks before his MRI. Lovely news, not a medical finding, so it doesn't change the plan.\n\nHold a place for the Regulatory Triple-Check, three checks on privacy, Medicare and who pays first, which we clear in the Treatment Phase. File the Three-Pillar plan inside the state's window, often 30 to 60 days, and calendar it.\n\nEvery goal gets a measure, an owner and a date. And the big one: flag bad facts loudly. The plan suggests next steps; value and settlement belong to Atty. Thorne. {name}, here's your test.\n\nFlag bad facts loudly. Leave value to the attorney.",
   "ask": "{name}, “request the MRI” or “worth policy limits”: which one can you write in the plan, and why not the other?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here it is, the reveal. The plan has five bands stacked top to bottom, each with an orange number.\n\nOne is the Case Snapshot and the acceptance decision. Two is coverage, liability and projection. Three is the Regulatory Triple-Check, held open until the Treatment Phase. Four is the Three-Pillar Plan, split into Clinical, Functional and Financial columns.\n\nAnd five, the one I promised you, is Risks and Flags. It's red, it's visibly taller than the rest, and it holds one highlighted chip: 14-Day Treatment Gap.\n\nThat's on purpose. On a Case Plan, the bad news gets more room than the good news.\n\nTo the right, a routing line runs to a card that reads Atty. Elijah Thorne, review, with a calendar chip under it: file within 30 to 60 days.\n\nI once saw a plan where the risks section just said \"none.\" Three weeks later the adjuster found the gap the Case Manager already knew about. That conversation was not fun.\n\n{name}, tell the room why that red band is the biggest one.\n\nOn a Case Plan, the bad news gets the most room.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think the risks band is drawn taller than the band holding the plan itself?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let me take you through a real day on the desk. The verification call is done, so you record the acceptance decision in Case Notes: Accepted, Escalated or Declined, with a reason a co-worker could follow without calling you.\n\nIf your note needs a translator, you've written it like a doctor's prescription.\n\nOnce coverage is confirmed in writing, you open the Action Plan Editor, which is just the screen in the CMS where the plan gets written, and you draft all five bands. The risks band is never empty.\n\nThen you put the filing window on the CMS Calendar and send the plan to Atty. Thorne from the editor, before anything goes out that commits the firm to a position.\n\nWhy does this matter so much? Because that 14-day gap, flagged on day one, is something Atty. Thorne can explain in the demand.\n\nFound by the adjuster on day ninety, it's a reason to cut the offer. Same fact, very different settlement. {name}, you're up first on this one.\n\nFlagged on day one, explained. Found on day ninety, cut.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the very first thing you'd write in the risks band for John Doe, and why that?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Let's close with the Do and Don't list for acceptance and the Case Plan.\n\nTwo Dos. Give each pillar measurable goals, so return to work at Northgate Distribution gets a number and a date, not a hope. And give every decision a reason others can follow. If you mark John Doe's file Escalated, a colleague opening it cold next week should get why in one read.\n\nThree Don'ts. A plan that only covers clinical care, with nothing about work or who pays the bills. Gathering more information at acceptance, when the call is already yours. And letting an uncertain file sit in limbo for weeks.\n\nA timid Case Manager waits until the file feels safe. A strong one sends Atty. Thorne a summary, makes the call, and moves.\n\nAnd I know flagging a risk can feel like admitting the case is weak. It isn't. Flagging early is exactly what makes a case defensible later.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen on a real file?\n\nOkay, your mystery. That 14-Day Treatment Gap chip is sitting in red, and John Doe still hasn't seen a doctor. Nobody has written down what's stopping him.\n\n{name}, by the end of the next block, I want your best theory, backed by something already on the file. {name}, you're checking their evidence.\n\nFlagging early is what makes a case defensible.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's one fact on John Doe's file that might explain why he hasn't seen a doctor?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-tplan": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The assessment has four areas, and I want you to guess which one new Case Managers skip.\n\nFirst, Clinical Evaluation: his physical, thinking and emotional problems, written down the way he tells them and the way the records show them. We never diagnose.\n\nSecond, Social Determinants, a fancy phrase for his life outside the doctor's office: housing, family support, money. John lives with his mother, Margaret Doe, he owns no car, and her Civic was damaged in the crash.\n\nThird, Pre-Morbid Status, which just means how he was before the crash. It's the before photo that makes the after photo mean something, and nothing's on file for it yet.\n\nFourth, the Vocational Outlook: can he go back to his job, or will he need retraining? He's a warehouse shift lead at Northgate Distribution, on 8-hour shifts at $22.00 an hour.\n\n{name}, which one did you guess? Most people skip the social one, and it's usually the one that causes the gap.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why would skipping the social area leave a hole in John Doe's plan?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play Two Truths and a Lie, John Doe edition. I tell you three things about him, you vote on which one is fake.\n\nOne: he owns his own car. Two: he started a new job last month. Three: he declined the ambulance after the crash. Hands up for one. Two. Three. {name}, are you voting with the crowd or against it?\n\nThe lie is number one. John Doe doesn't own a car, and that one little fact might be the most important thing in this whole block.\n\nAssessment is more than reading medical records. Back at intake, on 02/09/2026, there weren't any. There's the EMS Run Report, the ambulance crew's write-up, which says restrained front-seat passenger, neck stiffness, declined transport. And there's John himself.\n\nA plan that only treats the chart is like a map with no roads on it. It misses what actually stops people getting care: no ride, a new job he's scared to miss, a worry about who pays.\n\nThat's how a Treatment Gap is born. Treat the person, not just the chart.",
   "ask": "{name}, what could stop John Doe from getting to a first appointment this week, and how would you find out?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Let's put the rules together. \"Neck pain, follow up. Done.\" That's the bare-minimum assessment, and it's nowhere near enough.\n\nA real assessment looks at the whole person, and on this file that started with the intake call and the Run Report. You write medical findings in his words and the record's words, never your own diagnosis.\n\nYou dig into his life: who he lives with, how he'll get to care, whether cost is holding him back. You ask about every old injury, treatment and claim, with dates and doctors, and you request those records yourself.\n\nWhy? If the defense finds an old back injury before we do, it looks like we were hiding it, and that can knock real money off a settlement.\n\nYou write down what his job asks of his body. Then you build SMART goals the doctor confirms, map the four care groups, and calendar every risk before it turns into a gap.\n\nThe plan coordinates care. It never prescribes it.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a client mentions an old back injury, what do you write down, and what don't you write?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the desk. When the intake call is done, you record the four-area assessment in Case Notes, every area dated, and anything you don't know yet is listed as an open question.\n\nIf an old injury comes up, you request those records through Medical Records Requests. The records office will play you the same hold song until you know every word, so request early, and write the before-the-crash picture as plain facts: body part, dates, doctor, outcome.\n\nThen you open the Action Plan Editor and write the SMART goals and the four care groups into the Clinical and Functional pillars, each with a measure, a date and an owner, and you ask the first doctor to confirm them.\n\nWhen you spot a life problem, like no ride to care, you calendar it as a treatment risk and flag it before it becomes a gap.\n\nTrue story. A client of mine missed three weeks of therapy because his ride was his cousin, and the cousin got a girlfriend. Three weeks. Nobody asked, and that gap cost him on the offer.\n\n{name}, don't let a cousin's love life cost John Doe money.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you set up this week so John Doe's ride never becomes a gap?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-tmap": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four ideas, kept simple.\n\nThe Standard Treatment Map is the usual route of care after a crash, stop by stop: the ambulance and the emergency room, then imaging like X-rays and MRIs, then chiropractic care and therapy, then orthopedics, which are bone and joint specialists, and pain management, and only when it's serious, surgery and the care after it.\n\nIf a stop gets skipped or taken out of order, there needs to be a medical reason on file.\n\nThe Clinical Pathway asks one question at every check-in: is he getting better? Yes, care continues. No, and we're in what's called the variance loop, and what happens next is the doctor's decision. Variance analysis just means figuring out why he's off track before the insurer stops paying.\n\nAnd Reasonable and Necessary is the insurer's test for paying a bill: is this normal care for this kind of injury, and is he actually doing more, not just saying he hurts less?\n\n{name}, keep that last part in your pocket. Doing more beats feeling better.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would an insurer care more about a range-of-motion number than the client saying he feels better?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick show of hands. Who here has ever used a map app and ignored the route because you thought you knew a shortcut? Keep them up if it actually worked.\n\nYeah, that's about what I thought. {name}, I saw your hand go down fast.\n\nTreatment works the same way. Every adjuster, every IME doctor, which is a doctor the insurance company hires to examine our client, and every defense lawyer will hold John Doe's records up against a standard map of how recovery is supposed to go.\n\nWhen care drifts off that map, that drift is the number one reason insurers deny treatment and order an IME.\n\nSo you've got a choice. You can sit back and find out about the drift when the denial letter shows up. Or you can know the map, spot the drift first, ask the doctor why, and have the answer on file before anyone turns it into an argument.\n\nAnd there's one week on this map that adjusters circle in red. I'll tell you which one shortly.\n\nKnow the map before they use it against you.",
   "ask": "{name}, when a client's care drifts off schedule, who usually notices first: us, the adjuster, or defense counsel?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how you work the map. One Case Manager checks in, writes \"client still treating,\" and hangs up. That tells nobody anything.\n\nThe one you want to be counts the phase from the date of loss, counts the weeks of care from the first visit, puts him on the map every single check-in, and asks: is he improving?\n\nIf not, you name the type of problem before anyone acts on it. Clinical, meaning a medical issue. Psychosocial, meaning life or mood is getting in the way. Or systemic, meaning the system's stuck, like an insurance approval that never came, or a clinic booked solid until next spring, and that's never the client's fault.\n\nYou get the doctor's measurements on file. You raise concerns at week 6 and the 12-week plateau, not after a denial. A real red flag goes into A-C-T, Assess, Coordinate, Track, which you'll learn later today.\n\nWhy bother? A demand that explains every bump in treatment gets paid. One with unexplained bumps gets cut.\n\nAnd through it all, the doctor decides care and Atty. Thorne decides what it means for the case.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the difference between asking a doctor about a specialist and telling John Doe he needs one?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job, it looks like this. At every check-in with John Doe or a doctor's office, you open the treatment timeline in the CMS and write down his phase, his week from the date of loss and his week of care, and whether he's improving, with the date.\n\nIf he isn't, or a stop gets skipped, you label the type of problem and call the doctor's office to ask what's planned. That answer goes in Case Notes.\n\nAt week 6 of care and every 30 days after, you request the measurements. And when a problem could cost the claim, you send a Risk Flag to Atty. Thorne the same day, in the map's language, facts only.\n\nHere's the payoff. Months from now, when the adjuster asks why therapy paused in week 4, the answer is already in the file, dated, from the doctor.\n\nThat's the difference between defending a number and watching it get cut. {name}, let's see how sharp your instincts are.\n\nExplain every bump before someone else does.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would make you flag a problem to Atty. Thorne the same day rather than at the next status report?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-tcare": {
  "map": {
   "say": "Here's the road for this lesson, five stops.\n\nFirst, you read the records for red flags, meaning warning signs, by specialty: physical therapy, surgery, medication and mental health.\n\nSecond, you listen on your calls for yellow flags, which are life and mood problems that get in the way of recovery, and you write them down in his own words.\n\nThird, you talk using OARS, which stands for open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries. Don't worry, I'll show you what each one sounds like.\n\nFourth, you handle the mid-treatment plateau, the point where he stops improving and starts losing interest, by bringing him back to his own goal and asking whether the plan still fits.\n\nAnd last, you send it to the right person. Medical danger goes to the doctor, legal danger goes to Atty. Thorne, and then A-C-T, that's Assess, Coordinate and Track, which we'll run properly later today.\n\n{name}, before we start, which stop do you think will be hardest for you?",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these five stops do you think a busy Case Manager skips first?"
  },
  "theory": {
   "say": "You can't spot something off-track if you don't know what on-track looks like, so a Case Manager has to know the standard treatment patterns.\n\nA red flag is a step away from that pattern, and it signals one of three things: medical danger, a case that's stuck, or treatment being stretched out just to bill more.\n\nPhysical therapy is the starting point of most care, so its flags tell you the case is stalling or being over-treated. Surgery is the most valuable part of a claim, so its flags are about safety and whether the care met the normal standard.\n\nOkay, the reveal. The specialty adjusters pick apart hardest is pain management and medication, because of opioid rules and audits on whether the drugs were truly needed.\n\nMedical danger, like a fever after surgery, goes to the doctor the same day. Legal danger goes to Atty. Thorne. {name}, did you guess medication? Be honest.\n\nKnow normal, so you can spot off-track.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think adjusters look hardest at medication, of all the specialties?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game time. Would you rather have a client who calls you every single day, or a client who never calls at all? Left side of the room, every day. Right side, never. Go.\n\n{name}, defend your side in one sentence.\n\nHere's the twist. The client who never calls is the scary one, because silence is where cases quietly fall apart. Like a smoke detector with no battery, no noise doesn't mean no fire.\n\nAt intake John Doe hadn't started treatment. All we know medically is the EMS report from 02/06/2026: neck stiffness, and he declined transport.\n\nBut once care begins, you're the one person on this file who reads every record as it comes in and hears from him between visits.\n\nA red flag missed in a record, a yellow flag missed on a call, or a stall nobody talks about can quietly pull him off the plan his doctors set, and leave Atty. Thorne without the facts he needs.\n\nSo, two tools: a watch-list and a way of talking. And one specialty on that watch-list gets picked apart by adjusters harder than any other. Hold your guess.",
   "ask": "{name}, between appointments, who else on this file is actually hearing from John Doe?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's the line. Filing the therapy notes without reading them is how flags slip by. Reading every note against the watch-list is how you catch them. You spot a red flag and report it, and the doctor judges it.\n\nIn therapy, watch for no gain after 12 sessions, passive-only care, and two or more missed visits. In surgery, watch for surgery before simpler care was tried, a left and right mix-up, and anything after surgery that someone wants to leave until the follow-up.\n\nIn medication, watch for what's called the Holy Trinity, that opioid, relaxant and sedative combo, plus rising doses and drug test mismatches. Raise them with the prescriber as facts and a question, never an accusation.\n\nIn mental health and head injuries, write down the date every symptom was first reported, because a symptom that shows up late looks made up to the defense.\n\nRecord yellow flags in his words. And at the plateau, bring him back to his goal.\n\nIf he's not reaching it, it's usually the plan, not the person.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a client says his pain pills make him drowsy at work, what do you say to him, and who do you call?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it looks on the desk. Every time a treatment record, prescription record or bill comes in, usually by fax, forty pages at a time, in an order only the fax machine understands, you read it against the red-flag list.\n\nYou log any flag in Risk Flags, with the record it came from and the date you first saw it.\n\nIf it's medical danger, like a fever after surgery or a medication concern, you call the doctor's office that same day, note the call in Case Notes, run A-C-T and tell Atty. Thorne.\n\nOnce John Doe's care starts, every check-in runs on OARS, with specific questions about visits, pain and what he can actually do. Any yellow flag goes in his own words, and the GIRP note, a structured call note covering goal, intervention, response and plan, gets written the same day.\n\nWhy does the date you first saw it matter? Because months later, someone will ask when the firm knew. A dated log protects the client, the firm, and the settlement.\n\n{name}, here's the question that ties it together.",
   "ask": "{name}, why log the date you first saw a flag, and not only the date on the record?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-emc": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game. True or false: car insurance works the same in every state. {name}, you're first.\n\nIf you said true, welcome to the club of every new Case Manager ever. It's false. PIP, the client's own insurance that pays Medical Bills no matter who caused the crash, is written state by state.\n\nAnd Florida has a rule that can decide, within days of the accident, whether PIP pays any medical at all, and whether the client has $2,500 or $10,000 to draw on. It's called the Emergency Medical Condition rule, EMC for short. Miss it and no demand letter fixes it later.\n\nFlorida: the only state where the insurance rules have as many twists as the weather.\n\nNow, John Doe's file is in Riverton. Riverton is an add-on PIP state with no EMC rule, so his $5,000 of Harbor Point PIP isn't capped by one. But the day you open a Florida file, the clock starts at the date of loss, and you're the one who calendars it.\n\nThere's one person on John's own file who could never make the EMC call. I'll tell you who in a minute.\n\nCheck the state before you apply the rule.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you apply any PIP rule to a file, what's the first fact you need to check, and why that one?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's Florida's rule, Fla. Stat. section 627.736. PIP is $10,000, paying 80% of reasonable medical expenses and 60% of lost income.\n\nInitial care has to happen within 14 days after the accident, or PIP pays nothing for medical. Think of it like a store coupon with an expiration date: day 15, and it's just paper.\n\nThe full $10,000 is there only if an MD, a DO, a dentist, a physician assistant or an advanced practice registered nurse determines there was an Emergency Medical Condition, meaning an injury serious enough that going without care could seriously harm the client. Without that finding, medical stops at $2,500.\n\nA chiropractor can give the initial care but can't make the EMC finding, and massage and acupuncture aren't covered. So there's the reveal: John's chiropractor couldn't make that call.\n\nHad John's file been in Florida, 02/20/2026 was day 14, the last day inside the window. Don't hope he gets around to a doctor. Have day 14 on the calendar before he's even home from the tow yard.\n\nAnd always confirm the current statute, because legislatures love a sequel. Fourteen days, and the right provider.",
   "ask": "{name}, a Florida client is first seen on day 14 by a physician assistant. What does PIP pay, and why?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run a Florida file. At intake you calendar the 14-day initial-care deadline from the date of loss, and you tell the client that deadline and where and when care is available. That's facts and logistics, never a medical recommendation.\n\nWhen the first record arrives, you check three things: the date against day 14, who saw him, and whether an EMC determination is written down. If a qualifying provider's record doesn't have one, you ask the office whether it was assessed and request it in the records.\n\nYou keep the $2,500 or $10,000 bucket current in the PIP Log, our running record of every PIP payment, and a missing EMC or a late first visit goes to Atty. Thorne the same day. The provider decides the EMC; Atty. Thorne decides what it means.\n\nI once watched a Florida client lose every dollar of PIP medical because his first visit landed on day 15. He said he was waiting for the swelling to go down. The swelling went down. So did his coverage.\n\nYour mystery for the next block: what could you have told that client on day one, without giving medical advice, that would have changed everything? {name}, I want your exact words.",
   "ask": "{name}, a Florida client's first visit is with a chiropractor on day 10. What's covered, and what's still missing?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Flag the Treatment Gap in two places: the Risk Flags in the Case File, and the risks band of the Action Plan Editor, which is just the section of the plan where we list everything that could hurt the case.\n\nCompute it exactly. \"About two weeks\" is a shrug. 02/06/2026 to 02/20/2026, fourteen days, is a fact, and you set a watch for any later break in care.\n\nThen list the likely causes you'll look into, the steps you need before the gap can be explained, and route the flag to Atty. Thorne.\n\nI once had a Case Manager write \"client seemed busy\" as the reason for a gap. The adjuster quoted that line back to us in the offer letter. We spent a month digging out of three words.\n\n{name}, you're up first. What's the exact date range you're typing in, and where does each date come from?\n\nExact dates, facts only, routed today.",
   "ask": "{name}, what are three likely reasons a client goes two weeks before seeing anyone, and how would you confirm each without guessing for him?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Quick game first. Hands up if you've ever put off going to the doctor because you were busy. Keep them up if it was more than a week.\n\nLook around. Half the room. Hold that thought, because an insurance adjuster thinks every one of you was faking.\n\nHere's what just landed. On 03/13/2026, a CMS notice: Riverton Urgent Care's 02/20/2026 note and Riverton Spine & Rehab's visits since 02/24/2026 are now on the treatment timeline, right beside the 02/06/2026 EMS Run Report.\n\n{name}, the crash was 02/06/2026. When was John Doe first seen? Count it out loud.\n\nFourteen days. That's a Treatment Gap, which just means days where an injured person got no care at all. Picture a blank page in a diary, and the adjuster gets to write whatever story he likes on it.\n\nNow read the last line of the notice. Risk Flags: none recorded. Nobody has caught it. Not the system, and not the adjuster, whose out-of-office reply has said \"back Monday\" since February.\n\nEverything on Days 3 to 5 leans on this flag being right. Today, it's yours.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you call John Doe, what documents could show where he was and what he was doing in those fourteen days?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, let's check it the way the adjuster would.\n\n{name}, read me your gap. It should say fourteen days, 02/06/2026 to 02/20/2026, not \"about two weeks.\" Is a watch set for the next break in care, so each one gets documented the same way?\n\nThe risks band holds facts only: the dates, the length, and a note that John Doe's own explanation and the papers behind it are still to come. Nothing about what the gap will do to the claim; that's Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nYour investigation steps are listed, his own account first, then work schedules, appointment records, anything in writing. And it's routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\nWhy am I this picky? When an adjuster finds a gap before we do, we don't get a phone call. We get a very polite email, and then a lower offer. A gap we flagged and explained early hurts far less than one they find first.\n\nSo, your mystery. Something kept John Doe out of care for fourteen days, and tomorrow we find out what. {name}, what's your best guess, and what on the file backs it?",
   "ask": "{name}, did you set a watch for the next break in care, and where will it show up?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-l2": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick one, true or false? Getting your plan sent back with comments means you did a bad job.\n\n{name}, true or false? Hands up for true. Okay, every hand that went up, put it down, because it's false.\n\nHere's the thing. A plan sitting in draft does nobody any good. It only works once Atty. Thorne has seen it and the file runs on an approved version.\n\nThat step of sending it to him is called routing, and it's where your work becomes approved work. So routing isn't paperwork.\n\nYes, your plan will sit in an attorney's review queue, and yes, some days that queue moves at the speed of a Monday morning. It still beats a plan nobody ever saw. And being sent back is the system working.\n\nI once trained someone so scared of getting comments that she quietly left the scariest risk off her plan. Guess which risk the adjuster found first? Yep.\n\nBy the end of this, you'll know the one move that keeps that from ever happening to you.\n\nSent back with comments is the system working.",
   "ask": "{name}, how would you feel if your first plan came back with comments, and what would you do next?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Draft in the section templates, not your own made-up layout. Put each task's owner and date in their own fields, not buried in a sentence.\n\nRoute for review before you do anything that speaks for the firm outside the office. When a plan comes back, answer every comment and re-route. Never run a plan that's half fixed.\n\nRecord the approved version, because the file runs on the approved plan, not your latest draft. And when an important fact changes, re-route.\n\nCompare two notes. \"Updated the plan, it's fine.\" Versus \"Marked version two superseded, drafted version three with the new fact, routed to Atty. Thorne at 2:15.\"\n\nNotice the second one never says \"per my last email.\" The version history says that for you, politely. And it's the one that holds up when someone asks what you knew and when.\n\nKeep notes clean and factual, legal strategy only in the legal track, and legal questions go to Atty. Thorne.\n\nThe file runs on the approved version, not your latest draft.",
   "ask": "{name}, right now, which version of the plan is John Doe's file running against?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's follow one plan through its life. You've drafted all five bands, so you hit route for review, and the CMS stamps the time.\n\nIt comes back with comments. You fix every single one under Revisions and re-route.\n\nThen, after it's approved, a big fact changes. The tempting move is to open the approved plan and just type over it. Please don't.\n\nI've seen that exact move turn a deposition into an hour of \"So what did the plan say before you changed it?\"\n\nInstead, mark it superseded under Version and route a new one, so the old version is kept and the new one goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nWhy does it matter? Because a clean version history tells the adjuster this firm knows exactly what it knew and when.\n\n{name}, here's your challenge: what fact on John Doe's file do you think will force the first re-route?\n\nNever type over an approved plan. Supersede it.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a fact changes after approval, what's the very first button you click?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You're drafting the complete Case Plan for John Doe v. Robert Smith in the Action Plan Editor. All five bands.\n\nThe acceptance decision with its reason. Coverage as a stack, in the order it pays, plus liability, which just means who's at fault, and an early projection. The triple-check, held open until the Treatment Phase.\n\nClinical, Functional and Financial pillars with goals you can measure, every task with an owner and a date. And the risks band, with the 14-day gap and the risk of a later break in care, right up front. Docket the filing window, then route it.\n\n\"Follow up with doctor\" is how a task sits on your list so long it starts asking for its own desk. \"Call Riverton Spine & Rehab for visit notes, owner me, due Friday\" actually gets done.\n\nAnd a hint: the route button stays locked until every band is complete. {name}, if you can't route, that's the plan telling you something.\n\nEvery task needs a who and a when.",
   "ask": "{name}, if your route button is still locked, which band do you think is incomplete, and how would you check?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Little guessing game. {name}, how many questions do you think Atty. Thorne should have to ask you after reading your Case Plan? Shout a number.\n\nThe answer is zero, and that's the whole activity. This is the gate out of Claim Set Up, not a wrap-up.\n\nEverything is in the file: parties mapped, the $40,000 stack verified, the onboarding packet delivered and tracked, and the 14-day Treatment Gap flagged. The stack, by the way, is just the layers of insurance stacked up to pay this claim.\n\nAnd on 03/16/2026, this lands from Atty. Thorne: he needs the full Case Plan on John Doe, all five bands, routed to him by end of day.\n\nThe acceptance decision and why, the stack in order with a rough early estimate, liability, and the triple-check on privacy, Medicare and who pays first, held open.\n\nThen the three pillars, clinical, functional and financial, with owners and dates, and the risks band. Docket the filing window.\n\nIf he has to ask, it isn't finished.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's one thing that would make Atty. Thorne come back to you with a question?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Read your plan the way Atty. Thorne will. {name}, read me your snapshot: parties, 02/06/2026, statute of limitations 02/06/2028, which is the deadline to file the lawsuit, and the client as a passenger.\n\nAcceptance decision with its reason. Coverage at $10,000, $20,000 and $10,000, total $40,000, in order: BI, the other driver's policy, then Host UM, from the car John rode in, then Relative UM, from his household relative's policy. The projection is marked preliminary, with no number promised before coverage is confirmed.\n\nLiability preliminary until the Police Report and driver statements back it up. Triple-check held open. Every task with an owner and a date. Gap up front. Plan routed with a timestamp.\n\nAnd if Atty. Thorne does ask you something, the answer can't be \"I'll have to check with my supervisor.\" On this file, you're the one who knows.\n\nNow the gate: LORs out and logged, stack verified at $40,000, gap flagged, plan routed. All four, or the Treatment Phase doesn't start.\n\nI once saw a whole demand delayed a month because one LOR was never logged. Nobody knew it was missing.\n\nFour done, or the file doesn't move.",
   "ask": "{name}, what question could Atty. Thorne still have after reading your plan, and where in the plan would you answer it?"
  }
 },
 "d1m4-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the game show! Today's category is Claim Set Up, and the prize is bragging rights for the rest of the week.\n\nHere's how it works. You answer from the file, in your own words, typed under each question, and then I'll pull a few contestants up to read theirs.\n\nQuestion one: lay out the five bands of the Case Plan and what goes in each, including the three pillars, clinical, functional and financial, or body, life and money.\n\nQuestion two: explain why a bad fact buried in the middle of a plan hasn't really been told to Atty. Thorne at all.\n\nQuestion three: state John Doe's coverage exactly as it belongs in the plan, the layers, limits, total, the order they pay in, and where the projection stands.\n\n{name}, you're my first contestant on question two. {name}, you've got question three. No pressure, but the whole room is your studio audience.\n\nCoffee refills are allowed between questions. I'm a Case Manager, not a monster.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you had to bury one bad fact so nobody noticed, where would you hide it, and why is that exactly the wrong instinct?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the Self-Summary, in your own words, and one last scenario for the day.\n\nIt's 03/11/2026. Keystone still hasn't confirmed its limit in writing, and John Doe started treatment fourteen days after the crash. {name}, what three things do you do today, in what order, and what goes in the risks band?\n\nA war story to help. I had a Case Manager who took a carrier's word on the phone that the limit was what we thought. Never got it in writing. Months later, a different adjuster said the policy was smaller. Guess who had nothing to prove it?\n\nThe only thing on the calendar for that limit was a reminder that said \"get it in writing,\" snoozed eleven times.\n\nThe plan is the foundation for everything that comes next. Every bill and every lien check in the Treatment Phase reads from the stack you verified today.\n\nGet it wrong now, and every number after it is wrong too.",
   "ask": "{name}, if an approved plan changes after a new fact arrives, do you edit it or re-route it, and why?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last thing for Claim Set Up: the Skill Scale. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each skill, then submit it. I'll compare your ratings with your scores across Intake and Claim Set Up, and we'll talk about any big gaps.\n\nBefore anyone heads to the Treatment Phase, let's say the gate out loud together. LORs, the talk-to-our-lawyer letters, out and logged. The three-layer stack of BI, Host UM and Relative UM verified at $40,000. The gap flagged. Plan routed.\n\nNow, your mystery. We have three insurance companies on this claim, and one still hasn't put its limit in writing. Our letters asked every carrier to commit, and in the Treatment Phase we start chasing John Doe's care while we wait.\n\n{name}, your homework question: if Keystone comes back with a smaller number than we expect, what happens to that $40,000, and who do you tell first?",
   "ask": "{name}, looking at the four gate items, which one are you least confident you've truly cleared, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-l1": {
  "map": {
   "say": "Before the detail, here's the road, five stops.\n\nOne, draft one LOR per insurance company on the firm's template, and each one names that company's own insured, meaning their own customer.\n\nTwo, each letter asks for the limits and the Declaration Page, which is the policy's one-page summary, and asks them to preserve photos, statements, video and the car's data. Three, protect the client: the letter to the other driver's insurance gets his name and nothing more.\n\nFour, send each one the way that company prefers, signed by Atty. Thorne and logged by you. Five, if nobody acknowledges it by the third business day, you follow up.\n\nFive steps, three carriers, one day.\n\nAnd my favorite real-life moment on step four: the letter is ready, the clock is ticking, and the one person who has to sign it is always, always at lunch. {name}, that's why you plan the signature before the deadline, not at it.\n\nPlan the signature before the deadline.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these five stops do you think gets skipped most when the office is busy?"
  },
  "theory": {
   "say": "Claim set-up is really coverage determination. In other words, finding out which policies apply, how much they pay, and what they won't cover.\n\nThe LOR is how the firm gets onto each one. It's formal notice that we represent the client, and it moves all contact onto us.\n\nCoverage determination comes down to four questions. Is the coverage valid? What type applies? What are the limits? Are there exclusions, meaning things the policy refuses to cover? Every request in an LOR chases one of those answers.\n\nThen there's liability assessment, which asks who's legally responsible and by how much. Atty. Thorne weighs that; you secure the proof.\n\nAnd remember first-party versus third-party. The third-party insurer, Robert Smith's, is on the other side. The first-party policies cover our client. So each letter reads differently.\n\nOne crash, three insurance companies, and every one of them is convinced one of the others should be paying. {name}, hang on to that picture.\n\nEvery letter is written for the reader on the other end.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would a letter to Robert Smith's carrier read differently from one to Harbor Point?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game time. Picture five envelopes on this table. On 02/17/2026, five letters leave John Doe's file.\n\nYou've got ten seconds. Each of you, pick the envelope you think costs John Doe the most if it's written wrong.\n\n{name}, you go first, and you have to defend it. {name}, you disagree, I can see it on your face. Good, argue it out.\n\nOkay, here's the thing. The Letter of Representation, the LOR, is the letter that tells an insurance company, from now on, you talk to us, not our client. It's the moment every conversation moves off John Doe and onto the firm.\n\nOne letter wrong, the wrong insured named, a UM carrier nobody noticed, a notice that accidentally reads like a demand for money, and that mistake follows the claim all the way to the end. It can put $30,000 of UM coverage at risk.\n\nBy the end of this lesson you'll know which envelope wins, and I bet it's not the one most of you picked.\n\nOne wrong letter can follow the claim all the way to the end.",
   "ask": "{name}, of the five letters going out on 02/17/2026, which one, written wrong, costs John Doe the most?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules, and they're specific. Send the LOR once the retainer's signed, and early. The claims opened by phone 02/16 to 02/17/2026, and the LORs went out 02/17/2026.\n\nUse the firm template and address the adjuster by name, like “Attn: Dana Whitfield”. With no adjuster yet, it's “Attn: Claims Department”, which is Cardinal State today.\n\nEach letter names the client, the date of loss, the claim number and that carrier's own insured: Robert Smith for Keystone, Kevin Alvarez for Harbor Point, Margaret Doe for Cardinal State. Say he was injured, a Demand Package will follow, and all contact goes to the firm. Nothing more.\n\nAsk for limits, Declaration Pages and preservation, including the event data recorder. Notify all three stack carriers now, because late notice hands an insurer an excuse not to pay. But notice isn't a demand.\n\nKeystone gets his name only, plus the Property Damage carve-out, the one exception that lets Keystone talk about the car and only the car, for Leah Moran with 5 business days to inspect.\n\nAtty. Thorne signs, and you log delivery. That signature line is the legal line. {name}, you draft, you send, you never sign.\n\nYou draft, you send, Atty. Thorne signs.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does Cardinal State get a letter today, when it pays nothing until BI and Host UM are done?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run it the way it actually happened on this file. The claims opened by phone on 02/16 and 02/17/2026.\n\nFor each, you logged the claim number, the adjuster or “none yet”, and the preferred delivery method in the Insurance tab and the Communications Log.\n\nThen the LOR set gets drafted, and all five letters go to Atty. Thorne for signature through Documents, Outgoing, with a task attached. Not one is signed by you.\n\nThe letters go out on 02/17/2026. You log the method and the time, and calendar the acknowledgement check, with a follow-up dated 02/20/2026 for anybody still silent.\n\nWhen an acknowledgement arrives, you file it and record any coverage statement or limit with its source and date.\n\nMonths from now, this is what saves you. If an insurer says, “We never got notice,” your log with the date and the time ends the argument in one sentence. Without it, you're arguing with a $30,000 hole in the stack.\n\n{name}, which log entry would you want to show them?\n\nA dated log ends the we-never-got-it argument.",
   "ask": "{name}, it's 02/20/2026 and Cardinal State still hasn't acknowledged. What exactly do you do and log?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. You're building the complete Policy Stack for John Doe v. Robert Smith in the CMS Insurance tab.\n\nEnter all three layers in the order they pay, with the insurer, policy number, claim number, limit and adjuster for each, and mark each one first-party or third-party.\n\nIn the Comments box, write the insurer's actual name, the driver's name where it's different from the owner's, and the adjuster's details, or say plainly that none is assigned yet.\n\nThen check the total, record the order the layers must be pursued in, and mark the projection preliminary, meaning it's a forecast and not a promise, until every limit's confirmed in writing.\n\nPlease don't enter these as three equal claims. UM owes nothing until BI is used up.\n\n{name}, you're entering BI. {name}, you're on the two UM layers. And I promise at least one of these insurers will ask you to spell “Doe.” Twice.\n\nThree numbers in boxes isn't a stack. A real stack tells Atty. Thorne the order, the source, and what's still a guess.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, what could happen if the BI claim were settled without the UM insurers' written consent?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Quick warm-up. I read three numbers, you shout which one pays first: $20,000, $10,000, $10,000. {name}, go. Interesting. Hold that answer.\n\nIt's the morning of 02/17/2026, and the letters haven't gone out yet. All three insurance companies opened claims by phone on 02/16 and 02/17 and told us their limits on those set-up calls: Keystone Mutual's BI, Harbor Point's Host UM, and Cardinal State's Relative UM, with no adjuster assigned there yet.\n\nHere's the catch. Nothing is confirmed in writing. Today's LORs will ask each one for its Declaration Page, and Keystone will want John Doe's signed authorizations first.\n\nNothing's been entered in the Insurance tab yet, and the letters can't pull the insurance details until it is.\n\nNow the reveal. Say the order with me, bottom-up: BI pays first, and only once it's used up does Host UM open. Think of a stack of buckets, where the top one only fills when the one below overflows.\n\nSo the $10,000 BI pays first, even though it's the smallest. {name}, did you get it?\n\nOrder beats size. Every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, before any letter goes out, is the $10,000 BI limit confirmed or still preliminary, and how do you know?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Let's check the stack before you log it, and I'm calling on people.\n\n{name}, are all three layers there, BI, Host UM and Relative UM, with limits of $10,000, $20,000 and $10,000, adding up to $40,000? Is the order recorded as BI, then Host UM, then Relative UM? BI marked third-party, both UM layers first-party?\n\nPolicy and claim numbers filled in for every layer, and the Relative UM adjuster recorded as “No adjuster assigned yet,” not left blank? The source of each limit noted, the set-up call for now, with the Declaration Page to be requested in the LOR? And the projection marked preliminary?\n\nDon't predict value before coverage is confirmed, and don't quote a likely limit to the client. Clients hear a number once and it gets tattooed on their brain.\n\nA right total is nice. A right total where you can prove where every number came from is the job.\n\nHere's your mystery for the letters: one layer will need something signed by John Doe before it tells us anything at all.",
   "ask": "{name}, if your total isn't $40,000, which rung is in the wrong place, and how would you find it?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the job. Generate John Doe's full letter set on the firm template in CMS Documents, under Outgoing.\n\nThat's LORs to Keystone for BI with the Property Damage carve-out, to Harbor Point for Host UM and PIP, and to Cardinal State for Relative UM, plus notices to Bluewater and Peach State Health Plan.\n\nGive each the right Attn line, insured, claim, policy or member number, and the date of loss, with requests for limits, Declaration Pages, any other coverage and preservation.\n\nRoute the set to Atty. Thorne for signature, send each the way that company prefers from the Email Workspace, and calendar the acknowledgement checks.\n\nWhere I'm grading your judgment: what Keystone's letter leaves out, and why no UM letter asks for money. {name}, you've got Keystone. {name}, you've got the two UM letters, and you'll explain your choices to the room.\n\nThink of Keystone like a neighbor with a perfect memory. Everything we tell Keystone, Keystone remembers forever.\n\nFive letters sent is the floor. Five letters that each give that insurer exactly what it needs, and not one word more, is the job.",
   "ask": "{name}, what goes in the Attn line for Cardinal State, and why not a name?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Before I show you what just landed, a quick game: two truths and a lie. One, Cardinal State has an adjuster assigned. Two, Leah Moran accepted Property Damage liability this morning. Three, John Doe hasn't started treatment. {name}, which is the lie?\n\nOkay, the reveal. It's 02/17/2026, and a message from Atty. Thorne just landed: letters of representation go out today.\n\nWhere the claims stand: Keystone BI, KM-26-440871, with Dana Whitfield, and Keystone PD with Leah Moran, who did accept PD liability this morning. Harbor Point, HP-26-7741, with Marcus Bell, for Host UM and PIP. And Cardinal State, CS-26-20355, with no adjuster yet. That's the lie.\n\nHe wants the three LORs, plus notices to Bluewater and Peach State, on his desk for signature today. Preferred delivery methods are in the Communications Log.\n\nIn real life, “on his desk today” means before the one person who signs everything disappears to lunch. Letters that need a signature can sense lunchtime.\n\n{name}, you've got the claim numbers and the call log. That's all you need.\n\nFive letters, one signature, today.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you draft a word, which two letters do you think need the most care, and why?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your set before you log it. {name}, count with me: LORs to Keystone, Harbor Point and Cardinal State, notices to Bluewater and Peach State. Five.\n\nAttn lines: Dana Whitfield, Marcus Bell, and “Attn: Claims Department” for Cardinal State.\n\nNow the insured lines, and look closely, because these get swapped all the time: Robert Smith, Kevin Alvarez, Margaret Doe, and Margaret Doe again on Bluewater, because the Civic and its policy are hers.\n\nSubject lines carry the claim number and 02/06/2026; Peach State's carries PSH-55017742 and GRP-41208. Keystone gets his name only, with the carve-out limited to the Civic and 5 business days to inspect.\n\nNeither UM letter asks for money. Bluewater's asks for the signed rejection form from 03/14/2024. John Doe's told to preserve the doorbell-camera video.\n\nEvery letter went to Atty. Thorne for signature, sent 02/17/2026, with a follow-up on 02/20/2026. A letter he didn't sign fails, however beautiful it is.\n\nMystery for later: one of these letters will get its answer a full month after the others. Which one would you bet on?",
   "ask": "{name}, on your Harbor Point and Cardinal State letters, whose name is in the insured line, and why that person?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Coverage verification, simply put, is turning “someone told me” into “here's the document,” with every limit, form and exclusion traced to the paper that proves it.\n\nThe Coverage Matrix is one row per insurance company, and it's the file's single coverage record. Status, source and date on every row show what's proven.\n\nThe statuses run reported, noticed, acknowledged, disclosed, confirmed in writing, and only the last one is proof. Every earlier one is a lead.\n\nThe Declaration Page, the Dec Page, is the policy's own summary of who and what it covers, and for how much. It's the one page in insurance written to be read, and even then the important line is somewhere near the bottom, in a font that requires reading glasses.\n\nThen limit types. Per person caps one injured person. Per accident caps everyone in the crash put together. A combined single limit is one number for all of it.\n\n{name}, those three phrases sound alike and pay very differently.\n\nOnly confirmed in writing counts as proof.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone's BI is $10,000 per person. Whose claim does that figure cap?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play Guess the Status. I'm putting five words on the board, in order: reported, noticed, acknowledged, disclosed, confirmed in writing.\n\nI give you a date and an insurance company, and you tell me where it really stood. Ready? Keystone's BI limit on 03/11/2026. {name}, your guess? {name}, do you agree?\n\nThe reveal: not confirmed. Not even close. Nobody on this file had seen Keystone's Declaration Page until 03/20/2026.\n\nBoth UM carriers had confirmed in writing by 03/09/2026, but the company that pays first was the slowest to prove it.\n\nThat's the lesson in one sentence. A coverage fact nobody has verified is just a guess the whole file is built on.\n\nAnd a phone call where an adjuster says “I think it's ten” is not proof. It's a rumor with a claim number.\n\nBy the end of this lesson you'll be able to look at any coverage line and tell me in five seconds whether it's proof or a guess.\n\nUnverified coverage is just a guess with a claim number.",
   "ask": "{name}, on 03/11/2026, which of the five statuses did Keystone's BI limit really have?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules. The Coverage Matrix is the single record: carrier, insured, claim number, adjuster, limit and type, status, source, date and next follow-up.\n\nA limit is preliminary until a Dec Page or Coverage Letter confirms it. A phone figure or a number in the Police Report is a lead, and you never quote it to John Doe.\n\nRead the Dec Page line by line, including a policy period that covers 02/06/2026. Keystone's shows BI $10,000 per person. Ask each UM insurer for its form in writing; Harbor Point confirmed add-on on 03/02/2026.\n\nStack only three layers: $10,000 plus $20,000 plus $10,000, which is $40,000. Verify the gaps too: the Bluewater rejection from 03/14/2024 goes in the file.\n\nFirm timing: 24 to 48 hours on an unopened claim, the third business day on an LOR, and a dated next follow-up within two business days. Legal deadlines, policy duties and bad-faith questions go to Atty. Thorne to confirm.\n\nWhy so strict? Because the demand is built on these numbers. One wrong limit and the demand asks for money that doesn't exist. {name}, that is a very awkward phone call.\n\nPreliminary until the paper says otherwise.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the difference between a follow-up you made and one you only scheduled?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the rhythm on the job. A written coverage confirmation arrives. You enter the limit, form and terms on the Coverage Matrix with source and date, and you mark it confirmed in writing only with the document attached.\n\nOn 03/03/2026, Teresa Okafor is assigned at Cardinal State. So you replace “not yet assigned” with her name and direct contact in the Insurance tab and Parties, and re-address your letters to her.\n\nWhen a follow-up date arrives and the item's still open, you follow up, log the result and set the next date, so no open item ever carries a date in the past.\n\nAnd when you spot a legal or policy deadline, you send it to Atty. Thorne to confirm the rule, then put it on the calendar with its source.\n\nHere's the payoff. When the demand goes out, Atty. Thorne quotes a $40,000 stack and doesn't have to wonder whether it's real. That confidence shows in negotiation. {name}, trust me, adjusters can smell a guess.\n\nNo open item ever carries a date in the past.",
   "ask": "{name}, Teresa Okafor's name lands on 03/03/2026. Which places on the file have to change?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-afd": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four ideas, and the first one carries the rest.\n\nAn affidavit is a written statement of facts, sworn under oath before a notary, by someone who knows them first-hand. It ends in a jurat, “sworn to and subscribed before me,” and a false one is perjury. That's why it's proof and a letter isn't.\n\nThen three families on our file. Coverage: Robert Smith swears he has no other insurance, and John, if a carrier asks, swears he lives with Margaret and owns no car.\n\nDamages: each provider's records custodian swears the bills and records are true copies, and Northgate verifies the wages.\n\nAnd court: the process server swears Smith was served, which starts his answer clock.\n\n{name}, in your own words, what makes a letter just a letter, and an affidavit proof?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "{name}, here's a test. Keystone's adjuster writes to say Robert Smith has no other insurance. Would you settle at the $10,000 limit on that letter?\n\nI wouldn't, and neither will Harbor Point or Cardinal State.\n\nA letter is somebody's word. An affidavit is somebody's word under oath, from the person who actually knows: Smith himself, sworn on 08/10/2026.\n\nIt's the difference between a friend saying “trust me” and a friend saying it in front of a judge.\n\nEvery fact this file leans on, the coverage, John's residency, the bills, service on Smith, is only as strong as the paper that proves it.\n\nGet the sworn version, and nobody can wave it away.",
   "ask": "{name}, which fact on John Doe's file would hurt most if we could only assert it, not prove it?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules are about who and what. Smith swears the affidavit of no other insurance; it's a condition of settling at the limit.\n\nJohn swears residency and no owned vehicle only if a carrier asks, and only from a draft Atty. Thorne has reviewed.\n\nEvery certified records request asks for the custodian's affidavit in the same letter, because that's how the bills get into evidence without calling each provider.\n\nNorthgate's wage letter proves the $1,584.00, sworn later if we sue. The process server's affidavit of service gets filed, 10/16/2026 on our file.\n\nAnd every affidavit gets checked before anyone relies on it: the affiant, the numbers, the dates, the signature, the jurat and the seal. A defect goes back to the sender.\n\n{name}, the one rule: you never sign, notarize or fix someone else's sworn words."
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it comes down to three moments.\n\nWhen Atty. Thorne approves a demand at the limit, you request Smith's affidavit through Keystone and docket it. Ours came back sworn on 08/10/2026.\n\nWhen a records request goes out, the custodian's affidavit is in the same request, so the set comes back ready for evidence. One letter, two jobs.\n\nAnd when any affidavit arrives, you check it against the file, route a defect with a task, and file it where it'll be used: the demand conditions, the UM packages, the trial binder.\n\nSo here's your mystery, {name}: if an affidavit came back with the right signature but no notary seal, what would you do, and who would you tell?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-liab": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Here's the thing about coverage: it only tells you there's money. Whether anyone has to pay it is a different question, and that's liability.\n\nThink of coverage as the bank account and liability as the signature on the check. No signature, no money.\n\nJohn Doe's file has three coverage layers, but none of them pays a cent unless Robert Smith is legally responsible.\n\nAnd the evidence that proves it is freshest right now. Ruth Calloway's memory fades, Margaret Doe's doorbell camera records over itself, and the Escape's data recorder goes wherever the car goes.\n\nSo we investigate liability at claim set-up, right beside coverage, not at the demand. {name}, which of those three clocks is ticking fastest?\n\nRemember: evidence has an expiry date.",
   "ask": "{name}, which piece of John Doe's liability evidence would you lose first if nobody did anything this week?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Start with the Police Report, RPD-26-018842, and read every field, not just the citation.\n\nOfficer Garner cited Smith for failure to stop, and he named a witness, Ruth Calloway, who signed a statement on scene.\n\nThe report also lists the Escape as a 2021, while Harbor Point's Declaration Page says 2020. So you record both and correct our record from the policy. Small detail, but adjusters love a mismatch.\n\nThen preserve. The doorbell copy was secured on 02/19/2026 with its hash logged, and the data recorder needs a preservation request Atty. Thorne signs.\n\nAnd remember, Keystone accepting PD liability on Margaret's Civic doesn't bind its BI adjuster on John's claim. {name}, explain that one back to me in your own words.\n\nRead every field, save every copy, and never assume one adjuster speaks for another.",
   "ask": "{name}, the defense says John Doe wasn't wearing his belt. What on the file already answers that?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Three moments on the job.\n\nWhen claim set-up opens, you request the Police Report and read it field by field, logging the citation and the witness in the CMS.\n\nWhen you learn that video or vehicle data exists, you draft the preservation requests for Atty. Thorne's signature, and log the chain of custody once a copy is secured. Chain of custody is just the trail showing who held the copy and when, so nobody can say it was tampered with.\n\nAnd whenever a carrier states its liability position, you record it in the Insurance tab with its date and its source, one carrier at a time.\n\n{name}, here's your mystery: what on this file would you check to prove the doorbell copy is the same one Margaret's camera recorded?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-first": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Picture Atty. Thorne between two hearings with forty emails open.\n\nThe ones that start with a story make him read to the end to find out what he's being asked. And the question found last gets answered last.\n\nSo we flip it. Every note, email and brief to him opens with the decision or the action needed, and its deadline. Then why, then the facts, then what happens after each answer.\n\nIt's like ordering at a drive-through. You say “two coffees,” not the story of your morning.\n\nIt sounds small, but it's the difference between a yes by lunchtime and a deadline that slips past while the note waits.\n\n{name}, the rule in five words: ask first, explain second.",
   "ask": "{name}, think of the last long email you received. How far did you read before you knew what was being asked?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The first line is the decision and its deadline, and the subject line carries it too.\n\nSomething like: decision needed by 03/12/2026, approve declining Summit Imaging's LOP request, because the MRI goes to Harbor Point PIP, which applies first and still has $3,675.00 left.\n\nThen the facts with their sources, then the options. If yes, you send the decline and route the bill today. If no, you draft the LOP for his signature.\n\nOne decision per note.\n\nAnd once he answers, you log the note and his answer in the CMS that same day and carry it out.\n\n{name}, try it now: give me a first line for any decision on John's file. If he can say yes or no to it in ten seconds, you've nailed it.",
   "ask": "{name}, your note holds two decisions with different deadlines. How do you send it so neither one is missed?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the job. Audit the matrix against the documents in CMS Documents. Find every error, fix it, and cite the document and date that prove each fix.\n\nRestore the stack total, clear every overdue follow-up by actually doing it and setting a new date, and leave the correct rows alone.\n\nThen a live call in the Call Simulator with Dana Whitfield at Keystone. Confirm she has the written request and the signed authorizations, get a firm date for the Declaration Page in writing, share no treatment details and no demand figure. Then log the call and set the next follow-up by 03/13/2026.\n\nFinally, send a three-line audit note to Atty. Thorne: confirmed in writing, still preliminary, escalated.\n\n{name}, you're making the Keystone call. {name}, you're listening for anything she gets out of you that she shouldn't, and you'll tell us what it was.\n\nExpect to hear “I'll have to check on that.” Your job is to turn that into a date.\n\n“She'll get back to me” isn't an outcome. A date in writing is.",
   "ask": "{name}, on 03/11/2026, what status can you honestly give the BI row, and why?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Game first: guess the number. I tell you a total on a chart, you guess how many mistakes are hiding behind it. Ready? $45,000. {name}, your guess? {name}, higher or lower? Not telling.\n\nHere's what landed. It's 03/11/2026. A colleague has been keeping John Doe's Coverage Matrix, and the CMS has flagged it.\n\nThe Keystone BI follow-up for the Declaration Page was due 03/05/2026, and nothing's logged. The total reads $45,000, while the Case Plan says $40,000.\n\nSince the matrix was last edited, new documents arrived: Harbor Point's letter of 03/02/2026, Cardinal State's adjuster assignment on 03/03/2026, and its Coverage Letter on 03/09/2026.\n\nKeystone's written request with the signed authorizations went out, but no Declaration Page yet. And this morning Summit Imaging asked for a Letter of Protection, which was declined because PIP pays first.\n\nYou've got the matrix and the documents. It's a spot-the-difference puzzle, except every difference is somebody's money.\n\nA total is only as true as the rows under it.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you'd check when a stack total reads $45,000 instead of $40,000?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your audit before you log it. BI should read preliminary, verbal from the set-up call, written request sent, because Keystone's Declaration Page doesn't arrive until 03/20/2026.\n\nThe 03/05/2026 follow-up was overdue, so you called Dana Whitfield on 03/11/2026, logged it, and set the next date by 03/13/2026.\n\nHost UM's form is add-on, from Harbor Point's 03/02/2026 letter. Teresa Okafor replaces “not yet assigned.” PIP moved out of the stack, and the total corrected from $45,000 to $40,000.\n\nBluewater's “UM: none” needs the signed rejection form from 03/14/2024 chased. Keystone's PD row stays outside the stack, marked preliminary; moving it in or deleting it is a false alarm. Finding Cardinal State's unstated form and its excess clause earns extra credit.\n\nEvery fix cites a document. {name}, read us your audit note the way Atty. Thorne would read it, in ten seconds, between two meetings.\n\nMystery to chew on: Keystone's Declaration Page finally lands on 03/20/2026. Which line on it will you read first, and what changes on the matrix the same day?",
   "ask": "{name}, in ten seconds, does your audit note tell Atty. Thorne what he must decide?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the Learning Check, lightning-round style. I read each question, and before you type, I pick one person to shout a five-word headline answer. Then everyone writes the full version in their own words from John Doe's file. {name}, you're up for question one.\n\nFirst, name every coverage on the file, whose it is and what it pays, then tell me which three make the $40,000 Recovery Stack and why the others stay out.\n\nSecond, before the letters went out you built the Policy Stack in the CMS. Give each layer's insurer, claim number, limit, first- or third-party, and the order it's pursued in. {name}, you've got the headline on that one.\n\nThird, list the five letters that left the file on 02/17/2026, with the insured each one named and how it was addressed.\n\nFor every “confirmed,” give me the document behind it. No answers like “the adjuster said so on the phone.” Adjusters say a lot of things on the phone.",
   "ask": "{name}, five-word headline: which three coverages make the $40,000, and which famous one doesn't?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Time for your Self-Summary: the principles in your own words.\n\nThen work the scenario. It's 03/11/2026. Keystone still hasn't confirmed its limit in writing, and John Doe started treatment fourteen days after the crash. Which three actions do you take today, in what order, and what goes in the Case Plan's risks section?\n\nAs you write, think about what you would say, and not say, to an insurance company that calls you about this file today.\n\nA story to keep you honest. I once had a Case Manager tell an adjuster, trying to sound on top of things, “Oh, he's been treating the whole time.” He hadn't. That one friendly sentence became the adjuster's favorite quote in the whole negotiation.\n\nFacts only, and only the ones they're entitled to. {name}, while you write, I'll come around and read the first line of yours.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Dana Whitfield called you today asking about the gap, what would you tell her, and what would you hold back?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last part, the Skill Scale. Rate each skill 1 to 5 and submit it for review.\n\nWhen the scores come back, I'll compare your self-rating with your score. A 5 sitting next to a $45,000 stack is a coaching conversation, and we'll have it, nicely.\n\nThe legal line works like a gate. A great score doesn't survive a letter signed for the attorney, or a coverage opinion given to an insurer or the client.\n\nRating yourself is a lot like a client describing their policy: everybody thinks they have full coverage until somebody reads the fine print. An honest 3 helps me more than a hopeful 5.\n\nNow your mystery. We've got a $40,000 stack, letters out, matrix clean. But one thing on this file could make every insurer offer less, and it has nothing to do with coverage. {name}, your guess before we move on?",
   "ask": "{name}, what on this file, besides coverage, could make every insurer offer less, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d2m1-cov": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Every policy answers two questions: whose is it, and what does it pay?\n\nThird-party coverage belongs to the at-fault driver, and here that's Robert Smith. First-party coverage answers to the client's side. Who you're claiming against changes how you talk to them, because one is on our side and one very much isn't.\n\nNext, the Recovery Stack. That's only the coverage that pays the claim itself: BI, which is bodily injury coverage on the at-fault driver, then the UM and UIM layers, the coverage that steps in when the at-fault driver has too little or no insurance. Medical Bills and the car sit outside the stack.\n\nThen there's no-fault medical coverage, MedPay and PIP. It pays accident Medical Bills no matter who caused the crash, and fast.\n\nAnd here's the twist that surprises everyone: UM follows the person, not the car. {name}, that's going to matter in a minute, because John Doe has no car policy of his own.\n\nUM follows the person, not the car.",
   "ask": "{name}, John Doe reaches two UM policies, and neither is his own. Which status gets him to each one?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "Four more ideas, and I'll keep them simple.\n\nFirst, set-off and non-duplication. Some policies subtract what PIP or MedPay already paid from what they owe, so you verify that in writing.\n\nSecond, subrogation. That's a fancy word for a payer's right to get its money back out of the settlement. A payer with subrogation wants repaying. A payer without it doesn't, and what it paid stays paid.\n\nThird, contract rate and write-off. When a doctor is in-network, they accept the Health Plan's price and write off the rest, so the client isn't billed the difference and the lien is smaller.\n\nFourth, Medicare Secondary Payer. Medicare pays only after the no-fault and liability coverage, it gets repaid by law, and the case gets reported to the BCRC, the federal office that tracks this.\n\nThe joke I tell new hires: the fine print on a policy is printed in a font designed by the same people who make prescription bottle labels. {name}, that's exactly why we read it anyway.\n\nBill first the payer that never asks for its money back.",
   "ask": "{name}, why is a payer with no right to be paid back the best one to bill first?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here's the table, row by row. Before I go through it, {name}, guess which row pays John Doe's bills fastest. Hold that.\n\nBI liability is the at-fault driver's, third-party, and it pays the claim for the injuries he caused. UM and UIM sit on the client's side, through the vehicle or the household, and pay when BI is too small, with consent rules that protect their right to be repaid.\n\nPIP comes from the policy on the vehicle the client was riding in, and pays Medical Bills without anyone proving fault, though not lost wages; its repayment terms you verify in writing. Where a state requires PIP with wages, it can pay part of wages and services too.\n\nHealth insurance is the client's own plan, at the contract rate, with a right to be repaid. Medicare and Medicaid come last, repaid by law, with a BCRC report.\n\nAnd the answer to my question? PIP. Fast, and with nothing to repay on this file.\n\nReading eight policies side by side is like reading eight phone contracts. Every single one has an asterisk, and the asterisk is where the money is.\n\nThe asterisk is where the money is.",
   "ask": "{name}, which row on this table pays John Doe's bills fastest, with the least to repay?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game. I'll say a sentence clients say to us every single week, and you tell me true or false. Ready? “I've got full coverage, so I'm fine.”\n\n{name}, true or false? Take a second.\n\nThe answer: there's no such thing. People say full coverage the way they say “I'm almost ready” when they haven't found their shoes. Every policy pays a different thing.\n\nSo here are two questions, and I want them as two columns in your head. Who pays the bills now? And who pays the claim later? People mix them up all the time, and that's exactly where the money leaks out.\n\nThere's a rule called the Payer-First Principle, which just means each bill goes to the payer that has to pay it first.\n\nGet the order right and a bill gets paid once, cheaply. Get it wrong and that same bill comes back later as a lien, a bill paid out of John Doe's settlement.\n\nAnd I'll show you one $2,395 bill on this file where the order made all the difference.\n\nBills now, claim later. Two columns, never one.",
   "ask": "{name}, who pays John Doe's bills now, and who pays his claim later? Are they the same?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how you sort every coverage, and you sort it twice.\n\nFirst by whose. Keystone BI and PD are Robert Smith's, third-party. Host UM, Relative UM and PIP answer to John Doe's side. Bluewater's collision is Margaret's. Peach State is his own Health Plan.\n\nThen by what it pays. BI and UM pay the claim, and only they make the $40,000 stack. PIP pays bills, and a share of lost wages, regardless of fault. This file has Harbor Point PIP, $5,000, on the Escape John rode in, and you verify it.\n\nJohn reaches Host UM as an occupant of Kevin Alvarez's Escape, and Relative UM as Margaret Doe's resident relative, meaning he lives in her household. Margaret's Bluewater UM was rejected on 03/14/2024.\n\nThe bill order: PIP first, because Harbor Point confirmed on 03/02/2026 it has no right to be repaid; then the Health Plan; a Letter of Protection last.\n\nSending every bill to whoever answers the phone is how liens get born. Route each bill in order and keep a ledger for every payer. And when two policies fight over who pays, that's Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nSort by whose, then by what it pays.",
   "ask": "{name}, before I tell you: where does PIP sit, and why does it go ahead of Peach State?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Two tiers on this slide, and there's one card that fools almost everybody.\n\nOn top is a coverage map of eight cards. Navy is third-party: Keystone BI, $10,000, and Keystone PD, $25,000. Orange is first-party: Harbor Point Host UM, $20,000; Cardinal State Relative UM, $10,000; Harbor Point PIP, $5,000; and Bluewater collision with a $500 deductible.\n\nGreen is health: Peach State Health Plan. And a grey card, crossed out: Bluewater UM, rejected 03/14/2024.\n\nEvery card is tagged the claim, the bills or the car, and only the three claim cards are bracketed as the $40,000 Recovery Stack.\n\nBelow, a staircase steps down the bill order: MedPay or PIP, then the Health Plan, then the Letter of Protection. The callout says it plainly: out of order means a lien that didn't have to exist.\n\nNow, that card that fools everybody. Keystone PD at $25,000 is the biggest number on the slide, and it isn't in the stack at all. {name}, that's your trap to dodge.\n\nThe biggest number on the slide isn't in the stack.",
   "ask": "{name}, which card on this map would most people wrongly add to the $40,000?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs across a normal week. As each claim opens, you list every coverage, its carrier and what it pays in the CMS Insurance tab, and tag it third-party, first-party or health.\n\nWhen coverage is confirmed in writing, you place it on the Coverage Matrix, our one master chart of every policy: in the stack, in the bill order, or outside both. So the stack reads $40,000, and PIP, PD and collision sit outside it.\n\nA doctor's office asks who to bill, and you route it in order using the PIP Log: PIP, then Peach State, then a Letter of Protection.\n\nIf a provider asks for an LOP, which is our written promise to pay them from the settlement, you check PIP and network status first, and Atty. Thorne approves any LOP.\n\nWhen a payer pays, you post it on its ledger so nothing paid gets sent around again.\n\nWhy care? Because every bill that lands in the wrong place becomes money John Doe pays back at the end. {name}, picture a client asking why his check is smaller than his neighbor's. This is usually why.\n\nWrong payer today means a smaller check later.",
   "ask": "{name}, a doctor's office calls asking who to bill for John Doe's MRI. Where do you look first?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Do and Don't list for coverages, and every line of it sits on John Doe's file.\n\nThe Dos. Verify every coverage in writing, which means a dated Declaration Page, not a phone call. Confirm the Health Plan was active on the loss date: Peach State began 02/01/2026, five days before the crash.\n\nAnd keep the Payer Order and the Recovery Stack apart. One says who pays the bills first; the other is the $40,000 that pays the claim.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Don't send an LOP to a provider PIP would have paid. Summit Imaging asked for one, but PIP paid the $2,395.00 with nothing to repay, instead of a balance sitting against John Doe's net. Same bill, totally different ending.\n\nDon't add PIP, PD or collision to the stack. Harbor Point's $5,000 and Keystone's $25,000 for the car never belong in that $40,000.\n\nAnd don't assume PIP, or UM on the client's own policy. John owns no car, and Margaret signed away UM on her Bluewater coverage on 03/14/2024.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen?\n\nNow your mystery: John Doe owns no car and no auto policy, yet he reaches $30,000 of UM. {name}, hold that answer.\n\nSame bill, right order, more money in John's pocket.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does John Doe reach $30,000 of UM at all, when he has no auto policy of his own?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Game time: guess the number. I've got John Doe's PIP Log up with the right-hand column covered.\n\nPIP, by the way, is coverage on the car he was riding in that pays Medical Bills no matter who caused the crash.\n\nHarbor Point has paid $685.00, $640.00, $2,395.00 and then $640.00 for the 03/27/2026 visit, out of $5,000. {name}, how much is left? Closest guess wins bragging rights. {name}, you're checking the math.\n\nIt's $640.00. Nicely done.\n\nSo why do I care whether you can add? Because treatment is the engine room of this file, and somebody has to pay for every visit while the claim is open.\n\nOn this file three payers stand in line: Harbor Point PIP, then Peach State, his Health Plan, then a Letter of Protection. Get the order wrong, and a bill an earlier payer would've covered turns into a lien, a debt that comes straight out of John Doe's check at the end.\n\nAnd fair warning: medical billing departments speak a language nobody else on Earth speaks. By the end of today you'll be fluent. Almost.\n\nRemember the order: PIP, then Peach State, then the LOP.",
   "ask": "{name}, with $640.00 of PIP left, where does the rest of a bigger bill go next?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. In treatment, the question is always, “Is the plan working?” And you run the Payer Order: Harbor Point PIP, then Peach State, then an LOP.\n\nJohn claims the $5,000 PIP as a passenger in Kevin Alvarez's 2020 Ford Escape, because no policy in his household carries PIP. PIP pays in date order, never wages.\n\nWhen Crescent Pain & Spine billed Peach State on 05/15/2026 with $640.00 of PIP left, you tracked both ledgers and never re-routed a paid claim.\n\nRiverton Spine & Rehab got an LOP because it's out of network. Summit Imaging's 03/11/2026 MRI went to PIP.\n\nIt's tempting to say yes to every LOP request, because it makes the provider go away. Don't. Prove from the ledger which payer should cover it.\n\nLOP terms are Atty. Thorne's: you prepare, route, send once he approves, and confirm receipt.\n\nI once had a billing manager call me four times in one morning about an invoice dated that same morning. She wanted it paid yesterday. {name}, you smile, log it, and never promise a provider an amount, a reduction or a date.\n\nThe line to keep: the ledger decides, not the loudest caller.",
   "ask": "{name}, Riverton and Summit Imaging both asked for an LOP. Why did only one of them get it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Picture a normal month on this file. A provider asks for an LOP.\n\nYou run the Payer Order and prepare one on counsel's template only for care PIP and Peach State can't carry, then route it for approval from CMS Documents, Outgoing. The payer check is recorded first, and nothing goes out without Atty. Thorne.\n\nWhen PIP pays a bill, you post the payment, the running total and the balance on the PIP Log. It ties to Harbor Point, hitting $5,000.00 on the 06/12/2026 visit.\n\nWhen an LOP statement arrives, you check every line against the visits. Through 04/02/2026 that's 16 visits at $185.00, or $2,960.00, all attended.\n\nFor a new referral, you do a warm handoff, meaning John and the new office are on the same call, and you confirm the payer before the first visit.\n\nWhy so fussy? Because at the demand the adjuster adds up our bills, and at the end the lien holders add them up too. If ours are the only clean numbers in the room, we win the argument. {name}, you're on the audit.\n\nClean numbers win arguments. Check every line against a visit.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Riverton bills a date John Doe didn't attend, what do you do with that line?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-eob": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four terms, and one of them sounds like a robot from a space movie.\n\nEvery payer reports back on a bill: the Health Plan with an EOB, PIP with its payment history. And you read both before you ever touch a lien.\n\nAn EOB is the plan's account of one processed claim. Not a bill, just proof of who paid what.\n\nBilled versus allowed is the first thing to see. Billed is what the provider charged. Allowed is what the plan agreed to pay under its contract. In network, the gap gets written off, which means it simply disappears.\n\nRemark codes are the little codes that explain each line, and here's our robot: CO-45 is the write-off code. AC1 flags a possible accident.\n\nAnd member responsibility, meaning the deductible, copay and coinsurance, is the most an in-network provider may bill the patient.\n\nSkimming the total and moving on is how mistakes get through. Read every line, because every line is a number someone may try to put in front of John Doe later. {name}, quick one.\n\nThe key idea: billed is the wish, allowed is the reality.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these terms have you seen on your own Medical Bills, and what did it mean there?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "Four more ideas, and honestly, these are where money quietly gets lost.\n\nBalance billing is when a provider bills the patient for the part the plan already wrote off. In network that isn't allowed, so it's an error. Not a lien, and not a debt.\n\nThe PIP payment history is the carrier's list of every check: the date of service, the amount paid and the limit remaining.\n\nExhaustion is what happens once that limit's spent. Later bills come back unpaid, and you send them to the next payer.\n\nAnd reconciliation means matching every provider balance to each payer's EOB or check, so no bill that's already been paid ever becomes a lien against John Doe.\n\nHere's a real one. A client once paid a balance bill because, and I quote, “it had a barcode, so it looked official.”\n\nThe barcode was very official. The bill was wrong. It took us two months to get his money back. {name}, help me out here.\n\nKeep this one: a balance bill is an error to fix, never a debt to pay.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a provider bills John Doe for a write-off, is that a lien, a debt or an error?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here's how you read an EOB, top to bottom, every single time. {name}, one of these steps is the one people skip, and it's the one that costs the client. Guess which as I go.\n\nFirst, match the claim: the member ID, claim number, provider and date of service against the CMS treatment record.\n\nThen go from billed to allowed: the charge, what the plan allowed, and the amount it wrote off.\n\nNext is the patient's share, the deductible, copay and coinsurance, because member responsibility is the most a provider may bill.\n\nAfter that, what the plan paid, with its date and its check or electronic payment number, since that's where any payback claim starts.\n\nRead the remark codes. CO-45 explains the write-off, and AC1 flags an accident and the plan's right to be paid back.\n\nFinally, reconcile and log it against the provider's statement and the lien register, and flag any balance billing.\n\nSo, the step people skip? That last one. They read it, nod, and never compare it to the provider's statement. That's how a $0.00 turns into a collections call.\n\nRemember: you're not done reading until you've compared.",
   "ask": "{name}, which step would you be most tempted to skip on a busy Friday, and why?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Show of hands: who's ever opened a letter from their health insurance, read the whole thing, and still had no idea whether they owed money? Keep it up if you just paid it to make it go away. Yeah. Me too.\n\nThat letter is called an EOB, an Explanation of Benefits, and it might be the most confusing document in America. It's not a bill. It's the Health Plan's report on what it did with a bill.\n\nToday, you become the person who can actually read one.\n\nTake out RS26, Peach State's EOB. {name}, find the bottom line: what does John Doe owe Crescent for the injection? The EOB says $0.00.\n\nEvery payer reports back. Peach State does it with an EOB, and Harbor Point does it with its PIP payment history.\n\nRead line by line, they tell you what was billed, what was paid, what was written off and what John Doe may still owe.\n\nThose papers are the proof behind every medical number in the demand and every lien we negotiate. Get them right, and nobody slips a wrong number past us.\n\nRemember: an EOB is a report card, not a bill.",
   "ask": "{name}, reading RS26, what does John Doe actually owe Crescent Pain & Spine for the injection, and which line tells you?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's the real one on John Doe's file. Peach State's EOB of 06/03/2026, claim PS-2605-449021, covers Crescent's 05/15/2026 epidural injection, a shot of medicine near the spine for pain. And it says THIS IS NOT A BILL.\n\nCrescent billed $3,400.00 and the plan allowed $1,860.00. The $1,540.00 difference is Crescent's write-off. The plan paid $1,860.00, and member responsibility is $0.00.\n\nCO-45 explains that write-off, and AC1 is how Peach State's right to be paid back attaches.\n\nNow Crescent's 07/01/2026 statement asks John Doe for $1,540.00. That's a balance bill. Not a debt, not a lien.\n\nYou could forward it to John with “FYI.” Please don't. Document it, route it to Atty. Thorne that day, draft a correction request and tell John what's happening.\n\nPeach State's claim starts at $1,860.00, never $3,400.00, and its accident questionnaire goes to Atty. Thorne. Harbor Point's five checks total $5,000.00, exhausted 06/26/2026.\n\nYou read and reconcile. {name}, whether he owes anything is counsel's call.\n\nThe takeaway: $0.00 member responsibility means $0.00, whatever the statement says.",
   "ask": "{name}, Crescent's $1,540.00 statement: is it a lien we missed, or a bill nobody owes, and which EOB line proves it?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Two documents side by side, and a little detective work.\n\nOn the left is Peach State's EOB for claim PS-2605-449021, with that red stamp, THIS IS NOT A BILL. Six rows: billed $3,400.00, allowed $1,860.00, the $1,540.00 adjustment, $0.00 deductible, $1,860.00 paid, and $0.00 member responsibility. Each orange tag tells you where that number goes on the file.\n\nOn the right is Harbor Point's payment history: five checks, with a limit bar falling from $5,000.00 to nothing, stamped exhausted 06/26/2026.\n\nAnd underneath sits Crescent's 07/01/2026 statement for $1,540.00, with a dashed line back to that $0.00 row and a question: balance bill?\n\n{name}, that dashed line is the whole case.\n\nBilling departments don't usually do this on purpose. Somebody in an office somewhere pressed “send statements” on every account with a balance, and nobody looked at the EOB. You're the one who looks.\n\nThe line to keep: the EOB beats the statement, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, which row on Peach State's EOB is the most Crescent may bill John Doe?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run it the way it happens on the file. An EOB arrives. You post billed, allowed, adjustment, paid and member share against the visit in CMS Liens and Documents, so the $1,860.00 plan payment is logged and claim PS-2605-449021 is linked to the visit.\n\nA PIP check clears, and you post it on the PIP Log with the date of service and the limit remaining, until it ties to Harbor Point's history with $0.00 left after 06/26/2026.\n\nThen a patient statement shows a balance. You compare it with the EOB's member responsibility, flag the $1,540.00, tell Atty. Thorne the same day and draft the correction request.\n\nWhen Peach State's itemized lien arrives, you match each line to an EOB and to the collision, so only those lines stand.\n\nAnd here's the payoff. At settlement, Peach State will want its money back. If you've matched every line, we pay back what's really owed.\n\nIf you haven't, they'll happily take more, and it comes straight out of John's check. {name}, think like a lien auditor for a second.\n\nEvery lien line needs an EOB behind it, or it doesn't stand.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you do with a line on Peach State's lien that no EOB supports?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Work both dates, and start each one by running the Payer Order.\n\nOn 02/23/2026 you prepare Riverton's LOP on the approved template, route it to Atty. Thorne for signature, and transmit it with the HIPAA authorization.\n\nThen you take the live call with Tammy Greer, the office manager. Introduce John's case, confirm the clinic accepts the LOP, and secure tomorrow's evaluation. Answer her questions on insurance, visit cadence and billing with facts.\n\nAfterwards you write the call up as a case note, open the follow-up tasks and file it in the CMS, so the Backup Drive keeps a copy.\n\nOn 03/11/2026 you decline Summit's request in writing, naming Harbor Point PIP and proving it from the PIP Log.\n\nAnd no payment gets promised to either provider. {name}, I'll be listening for that one.\n\nRemember: the LOP's terms are the only promise anyone gets.",
   "ask": "{name}, Summit says it won't do the MRI without an LOP. What do you say, and who do you tell?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Two dates, two emails, and one of these providers is about to get a no. Guess which by the end.\n\nFirst date: 02/23/2026. This email just came in from Tammy Greer, Office Manager at Riverton Spine & Rehab.\n\nJohn Doe is booked for his chiropractic and therapy evaluation tomorrow, 02/24. They're out of network with Peach State, and they want a signed Letter of Protection and his HIPAA authorization, the form that lets them share his records, today.\n\nHis first treatment was Riverton Urgent Care on 02/20/2026, $685.00, paid by Harbor Point PIP. Atty. Thorne has approved the LOP template.\n\nAnd John had three chiropractic visits in 2021 for low-back stiffness. Remember that 2021 detail, okay? It's going to matter.\n\nSecond date: 03/11/2026. Summit Imaging Center, booked for the neck MRI Dr. Reyes ordered on 03/05/2026, asks for an LOP too.\n\nNotice the word “today” in Tammy's email. Every provider's office wants it today. The really keen ones send the email at six in the morning, just to be sure.\n\n{name}, you take Riverton. {name}, you've got Summit.\n\nKeep in mind: the Payer Order decides, not the provider's deadline.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you open the template, why does Riverton get an LOP?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work before you log it. Was the LOP signed by John Doe and Atty. Thorne on 02/23/2026, before the 02/24 visit, with nothing signed by you?\n\nDoes your baseline show the 2021 visits were low back, resolved, a different region? Is the rental's 03/19/2026 end calendared as a treatment risk?\n\nSummit's decline names PIP, proved from the ledger with $3,675.00 left, enough for the $2,395.00 MRI.\n\nAnd your call with Tammy Greer is written up, tasked and filed, with nothing promised.\n\nIf she asked whether the firm guarantees full payment, {name}, the only right answer is the LOP's own terms and a referral to Atty. Thorne. “Don't worry, you'll be paid in full” fails, however friendly it sounds.",
   "ask": "{name}, when a provider asks whether they'll be paid in full, how do you answer them?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-lop": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: finish the sentence. “An IOU is…” {name}, go. {name}, give me yours.\n\nGood, because today's star is the most official IOU you'll ever see.\n\nJohn Doe needs chiropractic care next week, and nothing he has will pay for it. That's where a Letter of Protection comes in.\n\nIn plain English, it's a written promise that the provider gets paid out of the settlement, so he can be treated now instead of waiting.\n\nIt keeps care moving, and care that keeps moving keeps the file honest, with no gaps for an adjuster to point at.\n\nBut here's the twist, and it's the one clients never believe: it isn't free money. It's a balance against his recovery, and he still owes it. So we track it from the day it's signed.\n\nTakeaway: an LOP is treatment now, paid later, by John.",
   "ask": "{name}, if the settlement came in lower than the bills, who would still owe the provider, and why?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you prepare or file one, let's open up the Letter of Protection, or LOP. {name}, quick guess: is it insurance? Hands up for yes. Put them down, it isn't.\n\nIt's the firm's signed promise that a provider's reasonable charges will be paid from any recovery, so treatment can start now.\n\nIts legal purpose is to keep care going when no payer applies. The provider defers collection, meaning it waits to get paid, and in return its charges are protected from the settlement, judgment or verdict.\n\nIts evidentiary role, the job it does as proof, is to fix the lien. Riverton Spine & Rehab's letter, acknowledged on 02/23/2026, is why its charges come out of John Doe's recovery, and it says plainly he stays responsible whether or not there is one.\n\nYour handling: check every payer first, get it signed before the first visit, request the ledger every 30 days, and leave its meaning to Atty. Thorne.\n\nDon't send it and forget it. A surprise balance at settlement is money straight out of John's pocket.\n\nAn LOP is a pinky promise with a signature, and providers remember every pinky.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the letter say John Doe stays responsible even if there's no recovery?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how we handle it. First, an LOP is the last resort. We check health insurance, MedPay and PIP before anything else, and write down why none of them works.\n\nSecond, it gets signed before the first visit and filed. Riverton's was signed on 02/23/2026.\n\nThird, we ask for the itemized ledger every thirty days and update the Liens tab, so the balance never surprises anyone.\n\nAnd when John Doe asks where to go, we give him options with the facts, book the one he picks, and log his choice in his own words.\n\n“Sure, go to the place down the street, I'll send them a letter” is how files go wrong. A payer check on file, a signed LOP before visit one, and a ledger never more than thirty days old is how they go right.\n\nI once met a client who thought “Letter of Protection” meant he had a bodyguard now. He was a little disappointed. {name}, your turn: explain the three steps back to me.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does it matter that John Doe picks the provider, and not us?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it looks like this. When no payer covers the care, you document the payer check and route the LOP to Atty. Thorne to approve. When it's signed, you file it and set the recurring ledger task.\n\nAnd when John Doe asks where he should go, you hand him two or three options with their locations, hours and network status, tell him about any relationship the firm has with them, and book his choice.\n\nWhat you never say is which one is better for his case. That's the line.\n\nWhy it matters: at settlement, every LOP dollar comes off the top of John's check unless someone negotiates it down. A clean ledger is what we negotiate with.\n\nNow a mystery for the next lesson. John Doe is spending money on this crash every single week, and none of it shows up on any provider's bill. {name}, what is it, and who's writing it down?",
   "ask": "{name}, a provider asks you to promise they'll be paid in full. What do you say instead?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-cost": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Guessing game. {name}, how much does it cost John Doe, out of his own pocket, to get to therapy three times a week for a month? Rides, gas, parking, the copay. Shout a number. {name}, higher or lower?\n\nHere's the point. Picture John Doe's week once the rental's gone. He still has therapy three times a week.\n\nSomebody drives him, or he pays for a ride. There's a prescription, maybe a brace. None of that shows up on a provider's bill, but every dollar of it is a cost of the crash.\n\nAnd here's the catch: it's recoverable only if somebody wrote it down when it happened. That somebody is you.\n\nCopays are sneaky that way. Ten dollars here, twenty-five there, and by month three it's a car payment nobody tracked.\n\nYou keep the log. What the demand claims, and at what rate, is Atty. Thorne's call under the governing state's rules.\n\nThe line to remember: if it wasn't written down, it wasn't spent.",
   "ask": "{name}, what costs do you think John Doe is paying out of his own pocket right now?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules. Out-of-pocket costs are Specials, and each one needs its receipt or its log.\n\nStart the trip log at the first visit: date, provider, round-trip miles. The rate comes from the governing state's rules, confirmed by Atty. Thorne.\n\nA rental the carrier didn't pay is the owner's cost. Keep the receipt and send it with the Property Damage claim.\n\nWhen the rental ends, transport becomes a barrier to care, so plan the rides before a visit is missed. Check every payer first, so nothing is claimed twice.\n\nEnter each cost the day its receipt arrives, then total the log and route it to Atty. Thorne before the demand. And never promise the client he'll be repaid.\n\nTrue story: a client once brought me his receipts in a shoebox. Not sorted, not dated, and one of them was for a hot dog.\n\nWe kept the parking receipts. We did not claim the hot dog. {name}, tell me why.",
   "ask": "{name}, John Doe asks if he'll get his ride money back. What can you say without giving legal advice?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job, it runs like this. Treatment starts, so you open the trip log in Case Notes, set a task, and tell John Doe exactly what to keep: every receipt, and every trip with its date, office and miles.\n\nWhen a receipt comes in, you enter the cost in the Financial Ledger under Specials the same day and file the receipt in Documents.\n\nWhen MMI is reached, meaning the doctor says he's as good as he's going to get, and the demand is next, you total the log and route it to Atty. Thorne, who confirms the rate and decides what the demand claims.\n\nSmall numbers, big outcome. A few hundred dollars of documented mileage also proves John showed up, visit after visit, and that's worth far more at the table than the mileage itself.\n\nNow your mystery. Keystone ended Margaret's rental on 03/19/2026, and John's rides are now held together by a carpool. {name}, look at the file and predict the week his therapy is in danger.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the one thing you'd tell John Doe to start keeping today, and why that first?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-l2": {
  "map": {
   "say": "Here's the road for monitoring treatment before we dive in. Five steps, and {name}, I'm testing you on the middle three in about ninety seconds, so lock them in.\n\nYou check in on visits, pain, function and any barrier, which is anything stopping him getting to care.\n\nWhen something's off, you name the variance, the type of problem. Clinical, which is medical. Psychosocial, which is life or mood. Or systemic, which is the system being stuck.\n\nThen you run A-C-T. Assess, where medical danger goes to the provider and legal danger goes to Atty. Thorne. Coordinate, where you clarify with the office and remove the barrier.\n\nAnd Track, where every fix attempt goes into a GIRP note, a short note of the Goal, the Intervention, the Response and the Plan. Facts only.\n\nOkay, {name}, time's up. A, C, T. Go.\n\nCheck in, name it, run A-C-T. That's the whole loop.",
   "ask": "{name}, what are the three steps of A-C-T, and which one ends up written in the GIRP note?"
  },
  "theory": {
   "say": "Knowing the Standard Treatment Map is what separates a Case Manager who watches a recovery from one who drives it. Care that drifts off the map is what triggers denials and IMEs, the exams the insurance company orders from its own doctor.\n\nQuick recap. Acute is days 1 to 14: the ER, imaging, first therapy and proving the crash caused the injury.\n\nSub-acute runs weeks 2 to 8: active therapy, specialists and measured gains. Restorative is weeks 9 to 24: injections, work hardening, which is therapy that rebuilds the strength for his actual job, and anything blocking a return to work.\n\nThen maintenance: home exercise and visits as needed.\n\nMaximum Medical Improvement, or MMI, is the doctor saying more care won't make him better. It closes treatment and opens the pre-demand.\n\nWhen a client drifts off the map, you ask why first, before the funding stops. A watcher writes “missed visits.” A driver writes why, and what's being done about it. {name}, be a driver for me.",
   "ask": "{name}, which phase do you think John Doe was in when he missed his week-6 mark?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game: what's the date? I give you a date, you tell me what's due. {name}, 04/07/2026. Anyone?\n\nHere's a hint: count six weeks from John Doe's first therapy visit on 02/24/2026.\n\nGot it? It was his week-6 mark of care, the day the doctors check whether therapy is actually working. And John Doe wasn't there.\n\nSo why does that matter? On an LOP, nobody denies the next six visits in real time. The insurance company, Keystone, does it later, at the demand, by calling the care unnecessary or the injury minor.\n\nThat leaves you as the only real-time check that John Doe's plan is working, and the person who acts the very day it isn't. No pressure, right?\n\nAnd by the end of this lesson, you'll know exactly why he missed it. Here's a head start, though: it's not because he felt better.\n\nKeep this: you're the only real-time check on an LOP file.",
   "ask": "{name}, on an LOP file, who denies unnecessary care, and when, and what does that make you?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Monitor outcomes, not just attendance. Ask about visits, pain and what he can't do at work or home, and hunt the barriers: inside ones like fear or mood, outside ones like transport or money.\n\nOn 04/08/2026, day 61, he's in week 9. His week-6 mark fell on 04/07/2026, which is 02/24/2026 plus 42 days. The next step, whether that's medication, injections or a specialist, is the doctor's decision.\n\nName the variance before you fix it. Run A-C-T for every red flag, and adjust the plan first, because honestly, it's usually the plan, not the client.\n\nClimb the ladder one documented rung at a time: clinical, then administrative, then legal. Write every intervention as a GIRP note.\n\nYou coordinate care; you never direct it. Never tell him to skip treatment, or that missed visits cut his claim.\n\nAnd make friends with the clinic's front desk. The doctor runs the treatment, but the front desk runs the building, the schedule and possibly the weather. {name}, they'll know he missed before anyone.\n\nOne line to keep: you coordinate care, you never direct it.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does writing “this will help the case” in a GIRP note put the file at risk?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's live a few weeks of this file. A provider's missed-visit alert comes in.\n\nThat same day, you reach out to John Doe. If he's gone silent, you start the No Contact Protocol, logging the first attempt in the Communications Log the day the alert arrived.\n\nAt week 6, 04/07/2026, you ask the provider for the re-evaluation measures: range of motion, pain score and disability index.\n\nWhether a PHQ-9 or GAD-7 screen is indicated is the provider's call. Anything that puts his mental health in the record gets flagged to Atty. Thorne.\n\nIf the barrier isn't fixed at the clinical rung, you climb one rung, with a written notice to the carrier causing it, copied to Atty. Thorne, and only property facts to Keystone PD. Each rung dated.\n\nAnd red flags that can cost the claim go to Atty. Thorne with the fix you attempted. {name}, that's the whole job in one sentence: catch it, try to fix it, write it down.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the GIRP note need to be dated the day the alert arrives, not the day you finish?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-pip": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Would you rather spend $5,000 on one big bill nobody will ever discount, or on twelve small bills that might get discounted later? {name}, pick one. {name}, argue the other side. Hold that thought.\n\nHere's the thing about PIP: you only get to spend it once. Like a gift card with one balance.\n\nHarbor Point's $5,000 paid five of John Doe's bills, and every dollar was a bill his settlement will never have to pay back. But where those dollars landed decided what was left for Peach State, for the Letter of Protection, and in the end for John's net, his take-home.\n\nThe same ledger tells a BI adjuster, the one handling the at-fault driver's injury coverage, how serious the case looks, and in some states whether the defendant gets a credit for it.\n\nAnd last lesson's mystery, the $1,267.20 in wages? The bills used up the whole limit first.\n\nSo forecasting the limit, checking for caps and keeping the ledger exact protects the client's net months before anyone writes a demand.",
   "ask": "{name}, if PIP had paid twelve chiropractic visits instead of Summit's MRI, what would John Doe's file look like now?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's the strategy, and the answer to the would-you-rather. On a typical file, PIP does the most good on fixed costs, like the ER, imaging and specialist visits, because hospitals rarely reduce at the end and LOP providers often will.\n\nJohn's file shows it: PIP paid urgent care, Lakeside three times and Summit's $2,395.00 MRI, while chiropractic and therapy ran under the LOP.\n\nYour part is the forecast, not the choice. Routing outside the Payer Order is Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nThe limit is shared, so the bills used it by 06/26/2026 and the $1,267.20 wage benefit was never paid.\n\nIn states with an EMC rule, meaning PIP can be capped unless a doctor documents an emergency medical condition, check the records early and never ask a doctor to make that finding.\n\nWhere set-off applies, the defendant gets credit for PIP paid. Riverton is add-on, meaning PIP is a bonus on top, so it doesn't reduce BI or UM, the underinsured coverage after it.\n\nPaying bills as they arrive is easy. Seeing the empty tank coming is the job.",
   "ask": "{name}, John Doe's PIP took four months to run out. Does that mean his case is minor, and what figure tells the real story?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Three moments on the job. When a PIP check clears, you post it in the PIP Log and record what's left, what's still coming and the wage claim, so you see the empty date before it arrives.\n\nWhen a bill could go to PIP or to an LOP, you lay out both routes with the figures in an email to Atty. Thorne and log his decision. You never re-route a claim another payer already paid.\n\nAnd on a file in an EMC state, when the first treating records arrive, you check the rule and look for the finding. If it's missing, Atty. Thorne hears about it that day.\n\nQuick war story. A Case Manager I trained found out a client's PIP was empty from a cheerful letter: “Benefits exhausted! Have a wonderful day!” Three bills had already bounced.\n\nDon't let a form letter tell you news your ledger should've told you weeks ago.\n\nNow the mystery: there's a piece of paper that lets John Doe get treated now and pay later. {name}, before the next lesson, guess what it costs him.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you record when a PIP check clears, and why that one?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the job. Start with the Step 1 review, then send your notices by escalating channel and log each one: a call and a text on 04/08, a call and an email on 04/09, then Margaret on 04/10.\n\nEscalate to Atty. Thorne and send the 10-day Final Warning Letter by certified mail and email, with a response date of 04/20/2026, and set the Drop Warning status.\n\nWhen John calls on 04/17, you run a trauma-informed interview. Safety first, his pace, his choices, his words.\n\nYou don't diagnose, you don't send him to a counselor, and you say nothing about his claim.\n\nWith his consent you set up a call with Dr. Reyes and evening visits under the LOP. Then you restore the file and confirm 04/21 and 04/23. {name}, you're taking that call.\n\nKeep this: safety first, his pace, his words, no labels.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the letter's 04/20 date not mean the file closes on 04/20?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Okay, the clock starts at 04/08/2026, with this alert from Riverton Spine & Rehab on LOP account RSR-26-0412.\n\nJohn Doe missed 04/03 and 04/07 with no call. His last visit was 04/02/2026, and he isn't answering the clinic's calls or texts. Your own last contact with him was a check-in on 03/31.\n\nAs you work, the file keeps moving. He misses 04/09 and 04/14. His mother Margaret tells you on 04/10 that he's safe but keeping to himself. And on 04/16 the CMS fires its 14-day no-visit trigger.\n\nI'll release each update when you've earned it, {name}, including the call that comes in on 04/17. Trust me, you'll want to be ready for that one.\n\nRemember: the file keeps moving even when the client doesn't.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you do today, before you dial John's number?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log your work, check it. Was your Step 1 review dated 04/08/2026, before any outreach?\n\nDid your notices climb by channel, 04/08, 04/09 and Margaret on 04/10, each with its date, time, channel and outcome?\n\nDid the Final Warning Letter go by certified mail and email, with 04/20/2026 as the response date, withdrawal possible and not decided, and nothing about the claim?\n\nIn the interview, did you quote John's own words about the panic at the corner on 04/02, his sleep and his mood, and keep every label out of it?\n\nThen the restoration on 04/20: Drop Warning cleared, the 14-day trigger reset, the Action Plan updated.\n\nFour missed visits, 19 days, attendance on 04/21 and 04/23. {name}, that's a file that came back from the edge.",
   "ask": "{name}, which word in a note would do the defense's work for it on these 19 days?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-a3": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Call John back and really listen.\n\nWhere he goes, among the options his doctors find appropriate, is his choice. What care he gets is Dr. Reyes's and the treating provider's call, so you give no medical advice at all.\n\nCheck Crescent's network status with Peach State yourself. Don't trust the break room.\n\nThen work with Janelle Brooks, Lakeside's referral coordinator, to send the referral to Crescent Pain & Spine, and book Crescent's first opening while John's still on the line.\n\nAfter that, every task that follows is yours to do or to delegate, with an owner and a due date. The cancellation, the calendar, Parties, Peach State and the Lien Tracker, the records request, transport, the GIRP note, the report to Atty. Thorne, and the attendance check after 05/15/2026.\n\n“I told John to call Lakeside” is the bare minimum. What we want is John booked, on the phone, before you hang up.\n\n{name}, you're making the call. {name}, you're playing Janelle.\n\nRemember: his choice, the doctor's care, your logistics.",
   "ask": "{name}, who decides where John has the injection, and who decides whether he has it?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Here's the voicemail. It's 04/22/2026, 12:18 p.m., and it's John Doe. Before I play it, {name}, guess his reason for not going. Ride? Money? Pain? Let's hear it.\n\nCare has just restarted at Riverton Spine & Rehab, on evening slots, with Margaret driving him after her shift. The Civic is still in the shop.\n\nYesterday Lakeside's scheduling desk booked the injection Dr. Reyes referred him for back on 03/27/2026. But they booked it at Millbrook Interventional Pain Center, out in Millbrook, for 05/14/2026.\n\nJohn says he isn't going. It's too far, he's got no ride out there, and somebody at work told him the place is out of network with Peach State.\n\nNotice where that last fact came from: somebody at work. That's the billing version of “my cousin read it online.”\n\nHe still wants the appointment. He just wants it somewhere he can actually get to. {name}, how close was your guess?\n\nThe takeaway: verify the facts yourself; rumors aren't records.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you call him back, which document on the file would you open first, and why?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work before you log it. {name}, did you return the call on 04/22/2026 and record John's reasons in his own words?\n\nDid you verify Crescent's network status with Peach State, instead of repeating what he heard at work? Crescent Pain & Spine, Dr. Omar Haddad, is in network for member PSH-55017742.\n\nIs 05/15/2026 booked, one day after the Millbrook slot it replaces, with therapy carrying on in the evenings, so there's no new gap?\n\nIs the Millbrook slot off the calendar, with a reminder call on 05/13/2026 and an attendance check on 05/18/2026? Does every task have a date or an owner?\n\nAnd read your notes back for advice. Saying one place gives better care than the other fails, however kindly it's meant.\n\nHere's a little mystery to carry into the Knowledge Check. That 05/15/2026 injection is going to produce a bill, an EOB and, weeks later, a statement asking John for money.\n\n{name}, you've already seen how that story ends. Who remembers the number?",
   "ask": "{name}, if Crescent's first opening had been weeks away, who would you tell, and what would you ask them?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-l2": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick show of hands. Who here has ever had a health insurance company pay a bill, and then months later send a letter saying, actually, we'd like some of that back?\n\nKeep them up if it made you mad. Yeah. {name}, I see you. Hold onto that feeling, because that's this whole lesson.\n\nI've drawn two lanes on the board. Lane one: who pays John Doe's Medical Bills while the claim is open? Lane two: who pays the claim itself at the end?\n\nMost of the confusion on this topic is just those two questions being treated as one. The Health Plan answers the first question, and then it asks to be paid back out of the second.\n\nSo here's my teaser. If a Health Plan's claim for repayment gets discovered after the demand goes out, guess whose pocket the money comes out of?\n\nNot ours. Not the insurer's. I'll tell you in a minute, and it's going to sting.\n\nThe plan that pays now asks to be paid back later.",
   "ask": "{name}, who pays John Doe's Medical Bills while his claim is open, and who pays the claim itself?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules. Clear the triple-check before medical planning, so the claim's properly set up and you're allowed to act.\n\nHIPAA first: an Authorization to Release Medical Information written for this claim, naming the crash date and allowing contact with legal and insurance parties.\n\nThen Medicare and Medicaid. If the client's 65 or older or on SSDI, you report to the Benefits Coordination and Recovery Center under MMSEA, 2007. John Doe is 34, so you verify his SSDI status and write down the answer.\n\nThen the Payer Order, and verify whether MedPay or PIP applies; never assume. And the Payer Order is not the Recovery Stack. One pays bills, the other pays the claim.\n\nWhen a Health Plan pays accident bills, flag its repayment right the first time you hear about it, and record the carrier, member ID and group number in the lien register that day.\n\n“I'll deal with liens at the end” sounds reasonable and costs the client money. A dated lien record from day one lets Atty. Thorne negotiate it down from strength.\n\nNever promise a provider or a plan any amount. {name}, that's how good intentions become expensive.\n\nFlag the lien the first time you hear about it.",
   "ask": "{name}, why flag the Health Plan's repayment right the first time you hear about it, instead of waiting for the demand?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the sequence on a live file. The retainer's signed, so you get the HIPAA authorization written for this claim and file it in Documents. It names 02/06/2026 and allows legal and insurance contact.\n\nOnce you know the client's age and benefits, you check SSDI status, report to the BCRC if he's 65 or older or on SSDI, and log the answer and its date in Case Notes.\n\nWhen the Health Plan is identified, you record the carrier, member ID and group, plus the Payer Order, in Liens, so a dated lien record exists. And the moment the plan pays an accident bill, you flag its repayment right against the case, before any demand is drafted.\n\nA real-life moment for you. You'll ask a client for his health insurance card, and he'll read you the number off a card so faded it looks like a treasure map.\n\nAsk for a photo of the front and back. Every digit matters, because one wrong member ID and the plan says it never heard of us. {name}, what's your backup if the card is unreadable?\n\nOne wrong digit and the plan never heard of us.",
   "ask": "{name}, when do you record that SSDI answer, and where would Atty. Thorne look to find it?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-sum": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four cards, and each one opens.\n\nEvery coverage gets sorted twice: whose it is, and what it pays. Only BI, Host UM and Relative UM make the $40,000 stack.\n\nThe Payer-First Principle sends each bill to Harbor Point PIP first, then Peach State, then an LOP last. Like a line at the deli: everyone waits their turn.\n\nHealth subrogation means the plan claims back only what it paid, for the collision's care. Peach State asserted $3,618.00 on 06/10/2026 and confirmed $1,860.00 in writing on 06/24/2026.\n\nAnd the two CMS flags go up the day you know, in facts only, and get routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, in your own words, why would the plan's first number and its confirmed number be so different?",
   "ask": "{name}, before I open the last card, what's the exact day the lien flag goes up?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "By now John Doe's treatment is under way, and three money questions are sitting on his file at once.\n\nWho pays each bill while the claim is open? Who's going to want paying back out of the recovery? And what has to be flagged in the CMS right now, before anyone argues about it?\n\nThis lesson puts all three on one page. Think of it as the cheat sheet you'd tape inside your desk drawer.\n\nEvery card has more behind it, and by the end I want you able to say the two flag rules word for word. {name}, I'll be asking you first.\n\nBecause a lien found at disbursement, or a gap nobody recorded, is how a good file loses money.\n\nRemember: flag it the day you know it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those three questions do you think gets missed most often, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules in the course's own words. Only BI and UM stack, and that's $40,000. PIP and PD sit outside it.\n\nBills go PIP, then Peach State, then an LOP.\n\nThe lien is flagged in CMS Liens on first notice, which on this file was Peach State's letter of 02/27/2026, and always before any demand is drafted.\n\nThe gap is flagged the day the records show it. 02/06/2026 to 02/20/2026 is 14 days, counted exactly, recorded as facts, routed, with a watch for any later break.\n\n{name}, here's the subtle part. Neither flag is a judgment. Whether a lien is enforceable, or what a gap does to value, belongs to Atty. Thorne.\n\nA flag is a smoke alarm, not a fire inspector."
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it comes down to three triggers.\n\nA reimbursement right is first noticed, so you flag the lien in CMS Liens with its identifiers, dated before any demand.\n\nThe plan's itemized ledger arrives, so you audit each line, dispute what isn't the collision's care in writing, and change the register only when the plan confirms the figure, here $1,860.00.\n\nAnd the records show first care on 02/20/2026, so you flag the 14-Day Gap in the Action Plan Editor's risks band in facts only, route it, and keep watching.\n\nThat watching is how the 19-day break in April was caught the day the alert came in.\n\n{name}, predict what happens to John's net if that lien flag had waited until disbursement. Three triggers, three same-day moves.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you write in the risks band, word for word?"
  }
 },
 "d1m1-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "On 03/12/2026 you register Peach State's claim in CMS Liens and flag it against the matter: carrier, member ID and group number, its place in the Payer Order, the basis it asserts, and the date of the flag, with the Triple-Check results beside it. Say that date out loud when you set it.\n\nThen you answer Dion Carver in the Email Workspace, noting the claim and asking for an itemized ledger once anything is paid.\n\nOn 06/10/2026 you audit that ledger line by line, send the dispute, get the corrected balance confirmed in writing, and file the exchange in the CMS and on the Backup Drive.\n\nAnd in one line, tell me what has to happen before this lien can be compromised.\n\nFlag early, audit every line, get it in writing.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the date you're setting on that flag, and why that day?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "This one runs on two dates, so stay with me.\n\nOn 03/12/2026 Dion Carver at Peach State Health Plan writes about member PSH-55017742, recovery file SRU-26-03318. Following the plan's 02/27/2026 notice, it's processing John Doe's accident-related claims and will assert reimbursement from any recovery. No lien record exists on the matter yet.\n\nThen on 06/10/2026 the plan's interim itemization arrives, and it asserts $3,618.00 across five claim lines.\n\nThe pre-demand audit is still ahead of us. Picture wet cement: whatever figure we accept now is the figure the demand and the Net Sheet will carry.\n\nDon't accept the number. Audit it.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you typed $3,618.00 into the lien register today, who would that cost, and how?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "Here is Peach State's interim itemization, and I want a call on every line.\n\nA plan's recovery vendor pulls every claim from the coverage date. Think of a fishing net that scoops up everything in the water. So you'll find a 02/03/2026 eye exam from before the collision, a sinus infection on 03/18/2026, and an allergy visit on 05/06/2026 for a condition he already had.\n\nThe Crescent injection is the subtle one. The paid column says $1,860.00, but the plan is claiming the billed $3,400.00. Summit's MRI is fine at $0.00, because PIP paid it.\n\nAnd the cover letter wants full repayment with no reduction, which is not yours to answer.\n\nThese results come back in minutes here. On a real file a corrected itemization takes weeks.\n\nA plan can only claim what it paid, for this crash.",
   "ask": "{name}, which number on the Crescent line may a Health Plan claim, and why?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Run your record against this before you log it.\n\nPeach State is the carrier, with PSH-55017742 and GRP-41208 both entered. Its position sits after PIP and before the LOP providers. The three unrelated lines are disputed and Crescent is at the $1,860.00 the plan paid.\n\nThe flag is dated before any demand is drafted. And the $1,860.00 is confirmed in writing on 06/24/2026, with any compromise left to Atty. Thorne.\n\nWhen Dion Carver asked whether John would repay in full and what the limits were, the right answer acknowledged the figure, honored the notice and routed the rest.\n\nRegistering a lien isn't conceding it. The lien stage is where it gets reduced.",
   "ask": "{name}, reading your record cold at the lien stage, what would a negotiator still have to go looking for?"
  }
 },
 "d2-drive": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. File the four documents in the CMS from Work From, if they aren't filed yet, so each copy lands on your backup drive in its folder.\n\nNotes, evaluations and consults go to Medical Records. Invoices go to Bills and Invoices.\n\nThen open your Google Drive, find the Summit invoice sitting in Medical Records, and move it, don't copy it, into Bills and Invoices.\n\nOpen My Backup Drive and run Check my drive. Last, two sentences in Case Notes: the records versus bills rule, in your own words.\n\nA copy in both folders just doubles the confusion. One copy, in the one place the audit will look, is what we want. {name}, you're up first.",
   "ask": "{name}, the Letter of Protection is about money. Why does it still travel with the provider's evaluation?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 04/24/2026, half past three. John's back in care, and the last two months of treatment paper are filed in the CMS.\n\nThere's the Urgent Care note from his first visit, the Spine and Rehab evaluation with the Letter of Protection, the Lakeside consult, and the Summit Imaging invoice for the MRI.\n\nA colleague helped with the backup last week, and the Summit invoice landed in the medical records folder. Sounds harmless, right?\n\nNow picture the pre-demand audit in July. Someone totals John's bills from the bills folder, and twenty-three ninety-five isn't there.\n\nIt's like a sock in the wrong drawer. It's not lost, but nobody's wearing it on the day it matters.\n\n{name}, what happens to a bill nobody can find when the Specials get added up?",
   "ask": "{name}, a bill and a record from the same visit: which one proves what happened to John, and which one proves what it cost?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check yourself, and I'm calling on a few of you.\n\nAre the Urgent Care note, the evaluation and the Lakeside consult in Medical Records? Is the Summit invoice in Bills and Invoices, and only there?\n\nLook at your dashboard: does the number backed up match what you filed today? Did Check my drive come back with nothing missing?\n\nAnd your Case Note: read me the rule. {name}, go.\n\nRecords say what was done to John. Bills say what it cost.\n\nKeep them apart, and the Specials add up to the cent.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you found a bill in the records folder in July, the week before the demand, what else would you check?"
  }
 },
 "d2m2-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Game show time! This is your Knowledge Check, and I'm your host. Answer from this file, in your own words, typed under each question.\n\nHere's what's on the board. You'll read Peach State's 06/03/2026 EOB for claim PS-2605-449021 line by line and say where each amount goes.\n\nYou'll handle Crescent's 07/01/2026 statement asking John Doe for $1,540.00. And you'll explain from the Payer Order why Riverton got an LOP on 02/23/2026 and Summit's 03/11/2026 request was declined.\n\nThen there's a pressure round later on: an office manager who wants a promise. Bonus points to whoever resists her the longest.\n\nThe right answer promises nothing new, points her to the LOP signed on 02/23/2026, and gets Atty. Thorne involved before the appointment.\n\n{name}, you're our first contestant. {name}, you're the judge.\n\nThe takeaway for the whole module: follow the Payer Order and promise nothing.",
   "ask": "{name}, what may you tell Summit Imaging when you decline its LOP request, and what may you not?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now write your Self-Summary. It's 04/08/2026. Riverton Spine & Rehab reports John Doe has missed two visits, he isn't answering anyone, and Dana Whitfield has asked John for a Recorded Statement.\n\nTell me what you do first, second and third, and what each step protects.\n\nA couple of things I'll be looking for. On the 2021 visits, documenting low back versus neck, resolved, no prior neck complaints, is proof. Saying they “don't matter” is arguing.\n\nOn medication red flags, the provider hears the safety issue, Atty. Thorne hears the legal one, and you document the conversation. {name}, I might ask you to read yours out.\n\nRemember: you document and route; the provider and Atty. Thorne decide.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these three problems would you hand to Atty. Thorne before anything else today?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Finish with your Skill Scale. Rate each competency from one to five, honestly, and submit it for review. I'll compare your self-rating with your score, and if there's a wide gap, we'll talk it through.\n\nOne thing before you rate yourself. The line on legal advice works as a gate. A strong score doesn't survive a single payment promise to a provider, so if you made one anywhere today, rate with that in mind.\n\nThink of it like a copay: small, but it always gets collected.\n\nAnd your mystery for tomorrow. John Doe's file now has nineteen empty days in it, and we know exactly why.\n\nTomorrow we find out what the other side thinks those days mean, and whether our notes are strong enough to beat them.\n\n{name}, before you leave, tell me the one note on this file you'd want an adjuster to read first.",
   "ask": "{name}, where do you think your self-rating and your score will be furthest apart?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "So here's the road for the gap interview, nice and simple. You plan your questions, never the answers you're hoping for.\n\nYou open on the record with the date, your role and his consent to record. Then you ask open questions and follow his words. You ask for the paper, you read his story back to him, and you write it all down, with a dated task for every document he mentions.\n\nUnderneath all of it sits one rule: coordinate, don't commingle. That just means the medical side and the legal side don't get mixed up in your notes.\n\nYou write facts, never conclusions. Why? Because your opinion in a note is something the defense gets to use.\n\nHonestly, TV has ruined us a little. On TV the detective pounds the table and the witness cracks.\n\nOn this job, the detective asks a soft open question, then shuts up and types. {name}, which of those two do you think actually gets the truth?\n\nThe one thing to keep: ask soft, then shut up and type.",
   "ask": "{name}, why is staying quiet after an open question so much harder than it sounds?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Okay, quick game. It's called Better Question. I've got two questions up on the board, and you vote with your hands.\n\nQuestion one: “Was it because you had no car?” Question two: “What, if anything, made it hard to get seen sooner?” Hands up for one. Now hands up for two.\n\n{name}, I see you, you went with one. You've got ten seconds to defend it. Go.\n\nHere's the reveal. Question one gets you a yes. Question two gets you the truth.\n\nThe only gap explanation that survives an adjuster is John Doe's own, in his own words, backed by paper you can check: a work schedule, a doctor's availability note, a receipt.\n\nSo your job is to draw it out and write it down exactly as he says it. You don't explain it for him. You don't defend it, and you definitely don't judge it.\n\nOne more thing before we move on. By the end of this block, I'm going to show you how one friendly little sentence on this call can hand the defense their whole argument.\n\nRemember it this way: you're the stenographer, not the defense lawyer.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those two questions would the defense lawyer rather you asked John Doe, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules for the gap call. You investigate; you don't advocate.\n\nRead the EMS report, the 02/20/2026 urgent-care history and the timeline, then write a plan that holds questions only. Nothing else.\n\nRun it like a funnel. Broad open questions first, then follow-ups built on his words, and short yes-or-no questions last, only to pin down a date or a document. Never lead, and never judge.\n\nYou already know the usual reasons, like rides, co-pays and work schedules. You just never offer them to him.\n\nRecord only with his spoken consent. If Keystone wants a Recorded Statement, which is the insurance company taping his version of events, he politely declines and sends them to us.\n\nConfirm his 03/05/2026 consult with Dr. Reyes, and ask how he'll get to care once the rental ends 03/19/2026.\n\nNow, that friendly sentence I promised you: “Don't worry, the gap won't matter.” Sounds kind, right? It's legal advice. Reassure the process, never the outcome.\n\nAnd when a client says “I was busy,” that's not an explanation, that's a chapter title. {name}, what's your next question? Right: “Busy with what?”",
   "ask": "{name}, if John Doe's reason is true anyway, why does it matter how you asked for it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Now let's follow this on the job. It starts when the records show the 14-day gap.\n\nYou read the EMS report, the urgent-care history and the treatment timeline in the CMS. Then you save your question plan in Case Notes before the call, with no question in it naming a reason.\n\nWhen the call connects in the Call Simulator with AI John Doe, you state the date, who's on, and that it's recorded. You get his consent and confirm he's somewhere private.\n\nTrue story, more or less: I once had a client take this call from a packed barbershop, with three guys chiming in on what really happened. Privilege, which keeps our talk with the client protected, does not love an audience.\n\nIf he asks what the gap does to his case, no opinion. Log it and send it to Atty. Thorne through Tasks the same day.\n\nSo why all this care? Because months from now the adjuster will read that gap and try to knock thousands off the offer. A clean, documented explanation is what keeps that money on the table.\n\nNo opinions on the call, just his words, logged and routed.",
   "ask": "{name}, where in that sequence do you think a nervous new Case Manager is most likely to slip?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll run a recorded call with AI John Doe in the Call Simulator to find out why he first treated on 02/20/2026, fourteen days after the collision.\n\nOpen on the record with the date, who's on the call, your role, his consent to recording, and that he can talk privately.\n\nAsk open questions, follow his words, and save yes-or-no questions for pinning dates and documents. There are four reasons to draw out.\n\nFind out who he told and what he kept, like the texts, the call-outs and the 02/07 receipt, then read his account back. Close by confirming his 03/05 consult and his transport after 03/19.\n\nThen hand in a privileged GIRP note in Case Notes, that's Goal, Intervention, Response, Plan, with a dated task for every document.\n\n{name}, you're calling first. {name}, you're listening and counting leading questions.\n\nAnd if he says “I was busy,” remember what we said. That's a chapter title, not an answer.\n\nThe rule for this call: open questions earn facts, leading ones earn yeses.",
   "ask": "{name}, which reason was hardest to get, and what question finally got it?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Something landed on the file on 03/04/2026. Before I open it, {name}, guess: good news or trouble? Let's see.\n\nThe first records are in. Riverton Urgent Care's 02/20/2026 note says onset at the MVA, the motor vehicle accident, on 02/06/2026, getting worse, with numbness to the right hand since 02/18.\n\nThe EMS Run Report records neck stiffness on 02/06/2026. John Doe's been at Riverton Spine & Rehab since 02/24/2026, and Margaret's rental runs to 03/19/2026.\n\nNow the trouble: nobody has asked him why he waited 14 days.\n\nAtty. Thorne wants him on a recorded call today. Open questions, nothing suggested, no opinions on what it means, and a GIRP note when you're done.\n\nThere are two ways to fail before anyone even dials: a leading question or a suggested reason, and any version of “won't matter” or “will hurt the case.”\n\nAnd here's the teaser. Hidden in this call are four separate reasons. Most people find two.\n\nThe goal is simple: his reasons, in his words, with his paper.",
   "ask": "{name}, knowing what's on this file, what question will you be most tempted to ask him first?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This is your run sheet for the call. The simulator screen on the left shows AI John Doe, recorded call, 03/04/2026, with the red recording dot and a transcript below.\n\nUnder the transcript there's a meter counting your questions: open, closed and leading. Leading is in red, and you want that one at zero.\n\nThis isn't a TV interrogation room. Nobody gets points for a dramatic “Admit it, you had no car!”\n\nOn the right are four empty reason slots, each with a space for a document beside it, and they only fill in as you actually surface them.\n\nUnderneath sits a red warning: a suggested reason earns a yes, not a fact. Reasons are earned by questions. You never supply them.\n\nOne slot filled isn't done. All four, each with its paper, is the call we're after. {name}, what's your plan for the first two minutes?\n\nTakeaway: zero leading questions, four reasons, four documents.",
   "ask": "{name}, what happens to the three hidden reasons if you ask “Was it the car?” two minutes in?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check the call and the note before you log, and yes, I'm going to pick on a few of you.\n\n{name}, did the recording start with 03/04/2026, everyone on the call, your role, his spoken consent and privacy confirmed? Was your first question open, covering 02/06 to 02/20, with zero leading questions?\n\nHere's the full reveal, all four reasons in his words. One: no transport, with the Civic towed, Kevin Alvarez's Escape in the shop and no rental until 02/18/2026.\n\nTwo: he thought it was soreness, treated with ibuprofen and lidocaine patches. Three: he doubted his Peach State coverage and didn't know PIP, the coverage that pays his Medical Bills no matter who caused the crash, even existed.\n\nFour: the numbness from 02/18. He worked two shifts, then went to urgent care on his first day off.\n\nDid you surface the texts, the Northgate call-outs and the receipt? And grade your GIRP note as hard as the call: his words in quotation marks, a dated plan, labelled privileged.\n\nYour mystery: there's a second interruption later in his care. {name}, find the dates.",
   "ask": "{name}, does your GIRP note record an intervention and a plan, or only how John Doe felt?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "So here's what sits behind the paperwork. Continuity is proved by what the record already shows, never by what it's asked to say.\n\nContinuity of care just means an unbroken path of treatment, each step following from the last. And that chain is exactly what an adjuster tests.\n\nA Treatment Gap is any break in it, and adjusters read a gap as minor, healed or unrelated.\n\nRecords made at the time, written by EMS, doctors and employers as things happened, are your strongest proof, because nobody wrote them for the claim.\n\nAnd consistent histories, the same story of how, when and where it hurts across every chart, make continuity believable.\n\nOn TV, the detective finds one magic piece of evidence. In real life, you find twelve boring receipts that agree with each other. Boring wins, every time.\n\n{name}, before I ask, take a guess at who wrote the first thing about John Doe's neck.\n\nRemember: the record proves continuity, not your explanation of it.",
   "ask": "{name}, who on John Doe's file wrote something down about his neck before anyone thought about a claim?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "Four more ideas, and one of them can end a career.\n\nA causation opinion is a treating doctor's opinion linking the injury to the collision. It's the bridge across a gap, and Atty. Thorne requests it, not us.\n\nRecord integrity means a record changes only by its author's own dated addendum, an added note with a date on it. Altering a record can be evidence tampering or fraud. That's the career-ender.\n\nExcuse notes prove only what their author saw or ordered at a visit, so days nobody examined him need other proof.\n\nAnd generic notes, ones that don't tie the complaints to the collision, weaken causation. Under A-C-T, those get flagged to Atty. Thorne. You don't fix them yourself.\n\nIt's so tempting to just call the clinic and ask them to “update” a note. Resist it. Flag it and let counsel decide.\n\n{name}, now think about the week of 02/09.\n\nThe career-saving rule: you never ask anyone to change a record.",
   "ask": "{name}, what proves John Doe couldn't work 02/09 through 02/13, when no doctor saw him?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "These are the treatment red flags, and each one is a gift to the defense. Before I go through them, {name}, guess which one shows up most. Hold it.\n\nIn physical therapy, more than 12 sessions with no gain and no specialist or imaging suggested reads as a stalled plan or over-treatment.\n\nPassive care only, meaning heat packs, TENS, which is a little electrical pulse machine, and massage with no exercise, is feel-good care adjusters discount.\n\nTwo or more missed appointments let the defense say the injury wasn't serious. And surgery recommended before therapy or injections, outside an emergency, invites a challenge on whether it was reasonable and necessary.\n\nIn pain management, an opioid, a muscle relaxant and a sedative together, with doses rising, is a safety risk.\n\nIn the records, left and right switched, or notes about life stress instead of the crash, weaken both credibility and causation.\n\nAnd the answer to my guess? Missed appointments, by a mile.\n\nTakeaway: spot the red flag before the defense does, and raise it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these red flags do you see on John Doe's file right now?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game time: Prove It. The adjuster says the gap proves John Doe healed. You've got thirty seconds to shout out everything you'd collect to prove he didn't. Go!\n\n{name}, you're writing them up. Ooh, good list.\n\nNow let's sort it into two boxes: things written down at the time, and things written up for the claim.\n\nAnd here's the reveal on last block's mystery. The paper made before anyone thought about a claim is the 02/07/2026 Riverton Drug receipt, one day after the crash, for pain relief. Nobody wrote that for a lawsuit.\n\nRecords made at the time do the heavy lifting. Provider notes that place the pain at the collision, histories that agree, the EMS report and the work records, and later a causation opinion, which is a doctor's letter linking the injury to the crash.\n\nAnything in the other box, a record made to order, destroys the very case it was meant to save.\n\nThe takeaway: made at the time beats made to order, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, which item on our list would an adjuster find hardest to argue with, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules. Continuity is proved by records made at the time, then by a doctor's opinion that reads them, and never by explanations made to order.\n\nRead every history for three things. How it happened: the collision. When it started: 02/06/2026. Where it hurts: the neck and right arm, since the 2021 visits were low back.\n\nIf the therapy notes and Dr. Reyes disagree, flag it to Atty. Thorne that day. Never ask a provider to change, back-date or add to a record, and never order an excuse note.\n\nFor 02/09 to 02/13, Northgate's Attendance Record is the proof. The EMS report covers day zero, so record “declined transport” exactly as written.\n\nKeep the 02/07/2026 Riverton Drug receipt and the full text thread to Margaret Doe. A causation opinion is Atty. Thorne's request; you assemble and track, never draft.\n\nAnd no, {name}, “I was going to scan it, I was just busy” doesn't count. If it's not in the file, it doesn't exist.",
   "ask": "{name}, Riverton's front desk offers to add a line saying the pain began at the crash. What do you say, and who do you tell?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This is a compare board, and the heading says it all: made at the time versus made to order. {name}, read me the dates on the left card while I count them.\n\nOn the left, under a green rule, is what counts: the 02/06 EMS report, the 02/07 receipt, the 02/09 to 02/13 call-outs, the 02/20 urgent care note and the 06/19 Causation Letter.\n\nOn the right, in red, is what never happens, every item struck through.\n\nNow look along the bottom. There's a timeline from 02/06 to 06/19 with the left card's documents pinned onto it, so you can see the proof was being laid down while the gap was actually happening.\n\nTiming is what makes a document believable.\n\nAnd that's real money. A gap the adjuster can't explain away is a gap he can't use to cut the offer.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the 06/19 Causation Letter belong in the left column, even though it came months later?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the rhythm on the job. A provider record arrives, and you compare how, when and where against the other histories on the treatment timeline. Either it's logged consistent, or it's flagged with the conflicting line quoted.\n\nIf one history conflicts with another, you flag it to Atty. Thorne through Tasks that same day. You don't call the provider about the wording.\n\nWhen Atty. Thorne decides to seek a causation opinion, you assemble the records for his letter and put the follow-up on the calendar.\n\nAnd when a work or excuse note arrives, you check the signer, the date and the covered days against the visit record.\n\nI once watched a Case Manager print the entire file, four hundred pages, “just in case,” to find one date. The date was on page two of the CMS timeline.\n\nSearch first, print never. {name}, you'd have found it in ten seconds, right?\n\nRemember: compare, flag, route, all on the same day.",
   "ask": "{name}, why shouldn't you call the provider yourself to sort out a conflicting history?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-cause": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Four ideas sit under this lesson. First, the two halves. Duty and breach, meaning Smith had to drive safely and didn't, prove negligence. Causation and damages prove what it cost, and a claim needs both.\n\nSecond, who writes it: a treating physician or surgeon who examined the patient, or an independent medical expert who reviews the records. Dr. Reyes is John Doe's treating spine surgeon.\n\nThird, reasonable medical probability, which simply means more likely than not. Words like “possibly” or “could be” generally fall short, and the exact standard is Atty. Thorne's to confirm.\n\nA doctor writing “possibly” is basically a Magic 8-Ball answer: ask again later.\n\nFourth, prior conditions. A prior injury in the same body region turns the question into aggravation, meaning whether the crash made an old problem worse.\n\nJohn Doe's 2021 care was for his low back, a different region, and Dr. Reyes says it's unrelated. {name}, which of those four would you bet the defense attacks first?\n\nThe phrase to remember: more likely than not.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does it matter that the 2021 visits were for his low back and not his neck?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: guilty or not proven. I'll say a fact, and you tell me if it proves Keystone has to pay for John's neck.\n\nRobert Smith was cited for failure to stop. {name}? Ruth Calloway, the witness, saw it happen. {name}?\n\nHere's the twist: neither one is enough.\n\nProving negligence, that Smith was careless, is only half the battle in personal injury law. The other half is causation and damages.\n\nThat means proving this collision, and nothing else, caused his C5-C6 herniation, which is a disc bulging out between two neck bones, plus his Medical Bills and the right-arm symptoms he still has.\n\nAnd Keystone already has two other causes lined up: the 14-day gap and his 2021 chiropractic visits.\n\nThe document that links the crash to the injury is the Causation Letter. On this file it's Dr. Alana Reyes's letter of 06/19/2026, and the demand, the rebuttal and every binder after it lean on it.\n\nThink of it like a group project. Smith did the crash, but somebody still has to prove who did the damage.\n\nKeep this: fault gets you in the door, causation gets you paid.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Keystone admitted fault tomorrow, what could it still refuse to pay for?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you ever handle one, let's open this document up. I'll save the punchline for last.\n\nA Causation Letter is a formal opinion from a medical professional, a treating physician, a surgeon or an independent medical expert, linking the accident to the specific injuries.\n\nIts legal purpose is the half negligence can't prove: that the defendant's conduct directly caused the harm, the medical expenses and the ongoing limitations claimed.\n\nIts evidentiary role on our file, meaning the job it does as proof, is big. Dr. Reyes holds her opinions more likely than not, ties the herniation to 02/06/2026, and answers both the gap and the 2021 visits.\n\n{name}, guess what your job with this letter is. Here's the punchline: you handle the letter's path, never its words.\n\nAtty. Thorne requests it. You assemble the records and docket the follow-up, check it against the file when it lands, and file it and cite it.\n\nAnd read it the same day. Don't just file it unread, because a wrong date in a doctor's letter is a gift to the defense.\n\nHonestly, a Causation Letter that says “maybe” is a fortune cookie, not evidence.",
   "ask": "{name}, which part of handling this letter belongs to you, and which part never does?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules that keep this letter worth something. Atty. Thorne requests it, in his own letter, with John Doe's written authorization.\n\nYou build the record set Dr. Reyes needs, and that means everything: the EMS report, the urgent-care note, Riverton Spine & Rehab's notes, the MRI report, Crescent's procedure note, and the 2021 Birch Avenue records too. Then you docket the follow-up and track the reply.\n\nThe day it arrives, you check it against the file: date of loss 02/06/2026, C5-C6 on the right, the visit dates, the records she lists. A mismatch goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nYou file it under Medical, dated 06/19/2026, and cite it on the Gap Explanation Record. And you never draft, edit or suggest a word of it.\n\n{name}, I know the temptation: leave out the 2021 records so the doctor never sees them. Send them anyway.\n\nA doctor who never saw the prior records gets taken apart at deposition, and her whole opinion goes down with her. Hiding records from your own expert is like hiding the map from your own driver.\n\nThe takeaway: give the doctor everything, write nothing for her.",
   "ask": "{name}, why send Dr. Reyes the 2021 records when they're the defense's argument?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "This table turns Dr. Reyes's letter into a map of the fight. Before I read it, {name}, guess how many separate opinions she gives. Four.\n\nHer four opinions are on the left, the defense each one answers is in the middle, and the records holding it up are on the right.\n\nThe delay is clinically consistent, which answers “he waited two weeks,” with the EMS report and the 02/20 urgent-care note behind it.\n\nThe herniation was more likely than not caused by the collision. That answers “degenerative,” meaning just wear and tear from age, backed by the MRI's no significant degenerative change at the adjacent levels.\n\nThe 2021 care is unrelated, which answers a pre-existing condition. And the treatment was reasonable and necessary, with future injections anticipated in the 06/12 MMI note, MMI meaning he's as good as he's going to get.\n\nEvery opinion rests on a record, and that's why the record set matters so much. Pull one record out, and the opinion on top of it wobbles like a table with a short leg.",
   "ask": "{name}, which opinion would be weakest if one record were missing from her set?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it runs in four beats. When Atty. Thorne decides to request a causation opinion, you assemble the record set, prior records included, for his letter, and put the follow-up on the calendar.\n\nWhen the letter arrives on 06/19/2026, you check the date of loss, the injury level and side, the visit dates and the records listed against the file. You log each one as matching, or quote the mismatch and flag it.\n\nOnce it's checked, you file it and cite it on the Gap Explanation Record's 14-day band and in the audit's risks.\n\nAnd when the Demand Package comes together, the letter goes in beside the MRI report and the EMS report, served on 07/20/2026.\n\nWhy does it matter so much? Because the adjuster reads the Causation Letter first and the bills second.\n\nI once saw a Causation Letter that described the wrong arm. The doctor fixed it in a day, but only because the Case Manager caught it before it went out. {name}, that's the job: be the person who catches it.\n\nRemember: check it against the file the day it lands.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you do if the letter listed the wrong date of loss?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll build John Doe's Gap Explanation Record in the CMS Case File. It covers both interruptions with exact dates: the 14-day gap, 02/06 to 02/20/2026, and the 19-day disruption, 04/02 to 04/21/2026.\n\nPair every reason with the dated document that backs it up. Mark any reason with no independent document as “account only,” meaning it rests on his word alone.\n\nCite the complaints before and after each interruption, and the firm's follow-up during the disruption.\n\nFacts and citations only: no argument, no valuation, and nothing quoted from the privileged call. Run your defense-eye pass, then route it to Atty. Thorne through Tasks.\n\n{name}, you're building the 14-day band. {name}, you've got the 19-day band.\n\nWhy so precise? Because this record decides whether the adjuster gets to say “gap” and knock money off, or has to read our proof first.\n\nAnd no printing the whole file to find a date. I'm watching you.\n\nRemember: every reason sits beside its document, or it says account only.",
   "ask": "{name}, which reason on this file has the weakest document, and what do you do about it?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Game: Beat the Adjuster. I'm putting the adjuster's four arguments on the board: not serious, recovered early, treatment unnecessary, another cause.\n\n{name}, pick one and knock it down with a document, not a sentence. Ooh, good one.\n\nHere's what landed on 06/22/2026. John Doe reached MMI on 06/12/2026, and Dr. Alana Reyes's Causation Letter of 06/19/2026 is in.\n\nShe says delayed presentation is clinically consistent with a disc injury whose nerve symptoms build over days to weeks, and that the injury was more likely than not caused by the collision. Put simply, a late start to treatment makes medical sense for this injury.\n\nAtty. Thorne wants the Gap Explanation Record before the pre-demand session. Both interruptions at exact dates, every reason beside its dated document, “account only” where there isn't one, and a defense-eye pass first.\n\nAnd yes, there are two gaps. Here's last activity's mystery solved: the second one runs 04/02 to 04/21/2026.\n\nKeep this: answer an argument with a document, never an adjective.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the adjuster's four arguments does Dr. Reyes's letter answer most directly?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the shape the record takes. It's one CMS card, Gap Explanation Record, John Doe, split into two bands of equal weight.\n\nThe top band is the 14-day gap, 02/06 to 02/20/2026. The bottom band is the 19-day disruption, 04/02 to 04/21/2026, and its reason is the anxiety that followed the crash.\n\nEach band has three columns: the reason, the corroborating document and the date. Any row that rests only on John's word gets a grey account only tag. Down in the corner is the control to route it to Atty. Thorne.\n\nNow notice something, {name}. The second interruption gets exactly the same room as the first.\n\nThat's on purpose. Every reason sits beside its proof, in both bands.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think the 19-day band is drawn exactly as big as the 14-day one?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Now check your record. The 14-day gap runs 02/06 to 02/20/2026. The 19-day disruption runs 04/02 to 04/21/2026, with missed visits on 04/03, 04/07, 04/09 and 04/14.\n\nFor no transportation in February, you've got the tow record and Keystone's rental from 02/18/2026. For expecting it to pass, there's the 02/07/2026 Riverton Drug receipt.\n\nFor April, the reason is crash-related anxiety after the panic at the corner on 04/02. Its documents are Dr. Reyes's 04/20 note and the counselor's 06/10 summary, with his own statement marked account only.\n\nThe rental and the carpool are background, not the cause.\n\nAnd did you file the referral, the summary and his statement in the CMS and Drive for Atty. Thorne's demand decision? {name}, keep the privileged calls out of all of it.\n\nFacts and citations only. That's what makes this record bulletproof.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the difference between this internal record and a gap timeline that goes to Keystone?"
  }
 },
 "d2m3-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Learning Check, and we're starting with a live round. I'll play John Doe, and {name}, you're the Case Manager.\n\nI'm going to ask you, “Is this gap going to wreck my case?” If I hear “don't worry,” I ring the buzzer and {name} takes over. Ready? Good.\n\nNow the written part. Answer from this file, in your own words, right under each question.\n\nName the four reasons for the 14-day gap, each with its document and date. Rewrite “Was it because you had no car?” so it surfaces the same reason without suggesting it, and explain what the leading version costs.\n\nAnd the last one: Robert Smith's citation proves fault, so what else must the file prove, with which document, and what's your part in getting it?\n\nA tip from someone who's graded a lot of these: “see attached” is not an answer. Neither is a smiley face, which, yes, I've received.\n\nRemember this one: a reason you suggest is worth nothing, a reason he tells you is worth money.",
   "ask": "{name}, you're the Case Manager first. What do you say when John Doe asks if the gap will wreck his case?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Time for your Self-Summary. Same date, 04/08/2026. Riverton Spine & Rehab says John Doe has missed two visits, he isn't answering anyone, and Dana Whitfield wants a Recorded Statement from him.\n\nWhat do you do first, second and third, and what does each step protect?\n\nWhile you're thinking about records, chew on this. {name}, in your own words, why is a provider's offer to add a line to a chart more dangerous than a missing note?\n\nHere's the answer. A missing note is a weakness. An altered one is a credibility collapse, and possibly a fraud question.\n\nSo: a hole in the record hurts. A patch on the record kills.",
   "ask": "{name}, why is a provider's offer to change a record more dangerous than a note that's simply missing?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last up is the Skill Scale. Give yourself a one to five on each skill and submit it for review. I'll compare each rating with your score and talk through any big gap with you.\n\nBe honest. A five on everything tells me you're either a genius or you didn't read the list.\n\nAnd remember, UPL is a gate here too. A strong score doesn't survive a coached answer or a requested change to a record.\n\nIf you couldn't rewrite that leading question cleanly, say so. It just means another round in the simulator.\n\nNow the mystery to carry into the next check. You've explained every gap. But does the paperwork add up to the same number everywhere it appears?\n\n{name}, I bet one total on this file disagrees with itself somewhere. Go find it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which skill from today would you most want another run at in the simulator?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Think of the pre-demand audit as the final stress test, the point where we stop collecting and start using, leaving the defense zero easy outs.\n\nIt starts with what we call the trinity of case alignment. Fancy name, simple idea: the story, the physical evidence and the medical records all have to agree. Like three legs on a stool. Lose one and the value collapses.\n\nMaximum Medical Improvement, MMI, is the doctor's finding that he's as good as he's going to get. Once that happens, the bills are complete and the audit can begin.\n\nSpecial damages, or Specials, are the provable dollar loss: Medical Bills plus lost wages plus Property Damage. Every line needs a document.\n\nThe medical and billing audit runs three passes: dates for gaps, billing codes for duplicates, and prior history for old injuries.\n\n{name}, quick guess before the next slide: which pass catches the most money, and why?\n\nRemember: no document, no dollar.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these audit ideas is new to you, and which part of John Doe's file would it test first?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "Four more pieces. Liens and subrogation come first. Subrogation is just a payer's right to be paid back out of the recovery.\n\nHealth Plans, government payers and LOP providers, who treated now and get paid later, may all claim repayment. And every lien shrinks his net, like hands reaching into the same pie.\n\nThe Property Damage deep dive looks at the photos of where the car was hit, the estimate against the final bill and any supplements, because the car proves the force behind the injury.\n\nThe defense-eye audit is reading the file the way the adjuster will: social media, a minor-impact label, the venue, which is where the case would be tried, and naming each value killer.\n\nAnd policy-limits targets are the verified limits in the order they open. That's what each policy can pay, which isn't the same as what the claim's worth.\n\n{name}, put your adjuster hat on and predict the first thing you'd attack.\n\nThe one line: what a policy can pay and what the claim is worth are two different numbers.",
   "ask": "{name}, what on John Doe's file would an adjuster point to for a minor-impact argument?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "This is the Master Audit Checklist, and honestly, I love a checklist. A pilot runs one before every flight, even after twenty years, and nobody calls the pilot lazy. The only lazy checklist is the one you tick from memory without opening the file.\n\nOn the money side: Specials, meaning Medical Bills, lost wages and Property Damage, every line documented.\n\nLost wages need the employer's wage letter plus a doctor's work note. Without both, they usually get rejected. Then lien resolution, every payer who wants to be paid back.\n\nFor liability proof: the final Police Report, witness contact and any drift in their statements, and whether the crash physics even works. The Property Damage deep dive checks the point of impact and any supplement showing hidden structural damage.\n\nMedical and billing gets the date check with every gap explained, the code check with no duplicates, and five to ten years of history.\n\n{name}, read the third line aloud, then tell us why it needs two documents, not one.\n\nTakeaway: tick it from the file, never from memory.",
   "ask": "{name}, which line of this checklist do you think is weakest on John Doe's file today?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Pop quiz. No pressure. Mostly.\n\nThere's $7,965.00 in liens waiting behind a $10,000 BI limit. A lien is a bill that gets paid out of the settlement before the client sees his share, and BI is the at-fault driver's bodily injury coverage.\n\nMental math, everyone: how much of that layer is left for John Doe? {name}, you're first. {name}, check that answer.\n\nRight, $2,035.00. Feel that number? That's why pre-demand matters.\n\nThe demand goes out later today, but what it can claim gets decided right here. Think of the audit as packing the suitcase: the demand can only bring what you packed and can prove.\n\nOne duplicate line or one unchecked limit is Keystone's easy out.\n\nAnd remember last block's mystery, the total that disagrees with itself? It's hiding in one provider's statement. I'll reveal it on the model slide.\n\nThe takeaway: the demand can only spend what this audit proves.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why is $2,035.00 a scarier number for John Doe than $10,000?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "First question: who decides John reached MMI? Not you, and not John. It's Lakeside Orthopedics, recorded from its 06/12/2026 note with the future-care plan.\n\nAudit for verification, valuation, risk and compliance, with the SOL, the deadline to sue, of 02/06/2028 on the calendar. The Civic runs on its own Keystone PD claim, so his BI Specials are medical plus wages.\n\nThree passes: dates for the 14 days and the 19-day disruption, codes for duplicates, and prior history, the 2021 low-back visits. Show billed beside paid, because that choice is Atty. Thorne's.\n\nNorthgate verifies 9 shifts at 8 hours and $22.00, so $1,584.00. Flag the five shifts with no note. The $3,400 to $6,800 in future injections stays out of the total.\n\nRegister every lien, including Peach State's $1,860.00. $16,401.40 already exceeds the $10,000 limit.\n\nYou audit, total and flag; you don't value the case. {name}, explain why that line matters.\n\nAdding up bills is arithmetic. Proving every one of them is the job.",
   "ask": "{name}, who decides that John Doe reached MMI, and where do you record it from?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the reveal. Read this one left to right.\n\nFirst there's a gate, MMI on 06/12/2026, with a green tick. Nothing enters the ledger until that's passed.\n\nIn the centre is the ledger itself, and there's our mystery: the Riverton Spine & Rehab row. $6,290.00 is struck through in red and replaced by $6,105.00, with a badge flagging a $185.00 duplicate on 05/04/2026. One visit, billed twice. That's what the code check catches.\n\nUnderneath is the sum: $14,817.40 in medicals plus $1,584.00 in wages, which gives $16,401.40. The future medical card sits outside the frame, in the story, not the total.\n\nOn the right are the targets in the order they open: $10,000 BI, $20,000 Host UM and $10,000 Relative UM, each with its source.\n\nUM is the coverage that steps in when the at-fault driver's insurance isn't enough. Host UM sits on the car John was riding in, and Relative UM on the policy of a relative he lives with.\n\n{name}, the floor is yours. One duplicate caught is one excuse taken away.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would Keystone have done with that $185.00 duplicate if we hadn't caught it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "The story starts on 06/12/2026, when the MMI note arrives. You record MMI and the future-care plan on the treatment timeline, and open the pre-demand audit as a task with an owner and a date.\n\nAs the final bills and records come in, you build the Specials ledger and match it against the dates, until every billed date matches a visit and the medical total is $14,817.40.\n\nThen you register every lien and record billed beside paid in the Liens tab and the Net Sheet's Lien Tracker, with the billed-versus-paid question routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\nFinally, before the ask, you re-verify each limit in the Coverage Matrix: $10,000, then $20,000, then $10,000, $40,000 in all, each with its written source.\n\nI once saw a demand go out citing a limit that had changed three weeks earlier. The adjuster's reply was one line long, and it was not a compliment.\n\n{name}, you're up. Remember: verified last week isn't verified today.",
   "ask": "{name}, why re-verify the limits now, when the Coverage Matrix was confirmed weeks ago?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-wage": {
  "task": {
   "say": "First you write the Employer Verification email to Angela Ruiz in the Email Workspace, with the authorization.\n\nYou're asking for the Wage Loss Verification Form, payroll records and his W-2, and the employer's confirmation of his rate, his shift hours, each missed date, any overtime and the physical demands of the job.\n\nWhen she replies, she'll ask for his MRI and whether the settlement repays him. Your answer sends no records and says nothing about the claim.\n\nThen you match every missed shift to a provider record, flag the five February shifts to Atty. Thorne, audit the draft to the verified total, and enter it in the Specials and the Net Sheet.\n\n{name}, you're writing to Angela. Remember: the employer gets what it needs to verify, and not one page more.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the authorization not let you send Northgate his MRI?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 06/22/2026, ten days after Lakeside declared MMI. John Doe signed Northgate Distribution's employee authorization this morning.\n\nWhat we have on his wages is a draft worksheet somebody built from his own account over the phone, and it claims $1,760.00. Nothing in it comes from a record yet.\n\nAngela Ruiz runs HR at Northgate, and the Special Damages Audit is due on 07/01/2026.\n\nEvery missed shift that reaches the demand has to stand up to an adjuster who'll check it against payroll and the medical chart. Think of it like a timesheet your strictest boss is going to sign.\n\n{name}, take a guess: will the verified number go up or down from $1,760.00?",
   "ask": "{name}, looking at that worksheet, what in it came from a record?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "Now call every row of the draft against Northgate's records and the doctors' notes. It's a spot-the-difference puzzle, and there are five.\n\nYou'll find 02/18 and 02/19 claimed, and he worked both. The MRI is written as 03/12, and it was 03/13.\n\nThe 05/18 shift rests on his memory, when Crescent's work note covers 05/15 through 05/18. Light duty and overtime appear that Northgate says never happened.\n\nAnd the five call-outs from 02/09 to 02/13 were simply left out because there's no doctor's note. Those stay in, flagged, never dropped.\n\nNorthgate answered in minutes here. A real employer can take weeks, which is why the request goes out the day MMI is declared.\n\n{name}, which of the five would an adjuster spot first?",
   "ask": "{name}, why is dropping the February shifts worse than claiming them with a flag?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work before you hand it in. Northgate confirmed the rate, the hours, no overtime and the job's demands: lifting to 50 pounds, forklift, on his feet all shift.\n\nThe 03/05, 03/13, 05/15 and 05/18 shifts are each matched to a provider record. The 02/09 to 02/13 shifts are kept and flagged with the call-out record, the EMS report and the 02/07 receipt.\n\nThe draft went from $1,760.00 to $1,584.00, nine shifts at eight hours and $22.00. {name}, how close was your guess?\n\nAnd not one page of his medical records went to his employer.\n\nThe takeaway: a smaller number you can prove beats a bigger one you can't.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Keystone asked how you know he missed 03/13, what would you show them?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll build John Doe's audited Specials ledger in the CMS Financial Ledger. Enter each provider's charges with dates, the billed amount and who paid.\n\nMatch Riverton Spine & Rehab's lines against its visit notes, and take out any line no visit supports.\n\nTotal the medical, add the verified wages and show billed beside paid, meaning what the provider charged next to what was actually paid. Future medical goes in the narrative, not the ledger.\n\nList every lien with its amount in Liens and the Lien Tracker, and re-verify the three policy-limits targets in order on the Insurance tab.\n\n{name}, you're on medical lines. {name}, wages and liens. Part of your grade is what you flag rather than decide.\n\nCopying totals off the statements isn't an audit. Proving every line is.\n\nAnd “I was busy, so I trusted the statement” is exactly the sentence Keystone is hoping you'll say.",
   "ask": "{name}, Peach State paid $1,860.00 on a $3,400.00 bill. Which number goes in the demand, and who decides?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "This landed on 07/01/2026: billing complete, Special Damages Audit due, LSH-2026-MVA-770412.\n\nLakeside Orthopedics declared MMI on 06/12/2026, and Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter arrived 06/19/2026. Every bill's in.\n\nRiverton Urgent Care at $685.00. Riverton Spine & Rehab's statement of 34 lines at $185.00, or $6,290.00, with its visit notes. Three Lakeside Orthopedics visits at $640.00. Summit Imaging at $2,395.00. Crescent Pain & Spine at $3,400.00, which Peach State paid $1,860.00 on. And Riverton Drug at $312.40.\n\nNorthgate's HR letter of 06/30/2026 verifies 9 missed shifts at 8 hours times $22.00.\n\nI'm handing you the bills, not the totals. Think of it like a restaurant check: you don't pay the bottom line until you've read every item.\n\nTeaser: there's one number in that list that doesn't add up, and you already know which provider it's hiding in. {name}, don't say it yet. Let your group find it.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you total Riverton Spine & Rehab's statement, how many visits do its notes actually show?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your ledger to the cent. Riverton Spine & Rehab's notes show 33 visits against 34 lines, so the 05/04/2026 duplicate comes out and $6,290.00 becomes $6,105.00, with a corrected statement requested.\n\nYour medical total should be $14,817.40, down from $15,002.40. Wages are 9 shifts at 8 hours and $22.00, so $1,584.00. That gives total Specials of $16,401.40.\n\n{name}, read me your number. If it's anything else, a line is wrong.\n\nHere's the subtle one. Five of those nine shifts, 02/09 to 02/13/2026, fall before any provider saw John. Flag them with Northgate's Attendance Record; never drop them.\n\nBilled sits beside paid, and the choice goes to Atty. Thorne. Future medical stays in the narrative, and the targets run $10,000, $20,000, $10,000.\n\nThe takeaway: every dollar on the ledger has a visit, a document or a flag behind it.\n\nYour mystery: Atty. Thorne will push on three weak spots in the strategy session. {name}, name them before he does.",
   "ask": "{name}, why flag those five early shifts instead of quietly dropping them from the wage claim?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "A pre-demand audit is the file's sanity check. The strategy brief carries its findings, not conclusions, to Atty. Thorne, who makes every call.\n\nThe brief reports on the four core audit objectives, which I'll reveal on the next slide.\n\nIt flags statement drift, which is a witness whose story changes between tellings, because drift is an opening for the defense.\n\nIt names the value killers the defense-eye audit turned up, like social media, a minor-impact label or the venue, and counsel weighs each one.\n\nAnd it always carries the lien totals. When the math is wrong or a lien gets missed, you get a short-funded settlement: after everyone's paid back, the client takes home little or nothing. Imagine making that phone call.\n\n{name}, in your own words, what would you have to tell John Doe if that happened?\n\nThe point to keep: you bring findings; counsel makes the calls.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would a short-funded settlement mean for John Doe, in plain terms?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game: Ten-Second Boss. I'm Atty. Thorne, I've got a hearing in ten minutes, and I'll read exactly one line of your memo.\n\nHere are two first lines. One: “On 02/06/2026 John Doe was a front-seat passenger…” Two: “John Doe is at MMI with $16,401.40 in audited Specials against a $10,000 BI limit, and I need your decisions.”\n\n{name}, which one do I read before I walk out? Right, the second. And that's last block's mystery answered.\n\nHe makes the strategy calls from your brief, so its shape matters. A brief that opens with the story buries the decision. One that hides a risk misleads him. And one that makes the call for him crosses a line that isn't yours to cross.\n\nWe've all sat in a strategy meeting that could've been an email. Today you learn to write an email so good the meeting is fast.\n\nRemember it like a newspaper: headline first, story second.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those two opening lines could Atty. Thorne act on in the first ten seconds, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Lead with the bottom line in one sentence: MMI 06/12/2026, $16,401.40 in audited Specials against a $10,000 BI limit, decisions needed before any demand is drafted.\n\nNext the facts, audited and sourced: report RPD-26-018842, Smith cited for failure to stop, Ruth Calloway checked for drift, and the doorbell video.\n\nOn force, John's door was struck at about 28 mph, and the Civic went from $6,940.00 to $9,255.00 with a $2,315.00 A-pillar supplement. The A-pillar is the frame post beside the windshield, so a supplement there means real force.\n\nThen the risks, each paired with its answering document. There's one almost every new Case Manager leaves out. {name}, guess. It's the 2021 low-back visits, and Keystone will find them.\n\nThen decisions as questions, and options with consequences. Record his decisions the same day.\n\n“Specials exceed the limit” is a finding; “we should demand limits” is his call.\n\nBad news travels best when you deliver it first, with the answer stapled on.",
   "ask": "{name}, which risk would you be tempted to leave out of the brief, and what happens when Keystone finds it first?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So the audit's complete. You draft the one-page memo and the exhibit index, the numbered list of every attached document, in the Action Plan Editor. Five sections in order, every exhibit label matching its page.\n\nOnce it's ready, you book the strategy session on the CMS calendar and send the memo ahead through the Email Workspace. The session happens before any demand is drafted.\n\nAfter the session, you record each decision in counsel's own words in Case Notes and open its tasks, each one dated, attributed to Atty. Thorne, with an owner and a due date.\n\nSince those decisions change the plan, you mark the Case Plan superseded, meaning replaced, and re-route the new version the same day.\n\nWhy so strict? Because six months from now, if anyone asks why we demanded what we demanded, the answer is in the file, in his words, with a date.\n\n{name}, that protects you too. His decision, his words, your record.",
   "ask": "{name}, why re-route the Case Plan as a new version instead of just editing the old one?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll run the pre-demand strategy session with AI Atty. Elijah Thorne in the Call Simulator.\n\nBrief him in order. Start with the bottom line, MMI, $16,401.40 in Specials and the $10,000 BI limit. Then the audited facts, the risks with their answering documents, the decisions he has to make, and the operational consequences of each option.\n\nMeet every challenge with dated documents, not adjectives. “It was a serious crash” doesn't answer anything.\n\nRecommend only on operations, and get his decisions on the demand, the gap, the UM consents and billed versus paid. Read each decision back before you close.\n\nAfterwards, record them in Case Notes, open a task for each in Tasks, and re-route the Case Plan.\n\n{name}, you're in the chair first. {name}, {name}, you're observers, and you're writing down his decisions word for word.\n\nRemember the chain: these decisions drive the demand, and the demand drives the offer.",
   "ask": "{name}, as an observer, what did Atty. Thorne decide, in his own words?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 07/08/2026, and this is Atty. Thorne's note. He's read your audit, and the strategy session on John Doe is today at 2:00 p.m. in his office. Bottom line first, he says.\n\nExpect him to push the way Keystone will: on low speed, the 14 days and the “degenerative” read, which is the argument that John's neck was just wearing out with age.\n\nBring the facts, the documents and the operational options, and remember the decisions on the demand are his.\n\nYour audit's done: MMI 06/12/2026, Specials of $16,401.40, liens of $1,860.00 and $6,105.00, and targets of $10,000 BI, $20,000 Host UM and $10,000 Relative UM.\n\nAnd there's your answer to last activity's mystery: low speed, the gap, and degenerative.\n\nFair warning, he'll interrupt you inside the first minute. This is not the meeting that could've been an email. This is the meeting your email made possible. {name}, ready?",
   "ask": "{name}, if he says “Keystone will call this low-speed,” what objective fact do you answer with first?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Grade your decision record, not the conversation. If a decision isn't dated, attributed and tasked, the session didn't happen.\n\nDid you lead with the bottom line, figures exact? Did you meet the low-speed challenge with objective facts: Smith running the stop sign and getting cited, John's door struck at about 28 mph, and the $2,315.00 A-pillar supplement, the extra frame repair that proves force?\n\nAnd the C5-C6 herniation, a slipped disc in the neck, on the 03/13/2026 MRI? Did you meet the gap with documents rather than an apology, and raise the 2021 visits and the “degenerative” read unprompted?\n\nHis decisions should be recorded: the time-limited BI demand to Dana Whitfield, served 07/20/2026, the gap addressed up front, the UM consent plan, and billed versus paid.\n\nAnd when he asked what you think we should demand, did you hand the figure back? {name}, did you?\n\nYour mystery: a Time-Limited Demand has a clock. The insurer has to say yes inside a set window or the offer goes away, like a coupon that expires at midnight. {name}, what happens on the file the day it runs out?",
   "ask": "{name}, when AI Atty. Thorne asked what you'd demand, what exactly did you say back?"
  }
 },
 "d2m4-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Look at the gate bar. This module turns the last two chips green: Specials audited at $16,401.40, and strategy approved by Atty. Thorne. The demand doesn't go out until all six are green.\n\nQuick warm-up before you write: Price Is Right, audit edition. I'll name a number from the file, and you shout higher or lower than what it should be.\n\nRiverton Spine & Rehab, $6,290.00? {name}? Right, lower: $6,105.00. That's the answer to the last mystery.\n\nNow answer from this file, in your own words. Total John Doe's Specials from the bills, showing the Riverton correction, the medical total, the wage math and the final figure.\n\nSay who decides MMI, when John Doe's was, why the demand waits for it, and where the $3,400 to $6,800 in future medical goes.\n\nAnd name the four audit objectives, with one finding each on this file.\n\nExplain it like you're teaching it to the next new hire. If you can teach it, you've got it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which two chips on the gate bar does this module turn green, and what does each one prove?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary, in your own words. The scenario takes us back to 04/08/2026.\n\nRiverton Spine & Rehab reports two missed visits, Keystone ended Margaret's rental on 03/19, his carpool ended 04/01, and Dana Whitfield has asked him for a Recorded Statement. Tell me what you do first, second and third, and what each one protects.\n\nAnd while you write, picture this. You find an error in an exhibit the day before the 07/20/2026 service date.\n\nSending it anyway and fixing it later feels efficient. It isn't. Stop the package, fix every total that cites it, and tell Atty. Thorne before anything goes out.\n\nBecause once Keystone has the wrong number, it's their favorite number.\n\n{name}, have you ever hit send and then seen the typo? Then you already know this one: a late right answer beats an on-time wrong one.",
   "ask": "{name}, which number on this file would you defend line by line to Dana Whitfield, and where does every cent come from?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "And to close out the pre-demand audit, your Skill Scale. Rate each skill from one to five and submit it for review.\n\nI'll put your self-rating beside your score, and where they're far apart, we'll sit down and talk it through.\n\nBe especially honest on the billed-versus-paid question. If any of your answers picked a column for the demand, that loses the UPL marks, however good the rest of the work was.\n\nNow your mystery. The audited figure is $16,401.40, and the BI limit is $10,000. The demand is going to ask for one specific number.\n\n{name}, without telling me what it should be, because that's Atty. Thorne's call, tell me where on the file you'd look to find out what he decided.",
   "ask": "{name}, looking back over the pre-demand audit, which skill feels most solid to you now, and how would you prove it?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "So here's the tool: a Time-Limited Demand. It's a letter that asks for the full policy limit and gives the insurer a set number of days to say yes.\n\nThe road has five stops. Confirm the foundation, meaning the deadline to sue, who's at fault and how much coverage there is, each from its own document. Demand within limits, with clear terms. Give a reasonable window.\n\nThen send a package complete enough to judge, and email it to the adjuster, with the clock starting from her written acknowledgement that she got it. Atty. Thorne decides every term.\n\nNow four ideas. One, a demand isn't begging for money; it presents a debt owed. Two, a Time-Limited Demand forces a decision on the record.\n\nThree, the Final Scrub catches the adjuster's favorite traps before anything goes out. It's the last line-by-line clean-up: duplicates out, every balance confirmed with the provider, old injuries disclosed. Think of it as checking your teeth for spinach before the big meeting.\n\nAnd four, the reason most demands get cut: the Why Factor. Every bill carries the reason the treatment was needed. A bill without a why is just a receipt, and adjusters treat receipts like suggestions.\n\nFive stops, four ideas, and one rule over all of it: every bill carries its why.",
   "ask": "{name}, where on John Doe's file would a missing Why Factor hurt us most?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Three habits sit under every demand we send. Before I show you the first, {name}, a guess. Once the doctor says the client has healed as much as he's going to, how many days do we give ourselves to send the demand?\n\nThat moment is called MMI, Maximum Medical Improvement. The answer is thirty, and that's the 30-Day Rule.\n\nJohn Doe reached MMI on 06/12/2026, so the target was 07/12/2026. Ours went out on 07/20/2026, and that's fine, because any later date belongs to Atty. Thorne, with his reason recorded.\n\nHere's the difference that matters. A date that just slides, with no note, looks like a mistake. A late date with a reason on the record looks like a decision.\n\nSecond habit, photo impact. We lead with the most graphic image on file, the Property Damage or the injury, because it sets the tone before the adjuster reads a word.\n\nThird, the Why Factor again. Every bill in the demand carries why that treatment was necessary for his recovery.\n\nThirty days, the strongest photo first, and a why on every bill.",
   "ask": "{name}, the demand went out eight days after the target. What has to be in the file so nobody calls it a mistake?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Okay, quick game: guess the number. A Demand Package lands on an adjuster's desk. How many pages does she read before she's made up her mind? Shout it out.\n\n{name}, three hundred? Bless you. {name}, two? Closer. Honestly, the first page or two set the tone, and the rest is just checking.\n\nA firm I worked with once sent a demand so thick the courier asked if we wanted a dolly. The offer came back tiny, because nobody could find the good stuff.\n\nNow two numbers. John Doe's Specials, meaning his bills and lost wages, the damages with a hard number on them, come to $16,401.40. Robert Smith carries $10,000 with Keystone.\n\nSo what makes Keystone pay all of it, inside 30 days? There's a tool for that, and it has five parts. Miss any one and Keystone can say no and lose nothing.\n\nIf you remember one thing from this lesson, make it this: the first page decides the offer.\n\nI'm going to reveal them one at a time.",
   "ask": "{name}, before I reveal anything, what do you think makes an insurance company pay its whole limit, and fast?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you build a demand that asks for it, let's break down a document with a long name and a short job: the affidavit of no other insurance.\n\n{name}, guess who signs it. Us, John, or the other driver? It's Robert Smith. It's his sworn, notarized statement, sworn meaning under oath, that his Keystone policy is the only liability coverage for this collision. No umbrella, no business or employer's policy, nothing else.\n\nIts legal purpose is simple. Atty. Thorne makes it a condition of the demand, because settling at a $10,000 limit needs sworn proof that nothing else covers Smith.\n\nIts evidentiary role comes later. Smith swore it on 08/10/2026, Keystone sent it with its tender, its offer to pay the full limit, and both UM carriers reviewed it before they consented.\n\nYour part is the paperwork, never the meaning. You record it in the approved terms, check it's sworn and matches the file, file it with the tender and cite it on page one of each UM demand.\n\nA tired check stops at \"it's notarized.\" A great one says it's notarized, the names and policy match, and the UM carriers have seen it. Skip that, and a hidden umbrella policy shows up after John already signed.\n\nOne sworn page proves there's no hidden policy behind the $10,000.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would Harbor Point want to see Smith's affidavit before it consents to the settlement?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules, and where your role stops. Confirm the foundation first. The deadline to sue, the statute of limitations, is docketed for 02/06/2028.\n\nLiability comes from Smith's citation, Ruth Calloway and the doorbell video. Coverage comes from Keystone's $10,000 Dec Page, the policy's summary sheet that shows the limits. The demand is for exactly that $10,000.\n\nEvery term has to be acceptable as written: Smith and Keystone released, for the BI claim only, BI being the at-fault driver's injury coverage, plus Smith's affidavit, a sworn statement, of no other insurance.\n\nAsk only what Keystone can deliver in 30 days, with the Big Four complete. The clock starts at the adjuster's written acknowledgement of receipt, and we email the package to Dana Whitfield. If a statute requires a delivery method, Atty. Thorne confirms it.\n\nThe gap goes on page one, and causation leads with the 03/13/2026 MRI.\n\nBad faith is a legal conclusion, and it's Atty. Thorne's. It means an insurance company unreasonably refusing to pay a claim it should have paid, and whether that happened is never ours to say.\n\nYou build, audit, serve and prove delivery. You never set terms, grant extensions or value the claim. So why so strict? One vague word gives Keystone a free exit.\n\nYou build the demand. Atty. Thorne owns every word of its terms.",
   "ask": "{name}, we emailed Dana Whitfield on 07/20, and she confirmed receipt in writing on 07/22. When does the window close, and how would Keystone know?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Time for the reveal on that window, plus the five parts I promised. This is John Doe's demand with the five elements pinned to it.\n\nBadge one, the $10,000 amount: within limits. Two, the release and affidavit terms: clear. Three, the conditions and the 30 days to meet them: reasonable. Four, the list of everything enclosed: documented. Five, the deadline and delivery lines: timed.\n\nNow the clock bar on the right. Emailed 07/20/2026, receipt confirmed in writing 07/22/2026, and the window closes 08/21/2026. {name}, is that what you got?\n\nKeystone knows it because the letter says the clock runs from her written acknowledgement.\n\nSee the red warning up top? An unclear term is an escape hatch. Each badge is a door Keystone could walk out through.\n\nLeave one open and they'll walk out holding a lowball offer, like a guy offering to buy your car for the price of its floor mats. This demand shuts all five.\n\nFive badges, five shut doors, and a clock that starts at her written yes.",
   "ask": "{name}, if we'd left one of the five doors open, which do you think Keystone would try first?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So how does it run on the job? MMI is declared on 06/12/2026. You docket MMI plus 30, which means it goes on the calendar with reminders, and you open the Demand Package task in the CMS.\n\nThat lands on 07/12/2026, a Sunday, and since it's an internal target, we work it early, on Friday 07/10. Legal deadlines counted forward roll to the next business day instead, and a later service date gets recorded as Atty. Thorne's, with his reason.\n\nApproved terms go into Case Notes exactly, before you draft a word.\n\nWhen Dana Whitfield confirms receipt in writing on 07/22/2026, you docket 08/21/2026 from that acknowledgement, with reminders. No acknowledgement? Follow up with her that day and escalate to Atty. Thorne. Never docket from the send date.\n\nA Case Manager I trained once counted from the day she hit send. She landed two days early, and the other side had a field day with it.\n\nAnd if Keystone writes or calls before the deadline, log it word for word and route it to Atty. Thorne the same day. You change nothing on terms or time.\n\nDocket from her acknowledgement, never from your send button.",
   "ask": "{name}, two days pass and no acknowledgement comes back. What exactly do you do, and who hears about it?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two lists for the Time-Limited Demand, so let's go through them.\n\nOn the Do side, you docket the deadline from the adjuster's written acknowledgement, never the send date. You lead with the value-drivers, the MRI herniation, which is a disc pushed out of place, and the injection, and you remember the window is for Keystone to accept, not to pay.\n\nOn the Don't side: a demand with no stated deadline or clock trigger, a timeline that leaves the gap out, and demanding before MMI or a future-care plan.\n\nThe lazy demand says “please respond within thirty days” and counts from when you hit send. The high-value one names the deadline and its trigger, explains the gap with the records, and goes out after MMI on 06/12/2026.\n\nOh, and if I hear anybody call this a bad-faith letter, I'm stopping you right there. Bad faith is Atty. Thorne's conclusion.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen?\n\nNow your mystery for the next lesson. Even if Keystone pays every penny of its $10,000, there's a number on this file that says John Doe could walk away with nothing. {name}, find it before I show you.\n\nLeave Keystone no exit but yes.",
   "ask": "{name}, what makes a gap explanation hold up with an adjuster, and who supplies it?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Audit the draft against the Big Four and the Final Scrub in the Action Plan Editor, and log each deficiency as an assigned CMS task rather than fixing it quietly.\n\nAssemble the package with the gap answered on page one of the timeline. Once Atty. Thorne signs, email the demand to Dana Whitfield from the Email Workspace with the Demand Package attached, and log it.\n\nWhen her written acknowledgement of receipt comes back dated 07/22/2026, docket the acceptance deadline from it, with reminders.\n\nThen you'll take a live call from Dana Whitfield. Confirm receipt and the 08/21/2026 deadline as written, agree to no extension, log her requests in her exact words, and send nothing without approval.\n\n{name}, you're taking the first call. {name}, you're logging it.\n\nAnd if she says it's still under review on day two, don't panic. One adjuster I dealt with had a file under review so long it had a birthday.\n\nWhat you hand in is an audited, emailed demand, her written acknowledgement on file, and 08/21/2026 docketed.\n\nLog it, don't fix it quietly, and count from her acknowledgement.",
   "ask": "{name}, suppose Dana Whitfield emails on day 25 asking for two more weeks. Who answers?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Let's rewind the calendar. It's Monday, 07/20/2026, and an email just landed from Atty. Thorne. Before I read it, {name}, guess what he wants done today.\n\nHere's the reveal: the Keystone BI demand gets a final audit and goes out today.\n\nThe terms are approved for Dana Whitfield at Keystone Mutual, claim KM-26-440871. That's the $10,000 limits, a Limited Release of Robert Smith and Keystone only, Smith's affidavit of no other insurance, and 30 days from receipt.\n\nMMI was 06/12/2026, Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter and the Gap Explanation Record are on file, and the pre-demand audit put the Specials at $16,401.40.\n\nI'm handing you the demand specialist's draft. Fair warning, it has problems. Audit it against the Big Four and the Final Scrub, log every deficiency as a task, and bring it back for his signature.\n\nNothing goes out until the audit is logged. You're the last set of eyes before an adjuster goes looking for a reason to pay less.\n\nAudit first, log it, then it goes out. In that order.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you even open the draft, what are the first three things you'll check?"
  },
  "grade": {
   "say": "Before you touch the draft, look at what an A looks like, because this is the standard the platform audits you against.\n\nFive tests decide a Time-Limited Demand: within limits, clear, reasonable, documented and timed. The terms are Atty. Thorne's, word for word. The gap is answered on page one, before Keystone gets to raise it.\n\nInside the package, you've got the cover letter at the $10,000 limit, the liability proof, every itemized bill with its reason, the gap proof, Northgate's wage letter, and the narrative.\n\nBuild your own version from the case documents you download, in your training Drive, then upload the served set to the CMS.\n\nHonestly, a package where everything's there is just a pass. An A is a package where an adjuster can't find a single loose thread to pull.\n\nAn A leaves the adjuster nothing to pull on.\n\n{name}, you're going to tell me which test you'd fail first if you rushed.",
   "ask": "{name}, which one of the five tests, if missed, hands Keystone the safest refusal?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "Here's the enclosure set as the demand specialist built it, and here's your job, {name}: call every item. Keep it, replace it, pull it, add what's missing, or flag it for Atty. Thorne.\n\nSome of these are small, and some of them cost John Doe real money.\n\nExhibit E carries Riverton Spine and Rehab at six thousand two hundred and ninety dollars with the fourth of May billed twice.\n\nAnd Crescent's statement bills him fifteen hundred and forty dollars that an in-network provider can't bill him at all. In-network means the provider agreed to the insurer's prices, so it can't charge the client the difference.\n\nTwo things are missing that the letter leans on.\n\nHere's the thing about a duplicate bill: it's an adjuster's favorite gift. Find one, and she'll politely assume everything else is padded too.\n\nWhen you hand it in, you'll see the package as Atty. Thorne actually signed and served it on the twentieth of July, and you can mark your own audit against it.\n\nOne duplicate on a bill makes every other line look padded.\n\nThen the next mystery. The BI round ends, and two more carriers are waiting.",
   "ask": "{name}, which item in this set would you least want Dana Whitfield to find first?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log anything, check yourself, and yes, I'm going to pick on a few of you.\n\n{name}, is the amount $10,000, Keystone's BI limit per its 03/20/2026 Dec Page? Do the terms match Atty. Thorne's approval word for word? Are the Big Four complete, with Specials of $16,401.40 and the Riverton Drug receipts counted once?\n\nDid your Final Scrub catch the 05/04/2026 duplicate, taking Riverton Spine & Rehab from $6,290.00 to $6,105.00?\n\nIs the gap answered on page one, and are the 2021 low-back visits disclosed as resolved, with no neck history? Disclosed and distinguished, never hidden.\n\nHiding them is the shortcut that always backfires. The adjuster finds them anyway, and then she trusts nothing else in the package.\n\nEvery deficiency should be a dated, assigned task. The demand should be emailed 07/20 and logged, and 08/21/2026 docketed from Dana's 07/22 written acknowledgement.\n\nIf you counted from the send date, you had the right idea and the wrong day. And any extension request goes to Atty. Thorne the same day.\n\nEvery deficiency gets a task, a date and an owner.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you docketed from the send date, what day did you land on, and why is it wrong?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Think of UM as the client's financial safety net. Once the at-fault policy is used up, his own coverage makes up the difference, up to its limits. Four ideas hold it together.\n\nFirst, the net check. John Doe doesn't care about the gross, only what's left after fees and unreduced liens.\n\nSecond, BI exhaustion. Exhaustion just means the at-fault carrier paid every dollar of its limit, and that's the trigger that opens the UM claim as phase two.\n\nThird, consent and subrogation waiver. Subrogation is the UM carrier's right to go after the at-fault driver to get its money back. So before we release Smith, the UM carrier consents and gives up that right, or substitutes its own check.\n\nSkip that, and the UM claim may not survive the BI release.\n\nFourth, front-load the proof. The at-fault Dec Page, the signed release and the consents go up front, which strips away the technicality defense on page one.\n\nBury them in an appendix and you're inviting the argument. Lead with them and there's nothing to argue about.\n\nNet first, exhaustion next, consent before release, proof up front.",
   "ask": "{name}, have you seen a consent-to-settle request on a file before, and what happened when it was missing?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Would-you-rather, hands up. Would you rather get a check for $10,000, or a check for $6,000 with every bill already paid? {name}, which one? Most people grab the ten.\n\nHere's the payoff on our mystery. Take Keystone's $10,000, pull out the fee and costs, and you're at $6,179.92. His liens, the bills that get paid back out of the settlement, are $7,965.00 before anyone reduces them.\n\nDo the subtraction, {name}. Yep. He walks away with nothing.\n\nThat's why the UM layers matter so much. UM is his own insurance stepping in when the at-fault driver doesn't carry enough, and it changes his net.\n\nNow the uncomfortable part: a technicality can lose them. A release signed too early. A UM demand sent before BI is used up. A consent nobody got.\n\nA consent to settle is the UM carriers' written okay before we settle with the at-fault driver's insurer. Skip it, and the client can lose the UM money stacked behind it.\n\nI once watched a UM claim die because a release got signed on a Friday afternoon so everybody could get it off their desk. This lesson is about locking those escape hatches before Host UM and Relative UM open.\n\nGross is a headline. Net is what John actually lives on.",
   "ask": "{name}, if BI on its own leaves him nothing, what are the UM layers really worth to John Doe?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you prepare one, let's break down the UM Consent to Settle. There's a trap in here, and I'll reveal it in a second.\n\nIt's a UM carrier's written answer to our 30-Day Letter. It consents to the BI settlement, waives subrogation against Robert Smith, which means it gives up its right to chase Smith for what it pays, and says it won't substitute its own check.\n\nIts legal purpose is to protect that subrogation right. And here's the trap, {name}: settle and release Smith without it, and John Doe can lose the UM coverage.\n\nOn our file, it's the green light. Harbor Point consented on 08/24 and Cardinal State on 08/26/2026, each to a release of Smith and Keystone only, with both UM claims left open.\n\nYour part is the process. You prepare both requests for Atty. Thorne's signature, docket each window, file each consent, and nothing goes to John Doe to sign until both are in.\n\nA Case Manager who's cutting corners gets one consent and figures the other's coming. A good one waits for both, in writing, because one missing letter can cost a client tens of thousands of dollars.\n\nTwo consents in writing, then the release. Never the other way around.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Cardinal State still hadn't answered on 08/28, what would happen to the release?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules, and here's where you stop. BI exhaustion comes first. Host UM opens once Keystone's $10,000 tender of 08/12/2026 is documented. A tender is the carrier formally offering its full limit.\n\nProve Keystone is all there is with its Dec Page and Smith's 08/10/2026 affidavit of no other insurance.\n\nThe day after the tender, 08/13, the 30-Day Letters go to Harbor Point and Cardinal State asking each to consent and waive subrogation, or substitute its own check.\n\nNothing goes to John Doe for signature until both consents are in writing, 08/24 and 08/26, and Atty. Thorne approves the release. Limit that release to Robert Smith, Keystone and the BI claim.\n\nHarbor Point confirmed add-on in writing on 03/02/2026; get the same in writing from Cardinal State.\n\nYou prepare, track, catch and draft. You never interpret a policy or release for John Doe, or tell him to sign. Get the order wrong and $30,000 of safety net can vanish with one signature.\n\nTender, consents, then release, and in exactly that order.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone's draft release is in your inbox and Dana Whitfield wants it back today. What's wrong with it?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Now let's see how that runs at your desk. Keystone tenders the $10,000 on 08/12/2026. That's your trigger to prepare the 30-Day Letters, the consent-to-settle requests, to both UM carriers for Atty. Thorne's signature.\n\nYou use the template in the Action Plan Editor and docket each window. Both go out 08/13 with the tender, the Dec Page and the affidavit.\n\nOn 08/14, Keystone's draft release arrives. The easy move is forwarding it to the client with “please sign and return.” The right move is comparing who and what it releases against the approved terms and flagging the overbreadth.\n\nThat “all other insurers” language goes to Atty. Thorne as an escalated task, and nothing goes to John Doe to sign.\n\nI once saw a release with “standard language, no changes needed” highlighted in yellow at the top. Trust me, nothing in a release is standard.\n\nOnce both consents are in writing and the Limited Release is signed on 08/28, you open two tracked CMS tasks for the UM demands, each with its carrier, claim number and adjuster.\n\nEvery release gets compared to the approved terms before anyone signs.",
   "ask": "{name}, a release says “standard language” at the top. What do you check before you believe that?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-pkg": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "A Demand Package is proof, sorted by what each piece proves. Each part answers one question the adjuster asks, and a missing part is her discount.\n\nFirst, the Big Four: Medical Specials, the Narrative, Liability Proof and Special Damages, all flawless before Atty. Thorne reviews. That's the bills, the story, the proof of fault, and the other hard losses like wages.\n\nSecond, the Human Story: how the injury changed John Doe's work and daily life, told only from his words and the records.\n\nThird, the weak file against the legal trap. Three hundred pages of raw charts and a letter that says our client hurts get a lowball. A two-page summary and a strict clock make refusing the limits risky.\n\nFourth, documents by function: the UM package sorted into coverage, liability, special damages, general damages and the release.\n\nA template demand is like a birthday card signed “Dear Valued Customer.” Nobody feels a thing. {name}, which of the four feels most like a judgment call to you, and why?\n\nOne line to keep: every page proves one thing, or it doesn't belong.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the Big Four do you think Keystone found weakest when it offered $3,500, and what would have fixed it?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Two truths and a lie. One: adjusters love a 300-page stack of raw medical charts. Two: a missing document is a discount for the carrier. Three: we build the same package twice on John Doe's file.\n\n{name}, which one's the lie? Number one. Adjusters don't read 300 pages; they weigh them, sigh, and offer something small.\n\nNow the answer to our mystery. On 08/05/2026 Keystone offered $3,500 on a claim with $16,401.40 in Specials.\n\nThe two reasons: the 14-day gap and a “degenerative” MRI, meaning they're calling it wear and tear from age, not the crash. That's what an adjuster does with any opening a package leaves.\n\nSo today isn't about the demand letter; it's about everything behind it. Each part proves one thing: the bills, the story, the fault, the wages, and for the UM carriers, the coverage and the exhaustion.\n\nThink of it like a puzzle box. Every missing piece is a hole the adjuster puts her discount through.\n\nWe build it for the BI demand served 07/20/2026, and again for the UM demands that go out 08/31/2026.\n\nRemember this: a missing part is a discount.",
   "ask": "{name}, if you were Dana Whitfield, which part of a Demand Package would you open first, looking for a reason to pay less, and why?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you build the package behind it, let's break down the letter on top: the Time-Limited Demand. {name}, guess first. Does a 30-day clock on a demand start the day we hit send, or some other day? Hold that.\n\nIt's the firm's letter offering to settle John Doe's BI claim for Keystone's $10,000 per-person limit, open for a fixed time.\n\nIts legal purpose is to make Keystone decide on the record, on terms it can meet, and Atty. Thorne sets every one: payment of the $10,000, a Limited Release of Robert Smith and Keystone only, and Smith's affidavit of no other insurance as a condition.\n\nOn our file, it was emailed to Dana Whitfield on 07/20/2026 with 81 pages, Exhibits A through J, and it proves exactly what Keystone had in hand. The thumbnail is the final as served; the faulty draft is yours to audit later.\n\nNow the reveal. The 30 days run from her written acknowledgement of receipt, her reply saying she got it, on 07/22/2026, so the window closes 08/21/2026.\n\nNot from the send date, and no certified mail. The sent email and her reply are the proof. You log the email, get the acknowledgement in writing, docket 08/21/2026 from it, and change no term.\n\nThe clock starts when she says she got it.",
   "ask": "{name}, Dana's acknowledgement never arrives. What do you do that same day, and which date do you never docket from?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how you build it. Medical Specials come from line-item bills, meaning every visit and charge listed on its own line, reconciled to the audit: $14,817.40, with the 05/04/2026 duplicate gone, and every line carries its why. A two-page treatment summary goes in front of the records.\n\nThe Human Story comes from John Doe's own words and the records: five shifts missed at Northgate, numbness into his right hand from 02/18, and that numbness and tingling, paresthesia, still there at MMI.\n\nYou draft it, Atty. Thorne reviews it, and nobody dresses it up.\n\nLiability Proof is the Police Report, Ruth Calloway, the doorbell clip and the Property Damage photos. Special Damages is Northgate's $1,584.00 letter, for $16,401.40 in all.\n\nThen the Final Scrub, our last clean-up pass before anything leaves the building: no overlaps, the 2021 low-back visits disclosed, every balance confirmed by phone.\n\n“All documents attached” is a pass. Every number tied out to the penny is the job. For UM, rebuild by function, with exhaustion proof, not proof of uninsured status.\n\nIf it doesn't tie out to the penny, it isn't done.",
   "ask": "{name}, why is proof of uninsured status the wrong threshold document for John Doe's UM packages?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it runs like this. When MMI is declared on 06/12/2026, you open the Demand Package task, request every provider's line-item bill, and ask John Doe how the injury changed his days, in his own words.\n\nIn the week of service you run the Final Scrub: overlaps, the prior disclosed, and a call to every provider for its final balance, with a name and a date in the notes, until $16,401.40 ties out. Then you index every exhibit by what it proves and route the package and the narrative to Atty. Thorne.\n\nOnce the Limited Release is signed on 08/28/2026, you rebuild each UM package by function. The question of an impairment rating goes to Atty. Thorne, not to Dr. Reyes from you.\n\nAn impairment rating is the doctor's number, usually a percentage, for how much permanent loss of function the client is left with, and only a doctor gives one.\n\nA rule of thumb from the field: if your cover letter needs its own table of contents, start over.\n\nKeep this: build it once, right, and rebuild it by function for UM.\n\nNow your mystery for the negotiation lesson. Dana Whitfield is going to ask one question on the phone, six words long. {name}, guess it.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would a provider's final balance ever be different from the bill we already have?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Good news: you're not starting from a blank page. The audit closed on 07/15, and Atty. Thorne assigned the UM demands to the Demand Desk; their drafts are in the file. Your job is QC, quality control.\n\nHost UM goes to Marcus Bell at Harbor Point, HP-26-7741, for the $20,000 limit. Relative UM goes to Teresa Okafor at Cardinal State, CS-26-20355, for $10,000 as excess over Host UM.\n\nExhaustion is proven by the tender, the consents and the signed Limited Release, with the check copy to follow, and it belongs on page one of each.\n\nCheck every figure against the audit, answer the gap and “degenerative” with the MRI, and hand in two QC'd drafts routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, you take Harbor Point. {name}, you take Cardinal State.\n\nThen swap and check each other's, because fresh eyes catch what tired eyes skip.\n\nEvery figure ties to its source, and every legal term stays with Atty. Thorne.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does Cardinal State's demand say excess?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Look at the date first. It's Monday, 08/31/2026, and something we've been waiting for still isn't here. {name}, guess what. Yep, Keystone's check.\n\nHere's what landed from Atty. Thorne. BI is done. Keystone tendered $10,000 on 08/12 after Smith's affidavit of 08/10.\n\nHarbor Point consented on 08/24, Cardinal State on 08/26, and the Limited Release of Robert Smith and Keystone was signed 08/28.\n\nHe wants the Host UM demand to Harbor Point for $20,000 and the Relative UM demand to Cardinal State for $10,000, as excess. Exhaustion goes on page one of each, and the response deadline is left blank for him.\n\nAnd remember, on 08/05 Keystone leaned on the 14-day gap and called the MRI degenerative. Expect the UM carriers to try the very same thing.\n\nAdjusters share arguments the way teenagers share homework.\n\nExhaustion goes on page one, because that's where they'll look first.",
   "ask": "{name}, with no check in hand today, what proves exhaustion?"
  },
  "grade": {
   "say": "So what does good look like for the two UM packages? They're two separate packages for two carriers, never the BI demand with a new address slapped on it.\n\nI once saw a UM demand go out with the BI adjuster's name still in the greeting. The UM adjuster pinned it to her wall, and not as a compliment.\n\nEach one opens with the proof of exhaustion: Keystone's tender, its Declaration Page, Smith's affidavit, both consents and the signed Limited Release. Then coverage, liability and damages.\n\nCardinal State's demand is framed as excess, for damages above thirty thousand. A two-page treatment summary beats a stack of records. The response deadline stays blank, because Atty. Thorne sets it.\n\nBuild both drafts from the documents in your training Drive, tie every figure to its source, and upload them to the CMS with a follow-up task on each.\n\nTwo carriers, two complete packages, two correct greetings.\n\n{name}, you're the one checking the greeting lines.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does exhaustion go on page one rather than in an appendix?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Quick term first. An impairment rating is the doctor's percentage for how much permanent loss of function is left, and only a doctor gives one.\n\nNow check your two packages before you route them. {name}, are they truly two, each complete on its own? Cardinal State gets the whole file, not a cross-reference to Harbor Point's. Is Relative UM framed as excess, claimed for damages above $30,000?\n\nIs exhaustion on page one of each? That's the 08/12 tender, Keystone's 03/20 Dec Page, Smith's 08/10 affidavit, the consents, the 08/28 release, and a task for the check copy. Are the 03/02 and 03/09 coverage letters in?\n\nDoes your gap section answer “degenerative” with the radiologist's own words, “no significant degenerative change at the adjacent levels”? If it doesn't, you haven't learned from the BI round.\n\nIs there a two-page treatment summary instead of a record dump? And is the response deadline a marked blank for Atty. Thorne, with nothing served?\n\nEach package has to stand on its own, as if the other never existed.\n\nNow your mystery. We keep saying Keystone leaned on the gap on 08/05. In the next practice you'll hear exactly how she said it, live, on the phone.",
   "ask": "{name}, does Dr. Reyes's MMI note carry an impairment rating, and if not, what do you ask Atty. Thorne?"
  }
 },
 "d3-drive": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job: a backup audit before the demand goes out. Open My Backup Drive and compare the count of documents backed up with what's filed in the CMS. Any gap, back it up from Work From.\n\nThen the corrected documents. The current version goes in its folder, and the version it replaces stays right beside it, renamed SUPERSEDED with the date of the version that replaced it. Nothing gets deleted.\n\nThe MRI addendum goes in Medical Records, Crescent's corrected statement in Bills, the final wage record in Wages and Costs.\n\nThen file the draft demand and the exhibit packet so they land in Insurance, Demands and Negotiation, labeled DRAFT. Run Check my drive, and record the audit in Case Notes.\n\nEyeballing folders feels quick and misses things. Working down the CMS list, one document at a time, catches them.\n\nOld versions stay, labeled and dated. Nothing gets deleted.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why keep the superseded version at all, instead of deleting it?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 07/17/2026, three in the afternoon. The pre-demand audit closed on Wednesday, and the demand to Keystone goes out Monday.\n\nIn the last two weeks three documents were corrected. The MRI got an addendum fixing the history, Crescent sent a corrected statement down to zero, and the wage record went from a draft worksheet to a final.\n\nThe specialist's draft demand and exhibit packet are on the file for audit.\n\nHere's the question nobody asks until it's too late. If the CMS went down on Monday morning, could you put your hand on every page of that demand from the backup? {name}, what's your guess for how many firms could?\n\nA backup is like a spare tire. Nobody thinks about it until the highway, and then it's the only thing that matters.\n\nIf it isn't backed up, it doesn't exist on Monday.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Keystone says it never saw the corrected Crescent statement, where do we prove which version was current?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you move on, check yourself. {name}, what's your X of Y? They should be the same number.\n\nAre the three current versions in Medical Records, Bills and Wages and Costs? Is every old version still there, labeled SUPERSEDED with a date?\n\nAre the draft demand and packet in Insurance, Demands and Negotiation, marked DRAFT? Did Check my drive come back clean?\n\nAnd does your Case Note give the count, what you replaced and what you labeled, in plain words Atty. Thorne can read in ten seconds?\n\nThat's the standard before any package leaves this firm. X of Y, same number, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, a label that says SUPERSEDED but no date. What's wrong with it?"
  }
 },
 "d3m1-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Learning Check, game-show edition! The questions are on the screen, and your answers come from this file, in your own words, typed right under each question.\n\nHere's the twist. When you're done, I'll pull names, and you defend one answer out loud as if Dana Whitfield were on the line. {name}, you might be first.\n\nState the BI demand's terms, how it was served and the date its window closed, and show me how you counted. Name the five elements, with this file's example of each, and tell me who decides bad faith.\n\nThen answer Keystone's degenerative and gap arguments with the documents, and say where each goes in the package.\n\nWhen you reach the pressure questions, I want the process, not the law. Use the file's own dates and figures, not general rules.\n\nNo phoning a friend, and definitely no phoning an adjuster.\n\nExplain the process with the file's own facts, and you've got it.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you start, what does a UM carrier's consent actually protect?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the Self-Summary. In your own words, write what you learned serving John Doe's demands, the principles that make a demand hold, not the clicks.\n\nThen your strategy. With both UM drafts on Atty. Thorne's desk on 08/31/2026, what's your next action, and why that one? And name the one thing you're least confident about.\n\nBefore anyone writes, look at the scenario on screen. Keystone's draft release arrives 08/14/2026 releasing all other insurers, Harbor Point hasn't answered the consent request, and Atty. Thorne is out until Monday.\n\nI've lived this one. A Case Manager I worked with got that exact kind of release on a Friday, with the adjuster calling every hour saying they just needed it back today.\n\nShe held it, flagged it, and that patience kept the client's UM claim alive.\n\nUnder pressure, the answer is always hold, flag, escalate.\n\n{name}, talk us through what you'd do first.",
   "ask": "{name}, with Atty. Thorne out until Monday, what must not happen to Keystone's release before he's back?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step for this module, the Skill Scale. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each competency, from docketing a Time-Limited Demand from receipt all the way to holding the UPL boundary on terms, extensions and releases.\n\nUPL is the unauthorized practice of law, giving legal advice when you're not a lawyer.\n\nBe honest with yourself. A self-rating isn't a dating profile; nobody's impressed by all fives.\n\nI'm going to compare your rating with your score. A 5 on a block scored at 60 is a conversation, just like a 2 on a block you aced.\n\nAnd remember, UPL is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive an ethics breach. When you're done, submit it for review.\n\nAn honest rating today is worth more than a pretty one.\n\n{name}, a teaser for the negotiation check coming up. There's one number on this file that comes out the same every time you do the math on a lowball. Find it.",
   "ask": "{name}, where's the widest gap between how you rated yourself and how you actually scored?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "In personal injury, the demand letter is your opening move, and adjusters protect the carrier by valuing claims low. So negotiation is prepared, never made up on the spot. Four ideas carry it.\n\nThe pitch. A demand that reads like a template gets treated like one, while a tailored presentation earns a real evaluation.\n\nValue drivers. Those are the few facts that would scare a jury, because they create the carrier's risk, so every call leads with them.\n\nAnticipate the response. Name your top two weak spots before the adjuster does, and have the documents in hand.\n\nAnd the professional pause. After a low number, stay quiet. Silence shifts the burden back, and the adjuster has to justify the figure with data.\n\nIt's the longest four seconds of your career. Count them in your head; I once heard a trainee whisper “one Mississippi” into the phone.\n\nAnd remember, this isn't a used-car lot. Nobody's going to go talk to their manager and come back with free undercoating.\n\nPrepare the pitch, lead with the drivers, know your weak spots, and hold the pause.",
   "ask": "{name}, honestly, how long do you think you could hold a silence on a call before you cracked?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: finish the sentence. I start something an adjuster always says, you finish it.\n\n“We're still…” {name}? Reviewing, yes. “I'll have to check with…” My supervisor, of course. I've never met the supervisor, but I hear he's extremely busy.\n\nNow the payoff from the last lesson. Here's the six-word question Dana Whitfield is going to ask, not the one we'd like her to ask: “Why did he wait two weeks?”\n\nAnswer me from documents only. No adjectives.\n\nHere's the reality of this file. Keystone won't argue the crash. Robert Smith was cited, and the doorbell camera caught him.\n\nSo it'll argue the 14-Day Gap, it'll call the MRI degenerative, and it'll stall toward 08/21/2026.\n\nA Case Manager who has mapped both attacks, and the documents that answer them, before the first call turns every stall into a record. That's what today is about.\n\nAnswer every attack with a document, never an adjective.",
   "ask": "{name}, “Why did he wait two weeks?” Which documents answer her, with no adjectives?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules, and where your authority ends. The net floor is liens plus costs plus fee. $7,965.00 plus $486.75 is $8,451.75, and grossing that up for the one-third fee gives $12,677.63.\n\nGrossing up just means working out how big the settlement must be so that much is left after the fee. That's above Keystone's whole $10,000 limit, so every layer matters.\n\nLead with the value drivers: Smith on video with an independent witness, the C5-C6 herniation and injection, residual right-arm paresthesia, the numbness and tingling, at MMI. Anchor on $16,401.40 in total Specials, never PIP's $5,000.\n\nOn the call, soft lead, restate the demand and its 08/21/2026 deadline, then pause and ask for her data. Name every stall.\n\nKeystone's $3,500 is a counteroffer, logged in her exact words, and whether it ended the demand is Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nYou present, ask, restate and relay. You never counter, move the number or the deadline, or tell John Doe what an offer's worth.\n\nYou hold the line. Atty. Thorne moves it.",
   "ask": "{name}, if this file settled for Keystone's full $10,000 and nothing else, what would John Doe net?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So here's how it plays out on the job. Keystone receives the demand on 07/22/2026, and that's your trigger to build the battle map and compute the net floor on the Net Sheet.\n\nThat's $12,677.63 gross on unreduced liens, with every attack matched to a dated document in Case Notes.\n\nWhen the adjuster calls with an offer, you soft-lead, pause, ask for her data and restate the approved demand. You'll practice exactly that in the Call Simulator against Dana Whitfield. No counter, terms and deadline unchanged.\n\nWhen the call ends, log the offer and her exact words in the Communications Log and report to Atty. Thorne that day.\n\nAnd if she stalls with “waiting on authority,” you name it and send a same-day email citing 08/21/2026. No open-ended callbacks.\n\nI once watched an adjuster crawl from $3,500 to $3,550 to $3,600 over three weeks. It was like watching a glacier haggle.\n\nWhat finally moved things wasn't patience. It was a dated email that put the deadline in writing and went straight to the attorney.\n\nEvery stall gets a name, a log entry and a dated email.",
   "ask": "{name}, she says “I'll get back to you.” What's your next sentence, and what goes out by email today?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll run this call with Dana Whitfield in the Call Simulator, log it in the Communications Log and draft the follow-up in the Email Workspace.\n\nBefore you dial, do the math check on $3,500: fee, costs, unreduced liens and John Doe's net.\n\nOn the call, pause after her number and ask what data supports it. Name each stall. On the gap, point only to the record: the EMS report, the Gap Explanation Record and Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter. Answer degenerative with Dr. Park's MRI language.\n\nRestate the $10,000 Limits Demand and the 08/21/2026 deadline, with no counter.\n\nThen log her exact words, report the offer to Atty. Thorne today for him to convey to John Doe, and draft the 08/06/2026 rebuttal for his approval. That's what you hand in.\n\nPause, ask for her data, read the record, restate the demand.\n\n{name}, you're on the phone first. {name}, you're timing the pause. Under three seconds and we go again.",
   "ask": "{name}, which sentence will you read to her word for word?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Here's what just landed, 08/05/2026, from Dana Whitfield on KM-26-440871. {name}, guess the number before I read it. Above five thousand? Below?\n\nKeystone offers $3,500.00 to resolve the bodily injury claim. On $16,401.40 in Specials. That's not an offer, that's a coupon.\n\nHer reasons: John Doe waited two weeks before seeking any treatment, and the MRI findings are degenerative in nature. She's calling this afternoon.\n\nKeep the facts in front of you. Keystone received the $10,000 Time-Limited Demand on 07/22, and acceptance runs to 08/21/2026.\n\nAtty. Thorne's written authority says restate the Limits Demand and the deadline, present the record, no counter, and report any offer to him the same day.\n\nA lowball is a starting point for the record, not a reason to panic.\n\nNow imagine she opens like this: “He waited two weeks. That's not a herniation, that's a 34-year-old's spine.” Okay, who wants to answer her?",
   "ask": "{name}, she just called it a 34-year-old's spine, not a herniation. What's your first sentence back?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log the call, check yourself. {name}, did you run the math before dialing?\n\n$3,500 minus the $1,166.67 fee and $486.75 in costs leaves $1,846.58 against $7,965.00 of liens, so John Doe nets $0.00. If you dialed without knowing that, you were negotiating blind.\n\nDid you pause and ask what data supports $3,500? Did you answer the gap with dated documents, and degenerative with Dr. Park's own sentence? Did you restate $10,000 and 08/21/2026 with no new figure?\n\nIf you said “let me see what I can do,” or named a number, that's a fail. The authority was written down so nobody improvises it.\n\nIs the offer logged in her exact words, reported on 08/05, with the rebuttal drafted for 08/06?\n\nKnow the net before you dial, and never name a number of your own.\n\nNow your mystery for the next practice. Weeks from now, one of John Doe's own carriers makes the same gap argument, plus a brand-new one. Guess the new one.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Dana Whitfield raises the April break, which record answers the 19-day disruption?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-bins": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll build Stream 1 in the LSH Net Sheet. {name}, you're on the keyboard; {name}, you're the calculator that checks the keyboard.\n\nEnter the $10,000.00 gross as tendered, not received. Take the fee at the pre-suit 33⅓% from the retainer, that's $3,333.33, and the five receipted costs, $486.75. Enter both liens as they stand today and leave the reduction column blank.\n\nThen run Atty. Thorne's 25% allocation, his split of costs and liens between the two streams: $121.69 of costs and $1,991.25 of liens, down to a projected net of $4,553.73. Label it a projection, with what it assumes.\n\nBeside it, run the BI-alone fact pattern: $6,179.92 left against $7,965.00, shared pro rata, meaning each lien takes its fair percentage, like splitting a pizza by how hungry everyone is. John is at $0.00.\n\nRoute it to Atty. Thorne today with the trust-hold flag. The allocation and anything about value are his.\n\nAnd if your calculator and your spreadsheet disagree by a penny, don't pick the one you like better. Find out why. That penny is where the real mistake lives.\n\nA projection is labeled, or it's a promise you can't keep.",
   "ask": "{name}, which figure on your sheet would you never read to John Doe, and why?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Pop quiz, hands up. The insurance company says \"we're tendering our limits.\" Who thinks that means the money is in our bank account? {name}, your hand's up proudly. Hold that thought.\n\nIt's 08/12/2026, a Wednesday, and Dana Whitfield has just tendered Keystone's $10,000 BI limits, inside the 08/21 window. Tendered means offered, the whole policy, on the table. It does not mean received.\n\nThink of it like a friend saying \"the check's in the mail.\" Lovely news. Still not in your wallet.\n\nGood news, and John Doe will hear about it soon. But no check has issued, no release is signed, and the UM layers haven't even been demanded yet.\n\nAtty. Thorne's memo asks for one thing before he sits down with John: the BI settlement Net Sheet, the page that shows where every dollar goes. Stream 1 on his 25% working allocation, and Stream 1 standing alone.\n\nThe liens, the bills that get paid out of the settlement, are where they were on 08/05: Peach State at $1,860.00 and Riverton Spine & Rehab at $6,105.00 audited, nothing reduced.\n\nSo picture John on the phone tomorrow saying Keystone paid ten thousand, where's my share. The answer is going to surprise him. It might surprise you too.\n\nTendered is a promise. Received is a check.",
   "ask": "{name}, if John Doe asks why he can't have his share of Keystone's $10,000 now, what does the math say?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check yourself before you route it, and I'm calling names. {name}, is the fee $3,333.33, and not $3,333.00 from typing 33.33%? Calculators are honest, but they're very literal.\n\nAre the liens $1,860.00 and $6,105.00, with no reduction pencilled in, because nobody has agreed one? Is Stream 2 open, with nothing entered? Is $4,553.73 marked as a projection, with the allocation and the open UM layers written beside it?\n\nDoes the BI-alone pattern show $6,179.92 against $7,965.00, a $1,785.08 shortfall, Peach State $1,443.15 and Riverton Spine & Rehab $4,736.77, and John Doe at $0.00?\n\nAnd did you flag, as a fact, that the money can't be paid out on its own and stays in trust until the liens resolve, and leave the rest to Atty. Thorne?\n\nA sheet full of numbers isn't enough. A good one tells Atty. Thorne which numbers are real and which are still hopes.\n\nYour mystery for the next activity: a $0.00 net today isn't the end of John's story. {name}, predict it: what would have to happen for that zero to grow?",
   "ask": "{name}, you posted Keystone's $10,000 to the Trust Ledger today. What's wrong with that entry?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-umw": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Let me give you the four ideas behind this.\n\nOne, subrogation is the paying carrier's right to recover what it paid from the at-fault driver.\n\nTwo, the consent to settle is the UM carrier's written permission for John to settle with Smith and release him.\n\nThree, each carrier then has a choice: waive subrogation, or pay the $10,000 itself to keep its claim against Smith. On our file both waived and neither substituted, Harbor Point on 08/24 and Cardinal State on 08/26.\n\nAnd four, Keystone's own payment condition. Dana Whitfield's 08/14 cover letter holds the check until both waivers arrive with the executed release.\n\n{name}, predict for me: what would have happened to the timeline if Harbor Point had chosen to substitute its own check?\n\nSo, four ideas, one chain. Every link has to hold before the money moves."
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Here's the problem with Keystone's $10,000. The moment Harbor Point or Cardinal State pays John Doe, it steps into his shoes against Robert Smith. That right is called subrogation.\n\nPicture it like a relay race. The UM carrier pays John, then takes the baton and runs after Smith to get its money back.\n\nIf John releases Smith before those carriers agree, there's nobody left to run after. Their right against Smith is gone, and the policy may let them refuse to pay. That's $30,000 of a $40,000 stack riding on two letters.\n\nSo Keystone won't cut its check, and John won't sign anything, until both UM carriers consent in writing and waive subrogation.\n\nBluewater, by the way, is not on that list. Margaret rejected UM on the Civic back on 03/14/2024, so there's nothing there to waive. {name}, in your own words, why does that matter?\n\nNo consent, no release. Two letters protect thirty thousand dollars.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would Keystone, the BI carrier, care whether the UM carriers waive subrogation?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Now the paper itself, because a letter isn't enough here. {name}, quick question. If Harbor Point's adjuster leaves the company next year and this claim ends up in front of an arbitrator, how do we prove Harbor Point gave up its rights against Smith?\n\nWe hand over the Waiver of Subrogation. It's a separate instrument from the consent letter, on the carrier's own form, signed by the adjuster and notarized.\n\nIt waives Harbor Point's right to recover from Robert Smith and Keystone, and only them. It says the UM claim stays open, and that Harbor Point won't substitute its own check.\n\nBecause it's sworn and acknowledged before a notary, it can be filed or offered as an exhibit without anyone having to come in and testify that the letter is real. Think of the notary stamp as the document vouching for itself.\n\nHarbor Point signed its own on 08/24, Cardinal State on 08/26/2026.\n\nYour job: get the waiver itself, check it names the right carrier, policy and claim, covers Smith and Keystone only, and has a complete signature and notary block. What it waives, and what that means for John, is Atty. Thorne's to explain.\n\nThe notarized waiver speaks for itself, long after the adjuster's gone.",
   "ask": "{name}, what can a notarized waiver do in front of an arbitrator that a consent letter can't?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The rules are short. Ask the right carriers, which on this file means Harbor Point and Cardinal State, never Bluewater.\n\nThe 30-Day Letter goes out the day after the tender, 08/13/2026, for Atty. Thorne's signature, with the tender letter and Smith's affidavit, and you docket each window.\n\nWhen a consent comes back, read what it covers: Smith and Keystone only, subrogation waived, no substitution. Anything wider, or a substitution offer, goes straight to Atty. Thorne.\n\nAnd John Doe signs nothing until both are in writing and Atty. Thorne has approved the release and gone through it with him.\n\nYou request, docket, chase and match. You never explain the consent clause to anyone. {name}, if John asks what it means, tell me who answers him.\n\nYour job is the paper trail, never the meaning."
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job it runs in three beats. Keystone tenders on 08/12/2026, and you prepare both consent requests for Atty. Thorne's signature and docket the windows. They go out 08/13 with the reason Bluewater isn't asked written on the file.\n\nOn 08/14 Keystone's cover letter makes payment conditional on the waivers, so you log that condition and hold the signing task against it.\n\nThen the consents land, Harbor Point on 08/24 and Cardinal State on 08/26, and you match each one's scope to the release. Both cover Smith and Keystone only, so both go into the transmittal packet and the front of each UM package.\n\n{name}, in your own words, why does the signing task wait on those two letters?\n\nMatch the consent to the release, word for word, every time."
  }
 },
 "d3m2-rel": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Open the audit workbench and call every clause of Keystone's draft: keep, replace, pull, add, or flag for Atty. Thorne. For each one, write what it would do on this file and the amendment you'd propose.\n\nThen email your review to Atty. Thorne for approval, with the clauses that are his to decide clearly flagged. Don't send the draft to John Doe.\n\nWhen Dana Whitfield writes asking whether John can just sign the standard form now, you'll route that, not answer it. Honestly, \"can he just sign the standard form\" is the most expensive sentence in this business.\n\nFinally, convert the whole exchange into a memorandum and file it in the CMS and on your Backup Drive.\n\n{name}, before you start, tell me in your own words what \"flag for Atty. Thorne\" means on a clause.\n\nEvery clause gets a call, and the draft never reaches John."
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's Friday, 08/14/2026. Keystone tendered its $10,000 on Wednesday, and our consent requests went to Harbor Point and Cardinal State yesterday.\n\nToday Dana Whitfield sends Keystone's own release form for John Doe's notarized signature. Her cover letter says the check won't issue until both UM carriers' consents and subrogation waivers arrive with it.\n\nOur demand asked for a Limited Release of Robert Smith and Keystone only. What landed is called a Release of All Claims.\n\nThink of the difference like a key. A Limited Release opens one door, Smith's. A Release of All Claims is a master key, and it opens doors we still need locked.\n\nSo before John Doe sees a word of it, we read every clause against what was promised and what's still open on this file. {name}, which open doors come to mind?\n\nNever let the carrier's form replace the deal we made.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first difference you'd expect between a Release of All Claims and the Limited Release we demanded?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "This is the audit itself, fifteen items. Some are the easy keeps: the parties, the $10,000.00, non-admission, the notary block.\n\nThen the ones that cost money. The catch-all reaching all other insurers would release Harbor Point and Cardinal State, $30,000.\n\nProperty Damage and Loss of Use reaches the Civic's claim, which is Margaret's and still open, handled by Leah Moran. That's like selling your car and accidentally throwing in your mom's.\n\nThere's no UM reservation, a confidentiality clause nobody agreed to, an indemnity clause with no ceiling, and no payment date.\n\nAnd three clauses aren't yours at all: unknown injuries, the Medicare representation, and governing law. Those you flag. {name}, explain why those three aren't ours to call.\n\nThe boring clauses are where the money hides.",
   "ask": "{name}, why can't the release stay confidential on this particular file?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check yourself before you hand it in. {name}, did you replace the catch-all and say exactly what it would release?\n\nDid you cut the claims back to bodily injury and add a reservation of both UM claims by claim number?\n\nDid you pull confidentiality, narrow the indemnity to the liens on the file paid from trust, and add a payment date for Atty. Thorne to set?\n\nDid you flag unknown injuries, Medicare and governing law, with the Medicare facts verified from the file?\n\nIs his 08/17 approval filed, and did you keep the draft away from John Doe the whole time? Then you're done.\n\nThe release John signs should match the deal, word for word."
  }
 },
 "d3m2-exec": {
  "task": {
   "say": "First, the live call with John Doe. Schedule the signing for Friday, 08/28, at the office.\n\nTell him to bring government photo ID in the name John Doe, that he signs only in front of the notary, and that Atty. Thorne will go through the release with him first.\n\nWhen he asks what a paragraph means, and he will, that question goes to Atty. Thorne. {name}, you're on the phone with John. Tell me now your exact words when he asks.\n\nAfter the session, confirm the execution requirements and upload the release to the CMS and his portal copy.\n\nThen email Dana Whitfield the executed packet and ask her to confirm receipt and the check date. Route her payee question, deposit the check on 09/04, and file the record.\n\nYou handle the when and where. Atty. Thorne handles the what it means."
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's Wednesday, 08/26/2026. Cardinal State's consent and waiver came in today, joining Harbor Point's from Monday. Keystone issued its Limited Release on 08/19, exactly as Atty. Thorne approved it.\n\nKeystone wants three things before it pays: a notarized wet-ink original, both waivers, and our W-9. Then it pays within 14 days. John Doe hasn't seen the release yet.\n\nThis is the part of the file where everything is logistics. Think of it like a wedding: the vows are settled, and now it's rings, witnesses and the right signature on the right line.\n\nLogistics done perfectly is what gets John's money into trust. Every legal question still leaves your desk.\n\nPerfect paperwork is what turns a deal into a check.",
   "ask": "{name}, why isn't this release going out for e-signature like the rest of John's paperwork?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Run your checks. {name}, was the signing scheduled only after both consents were in writing?\n\nOn the call, did you stick to date, place, ID and the notary, and route every question about meaning?\n\nDoes the ID match John Doe exactly, is the original wet-ink and notarized, every page initialed?\n\nDid the packet go to Keystone on 08/28 with both waivers and the W-9, and did you ask for written confirmation of receipt and the check date?\n\nDid her question about adding Peach State as a payee go to Atty. Thorne with no promise from you? And is check 0048213 in trust on 09/04/2026, with the record filed?\n\nSigned right, sent complete, deposited the day it lands."
  }
 },
 "d3m2-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "A UM settlement is won on paper. The package shows the other driver's fault, and it shows that the damages are more than what's been collected.\n\nPicture a weak file next to a legal trap. Raw charts and a casual letter carry no risk for the carrier, while a clear summary on a clock raises it.\n\nSort documents by function, coverage, liability and damages, and let the diagnostics and a causation narrative answer any “he had this before” defense.\n\nYou'll hear about bracketing, paired ranges that build a bridge across a gap and then close in the middle. That's haggling, and Atty. Thorne makes every move there.\n\nAnd PIP, the client's own coverage for Medical Bills, depends on the state. In a no-fault state, PIP is mandatory and pays first, whoever caused the crash; suing needs an injury that passes a threshold, and PIP paid is set off.\n\nUM is won on paper, and the state's PIP rules decide who pays first.\n\nIn an at-fault state, the at-fault driver's BI pays, and add-on PIP pays on top. John Doe's Harbor Point PIP is add-on. {name}, I'll want that back from you in one sentence.",
   "ask": "{name}, in a no-fault state, what would John Doe need before he could sue Robert Smith at all?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Treasure hunt! I've got Harbor Point's 03/02/2026 letter up, and somewhere in it is the sentence worth $10,000 to John Doe. {name}, you've had since the last lesson. Where is it?\n\nGive it a second. It's the add-on clause. Add-on means Harbor Point pays on top of what Keystone pays.\n\nOn a reduction policy, Keystone's $10,000 would have come straight off the top of the $20,000. One sentence, ten thousand dollars.\n\nThat's why we read insurance letters the way a detective reads a ransom note.\n\nOnce Keystone tenders, the money that makes John Doe whole sits with two first-party carriers that insure him, and both of them will argue against him.\n\nWe reach $40,000 by proving the gap above each layer, closing every reduction in writing first, and papering every single move.\n\nRead every letter from a carrier like the money is hiding in it, because sometimes it is.",
   "ask": "{name}, without that add-on sentence, what would Harbor Point's $20,000 have shrunk to?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules, and where you stop. UM pays the gap: damages as Atty. Thorne values them, minus the BI, up to its limit. Cardinal State's file has to show damages above $30,000.\n\nCheck the offset rule first. Harbor Point's 03/02/2026 letter answers the limit half of “BI already compensated him,” and the medical proof answers the damages half.\n\nBoth carriers turn adversarial at evaluation, so the first offer is rarely the last. Bracketing is Atty. Thorne's tool alone.\n\nWhen a carrier offers limits, confirm coverage, release scope, liens, payee and check date without accepting, and report that day. Never accept verbally under pressure.\n\nJohn Doe's Harbor Point PIP is add-on, and here it creates no lien and no UM cut.\n\nYou assemble, log, relay and paper John Doe's decision. You never accept, reject, counter, explain offset language to him, or accuse a carrier of bad faith.\n\nOn the phone, “sounds great, I'll tell the client” is how trouble starts. What you actually say is “thank you, I'll confirm that in writing and get it to Atty. Thorne today.”\n\nConfirm in writing, report the same day, and decide nothing yourself.",
   "ask": "{name}, Harbor Point insures Kevin Alvarez's car. Why does it owe John Doe anything, and why will it still fight him?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run it on the calendar. On 09/14/2026, Harbor Point's offer comes in. You log the offer and its stated reasons in the Negotiation Log on the Insurance tab and report it to Atty. Thorne that day.\n\nBecause the offer cites the BI payment and the gap, you assemble a counter-package for his approval. That's the 03/02/2026 letter, the MRI read, the Causation Letter, the MMI residuals, meaning what's still wrong after healing, and future care. He approves, and it goes out 09/16.\n\nWhen a carrier offers limits, you confirm the terms without accepting and draft the acceptance email. It goes only after John Doe's decision is logged and Atty. Thorne approves.\n\nAnd when each UM check arrives, you confirm its release is signed and deposit it to trust, the firm's account that holds client money, that day: 10/05 and 10/13/2026.\n\nTrue story. I once found a settlement check in a desk drawer, under a takeout menu, three weeks after it arrived. Same day. Every time.\n\nLog it, report it, and deposit it the day it lands.",
   "ask": "{name}, a UM check arrives and you can't find the signed release in the file. What do you do with the check?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Your job is to work both UM claims to resolution in the Negotiation Log. That's the CMS record of every offer, counter and stated reason, with dates, so anyone can pick up the file and see exactly where the money stands.\n\nLog Harbor Point's $8,000 with its stated reasons and report it to Atty. Thorne the same day.\n\nAssemble the 09/16 counter-package for his approval: Harbor Point's 03/02/2026 letter, the MRI read, Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter, the MMI residuals and future care. Log every later move.\n\nSome carriers write a counteroffer so polite you have to read it twice to realize it's a no. Log it anyway, in their exact words.\n\nWhenever a carrier offers or tenders, record John Doe's informed decision through Atty. Thorne. Then draft the confirming email in the Email Workspace for his approval, covering coverage, release, liens, payee and check date.\n\nYou'll finish by proving $40,000.00 gross on the Recovery Ledger, the part of the Net Sheet that adds up every dollar actually recovered, before fees and bills come out.\n\nEvery offer is logged in their exact words, and every decision is John's, through Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, you're running Harbor Point's log. {name}, you've got Cardinal State.",
   "ask": "{name}, Cardinal State has offered $6,000. Who decides whether John Doe takes it, and how does that decision reach the log?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Here's today's landing, 09/14/2026, from Marcus Bell on HP-26-7741. Remember the new argument I asked you to guess? {name}, what did you come up with?\n\nHarbor Point offers $8,000.00 on the Host UM claim. His reasons: John Doe didn't treat for two weeks after the accident, and the BI payment has already compensated him for much of this loss.\n\nThat second one is the new one. He wants to know your client's position.\n\nI've put his reasons on the board right beside Harbor Point's own letter of 03/02/2026. Take a good look before anyone drafts a word.\n\nThe carrier's argument is answered by its own paper. That's my favorite kind of rebuttal, because nobody can argue with their own letterhead.\n\nFor context, Keystone's $10,000 has been in trust since 09/04, and both UM demands went out on 08/31.\n\nA carrier's own letter is the hardest argument for it to beat.",
   "ask": "{name}, which line in Harbor Point's own 03/02/2026 letter answers “BI already compensated him”?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Run this check before you log your work, and {name}, you're first. Is Harbor Point's 09/14 offer logged with both reasons quoted and reported the same day?\n\nDoes the counter-package answer the offset half with the 03/02/2026 letter and the damages half with the medical proof, including the MMI residuals and future care of $3,400 to $6,800? Did it go out 09/16 with no figure below limits from you?\n\nFor Harbor Point's $20,000 on 09/29 and Cardinal State's $10,000 tender on 10/06, is each acceptance recorded as John Doe's informed decision through Atty. Thorne, with who, on whose advice, and when?\n\nA one-word “Accepted” in the log isn't a record. It's a shrug, and it's incomplete.\n\nYour Recovery Ledger should read $40,000.00 and nothing else. Add PIP's $5,000 or Keystone's Property Damage payments and you've mixed in money that doesn't belong.\n\nThe Recovery Ledger only counts settlement money, nothing else.\n\nHere's the mystery for the Learning Check. $40,000 sounds like a happy ending. Is it?",
   "ask": "{name}, was each UM release signed before its check arrived on 10/05 and 10/13?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-umns": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You're building Stream 2, the UM column, as it stands today. Post Harbor Point and Cardinal State to the Recovery Ledger, our running list of every dollar recovered and from whom, and show which check is in trust and which is only tendered.\n\nTake the pre-suit one-third fee. Then allocate costs and liens to Stream 2 at seventy-five percent, pro rata to gross, meaning in proportion to each check's size. Thirty of the forty thousand came from UM, so UM carries three quarters of the load.\n\nEach lien goes in at its asserted or audited figure, not the reduction you hope for. Hope is lovely. It's just not a column on the Net Sheet.\n\nMark every figure that can still change, and write what will change it. Tie Stream 2 to Stream 1 and to the Trust Ledger, the firm's record of client money held in trust.\n\nThen the part I'm grading hardest: the questions Atty. Thorne has to answer before John sees this sheet. It goes to him, not to John.\n\n{name}, you're writing question one out loud for the room. {name}, you're writing question two.\n\nBigger check, bigger share. That's all pro rata means.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why does Stream 2 carry seventy-five percent of the costs and liens?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Game time: firm or still moving. I'll read a number off John Doe's file, you shout \"firm\" if it's locked, \"moving\" if it can still change. {name}, you're the referee.\n\nIt's the sixth of October. Teresa Okafor has just tendered Cardinal State's ten thousand dollar Relative UM limits, and John accepted through Atty. Thorne.\n\nUM, uninsured or underinsured motorist coverage, is the coverage that pays when the at-fault driver's insurance isn't enough. Relative UM is the coverage John reaches through a relative's policy.\n\nHarbor Point's twenty thousand has been in trust since yesterday, and Keystone's ten thousand since the fourth of September. So the stack is closed at forty thousand, but only thirty thousand is actually in the account.\n\nTendered still isn't received, and I'll keep saying it until you say it in your sleep.\n\nThe liens haven't moved yet. Peach State asserts eighteen sixty, and Riverton Spine and Rehab's account is sixty-one oh five after our audit.\n\nAtty. Thorne's memo asks for the UM settlement Net Sheet before he goes through it with John. One of these numbers looks firm and isn't. Keep an eye out.\n\nOnly money in the account is firm.",
   "ask": "{name}, of everything on this file today, which numbers are firm, and which are still going to move, and why?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check it to the cent, and {name}, you're reading the totals back to me.\n\nGross thirty thousand. Fee ten thousand even. Costs: Stream 1 takes one twenty-one sixty-nine, so Stream 2 takes the remainder, three sixty-five oh six.\n\nPeach State, thirteen ninety-five. Riverton Spine and Rehab, forty-five seventy-eight seventy-five, on the audited figure, not the billed one. Projected net, thirteen thousand six sixty-one nineteen, labelled a projection.\n\nAdd Stream 1's forty-five fifty-three seventy-three and you get eighteen thousand two fourteen ninety-two, which is forty thousand less the whole fee, the costs and the unreduced liens.\n\nIf your two columns don't add up to that, your spreadsheet isn't broken, it's telling on you.\n\nIs Harbor Point's PIP at zero, with its 03/02 letter cited? Is Cardinal State receivable, not received? That's the number that looked firm and isn't. And are your four questions written down and routed today?\n\nHere's the mystery for the Learning Check. Those liens haven't been reduced yet. Who decides whether they will be, and what on this sheet changes the moment they are?",
   "ask": "{name}, John calls tomorrow and asks if he's getting thirteen thousand. What do you tell him, in your own words?"
  }
 },
 "d3m2-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Here's the Learning Check for the negotiation module, and the number I teased is $0.00, John Doe's net on every lowball.\n\nGame-show rules again. Answers come from this file, in your own words, typed under each question, and I'll pull names for a lightning round at the end. {name}, stretch now.\n\nFirst, run the math check on Keystone's $3,500: fee, costs, unreduced liens, net. Tell me what it shows Atty. Thorne and who tells John Doe. That's the big question, so show the $0.00 cleanly.\n\nSecond, answer Dana Whitfield's gap and degenerative arguments with four documents and the radiologist's own words.\n\nThird, explain what PIP's $5,000, exhausted and non-subrogated, meaning used up with no right to be paid back, means for valuation, and what would change in a no-fault state.\n\nCareful there, because “no set-off here” isn't the same thing as “no reduction anywhere.”\n\nShow the $0.00, then explain who tells John.",
   "ask": "{name}, why is this file free of reductions, and which document says so?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary. Write what you learned negotiating John Doe's file, the principles rather than the clicks.\n\nThen your strategy. If a carrier's offer landed today, what's your next action, and why is it the right one rather than a counter? Then the one thing you're least confident about.\n\nBefore anyone writes, here's a pressure question out loud. The offer dies at 5:00 p.m. and Atty. Thorne is unreachable. Turn to your neighbor, talk it through for a minute, and then write.\n\nA hint from the field: offers that expire at 5:00 have a funny way of still being alive at 5:01 once someone with authority calls back.\n\nThat's not a reason to ignore the deadline, though. It's a reason to escalate it, in writing, right now.\n\nA deadline gets escalated in writing, never decided by you.\n\n{name}, you and your neighbor go first. Keep the scenario on screen in mind as well.",
   "ask": "{name}, the offer dies at 5:00 p.m. and Atty. Thorne is unreachable. What do you say?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Okay, the Skill Scale, and the last thing for today. Give yourself a 1 to 5 on each of the six competencies, from building the battle map and net floor through to holding the authority line, where every number and decision runs through Atty. Thorne.\n\nI'll set your self-rating next to your score, and if they're far apart in either direction, we'll talk about it. That's coaching, not a penalty.\n\nJust keep in mind that UPL is a gate, so a strong score can't rescue an ethics breach. Submit it for review when you're finished.\n\nNow tomorrow's mystery. We just collected $40,000 gross. John Doe's liens are still sitting at $7,965.00, unreduced, and tomorrow we negotiate those bills.\n\nThe authority line is the skill under every other skill.\n\n{name}, come in with your best guess. How much of that $40,000 actually ends up in John Doe's pocket, and who decides how it gets split?",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the six competencies would you most want to practice again before your next file?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The legal muscle behind a lien decides how far it'll bend, so picture a ladder.\n\nTop rung, the easiest: medical providers who treated on an LOP, a Letter of Protection, which is our written promise that they get paid from the settlement. They're very negotiable, because they'd rather be paid now than wait.\n\nNext rung: health insurance subrogation, which just means a private plan wants back what it paid. That's moderate, and it's shaped by two doctrines, made whole and common fund, which Atty. Thorne argues, not us.\n\nMade whole means, in many states, the insurer gets paid back only once the client has been fully compensated.\n\nCommon fund means a lienholder that did none of the work should share the cost of getting the money, like a roommate chipping in for the pizza they're eating.\n\nLower down: Medicare and Medicaid, government payers with strict formulas, and slower than a calculator with a dying battery. Bottom rung: ERISA plans, self-funded employer plans under federal law, notoriously hard to reduce.\n\nYou could call every lienholder and beg for a discount. Or you could know which rung each lien sits on before you dial, because that's where the leverage lives. {name}, if you could only make one call today, which rung would you start on?\n\nKnow the rung before you dial.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these lien types have you run into before, and which one gave you the hardest time?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Before I show you where John Doe's two liens sit on this chart, {name}, place a bet. Is Peach State an ERISA plan or not? Hold your answer.\n\nHere's the chart. Medical providers, doctors or hospitals treating on a Letter of Protection, sit high; they often take a reduction to get paid now. Health insurance, private insurers looking for their money back, sits moderate, subject to made whole or common fund.\n\nMedicare and Medicaid, the government programs, sit low, with strict formulas and slow processing. ERISA plans, self-funded employer Health Plans, sit very low, governed by federal law and notoriously hard to reduce.\n\nNow the reveal. Riverton Spine & Rehab is our LOP provider, top of the ladder. And Peach State is a fully insured Health Plan, not ERISA.\n\nFully insured means the employer bought an insurance policy for its workers instead of paying claims out of its own pocket. So state law, not the federal ERISA rules, generally decides how its payback gets reduced, and Atty. Thorne confirms it.\n\nThat one fact changes how hard this fight is. If you'd guessed ERISA and treated it that way, you'd have surrendered before the first phone call.\n\nFully insured, not ERISA, and that one word changes the fight.",
   "ask": "{name}, where do Riverton Spine & Rehab and Peach State land on this chart, and why does it matter for John Doe's net?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Guessing game, gut answers only, no calculators. How many liens are on John Doe's $40,000? Hands up for one. Now two. Now three or more.\n\n{name}, you went for two, didn't you? I see you. Most rooms do.\n\nHere's the reveal: two asserted and five checked, each one written down with its source. A lien, quick reminder, is a bill with a legal right to be paid out of the settlement before the client gets his share.\n\nAnd liens are like glitter. You think you got them all, and six months later one turns up on the couch.\n\nThat $40,000 isn't John Doe's until every claim on it is found, sorted and resolved in writing.\n\nThey don't all bend the same way, either. A statutory lien bends to a formula, a Health Plan's right bends to a doctrine, and a provider's balance bends to a negotiation.\n\nFind every lien, sort it right, and resolve it in writing.\n\nSort one wrong and you either overpay it, which is John's money gone, or freeze the whole payout. In a minute I'll show you which lien on this file is worth the most to get right.",
   "ask": "{name}, counting the ones we checked and found absent, how many claims did we have to look at on John Doe's $40,000?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules, and where your role stops. Statutory liens, like Medicare, Medicaid, a hospital lien or workers' comp, come from the law and bend least; contractual claims rest on an agreement.\n\nInventory every claimant, absent ones included. Writing “no Medicare lien” tells nobody anything. Writing where you checked and when is what makes it stick.\n\nTwo liens are asserted, Peach State and Riverton Spine & Rehab, and every “none” gets its source.\n\nNot every payer is a lienholder. Harbor Point's PIP has no reimbursement right, and Crescent Pain & Spine can't balance-bill, meaning it can't send John Doe the leftover.\n\nPeach State is fully insured, and what follows is Atty. Thorne's call. He asked for the one-third common-fund share, so $1,860.00 minus $620.00 is $1,240.00.\n\nMade whole, comparative fault and quantum meruit are doctrines you can name, never choose. Comparative fault cuts a recovery by the client's own share of the blame, and quantum meruit means getting paid fairly for the work actually done.\n\nYou classify, gather, audit and draft. You never compromise a lien without his authority, or tell John Doe a lien is invalid.\n\nEvery “none” needs a source, and every reduction needs his authority.",
   "ask": "{name}, Peach State paid $1,860.00 on a $3,400.00 injection. Who can come after John Doe for the other $1,540.00?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Nobody can, by the way, because Crescent took the contract rate. {name}, if you said nobody, take a bow.\n\nNow, this slide sorts every claim on John Doe's $40,000. The ladder runs from most negotiable at the top, the provider LOP, through fully insured Health Plans and workers' comp, down to Medicare and Medicaid, with ERISA self-funded at the bottom.\n\nOnly two bars are solid: Riverton Spine & Rehab at $6,290.00 and Peach State at $1,860.00. Every other bar is hollow, with a green check saying none, verified.\n\nI love those hollow bars. Each one is a lien that tried to sneak in through the back door and got stopped by somebody with a clipboard.\n\nThere's an outlined prior-attorney bar tagged quantum meruit, which is a lawyer's claim to be paid for work already done. And there are three doctrine cards, common fund, made whole and comparative fault, with that last one greyed out.\n\nAnd the warning along the bottom: unclassified means overpaid or frozen.\n\nEvery claim gets a bar, solid or hollow, and nothing goes unclassified.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think the comparative fault card is greyed out on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's put it on the job. When the gross reaches $40,000.00 on 10/06/2026, you run the final inventory in the Lien Tracker. You re-verify the Day 2 register, asserted and absent, and classify each claim as statutory or contractual.\n\nTwo stay open. Medicare, Medicaid, hospital, workers' comp and PIP each get “none” with a source, and the prior attorney gets “none known to John Doe”.\n\nOnce the Health Plan lien is classified, you get the plan documents and escalate the funding status to Atty. Thorne. Fully insured goes on file, and the governing-law call is recorded as his.\n\nWhen he picks the reduction basis, you draft the request naming the doctrine and showing $1,860.00 minus $620.00 equals $1,240.00, held for his approval.\n\nWrite down what's absent as carefully as what's there.\n\nAnd for the prior-attorney question, ask John Doe, check intake, and log “no other lawyer known to John Doe” with its date and source. {name}, that line is boring right up until the day it saves the file."
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two lists for liens, a short Do side and an even shorter Don't side.\n\nOn the Do side, you record a verified “none” the way you'd record a lien, with its source and date. You name the doctrine and show the math in every Reduction Request, and you resolve every lien in writing before disbursement.\n\nThe Don'ts. Treating an ERISA plan, a job-based Health Plan governed by federal law, like a provider LOP. And asking for a “courtesy discount” with no legal basis behind it.\n\nThe lazy request says, “Can you knock a little off?” The high-value one names the rule, shows the numbers line by line, and asks for a specific figure in writing.\n\nOne heads-up: someone will ask whether riding in Kevin Alvarez's carpool makes this workers' comp. Good question, but it's a coverage question for Atty. Thorne.\n\nWe record that no claim was filed and no lien asserted, and we leave it there.\n\n{name}, which of those Don'ts have you seen happen?\n\nName the doctrine, show the math, and never assume a lien isn't there.",
   "ask": "{name}, John Doe knows of no other lawyer. Why does quantum meruit still get a line in the Lien Tracker?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's what you'll do, all in the Lien Tracker on the Net Sheet. Match the plan's itemized payments to the treatment record, classify the lien, record its funding status, and escalate the governing-law question to Atty. Thorne through CMS Tasks.\n\nThen draft the Reduction Request for his signature in Documents, Outgoing. Name the doctrine, show the one-third math down to $1,240.00, and cite the work that built the fund, beating the 14-Day Gap defense with the MRI. Flag made whole as his to argue.\n\nRecord every absent claimant as a verified none with its source.\n\nYou'll hand in the request ready for his signature, a fully cleared Lien Tracker, and the 10/09/2026 acceptance at $1,240.00 once it's confirmed in writing.\n\n{name}, you're matching the EOB to the treatment record. {name}, you're on the absent claimants.\n\nShow the math, name the doctrine, and leave the doctrine's call to Atty. Thorne.\n\nAnd yes, you may use a calculator. Nobody in this office does one-third of $1,860.00 in their head and lives to brag about it.",
   "ask": "{name}, who decides whether John Doe was made whole, and what do you write down if a lienholder asks you?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Something just landed, 10/06/2026, on PSH-55017742, and before I read it, a quick guess. {name}, which of John Doe's two liens do you think writes to us first?\n\nHere's the reveal: Peach State Health Plan. It asserts its reimbursement right of $1,860.00, paid for the 05/15/2026 epidural injection at Crescent Pain & Spine, and asks us to confirm its claim will be protected from the proceeds.\n\nThe notice of rights and the EOB, the plan's statement of what it paid, are attached.\n\nSome context. Cardinal State has just tendered its $10,000 limits, so the gross is $40,000.00.\n\nFunny how a lienholder can stay quiet for months and then write the very week the money shows up. They always know.\n\nBoth liens have been on the Lien Tracker since the pre-demand audit, and Atty. Thorne held the Reduction Requests until the gross was known.\n\nA lienholder gets what the plan paid, and the plan documents tell you the rules.\n\nNow look at the plan documents. There's one word in there worth real money.",
   "ask": "{name}, what one fact about Peach State decides which law governs its claim?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, check yourself. {name}, is the plan ledger matched to the one accident-related charge, the 05/15/2026 injection, $3,400.00 billed and $1,860.00 paid? The lien is what the plan paid, not what was billed.\n\nIs Crescent's $1,540.00 write-off recorded as not owed?\n\nIs the lien classified contractual, with fully insured status from the plan documents and the governing law recorded as Atty. Thorne's call? Is the common-fund math shown, citing the MRI and the 06/19/2026 Causation Letter?\n\nAre the absences complete? That's Medicare, Medicaid, hospital and workers' comp, each with date and source, the prior attorney as none known to John Doe, and PIP recorded as not a lien.\n\nAnd did you log $1,240.00 as final only with Peach State's written confirmation and Atty. Thorne's approval attached? Honestly, that's where people trip.\n\nFinal means in writing and approved, never just agreed.",
   "ask": "{name}, who checked Summit Imaging, which asked for an LOP on 03/11/2026?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the road to reducing the liens, nice and simple. First, audit each bill line by line and get any errors corrected before you ask for a reduction. Then run the pro-rata math.\n\nThen ask, with Atty. Thorne's written request and his target number. Pay nothing without a full-and-final Payoff Letter. And update the ledger the day a reduction is confirmed.\n\nNow the ideas behind it. An error earns a demand for correction, not a favor.\n\nUpcoding means billing a fancier visit than the records show, and unbundling means splitting one service into several bills.\n\nPro-rata distribution means that when there's not enough money for everyone, each lien gets a slice in proportion to its size.\n\nAudit, then math, then ask, then paper.\n\nAnd the common fund doctrine says a lienholder shouldn't ride free on the work and costs that created the money it's being paid from. {name}, which of these sounds most like something you've seen on a real bill?",
   "ask": "{name}, have you ever caught a billing error that the provider fixed without an argument?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here's the path from the bill to the payoff. And remember that mystery charge on Riverton's ledger, because it's coming.\n\nStep one, cross-reference the records against the bill, so a Level 3 visit billed as Level 5 gets corrected, not discounted.\n\nStep two, hunt for duplicate charges, especially overlapping dates between hospital stays and separate doctor groups.\n\nStep three, the common fund. In many states a lienholder reduces by its share of fees and costs, and that's Atty. Thorne's argument to make.\n\nStep four, the Payoff Letter. Never pay without a signed letter saying it's full and final satisfaction of the lien.\n\nIt's tempting to jump straight to step three and ask for a percentage off. Don't. Start at step one, because every error you find is money you never have to negotiate for.\n\nErrors get corrected. Only then do you negotiate.\n\n{name}, on John Doe's file, step two has already found something. Any guesses?",
   "ask": "{name}, what did the duplicate check find on Riverton Spine & Rehab's ledger?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "I've covered the last row of this table. It shows what would have happened with only Keystone's $10,000 BI.\n\nQuick vote with your thumbs: up if John Doe takes home more than a few thousand, sideways if it's a little, down if it's nothing. {name}, where's your thumb?\n\nHere's the reveal: $0.00. That's why this lesson exists.\n\nAnd if your palms started sweating when I said math, relax. You don't have to love math. You need a calculator and the patience to use it twice.\n\nHalf the job is getting money from the carriers. The other half is keeping it from the lienholders, because every dollar a lien gives up lands in John Doe's net.\n\nAnd what moves a provider isn't a plea for a favor. It's an honest audit and the pro-rata math, laid out so plainly the provider can check it themselves.\n\nEvery dollar a lien gives up lands in John's pocket.",
   "ask": "{name}, before I uncovered that row, what did you think John Doe would take home on BI alone?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's the reveal, and the rules. Audit before you ask: corrections first, reductions second.\n\nRiverton billed 34 lines at $185.00, $6,290.00, but the records show 33 visits and a duplicate on 05/04/2026, so you negotiate from $6,105.00. Match each code to that day's note; nothing may fall between 04/02 and 04/21/2026.\n\nWith only the BI, $6,179.92 would split pro rata, Peach State $1,443.15 and Riverton $4,736.77, and John Doe nets nothing. But on Track A the fund covers both liens, so argue true facts only.\n\n“Can you knock some off?” gets you nowhere. “Here's your bill, here's the duplicate, here's the corrected balance, here's the math” gets you a yes.\n\nNever say a provider would take only a share or that the client will refuse the settlement. Common fund is Atty. Thorne's argument.\n\nSend his approved request, call with his target, and remember, no Payoff Letter means no payment. You never offer or accept outside his authority.\n\nNegotiate from the audited number, never the billed one.",
   "ask": "{name}, Riverton billed $6,290.00. What number do you negotiate from, and why that one?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the pro-rata picture, on BI alone. That long bar is the $6,179.92 left after fee and costs, split 23.35% to Peach State, which is $1,443.15, and 76.65% to Riverton Spine & Rehab, which is $4,736.77.\n\nUnderneath, you can see $7,965.00 of liens hanging off the end in red hatching, and that little red card on the right says it all: John Doe nets $0.00.\n\nI call that red hatching the “so when's my check?” zone, because that's the question you'd be answering every day if this were the real file.\n\nAlong the bottom is the order of work. Audit, correct, calculate, ask with authority, confirm in writing, and then the Net Sheet gets updated.\n\nPro rata means each lien shares the pain in proportion to its size.\n\nThe math is the leverage, and the audit always comes before the ask. {name}, read me the two percentages and tell me which lien carries more of the pain.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do we compute each share from the fraction instead of the rounded percentage?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job, it runs in three beats. When the provider's Final Ledger arrives, you audit each line against the visit records you requested, and you ask in writing for any error to be corrected.\n\nOn John Doe's file, that's 33 visits matched, the 05/04/2026 duplicate removed, and $6,105.00 in the Lien Tracker.\n\nWith the audited balance confirmed, you draft the Reduction Request with the math and get Atty. Thorne's written authority, escalated through CMS, logged before the request goes out or any call is made.\n\nAnd when a reduction is agreed, you get the signed Payoff Letter, update the Lien Tracker and the Net Sheet, and recompute the net that same day.\n\nA Case Manager I trained once won a gorgeous 30% off a provider bill, celebrated with the whole team, then found two duplicate visits on it.\n\nShe'd negotiated a discount on money the provider was never owed. Audit first, celebrate second.\n\nAudit, authorize, then get the Payoff Letter.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those three beats would you be most tempted to rush, and what would it cost John Doe?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-l3": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Finish the sentence, fast, first thing that comes to mind: “A lien reduction isn't done until…” {name}, go. Somebody else?\n\nOkay, here's the reveal. Riverton Spine & Rehab has agreed to take less, and most people say the agreement is the finish line. It isn't. The finish line is the signed Payoff Letter.\n\nA reduction is won on paper first and on the phone second. The lienholder needs to see the audited balance, the recovery, the fee and costs, and the other liens, so it can see why taking less is fair.\n\nAnd it needs a letter it can sign that closes the bill for good. Skip the paper, and you either leave money on the table or reopen a bill you thought was closed.\n\nA verbal yes from a billing office is like a client saying “I'll fax it tonight.” Lovely to hear. Not something you can deposit.\n\nA reduction isn't real until it's signed.",
   "ask": "{name}, what goes to the lienholder before you ever pick up the phone?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you ever pay a lien, meet the most important piece of paper in lien negotiation. {name}, guess what it is.\n\nThe Payoff Letter. It's the lienholder's signed letter stating the agreed payoff in full satisfaction: no balance billing, which is billing the client for whatever's left, no collections, and a release of its lien.\n\nIts legal purpose is to put the reduction you won on the phone in writing, so once it's paid the account closes at $0.00 and John Doe isn't billed for it again.\n\nOn our file, that's Riverton Spine & Rehab's letter of 10/12/2026: $6,105.00 audited, $4,580.00 accepted, as long as it's paid within 30 days. That letter is what backs the disbursement.\n\nYour part is simple and strict. No Payoff Letter, no payment. Check every figure against the audited balance, docket the 30-day condition, and update the Lien Tracker and the Net Sheet the same day.\n\n\"They agreed on the phone\" isn't a record. Phone agreements have the shelf life of milk. Get it in writing, because a deal you can't prove is a bill that comes back.\n\nNo Payoff Letter, no payment.",
   "ask": "{name}, what happens to Riverton Spine & Rehab's balance if the $4,580.00 isn't paid within 30 days?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Four rules carry this. First, you negotiate from the audited balance, never the billed one: Riverton at $6,105.00, Peach State at $1,860.00, $7,965.00 in all.\n\nSecond, the written Reduction Request goes first, with Atty. Thorne's written authority already on file.\n\nThird, on the call you make his ask and nothing outside it, and you log the name, the date and every figure. A counter outside his authority goes back to him the same day.\n\n“They said they'd work with us” is a note that helps nobody. “Tammy Greer, billing manager, offered this figure on this date, routed to Atty. Thorne at this time” is a note that holds up.\n\nFourth, no Payoff Letter, no payment. The letter says full and final, with no balance billing or collections, meaning nobody can chase John Doe for the rest later. Then you update the Lien Tracker and the Net Sheet that day.\n\nAudited balance, written request, authorized ask, signed letter.\n\n{name}, which rule do you think gets broken most?",
   "ask": "{name}, if the provider counters below Atty. Thorne's floor, what exactly do you say on the call?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the job, start to finish. When the audited balance is confirmed, you draft the Reduction Request with the math and get Atty. Thorne's written authority before it goes.\n\nWhen the lienholder responds, you call with the authorized ask and log who you spoke to, when, and every figure.\n\nAnd when the reduction is agreed, you get the signed Payoff Letter, then update the Lien Tracker and the Net Sheet so John Doe can see his net change the same day.\n\nI had a client once who called every morning at 8:01 to ask, “So when's my check?” The day we sent him an updated Net Sheet showing his number going up, he stopped calling for a whole week.\n\nClear paper is the best client service there is.\n\nEvery agreed reduction shows up on John's Net Sheet the same day.\n\nNow your mystery. On BI alone, John Doe nets $0.00. On the real file, it's $20,359.92. {name}, before the next lesson, tell me what you think turned zero into twenty thousand.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a provider later says the bill is still open, which of these three steps would you check first?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "First, audit Riverton's ledger against the visit records and request a written correction before you ask for anything. Then draft the Reduction Request for Atty. Thorne's approval, and send it once he approves.\n\nNext comes the follow-up call in the Call Simulator with Tammy Greer, the billing manager. State the audited balance, $6,105.00 with the 05/04/2026 duplicate removed.\n\nExplain the coverage-capped recovery as facts, and ask for payment in full in the interest of global resolution.\n\nShe'll agree to $4,580.00 on the call. Accept nothing; it goes to Atty. Thorne. Ask for a signed Payoff Letter with full satisfaction and no balance billing.\n\nYou'll hand in the logged call, the Payoff Letter dated 10/12/2026, and a recomputed Net Sheet showing $20,359.92.\n\nI once heard a brand-new Case Manager hear a yes on a call and shout “Deal!” so loud the whole office cheered. The attorney hadn't approved it. The cheering stopped.\n\nA yes on the phone goes to Atty. Thorne, then into writing.\n\n{name}, you're on the phone; {name}, you're logging every figure she says.",
   "ask": "{name}, the manager just said yes to $4,580.00. What are your exact next words?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 10/09/2026, and Peach State has accepted $1,240.00 in writing. One down.\n\nNow here's what just landed from Riverton Spine & Rehab: John Doe's Final Itemized Ledger under the 02/23/2026 Letter of Protection, 34 visits at $185.00, balance $6,290.00. They understand the case is settling and want to know when to expect payment in full.\n\nNotice they said payment in full. Providers and clients have the same favorite question this week, and it's “so when's my check?”\n\nVisit notes are attached. Atty. Thorne has set a target for Riverton in writing, and the Reduction Request is yours to draft.\n\nWe're running this one live. {name}, you're making the call, and I'll warn you now, the billing manager is going to push back: “We gave him 33 visits on a promise of payment, and you want a discount?”\n\nAudit the bill before you ask for a single dollar off.\n\nYour answer is the audit and the true facts, not sympathy. And did you catch the number that doesn't match?",
   "ask": "{name}, she says 33 visits. So how many lines did Riverton actually bill?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check yourself before you log it. {name}, did you audit line by line, 34 billed lines against 33 documented visits, 16 before the disruption and 17 after?\n\nDid you request the written correction for the 05/04/2026 duplicate before any reduction, taking $6,290.00 down to $6,105.00? No lines between 04/02 and 04/21/2026? Was the request approved and sent before the call?\n\nDid you argue only true facts, a recovery capped at $40,000 of coverage, and never claim the client would refuse? When she said $4,580.00, did you say it goes to Atty. Thorne and will be confirmed in writing?\n\nIs the signed Payoff Letter on file with full-and-final and no-balance-billing language? And does the Net Sheet show $20,359.92?\n\nA number on its own is half a file. A number, a letter and a log is a finished one, and that letter is what lets the 10/22/2026 disbursement go out clean.\n\nA number, a letter and a log. That's a finished lien.\n\nSo here's the mystery: what would the error have cost if nobody had audited?",
   "ask": "{name}, if you'd opened with a discount on $6,290.00, how much error would you have negotiated around?"
  }
 },
 "d3m3-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "This is the Lien Reduction Verification, and let's treat it like a quiz show where the grand prize is John Doe's net. Answer from the file, in your own words, typed under each question.\n\nQuestion one. Classify each claim on John Doe's $40,000, that's Peach State, Riverton Spine & Rehab, PIP, Medicare and Medicaid, and a prior attorney, as statutory, contractual or none, and rank the liens by how far they'll bend.\n\nQuestion two. Explain why Peach State being a fully insured group plan matters, and who decides what follows. That's the big one.\n\nIf your answer is “state law applies, so we argue made whole,” you've just made Atty. Thorne's decision for him, and that's a buzzer.\n\nQuestion three. Show the math from $1,860.00 to $1,240.00, name the doctrine, and connect it to beating the 14-Day Gap with the MRI.\n\nClassify, explain, show the math, and leave the doctrine to Atty. Thorne.\n\nCalculators are allowed. Crying over the math is also allowed, quietly. {name}, you're first to submit, no pressure.",
   "ask": "{name}, on question two, where exactly does your job end and Atty. Thorne's begin?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Time for the Self-Summary. Put the principles of lien reduction into your own words, not the clicks.\n\nThen your strategy. With both liens resolved, what's the next action before the 10/22/2026 disbursement, and why is it the right one? Then name the one thing you're least confident about, and what would get you there.\n\nBefore you write, glance at the scenario on screen: Keystone's 08/14/2026 release with Harbor Point's consent still out and Atty. Thorne away.\n\nA Case Manager I knew once got a call like that on a Friday at 4:55, “sign today or the offer's gone,” with the attorney on a plane.\n\nShe didn't panic, she didn't sign. She documented it, called the attorney's cell, and left a written escalation. The offer was still there on Monday. It almost always is.\n\nStop, document, escalate. Every single time.\n\nHold onto that instinct for every pressure question: stop, document, escalate. {name}, you'll read yours out first.",
   "ask": "{name}, when a deadline and an absent attorney collide, what's the one thing you never do?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Now the Skill Scale for this module. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each skill, from classifying liens as statutory or contractual right through to holding the authority line in lien negotiation.\n\nI'll compare each rating with your score. If you gave yourself a 5 and scored 60, or a 2 and aced it, we'll sit down and talk about why.\n\nAnd remember, UPL, practicing law without a license, is a gate. No score survives an ethics breach. Submit when you're ready.\n\nThen here's a mystery to carry into the Final Accounting Check. A balance of $1,540.00 is going to turn up on John Doe's account. It's the exact gap between what Crescent billed and what Peach State paid.\n\nAuthority first, then accuracy, then speed.\n\nIs that a lien we missed, or a bill nobody owes? {name}, I want your gut answer now, and your proof from the file when we get there.",
   "ask": "{name}, which lien skill felt strongest today, and does your score agree with you?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The Net Sheet is your tool for honesty and control, and it works in two streams. Think of two buckets.\n\nThe first bucket is the third-party BI stream, the money from the at-fault driver's insurance company. Emptying that one first, all the way to its limit, is usually a legal step you have to take before you can touch the second bucket.\n\nThe second bucket is the first-party UM stream, from the client's own policy or a relative he lives with, and it only opens once BI is used up or the driver has no insurance.\n\nNow the trap I promised: double-dipping, which means the same dollar gets counted twice. Carriers audit closely, so tracing every dollar's source keeps liens paid correctly and the buckets from mixing.\n\nAnd the final total puts both streams side by side, so the client sees what the work added.\n\nTwo streams, two columns, and no dollar counted twice.\n\nOne long column is a mess waiting to happen. Two clean ones tell the story. {name}, which would you rather defend to an auditor?",
   "ask": "{name}, have you ever seen money counted twice on a bill or a paycheck, and how was it caught?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here are the two streams on John Doe's Net Sheet. Before I fill them in, {name}, guess how many insurance companies are feeding these two columns.\n\nStream one is third-party BI, the at-fault driver's insurance company. You use up its limit first, because that's usually a legal requirement, and here it's Keystone, $10,000.\n\nStream two is first-party UM or UIM, coverage from a host vehicle or a relative John lives with, which only kicks in once BI is used up or the at-fault driver is uninsured. On our file that's $20,000 from Harbor Point plus $10,000 from Cardinal State.\n\nSo the answer is three. Keep them in separate columns from the first entry to the last, and you'll never have to untangle them later.\n\nI once watched a team spend an entire Friday untangling one sheet where somebody had mixed the streams on line two. By five o'clock, the calculator had more personality than they did.\n\nKeep the streams apart from line one.",
   "ask": "{name}, why did Stream 2 only open after Keystone paid its full $10,000?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Two numbers on the board: $6,179.92 against $7,965.00. That's the BI stream alone.\n\nQuick game, closest guess wins bragging rights. What would John Doe have taken home? {name}, you first. Anyone else?\n\nThe answer is nothing at all. Now here's what he actually nets: $20,359.92. The difference is why the Net Sheet matters.\n\nThe Net Sheet is just the one page that shows every dollar coming in and every dollar going out.\n\nMost people read a Net Sheet once, squint, and then just ask what the bottom number is. That's fine for the client. It's not fine for us.\n\nHis $40,000 came from three carriers in two streams, and it goes out as five checks. The Net Sheet shows where every dollar came from and where it's going before a cent leaves trust.\n\nIf you can't trace a dollar, it doesn't move.\n\nIf you can't trace a dollar on the sheet, it doesn't move. And there's one trap on this sheet that catches even experienced people. I'll show you where it hides.",
   "ask": "{name}, what do you think turned $0.00 into $20,359.92 on this file?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Okay, the rules, and where your role stops. One Net Sheet, two tabs. The Recovery Ledger carries money in and every deduction, and the Lien Tracker proves each lien figure. Neither is final until they tie out.\n\nKeep the streams apart, BI $10,000.00 and UM $30,000.00. PIP's $5,000.00 is a payment source on the Lien Tracker, never recovery.\n\nPrice the fee per stream at 33⅓% pre-suit: $3,333.33 plus $10,000.00 is $13,333.33. Every cost needs a receipt, $486.75 in all, split 25% and 75%, $121.69 and $365.06.\n\nReduce liens globally, then allocate pro rata. Run the net both ways: $5,089.98 plus $15,269.94 is $20,359.92. Flag future ESIs, which are injections, of $3,400 to $6,800 for Atty. Thorne.\n\nYou build and explain the arithmetic. You never tell John Doe whether the net is enough.\n\nThat one's tempting when he asks, “Is that good?” Your answer is the math, and Atty. Thorne answers the rest.\n\nTwo tabs, tied out, run both ways.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does Harbor Point's PIP appear on the Lien Tracker but not in the gross?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's John Doe's Net Sheet, both tabs. The Recovery Ledger is in front, with three columns: total, Stream 1 BI and Stream 2 UM.\n\nDown the rows you've got the gross, the 33⅓% fee, costs, Peach State and Riverton Spine & Rehab, closing on that heavy orange net row: $20,359.92, which is $5,089.98 plus $15,269.94.\n\nSee those thin orange lines running from each lien row back to the Lien Tracker tab? They read proved by Payoff Letter. And in the corner, the reminder that BI alone meant $0.00 to John Doe.\n\nEvery figure on the net row traces back to its source. That's the whole idea.\n\nHere's the honest truth: nobody wants to read a Net Sheet twice. That's exactly why you build it so they only have to read it once.\n\nEvery figure on the net row traces to a document.\n\n{name}, trace one line for me, out loud, from the net row back to its proof.",
   "ask": "{name}, if the two stream nets didn't add up to $20,359.92, where would you look first?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job, the Net Sheet builds as the file moves. Each settlement check that arrives gets posted to its stream on the Recovery Ledger, with carrier and check reference: BI $10,000.00, UM $20,000.00 plus $10,000.00, $40,000.00 total.\n\nEach cost gets its receipt attached and is posted once; five receipts make $486.75, and nothing is charged to both streams in full.\n\nWhen a lien reduction is accepted, the Lien Tracker gets the asserted, audited and reduced figures and the Payoff Letter: Peach State $1,240.00, Riverton Spine & Rehab $4,580.00, letters dated.\n\nAnd once both tabs are complete, you tie the Net Sheet to the Trust Ledger, net $20,359.92 on both, and route it to Atty. Thorne before John Doe ever sees it.\n\nSo why does that order matter? Because the first number a client sees is the number he remembers forever.\n\nShow him a draft that's off by a hundred dollars, and you'll be explaining that hundred dollars until the day the file closes. {name}, I promise you, he will bring it up.\n\nAtty. Thorne sees it first, so the number John sees is right.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the Net Sheet go to Atty. Thorne before John Doe ever sees it?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You're completing both tabs of John Doe's Net Sheet.\n\nOn the Recovery Ledger, post each check by carrier and stream, the 33⅓% fee per stream, the five receipted costs, and costs and liens split 25% and 75%, down to the net per stream and in total.\n\nOn the Lien Tracker, list every provider and payer with charges, payments, adjustments and balance owed, then each lien's asserted, audited and reduced figure, the basis of each reduction and its payoff-letter status.\n\nTie both tabs to the Trust Ledger. You'll hand in a two-tab Net Sheet reaching $20,359.92 down both streams and across the total, with every lien figure proved and routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, you're on the Recovery Ledger. {name}, you're on the Lien Tracker. Calculators out, and run every number twice. In this job, “I was close” is not a total.\n\nEvery number twice, every lien proved, and the future flagged.\n\nAnd think hard about what you flag before John signs, because one future cost isn't on either tab yet.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Riverton Spine & Rehab had accepted $4,580.00 only on a phone call, could the line be marked final?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Here's what landed today, 10/13/2026. Cardinal State Mutual's $10,000.00 is deposited to IOLTA on CS-26-20355, but it hasn't cleared.\n\nThat joins Keystone's check, received 09/04, and Harbor Point's, 10/05, each deposited only after its release was signed. Trust has received $40,000.00.\n\nPeach State accepted $1,240.00 on 10/09 and Riverton Spine & Rehab accepted $4,580.00 on 10/12, both in writing. Costs stand at $486.75. Both Net Sheet tabs are half-built, and they need finishing before the Settlement Statement is drafted.\n\nNow a riddle before you touch anything. There are two lines on this file that look like money we owe, and we owe neither. {name}, can you name them?\n\nHere they are: PIP's $5,000.00 and Crescent's write-off. Add either to the payables and you've paid John Doe's money away.\n\nIt's like tipping twice because you forgot you already left cash on the table, except the money isn't yours.\n\nNot every line on a file is a bill we owe.",
   "ask": "{name}, why isn't Harbor Point's PIP $5,000.00 paid back out of the settlement?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check the arithmetic both ways before you log it, and yes, I'm calling names.\n\n{name}, gross: Keystone's $10,000.00 to Stream 1, Harbor Point and Cardinal State's $30,000.00 to Stream 2. Fee: $3,333.33 plus $10,000.00, $13,333.33. Costs: $486.75, split $121.69 and $365.06, charged once.\n\nLiens split 25% and 75%: Peach State $310.00 and $930.00, Riverton $1,145.00 and $3,435.00. Net: $5,089.98 plus $15,269.94 is $20,359.92, and $40,000.00 minus $19,640.08 lands in the same place.\n\nA one-cent drift means a stream got rounded on its own. I once spent an afternoon hunting one missing penny. Found it. Best day of my month.\n\nAre the zero-balance lines kept off the payables, with their sources? Is each lien's payoff status recorded, none final on a phone call?\n\nAnd the judgment call, the one future cost. Did you flag up to $6,800 in possible repeat injections to Atty. Thorne before John Doe signs? Getting the math right is the floor. Seeing what's coming is the job.\n\nTie it out both ways, to the cent.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the future-injection flag go to Atty. Thorne rather than straight to John Doe?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the road from a signed deal to a ledger at zero. Audit the release line by line. Get both UM consents before the BI release is signed, meaning the UM carriers agree in writing first.\n\nShow John Doe how $40,000.00 becomes his net. Deposit each check to IOLTA, the firm's trust account for client money, the day its release is signed. And disburse on Payoff Letters and Atty. Thorne's signature until the ledger reads $0.00.\n\nFour documents close a case. The Release of All Claims ends the right to sue, and its wording decides which claims survive. The Settlement Disclosure Statement runs from gross to net and says full and final.\n\nA Satisfaction of Lien is the provider's signed letter that its lien is paid. And where suit was filed, a Dismissal With Prejudice means it can't be filed again.\n\nFour documents close a case. Know them cold.\n\nYou can't close a file by collecting whatever paper happens to show up. You close it by knowing those four by heart. {name}, name them back to me.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these four documents has John Doe's file not needed, and why?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Remember that mystery line? Here it is, from Keystone's 08/14 draft, struck through: “all other persons, firms, corporations and insurers.”\n\nQuick vote. Hands up if you'd have caught that on a first read. Keep them up if you'd have caught it at 4:45 on a Friday. Yeah, that's what I thought.\n\n{name}, what do you think it would have cost John Doe if nobody had read it? $30,000. It would have released both UM carriers.\n\nA release, by the way, is the paper where the client gives up the right to sue in exchange for the money. That's why we audit every one.\n\nA settlement isn't over when a carrier says yes. It's over when the right release is signed, every check has cleared trust, every lien is paid off in writing, and the ledger reads $0.00.\n\nUntil then, the work isn't done, no matter how many times the client asks, so when's my check?\n\nSettled means signed, cleared, paid off and zeroed.",
   "ask": "{name}, when any release lands on your desk, what's the first thing you read for?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you audit one, let's break down the Limited Release, and I'll show you a sentence worth $30,000 in a minute.\n\nIt's the document John Doe signs to end his BI claim, and it releases Robert Smith and Keystone only, for the $10,000, and nobody else.\n\nIts legal purpose is to end the claim against Smith in exchange for payment while preserving the UM claims against Harbor Point and Cardinal State. Its evidentiary role is exhaustion proof, proof the first policy has been paid out in full: signed 08/28/2026, it sits in each UM demand.\n\nNow the reveal. Look at what Keystone actually sent on 08/14: a release of all other persons, firms, corporations and insurers. {name}, how much coverage just disappeared? That's $30,000 of UM coverage.\n\nYour part is to audit every line, quote the over-broad words in a redline memo, a memo marking exactly what's wrong, to Atty. Thorne, and hold any signature until both consents are in. What a clause means for John is his to explain.\n\nSkimming for the dollar amount won't save you. Read every \"and\" and \"all,\" because insurers hide the expensive words in the boring sentences.\n\nRead every word of a release, especially the boring ones.",
   "ask": "{name}, who are the other insurers on this file, and what would releasing them have cost John Doe?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here are the rules, and where you stop. Read every release line by line before John Doe sees it: who's released, which claims, what he promises.\n\nA BI release can't reach what's still open, like the Civic's property-damage claim with Keystone PD, and both UM consents come before it's signed.\n\nThe indemnity clause, which is John Doe's promise to repay the carrier if a lienholder later chases it, means every lien must be tracked and paid off in writing. Harbor Point's release must not reach Cardinal State.\n\nThe Settlement Statement shows $40,000.00 becoming $20,359.92. Log each check on arrival, deposit to IOLTA once its release is signed, and never disburse before funds clear on 10/20. No suit was filed here, so no dismissal.\n\nYou audit, flag, prepare and verify. Atty. Thorne explains, approves and signs.\n\nYou audit and prepare. Atty. Thorne explains and signs.\n\n{name}, here's a test of that line.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone's check is in. Why can't we pay Riverton Spine & Rehab today?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the job. When a settlement is agreed or tendered, request the payee's W-9, the tax form that lets accounting cut a check, the same day. Send it back with the signed release, so accounting never holds the check.\n\nWhen a carrier's draft release arrives, audit the parties, claims, indemnity, Medicare and confidentiality terms in the release audit, and route a redline memo. Every over-broad line gets quoted and flagged before John Doe sees it.\n\nA redline memo is a short note that quotes each bad line of the draft, marks the fix and says what it would cost the client, like a teacher's red pen.\n\nWhen a check arrives, confirm its release is signed, deposit it to IOLTA that day and log the clearance date: 09/04, 10/05 and 10/13, $40,000.00, all cleared by 10/20.\n\nAnd if a suit is pending, which isn't Track A, you prepare the stipulation of dismissal with prejudice, docket it and confirm the court entry.\n\nSkip the W-9 step and you'll learn the hard way. A check can sit in accounting for two weeks over one missing form.\n\nDo the boring step the day it comes up.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these steps would you put on your calendar the very day a settlement is agreed?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-close": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Here's the one everyone skips. But first, the rule.\n\nBefore a case is marked Archived, the Case Manager checks four document sets. Then the file goes under a retention policy, which is just how long we have to keep it.\n\nThe four sets are the money trail, proof of delivery, compliance and identity, and the closing memo.\n\nThe closing memo is the one people skip. It's one page, written by you, saying how the file started and how it ended, explaining every gap between the numbers and recording every exception, or saying none was granted. Think of it as the file's obituary: short, true, and complete.\n\nRetention means every document is tagged by type, with its destroy-by date on the calendar. And a late bill, one that shows up after the closing letter, gets checked, classified and routed the same day.\n\nIn the old days, a closed file went into a banker's box in the basement, next to the holiday decorations and a broken chair. Nobody could ever find anything.\n\n{name}, which set would you skip if you were in a rush?",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the four sets do you think goes missing most often when a file gets archived?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "The Trust Ledger hit $0.00 on 10/22/2026. Five checks went out, $40,000.00 in and $40,000.00 out.\n\nShow of hands: who thinks John Doe's file is closed? Keep them up if you'd bet your lunch on it. {name}, I see your hand.\n\nThe reveal: not yet. Zero is where closing starts.\n\nThink about who might open this file after we've put it away. An auditor, a lienholder, or John Doe himself, holding a bill that turned up after the money was gone.\n\nLiens have a habit of showing up after the party's over, like the guest who arrives just as you're stacking the chairs.\n\nIf the file can't show where every dollar went, who signed for it, and why each figure moved, it can't answer any of them. Closing is the proof that every dollar landed where the Net Sheet said it would.\n\nAnd there's one document in closing that almost everybody skips. I'll tell you which.\n\nA zero balance is the start of closing, not the end.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a bill for John Doe's treatment arrived after the closing letter, what would you need to find in the file to answer it?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Nothing gets archived until all four sets are present. The test: could an outside auditor trace every dollar from the file alone?\n\nThe money trail lists the three deposits and the five checks of 10/22/2026, each with its confirmation number. The zero balance counts only when the bank statement, the trust journal and John Doe's client ledger agree and every check has cleared. It also shows a second reviewer on every disbursement and payment details checked by phone.\n\nProof of delivery is the two satisfactions and John's signed receipt. Compliance is his payer disclosure and a current W-9. The closing memo explains every figure that moved.\n\nYou draft the closing letter, and Atty. Thorne signs it. Tag every document and docket its disposition, 7 years from closing on this file. A late bill goes to Atty. Thorne the same day.\n\n“Ledger's zero, done” isn't a close. {name}, a real close is a file a stranger could audit without calling you.",
   "ask": "{name}, Riverton Spine & Rehab's $6,290.00 became $4,580.00. What does the closing memo say about that, and why?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs. When the ledger hits $0.00 on 10/22/2026, you build the money trail in the Trust Ledger: three deposits, five disbursements, the confirmation numbers and the zero-balance report. Check that the bank statement, the trust journal and the client ledger agree once all five checks clear.\n\nWhen the satisfactions and John Doe's receipt come back, you file them with his payer disclosure and a current W-9, and draft the closing letter for Atty. Thorne's signature.\n\nWith the closing memo on 10/30/2026, you tag every document and docket its disposition, 7 years from closing.\n\nAnd if a bill turns up after the letter, you check it, classify it, route it to Atty. Thorne and call John Doe that same day.\n\nClose it so well that you never have to explain it.\n\nNow your mystery. A statement for exactly $1,540.00 from Crescent Pain & Spine is about to land after the closing letter. {name}, is that a lien we missed, or a bill nobody owes? Bring your proof to the Final Accounting Check.",
   "ask": "{name}, a statement for Crescent's $1,540.00 lands after the closing letter. What's the first document you pull?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-pal": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the work. Log the notice the moment it arrives and route it to Atty. Thorne the same day, and send nothing back to Vance & Kettering, not even to say no.\n\nOn his instruction, put a temporary hold on the disputed $6,666.67 in the CMS and update the Action Plan so everyone sees the 10/22 disbursement depends on it.\n\nThen investigate. Pull their agreement, compare its signatures with John's own, line its date up against our engagement, and audit the work they claim.\n\nCall John Doe and ask, neutrally, whether he ever hired them. Neutral means no hints, no \"you didn't, did you?\"\n\nDeliver the packet to Atty. Thorne and archive everything in the CMS and Drive. {name}, try it now: how would you word that neutral question to John?\n\nYou gather the facts. Atty. Thorne decides what they mean."
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's Wednesday, 10/14/2026. The $40,000.00 is in trust, both liens are reduced in writing, and disbursement is set for 10/22.\n\nThen an email arrives from a firm none of us has heard of, Vance & Kettering Injury Law. It says it was retained on 07/27/2026 and claims a charging lien, which is a lawyer's claim to be paid out of the settlement.\n\nIt wants one-half of the fee, $6,666.67, asks us to hold the whole settlement, and wants the case manager to confirm the split by Friday.\n\nNobody on this file has ever mentioned another lawyer. It's like a stranger showing up at the reading of the will with a napkin that says \"I get the boat.\"\n\nSo the question isn't whether to argue with them. It's what you do in the next hour.\n\nLog it, route it, say nothing back.",
   "ask": "{name}, who answers Mr. Vance, and why not you?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "In the workbench you'll test each claim in their notice against the file. Their agreement is the key exhibit, so you keep it whole.\n\nAnd it shows something important. The only signature is Margaret Doe's, as mother, and John's line is blank. {name}, what does a blank line on a contract tell you?\n\nYou add what the notice leaves out: John's own signatures from 02/09/2026, a timeline showing we were engaged and had served the demand before 07/27, his account in his own words, and the hold record.\n\nYou replace their claims of substantial services and carrier notice with what the record actually shows: 0.6 hours with Margaret and no carrier contact.\n\nAnd you flag the fee, the lien and their request for your confirmation.\n\nLet the documents do the arguing."
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you hand the packet over, check it. {name}, was the notice logged and routed on 10/14, with nothing sent to the firm?\n\nIs the $6,666.67 hold in place on Atty. Thorne's instruction, and does the Action Plan show it?\n\nDid you compare the signatures and cross-reference the dates against 02/09 and 07/20? Did you quote their time record rather than characterize it?\n\nIs John Doe's account in his words, with no view from you on the lien?\n\nAnd does every sentence in the packet state a fact tied to a document, with validity, quantum meruit and any reply left on Atty. Thorne's list?\n\nFacts tied to documents, and not one opinion of your own."
  }
 },
 "d3m4-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Start with the conditions precedent, and check each one against its document, not your memory.\n\nThat's Keystone's executed Limited Release and both UM releases. Cleared funds. The signed statement. The Payoff Letters. The prior-attorney lien withdrawn, with the hold released by Atty. Thorne.\n\nThen queue one disbursement command per Net Sheet line in the Trust Ledger for his approval: the fee and costs to the firm, Peach State, Riverton Spine & Rehab, and John Doe.\n\nOne command per line, and the ledger ends at zero.\n\nShow the ledger reaching $0.00, and request the lien satisfactions and John's receipt with the payments. {name}, you'll also take John's call about his check."
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 10/21/2026, the day before the money moves. All three checks, $40,000.00, had cleared trust by yesterday, the last being Cardinal State's from 10/13.\n\nJohn Doe signed the Settlement Statement this morning. Both Payoff Letters are in Documents. And on Monday, 10/19, Vance & Kettering withdrew its lien in writing and Atty. Thorne released the hold.\n\nDisbursement is tomorrow. {name}, your job today is to make sure every gate behind the money is genuinely closed, and then to line up five commands that take the ledger to zero.\n\nEvery gate closed, then the money moves.",
   "ask": "{name}, who lifts a hold on trust money, and how do you prove it on the file?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check it all before anything goes to Atty. Thorne. {name}, are the executed Limited Release and both UM releases on file? Did funds clear by 10/20, and did John sign the statement on 10/21?\n\nIs the 10/19 withdrawal there, and the hold's release recorded as Atty. Thorne's?\n\nDo the five commands match the Net Sheet exactly? That's $13,333.33 fee, $486.75 costs, $1,240.00 to Peach State, $4,580.00 to Riverton Spine & Rehab and $20,359.92 to John Doe, totaling $40,000.00 and leaving $0.00.\n\nIs every packet complete, and is every command waiting for his signature, not yours?\n\nFive commands, $40,000.00 out, $0.00 left, and his signature on every one.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you do if the hold had not been released by 10/21?"
  }
 },
 "d4-drive": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the drill. In your own Google Drive, delete Liens and Reimbursement from John's folder. Yes, on purpose. It feels wrong. Do it anyway.\n\nThen open My Backup Drive and run Check my drive. Read what it says is missing, and why that folder matters. Use Put the missing folders back, and run the check again until nothing is missing.\n\nThen back up today's papers by filing them in the CMS from Work From. Peach State's acceptance, Riverton's Payoff Letter and the three prior-attorney lien documents go into Liens and Reimbursement. The cost ledger and the closing letter go into Wages and Costs.\n\nThen a Case Note: what was missing, how you found it, what you put back, what's on the drive now.\n\n{name}, rebuilding the folder by hand feels faster. It isn't. Let the check find it and the standard put it back.",
   "ask": "{name}, why run Check my drive before you file into a folder, and not after?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 10/23/2026, two in the afternoon. Track A's money went out yesterday, and the closing letter goes to John today.\n\nThe lien file is finished. Peach State accepted twelve forty in writing, Riverton Spine and Rehab sent its Payoff Letter, and Vance and Kettering's charging lien came in, got repudiated and was withdrawn.\n\nAnd this week, a colleague tidying shared folders deleted a lien folder from a case backup. Nobody noticed. Oops.\n\nQuick poll: {name}, how long do you think a deleted folder goes unnoticed at a busy firm?\n\nLonger than you'd like. A backup nobody checks is like a spare tire that's been flat for a year.",
   "ask": "{name}, which single document proves Vance and Kettering has no claim on the fee?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check yourself, and I'm calling on a few of you.\n\nDid your first check name Liens and Reimbursement as missing? Did you put it back, and did the second check come back clean?\n\nAre all five lien documents in Liens and Reimbursement? The cost ledger and the closing letter in Wages and Costs? Does the number backed up match what you filed?\n\n{name}, read me your Case Note. What was lost, how you found it, what you restored.\n\nHere's the line to remember: a lien is only closed when its written answer is on the file and on the backup.",
   "ask": "{name}, a colleague recreates a deleted folder by hand, one letter off. What will Check my drive say, and what do you do?"
  }
 },
 "d3m4-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "This is the Final Accounting Check. Let's play it like a quiz show with only one rule: no peeking at your notes on question one. Your answers come from this file, in your own words, typed under each question.\n\nQuestion one is the big one. Take John Doe's $40,000.00 to his $20,359.92, stream by stream, with fee, costs and liens, and explain the 25% and 75% split.\n\nIf you can't do that without notes, the Net Sheet isn't finished in your head yet. That's okay. It just means we practice.\n\nQuestion two. Name the four document sets needed before the file is archived, the item on this file for each, and the retention period.\n\nQuestion three. After the 10/22/2026 disbursement the Trust Ledger reads $0.00. Which records prove it, and what would a leftover balance tell you?\n\n{name}, you look confident. You're up first, and I want it in your own words.\n\nIf you remember one thing: every dollar in has a dollar out, and a document that proves it.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, how does $40,000.00 become $20,359.92 down both streams, without looking at your notes?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the Self-Summary, in your own words: the principles behind the Net Sheet, the release audit and the Trust Ledger. Then your next action between the $0.00 ledger on 10/22/2026 and the 10/30/2026 closing memo, and why.\n\nBefore anyone writes, let's solve the mystery we've been carrying. A $1,540.00 balance, the gap between $3,400.00 and $1,860.00 on Crescent's injection, turns up on John Doe's account.\n\n{name}, lien we missed, or bill nobody owes? Look at Peach State's EOB before you answer, and tell me what it proves.\n\nI once had a client bring in a bill like that, convinced the whole settlement was ruined. Turned out a billing office hadn't applied the insurance write-off. Ten minutes on the phone, and it vanished.\n\nAnd keep the scenario on screen in mind. A deadline is never a reason to let John Doe sign a release that reaches $30,000 of UM coverage.\n\nThe takeaway: a scary bill is a question for the documents, not a reason to panic.",
   "ask": "{name}, is this $1,540.00 a lien we missed, or a bill nobody owes, and which document proves it?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Here's the Skill Scale for Net Sheets and closing. Score yourself 1 to 5 on each competency, from building a dual-stream Net Sheet that ties to the Trust Ledger, to holding the UPL boundary on releases, money and the client's questions.\n\nI'll put your rating next to your score, and any wide gap, high or low, is something we'll talk through together. As always, UPL is a gate, and a strong score doesn't survive an ethics breach.\n\nSubmit it for review when you're done, and well done on Track A, the version of John Doe's case that settled. Seriously, that was a lot of math.\n\nHere's what to carry out the door: the file isn't settled until the paper says so and the ledger reads zero.\n\nNow the mystery for the case that doesn't settle. Imagine Keystone had refused to pay its limit. No tender, no releases, no checks.\n\n{name}, predict for me: what's the first thing that changes on John Doe's file, and whose calendar does it land on? That's where we're headed, the road to court.",
   "ask": "{name}, looking back over Track A, which competency grew the most for you, and what's one thing you can now explain that you couldn't before?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Okay, four words to get straight, in plain English.\n\nFirst, impasse. It's not a feeling of being stuck. It's a stall the record actually proves.\n\nSecond, the stall itself. That's the adjuster's delay line, like “I'm waiting on authority,” which just means they're waiting for a boss to approve more money. It's built to wear you down. You log it, and you never accept it.\n\nThird, the rebuttal: a factual answer plus a firm reply date. And honestly, an unanswered rebuttal is a gift, because it becomes evidence of impasse.\n\nAlways ask the carrier to put its reasons in writing. You can answer a named reason. You can't answer a shrug.\n\nLast, the four elements every lawsuit has to prove: duty, breach, causation and damages. Did the driver owe care, did he fail, did that failure hurt John Doe, and what did it cost? Each one needs a checked exhibit behind it.\n\n{name}, which of the four do you think the defense goes after first on this file?\n\nYou can't answer a shrug, so get it in writing.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these four words have you heard before, and where would it show up on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Welcome to Day 5. Let's wake up with a quick game: guess the number. One insurance company on John Doe's file has a $10,000 limit. How much money do you think that one company can freeze?\n\nShout it out. {name}, you go first. {name}, can you beat that guess?\n\nOkay, the answer: $40,000. That's every dollar of coverage John Doe has.\n\nHere's how that happens. Yesterday, on Track A, Keystone paid its limit. Today we rewind to the same 08/05/2026 offer, and this time Keystone doesn't move. That's Track B, and we're living on it all day.\n\nSo Track A is the road where the case settles. Track B is the road where the insurer won't budge and we file suit. Same file, two endings, like a choose-your-own-adventure book with a filing fee.\n\nKeystone is sitting on the 14-Day Gap. Remember the order of operations from Day 2? The at-fault driver's coverage, BI, has to be used up before John Doe's own backup coverage, UM, opens. One stuck carrier locks the whole stack.\n\nAnd if you binge-watched courtroom shows to get ready, sorry. Nobody shouts “objection!” in this part. The drama is a date in a log.\n\nOne more thing. There's something Keystone never says that ends up proving our whole case. I'll tell you what it is before the day's out.\n\nOne stuck carrier can freeze the whole stack.",
   "ask": "{name}, if one $10,000 carrier can freeze $40,000, how careful do we need to be with every single date?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "This is where Track B splits off. Keystone holds at $3,500, calls it “final” on 08/12 and lets the demand lapse on 08/21.\n\nWe work the dispute path first, with evidence, new findings and the 09/02 supervisor escalation. We declare impasse only when its triggers are met, never out of frustration. By 09/04/2026, all five are on this file.\n\nNever bid against yourself. That means you don't lower our number just because they went quiet.\n\nAnd know what's at stake: $40,000 frozen behind a $10,000 carrier. The rules on using up coverage vary, though, so Atty. Thorne confirms.\n\nFiling suit is his decision. The inputs are yours: RPD-26-018842, Ruth Calloway, the doorbell video, the MRI, $16,401.40 in Specials, the SOL of 02/06/2028, cutoff 11/05/2027.\n\nRecord the lapse; don't interpret it. You never recommend suit, value the claim, judge Robert Smith's exposure, or advise John Doe on the $3,500.\n\nWhy so strict, {name}? Because one day a defense lawyer will read every line you wrote, out loud, to a room.\n\nRecord the facts. Atty. Thorne decides what they mean.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone hasn't said no. It has said nothing. When does silence become an impasse, and who decides that it has?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So here's how this runs on a regular Tuesday. An offer gets repeated, or a demand deadline passes. That's a trigger, and you log it in the CMS Communications Log with its date and its proof.\n\nEvery trigger is dated and tied to a document. “Called Keystone, no answer” is the bare minimum, and it's useless. What you want is the date, the time, the method, who you tried, and the email attached.\n\nThen the follow-ups go unanswered. You escalate to the adjuster's supervisor through the Email Workspace, and you send no new number. That escalation asks for Keystone's written position and evidence.\n\nWhen the escalation window closes in silence, you build the impasse record and the filing inputs. Send it to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate, with the SOL and our internal dates on it.\n\nThink about where this ends up. If the case is filed, that log becomes the story a judge reads. A clean one says Keystone stalled. A messy one says maybe we did.\n\n{name}, which one do you want your name on?\n\nIf it isn't dated and proved, it didn't happen.",
   "ask": "{name}, when the supervisor escalation goes unanswered, why do we send the record to counsel instead of trying one more follow-up?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Okay, here's the job. You're drafting the impasse memo to Atty. Elijah Thorne from the firm's memo template in the CMS, under Documents. You pull proof from the Communications Log and send it through Tasks, Escalate.\n\nStart with a dated chronology from the 07/20/2026 demand through 09/04, every event tied to its proof. State Keystone's reasons and the rebuttal evidence it never answered.\n\nShow the frozen coverage layer by layer: $30,000 of the $40,000 locked behind BI, with the documented Specials, which are the Medical Bills and costs we can prove. Then the clock: SOL 02/06/2028, hard date 02/04/2028, cutoff 11/05/2027.\n\nEnd with the decisions you need from him, written as questions. That's the graded judgment. The memo documents and asks. It recommends nothing, because filing is his call.\n\n{name}, you're building the chronology. {name}, you're checking every proof.\n\nAnd keep it boring. On TV, the lawyer finds the smoking gun at midnight. In real life, the smoking gun is a dated email you logged in August.\n\nThe memo documents and asks. It never recommends.",
   "ask": "{name}, what is the one thing this memo must not contain?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Something just landed on the file, dated 09/08/2026. Before I read it, {name}, guess who it's from: Keystone, John Doe, or Atty. Thorne?\n\nIt's Atty. Thorne: “Got your 09/04 escalation task. Keystone has said nothing since the 09/02 supervisor escalation. Before I decide anything, send me the full impasse memo today.”\n\nQuick background. Keystone, that's Dana Whitfield on claim KM-26-440871, offered $3,500 on 08/05. They pointed to the 14-Day Gap and a “degenerative” MRI, meaning they say it's wear and tear.\n\nOur 08/06 rebuttal sent Dr. Helen Park's read, Dr. Reyes's 06/19/2026 Causation Letter, the EMS report and the Gap Explanation Record.\n\nOn 08/12 Keystone called $3,500 “final.” The demand lapsed 08/21. Follow-ups on 08/24 and 08/31 got nothing, and so did the 09/02 escalation.\n\nSo before you write a single sentence, build your chronology, which is just a list of every event in date order. Trust me on this one. The dates are the memo.\n\nThe chronology comes first, because the dates are the argument.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you draft anything, which event on this chronology are you least sure you can prove?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, let's check it hard, and yes, I'm calling on people. {name}, does your chronology run 07/20 through 09/04 in order, with a receipt, a sent email or a log entry behind every event? An attempt with no proof is a claim, not a record.\n\nQuote Keystone's reasons exactly as Keystone gave them, and list the unanswered rebuttal.\n\nShow the whole stack: $10,000 BI not used up, $20,000 Host UM and $10,000 Relative UM closed. Host UM is the backup coverage on the car he was riding in. Relative UM is the backup on the policy of a relative he lives with.\n\nIf your memo says “$10,000 available, UM pending,” fix it. Specials are $14,817.40 plus $1,584.00, so $16,401.40. Clock dates are right, sent Tuesday 09/08 after Labor Day.\n\nYour decisions are questions, with no recommendation and no new number to Keystone. A so-so memo tells him what happened. A great one lets him decide in five minutes.\n\nWrite every date as if a defense lawyer will read it aloud.\n\nNow the mystery. If this goes to suit, a defense lawyer reads your memo's dates too. {name}, which one date would they attack first?",
   "ask": "{name}, does any sentence in your memo read like advice instead of a question for Atty. Thorne?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Let's make the lawsuit simple. Filing the Complaint, the paper that tells the court what happened and what we want, is what formally starts the case. From that moment, we're done negotiating and into court rules.\n\nCounsel writes the legal arguments. You check the facts underneath them.\n\nBefore counsel signs, you confirm three things. The Statute Date, which is the last day we're allowed to sue. The defendant's exact legal name. And, for a business, its Registered Agent, the person who officially accepts legal papers for it.\n\nLater, when we get to arbitration, a group like AAA or JAMS sends a strike list, which is a list of possible arbitrators. Each side checks them, ranks them and crosses names off before a deadline.\n\nIf your idea of court comes from TV, where a surprise witness bursts through the doors, I've got bad news. In real life the surprise is a misspelled name that gets the whole paper sent back.\n\n{name}, which of those three checks would you double-check twice?\n\nCounsel argues the law. You prove the facts underneath.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these pre-filing checks have you seen before, and where would John Doe's file need one?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "First, the mystery from last lesson. {name}, what did Keystone never say?\n\nIt never said no. It just went silent. And silence, dated and logged, is what proved the impasse. Nicely done if you got there.\n\nNow a quick game: fill in the blank. On 09/10/2026, before anything is filed, the case-number field on John Doe's file should say blank. Shout your answer.\n\nAnything but “Pending” is a guess, and a guessed number ends up on a court paper.\n\nSo what's the big deal about this lesson? Once Atty. Thorne authorizes suit, a court clock replaces the adjuster's clock. The status, the court fields, the folders and the calendar all have to change together.\n\nIf they don't, you've got a lawsuit running on pre-lawsuit habits while real court deadlines tick away.\n\nAnd a teaser for later. This file is going to meet two neutrals, people who aren't on anyone's side, and one of them has a power the other doesn't. I'll tell you which by the end.\n\nNew clock, new habits, all switched at once.",
   "ask": "{name}, on 09/10/2026, before anything is filed, what do you type in the case-number field?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Status changes to Litigation only on Atty. Thorne's dated authorization, and the change cites it.\n\nThe fee follows the filing: 33⅓% before suit, 40% once suit is filed.\n\nFill court fields only from the record. Until filing, the number reads “Pending.” Build the litigation folders at the switch, DOE, JOHN - CASE FILE, with folders 01 through 05, ready for the 09/24/2026 filing.\n\nName every PDF properly, never scan001.pdf. I once inherited a shared drive with a file called final_FINAL_v3_USE THIS ONE, and right next to it, final_FINAL_v4. Nobody knew which one went to court.\n\nDocket deadlines the day a document arrives. Seed the calendar: SOL 02/06/2028, hard date 02/04/2028, cutoff 11/05/2027, and 48-hour audits.\n\nNotify the UM carriers in writing as Atty. Thorne directs, with no number. Keep the doorbell video unaltered, and ask others to preserve what they hold.\n\nOne more: a binding award generally can't be appealed. What any of this means for John Doe is Atty. Thorne's to explain, {name}, not ours.\n\nNothing on a court paper is ever a guess.",
   "ask": "{name}, which one moves John Doe to the 40% tier, the status change or the filing, and why does that matter for the dates?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it plays out at your desk. Atty. Thorne authorizes suit, and that's your trigger.\n\nYou change the status and open the court fields in Case Summary and Litigation, citing his dated authorization, with the case number still “Pending.”\n\nThen you build the master folder and all five sub-folders under Documents, named exactly to the standard. Then you seed the Litigation Calendar with the SOL, the internal hard date, the cutoff and the 48-hour audits.\n\nLater, when a strike list or a proposed neutral arrives, you pull their résumés and past awards for Atty. Thorne and docket the strike deadline the same day. The strikes themselves are his call.\n\nDoing all this over three days, in between other things, is how files end up half-switched. Do it in one sitting, start to finish.\n\nAnd if the folder tool freezes halfway, put in your IT ticket, but don't wait on it. Honestly, IT tickets have their own statute of limitations, {name}, and it's longer than ours.\n\nSwitch the whole file in one sitting, or don't start.",
   "ask": "{name}, a strike list lands on your desk. What do you do before the end of that day?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You're working live in the CMS: Case Summary, Litigation, Documents, Outgoing, and the Calendar.\n\nSet the status on the 09/09 authorization. Open the court fields without guessing a case number. Record the fee tier exactly as the retainer sets it. Build the five litigation folders to firm standard.\n\nDraft both UM notices and the preservation requests for the doorbell video and the Escape's event data recorder, all for his approval. Open the ADR fields, the ones for mediation and arbitration, and seed the Litigation Calendar with 48-hour audits.\n\nWhat you hand in is a file in Litigation status where every field, folder, notice and date rests on Atty. Thorne's authority. The graded part is what those notices may and may not say.\n\n{name}, you're on status and fields. {name}, you're on the notices.\n\nAnd if the scanner jams while you're filing the authorization, it's not personal. That scanner jams for everyone. It just likes an audience.\n\nNotices state the facts and carry no number.",
   "ask": "{name}, Marcus Bell calls after reading your notice and asks what you're demanding. What do you say?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "This one landed on 09/10/2026, and honestly, it's the moment the TV shows skip.\n\nFrom Atty. Thorne: “Yesterday I authorized suit against Robert Smith in the State Court of Riverton County, filing target 09/24/2026, and went over the fee tier with John. Transition the file today.”\n\n{name}, before I go on, guess how many separate things that one sentence means you have to change. More than you'd think.\n\nHe wants impasse notices drafted to Harbor Point, that's Marcus Bell on HP-26-7741, and to Cardinal State, Teresa Okafor on CS-26-20355. He also wants preservation requests, letters asking people not to delete or destroy evidence, for the doorbell video and the Escape's data recorder, the car's built-in black box. All of it is for his signature.\n\nKeep in mind that Harbor Point's UM policy has an arbitration clause, the video copy was secured on 02/19/2026, and the 2020 Ford Escape is Kevin Alvarez's.\n\nWe'll run this as a live CMS session. As you make each change, I want to hear the authority behind it, out loud.\n\nEvery change you make needs an authority you can name.",
   "ask": "{name}, when you change the status, what exactly will you say is the authority behind it?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Okay, run down your work before you log it. Status is Litigation, dated 09/10/2026, citing the 09/09 authorization. The court is the State Court of Riverton County, case number “Pending, assigned on filing.”\n\nFee tier: 40% from the filing date, not 09/10. Note his talk with John Doe without explaining it.\n\nFive folders, named exactly. Both notices state impasse and intended suit, carry their own claim number, have no dollar figure, and go for approval.\n\nNow preservation, the step people skip because the video was secured in February. Ask Margaret Doe to keep what she still holds, and draft the data recorder request to Kevin Alvarez and Harbor Point. Flag the arbitration clause and UM deadlines as questions.\n\nCalendar: the 09/24 filing, the 09/22 audit, plus the SOL dates.\n\nFlipping the status isn't a transition. A real transition makes sure nothing that proves our case can quietly disappear.\n\nEvery notice goes out on his authority, never ours.\n\nMystery for the room: {name}, what evidence could still be lost even though we already have a copy?",
   "ask": "{name}, what does Margaret Doe still hold today, and who holds the Escape's event data recorder?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-efile": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Four moves. First, tag Nadia Brooks on the Litigation Desk with a task she can act on without calling you: the cover sheet and the summons, built from the Complaint he approved this morning, due tonight, routed to Atty. Thorne for signature. He signs on the twenty-third.\n\nSecond, on the twenty-fourth, you build the envelope in Court E-Filing, the court's online filing system: the caption, the court, the three documents, the filing attorney and the two hundred eighteen dollar fee. Open every page to check his signature first.\n\nThird, when it's accepted, you record the case number, file the stamped Complaint, the summons and the conference notice, and post the fee.\n\nAnd yes, check your spam folder for the acceptance email. Court systems love sending the most important message of the month straight to junk.\n\nFourth, you calendar what the filing starts, and you hold what it doesn't. {name}, you're Nadia; {name}, you're writing her the task.\n\nIf Nadia has to call you, the task wasn't finished.",
   "ask": "{name}, which deadline can you calendar the day you file, which one has to wait, and why?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Show of hands: who's ever sent an important email and then found the reply sitting in spam three days later? Keep them up. {name}, that's a lot of hands, and that's exactly why we check.\n\nIt's the twenty-second of September twenty twenty-six, and we're on Track B, the litigation track, the road where the case goes to court.\n\nThe file went to Litigation on the tenth, on Atty. Thorne's authorization, and the filing target is the twenty-fourth. So today is our forty-eight-hour audit, our two-day check that everything's ready before we file.\n\nThis morning he approved his Complaint against Robert Smith in the State Court of Riverton County. The Complaint is the document that starts the lawsuit, the starter's pistol.\n\nWhat we don't have yet is the civil cover sheet and the summons, the court's notice telling Robert Smith he's being sued.\n\nAnd there's a trap in the CMS: an older draft from the fifteenth, stamped not for filing, unsigned, no case number. Once the envelope is accepted, the fee tier moves to forty percent and the court's clocks start.\n\nTwo Complaints, one court. Only one of them should ever leave this building.",
   "ask": "{name}, two Complaints sit in the CMS today. In your own words, how will you make sure the right one reaches the court?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work against this, and {name}, you're grading the person next to you. Your task to Nadia names the documents, the approved Complaint, the due date and Atty. Thorne as signer.\n\nNothing went in unsigned, and the September fifteenth draft never left the CMS. The envelope was accepted on the twenty-fourth as twenty-six CV zero four four one seven, and that number replaced Pending in the court fields.\n\nThree named PDFs sit in zero one pleadings, and costs read seven hundred four dollars seventy-five.\n\nThe service deadline, the last day to formally deliver the lawsuit to Robert Smith, is January twenty-second, twenty twenty-seven, a Friday, a hundred and twenty days out, with alerts. Allied Process Services has its hand-off.\n\nThe conference is December tenth at nine, with Atty. Thorne attending. And the answer date says set on service, because his clock can't start until he's actually handed the papers.\n\nA case number is just the start. The real work is every clock that number started.\n\nYour mystery for the next block: Allied hasn't served him yet. {name}, predict it: what happens to our calendar if they can't find Robert Smith?",
   "ask": "{name}, if the answer date had been calendared today, what would it have been counted from, and why is that wrong?"
  }
 },
 "d4m1-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Learning Check time, game-show edition. You answer from this file, in your own words, typed right under each question.\n\nHere's the twist. After you type, I'm picking people to read their answer out loud, and the room votes thumbs up or thumbs down.\n\n{name}, you're first up on question 1, because it's the foundation. Explain the fork: what's the same on Track B as on Track A, what changes on 08/12/2026, and why a $10,000 carrier freezes all $40,000.\n\nIf you can't explain that last part in plain words, flag me. We'll go back over Day 2's order of operations together before moving on. No shame in it.\n\nAnd no, “because the insurance company said so” doesn't get a thumbs up. That's the answer the courtroom shows give, right before the commercial break.\n\nIf you can explain it to John, you understand it.",
   "ask": "{name}, in one sentence of your own, why does $10,000 freeze $40,000?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Next up is your Self-Summary: the principles in your own words, not mine.\n\nThen work the scenario. It's 09/04/2026, Keystone has ignored two follow-ups and the 48-hour escalation, and John Doe calls asking why nothing's happening and whether he should just take the $3,500. Tell me what you say, what you don't say, and what goes to Atty. Thorne today.\n\nThe weak answer tries to make him feel better with a guess. The strong one takes the question down, tells John it's going to Atty. Thorne today, and gives no view on the $3,500 and no explanation of the fee.\n\nThis matters down the road. A client who hears a guess from us remembers it as a promise, and every offer after that feels like a betrayal.\n\nI once had a client quote back, word for word, something a Case Manager said in passing eight months earlier. Clients don't forget.\n\n{name}, read me your first sentence to John.\n\nClients remember guesses as promises, so never guess.",
   "ask": "{name}, when John asks if he should take the $3,500, what are the first words out of your mouth?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step: the Skill Scale. Rate yourself on each skill from 1 to 5, honestly, then submit it for review.\n\nI'll compare your self-rating against your score. If there's a wide gap either way, we'll talk it through, no big deal.\n\nOne thing to remember, though. UPL, giving legal advice when you're not a lawyer, is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive an ethics slip.\n\nNow your mystery. The impasse memo you wrote is the first page of a lawsuit file now, and that file is headed for depositions, discovery and a defense lawyer who reads everything.\n\nYour memo is now evidence, so read it like the other side would.\n\n{name}, look back at your chronology and find the one line a defense lawyer could twist. Bring it to the next Learning Check, and we'll see if the room can defend it.",
   "ask": "{name}, where do you expect your self-rating and your score to disagree most?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Once suit is filed, the whole game changes. Statute, rule and court order set the dates, and your documents stop being paperwork and start being evidence.\n\nA missing or misfiled page isn't a little oops anymore. It can hurt a trial.\n\nLitigation runs on court processes you'll repeat every single week, each one driven by the calendar. Lots of steps have a built-in wait, like the notice we give opposing counsel before a subpoena to a third party goes out. You put that wait on the calendar just like a deadline.\n\nPicture two Case Managers. One says, “The records office said they'd send it by the 15th, so that's on them.” The other calls them five days ahead, because she knows their miss becomes her problem.\n\n{name}, be honest: which of those two is still employed in a year?\n\nIn suit, their miss becomes your problem.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these court habits will feel most different from how you ran John Doe's file before suit?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game to start: Beat the Calendar. I write a date on the board, and the first person to tell me the day of the week wins bragging rights. Phones down, no cheating.\n\nHere it is: 02/06/2028. That's John Doe's statute of limitations, which is just the last day the law lets us file. {name}, your guess?\n\nIt's a Sunday. And that matters more than you'd think.\n\nZoom out for a second. Keystone's stonewall on the 14-Day Gap pushed this file onto court time. From 09/24/2026 on, its dates are set by rule, order and statute, and they don't move because a carrier goes quiet or a client can't be reached.\n\nCourt deadlines are like that one relative who shows up to Thanksgiving whether you invited them or not. They do not care that it's your weekend.\n\nAnd there are two orange pins hiding behind that Sunday. Before this lesson's done, you'll know why those two dates, not the Sunday, are the ones we live by.\n\nCourt dates don't move, so we move first.",
   "ask": "{name}, why should a Sunday statute of limitations make a Case Manager nervous instead of relieved?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you calendar from it or put it in a binder, let's break down the Complaint, the paper that turns a claim into a lawsuit. {name}, quick guess: who writes it?\n\nAtty. Thorne drafts it and signs it, never us.\n\nIt has a caption, which is the heading with the court and the parties. Then jurisdiction, the facts, the causes of action, meaning the legal reasons we're suing, and a prayer for relief, which is just what we're asking the court for.\n\nLegally, its job is to preserve the claim. Filed before the SOL, it puts negligence and negligence per se against Robert Smith in front of the court. As evidence, it fixes what's pleaded.\n\nOurs was filed 09/24/2026 as No. 26-CV-04417. It pleads bodily-injury damages only, $14,817.40 in medical and $1,584.00 in wages, with no Property Damage, because John Doe owns no vehicle.\n\nYour part: verify the filing date against the SOL and Smith's exact legal name, file the stamped copy in 01_PLEADINGS, and always use the filed version, never the pre-filing draft.\n\nA draft in the binder is like bringing the rehearsal script to opening night.\n\nOnly the filed version is the real one.",
   "ask": "{name}, why doesn't John Doe's Complaint plead any Property Damage for the Civic?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the counting rules, and honestly, this is where good Case Managers turn into great ones.\n\nA hard rule is a date fixed by statute, court rule, order or the forum's rules. Miss one and the claim can go. Every date here is set for this training file, and Atty. Thorne confirms the real rule.\n\nLeave out the trigger day, count the last one. Robert Smith was served 10/14/2026, so his answer was due 11/13/2026.\n\nThen there's the mailbox trap. This file's rules add 3 days for mail, though e-service adds nothing. Forward counts roll to the next business day; backward counts roll earlier.\n\nRequests for Admission, or RFAs, the written questions they want us to admit, get their own entry, because unanswered ones can be treated as admitted.\n\nAnd that Sunday SOL? It becomes an internal hard date of 02/04/2028 and a cutoff of 11/05/2027. There's the reveal: those are the two orange pins.\n\nWhen you're unsure, docket the earlier date and flag it. You compute and docket, {name}. You never pick the rule or agree to an extension.\n\nWhen in doubt, docket the earlier date and flag it.",
   "ask": "{name}, discovery was mailed to us on 12/01/2026. Count it out loud: when are John Doe's responses due?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Picture the week of 12/10/2026. The Scheduling Order, the court's official timeline for the case, gets entered. Skimming it and typing in the trial date is the trap.\n\nWhat you actually do, that same day, is enter every date in Court E-Filing with its trigger, rule, owner and alerts. Read each verb, so “completed by” goes in as a deadline. Court E-Filing is the firm's official calendar of deadlines, each with its rule, owner and reminders.\n\nThe discovery that came by mail on 12/01/2026? You count 30 plus 3, see it lands on a Sunday, and move it forward.\n\nYou enter the RFAs separately in the Conflict Resolver, so both sit at 01/04/2027 with the deemed-admitted flag set. That's the CMS screen where you check every calendar entry against the paper it came from and fix the ones keyed wrong.\n\nOn SOL review, 02/04/2028 and 11/05/2027 go on the CMS Calendar, and nothing relies on 02/06/2028. When arbitration is invoked on 04/05/2027, you hard-code 06/02, 06/11 and 06/22/2027 with alerts that same day.\n\nWhy bother, {name}? A deemed admission can hand the other side a fact we'd have fought the whole case, and that shows up in the settlement number.\n\nEnter every date the day its paper arrives.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do the RFAs get their own entry instead of riding along with the rest of the discovery?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. You'll audit John Doe's calendar in the Case Docket and Calendar Conflict Resolver, against the 12/10/2026 order, the discovery mailed 12/01/2026 and the 02/06/2028 SOL. That's the screen where every date gets checked against the document it came from.\n\nRecompute every entry from its trigger document: trigger day out, 3 mail days in, forward counts to the next business day, backward to the earlier one. Correct the errors, add what's missing, and leave correct entries alone.\n\nEach entry gets its trigger, rule, math, owner and alerts, mirrored to the CMS Calendar. Rank errors by risk, log corrections in Case Notes and send them to Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, you're on the discovery dates. {name}, you've got the SOL.\n\nHeads up: Jenna Price, Atty. Mercer's paralegal, is going to call. She'll be lovely. She'll also ask for things. Agree to nothing, log her words exactly, and send it up today.\n\nEvery date gets recomputed from its own trigger.",
   "ask": "{name}, 12/31/2026 is wrong and 11/08/2027 is wrong. Which one could cost John Doe his claim?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Game first: Spot the Liar. I'm going to show you a calendar, and some of these dates are lying to you. {name}, before you look closely, how many lies do you think are hiding in there? Write your guess down.\n\nIt's 12/10/2026, and here's what just landed from Atty. Thorne.\n\n“The Scheduling Order was entered today; it's attached with the discovery Mercer mailed us on 12/01. While you were out, the calendar was re-keyed from the paper file: nothing verified, no alerts. Audit every entry against the order, the discovery and the SOL, correct what's wrong, and send me the correction log.”\n\nThe case is No. 26-CV-04417. The discovery is 25 Interrogatories, 30 Requests for Production and 15 RFAs.\n\nThe order sets plaintiff's expert disclosure 03/15/2027, defense 04/15/2027, mediation completed by 03/31/2027, discovery closing 05/28/2027, pretrial 08/12/2027, trial 09/13/2027.\n\nBefore you recompute anything, initial the entries you'd sign your name to. Just those.\n\nTrust nothing on a calendar you haven't checked against its paper.",
   "ask": "{name}, how many errors do you think are hiding on this calendar?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Here's what your audit should show before you log it. {name}, pull out your guess from the start and compare.\n\nResponses corrected to Monday 01/04/2027, since 12/01 plus 30 plus 3 lands on Sunday 01/03. The RFA entry added, all 15 due 01/04/2027, flagged deemed admitted if unanswered.\n\nThat RFA one is the one people miss, because they fix what's on the screen and not what isn't. And it's the nearest, most severe deadline here.\n\nExpert disclosures un-swapped. Mediation entered as completed by 03/31/2027. Internal hard date 02/04/2028 added. Filing cutoff corrected from 11/08/2027 to 11/05/2027, rolled earlier, never forward. Correct entries verified and left alone. Errors ranked, rights ahead of time.\n\nMany courts accept a Monday filing after a Sunday deadline, but the firm won't rely on it. And calling opposing counsel about an extension fails the authority check.\n\nDeadlines don't care that it's a holiday weekend, and neither does a deemed admission.\n\nWhat's missing is more dangerous than what's wrong.\n\nMystery for the next activity: the arbitration brings its own dates. {name}, which one lands on a weekend?",
   "ask": "{name}, did you recompute each date from its trigger document, or from the entry you were checking?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-medcal": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll work it in the Calendaring Simulators and mirror everything to the CMS Calendar.\n\nVerify the trigger first, meaning the document the deadline is counted from. The notice beat the order's 30-day rule, and 03/18 sits 13 days inside 03/31. So link that existing deadline and close the windows task instead of keying either again. Two entries for one event is how a calendar starts lying to you.\n\nEnter the session with its attendees. Then count back: binder due 03/11, Atty. Thorne's approval by 03/09, the binder QC on 03/04, the Litigation Desk's draft index on 03/02. Count forward to the mediator's report on 03/25.\n\nGet John's written confirmation and set his reminders. Open a cost task for the $1,200.00.\n\nAnd flag for Atty. Thorne that Harbor Point and Cardinal State must consent in writing before any BI settlement, because settling without the UM carriers' consent can put John's UM coverage at risk.\n\n{name}, you're counting back out loud. {name}, you're checking each count against the calendar.\n\nOne event, one entry, every step counted from it.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Keystone tenders its limits at the table on 03/18, what can't happen until both UM carriers answer?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Guessing game before I read you the notice. One piece of paper lands on the file. How many dates does it create? Shout a number.\n\n{name}, you go first. {name}, you go second. Closest wins bragging rights.\n\nIt's 01/08/2027, a Friday, and the Notice of Mediation was filed this morning. Mediation is the meeting where a neutral person, the mediator, helps both sides try to settle.\n\nHon. Carla Benton (Ret.), the mediator Atty. Thorne picked from the four windows we collected on 12/17, will see us on Thursday 03/18/2027 at 9:30 a.m., in person at her office.\n\nGetting a retired judge's calendar to agree with three lawyers' calendars is its own small miracle, so we don't waste it.\n\nThe statement and binder are due 7 days before, and John Doe has to be there. The order of referral from 12/10 says more: each party attends, lead counsel attends, and Keystone sends someone with full authority to settle. The fees are split equally, and our half is $1,200.00.\n\nOur 12/10 audit already put \"completed by 03/31/2027\" on the calendar.\n\nSo, the answer? More than any of you guessed. One notice, a whole family of dates.",
   "ask": "{name}, how many separate dates do you think this one notice creates on John Doe's file?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Here's what your calendar should show, and I'm going round the room.\n\nThe session once, on Thursday 03/18/2027 at 9:30 a.m., linked to the 03/31 deadline rather than duplicating it. Statement and binder on Thursday 03/11, no roll needed. Approval by Tuesday 03/09, QC on Thursday 03/04, draft index on Tuesday 03/02. The mediator's report watched on 03/25.\n\nJohn's confirmation requested today with a tickler on 01/15. The fee as half of $2,400.00, which takes advanced costs from $989.75 to $2,189.75, but only once the invoice is receipted.\n\nThe UM flag with its arithmetic: 30 days from a 03/18 notice runs to Saturday 04/17.\n\nPutting the mediation on the calendar is the easy part. A great calendar has every step that gets us there ready.\n\nNow the mystery, {name}. There's one conflict hiding in these dates. Our expert disclosure on Monday 03/15 lands between binder and session. Who needs to hear about that, and before which date?",
   "ask": "{name}, which entry on your calendar did you count from another entry instead of from its trigger document?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-um": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Let's name the two shapes in plain words. A third-party claim is against the at-fault driver's insurance: Keystone, for Robert Smith.\n\nA first-party claim is against your own insurance, the policy you pay for. Here that's Harbor Point for Host UM and Cardinal State for Relative UM.\n\nUM, by the way, is uninsured or underinsured motorist coverage. It's the part of a policy that pays when the other driver's insurance isn't enough.\n\nNow the paragraph I promised: the mandatory arbitration clause. It's a line in the policy that sends a dispute to an arbitrator, a private decision-maker, so the firm files a Demand for Arbitration instead of a lawsuit.\n\nAnd off to one side sits insurance bad faith. That's a separate suit about the carrier handling a claim unfairly, and it can reach above the policy limit. It's Atty. Thorne's territory, start to finish.\n\n{name}, which of these four would you least want to explain to John on the fly?\n\nThird party is their insurance. First party is his own.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would a carrier rather have a UM dispute heard by an arbitrator than by a jury?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Three steps here, and I want you to see the third one as a fork in the road.\n\nStep one, claim submission. The UM Demand Package goes out with proof that the at-fault limits were used up. On this file that went to Harbor Point and Cardinal State on the twenty-second of March twenty twenty-seven.\n\nStep two, dispute. The carrier offers far under value, or denies liability or coverage. Eight thousand from Harbor Point, six thousand from Cardinal State. {name}, quick math: what's that together?\n\nFourteen thousand, against thirty thousand in limits. That's like being handed the cracker plate at a conference-room lunch and told it's the buffet.\n\nStep three is the resolution route, and here the road splits. It's a Demand for Arbitration where the policy's clause binds, or a lawsuit against the carrier where state law bars that clause or lets the client opt out.\n\nBad faith isn't on this flow at all. It's a separate action, and it's Atty. Thorne's call alone.\n\nThe policy picks the road, not us.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those three steps is the one where a Case Manager can do real damage by guessing?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick round of Who Am I Fighting? I name an insurance company. You point left if it's the other driver's carrier, right if it's John's own. Ready?\n\nKeystone. Harbor Point. Cardinal State.\n\n{name}, I saw you point both ways on that last one. I see you.\n\nOkay, the answer. Keystone insured Robert Smith, the man who ran the stop sign, so that claim is against somebody else's carrier. Harbor Point and Cardinal State are different. They're John's own coverage, under contracts he can call on, and that changes what this file is.\n\nWhen Harbor Point came back with eight thousand dollars against a twenty thousand dollar limit, we didn't just head for court like the bodily injury claim would have.\n\nThere's one paragraph buried in Harbor Point's policy that decides where this fight happens. Reading it is our job before a single date goes on the calendar, and I'll show you which paragraph in a minute.\n\nKnow who you're fighting before you pick the battlefield.",
   "ask": "{name}, what changes about a claim when the insurance company on the other end is our own client's?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. First, know which claim you're on, because the evidence is the same but the relationship isn't.\n\nSecond, John owns no car, so no policy is his. The Bluewater policy is his mother Margaret's, on her parked Civic, and she rejected its UM in writing in March of twenty twenty-four. He reaches UM by status instead, as a passenger in Kevin Alvarez's Escape and as a relative living with Margaret.\n\nThird, and {name}, say this one back to me later: the route is read, never chosen. State law and the clause in that policy decide it.\n\nFourth, read each layer on its own. Harbor Point's clause doesn't speak for Cardinal State. It's tempting to see “UM” on both covers and slap the same label on both.\n\nI knew a Case Manager who'd label-make anything that stood still, the stapler, the plant, her lunch, and she still filed two policies under one clause.\n\nAnd every route has its own clock, which is not the February twenty twenty-eight statute of limitations. Ask Atty. Thorne in writing, and docket only from his answer.\n\nThe route is read, never chosen.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Harbor Point's policy has an arbitration clause, what do we actually know about Cardinal State's?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's the flow as it actually ran on John's file. {name}, before I show you the numbers, guess how much John's medical Specials, his provable Medical Bills, came to.\n\nThe reveal: sixteen thousand four hundred and one dollars forty.\n\nClaim submission, the twenty-second of March twenty twenty-seven. The demands carried the injuries, the records, those Specials, the wage letter, and the documented proof that Keystone's ten thousand was tendered, meaning offered up in full, and consented to.\n\nDispute: eight thousand on the thirty-first of March, six thousand on the second of April. Fourteen thousand offered against thirty thousand of limits.\n\nResolution route: Harbor Point invoked its arbitration clause, it was taken up in April twenty twenty-seven, and the hearing ran on the twenty-second of June before Laura Whitcomb.\n\nAnd the last row. Margaret's Bluewater policy on the Civic is not a route. She rejected its UM in writing, and John owns no car of his own.\n\nProve each step before you argue the next one.",
   "ask": "{name}, which document on this file proves step one was complete before step two could even be argued?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "On the job, this comes down to four triggers.\n\nWhen the UM offers come back low, you write the impasse memo, the note that says we're stuck and why, the same day. You pull both carriers' dispute clauses from the certified policies, quoted, not interpreted.\n\nWhen the clauses and the statute are in hand, you lay the route options before Atty. Thorne as findings, with each layer's own deadline put to him as a question.\n\nWhen he decides in writing, you record it: the ADR entry typed as UM slash UIM arbitration, set as required by the policy, and the case status matching the route.\n\nAnd when arbitration is invoked, you docket the hearing and everything counted back from it. You tell John what happens procedurally and nothing more.\n\nSo what's the payoff, {name}? A clean route decision gets John in front of a decision-maker months sooner, and months matter when the money's sitting with the carrier.\n\nQuote the clause, find the facts, and let him decide.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those four steps do trainees skip most, and what does skipping it cost John?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two habits to keep. Say which kind of claim you're on before you say anything else about it, because first party or third party decides everything that follows.\n\nAnd quote the clause in your memo, then stop. A paraphrase is already an interpretation, and interpretation isn't yours.\n\nThree things to avoid. Don't file, or tell the client the firm is filing, a lawsuit when the policy sends that dispute to arbitration.\n\nDon't read Harbor Point's clause onto Cardinal State's policy just because both say UM on the front.\n\nAnd don't tell John the carrier is acting in bad faith or that he can recover above his limits, however much you want to. I know. It's tempting.\n\nQuote it, then stop.\n\nNow your mystery. Cardinal State's certified policy hasn't arrived yet. {name}, until it does, what are we allowed to say about how Cardinal State's dispute gets decided, and what has to happen first?",
   "ask": "{name}, why is the bad-faith line the most tempting one to cross on a file like this?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "First, where these sessions sit. Before a lawsuit is filed, mediation usually comes after treatment and the demand, and arbitration comes only where a signed clause forces it.\n\nAfter filing, courts often order mediation during discovery, the stage where both sides swap evidence. Then the parties can agree to binding arbitration or go to trial. Both of John Doe's sessions sit on the filed side of that line.\n\nNow two words, in plain language. Non-binding, which is mediation: both sides have to agree, so nobody is forced to settle and the parties keep control.\n\nBinding, which is arbitration: the decision is final and enforced like a court order, and a court reviews it only on very narrow grounds.\n\nYou can explain those two words to John. What either one does to his claim is Atty. Thorne's to tell him.\n\nOkay, {name}, try the plain version on me.\n\nNon-binding means both agree. Binding means it's final.",
   "ask": "{name}, say it to me as you would to John Doe: what does non-binding mean, and what does binding mean?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Let's line the two up, row by row. The mediator's role is facilitator and negotiator. The arbitrator is a private judge and finder of fact.\n\nDecision power? The mediator has zero. She can't force a settlement, rule or order a payout. The arbitrator has total power and issues a binding Arbitration Award.\n\nControl of the outcome stays with the parties at mediation, since they decide whether to sign. In arbitration, the arbitrator decides liability and damages and can keep evidence out.\n\nReview afterward? Mediation needs none, because there's no ruling. Arbitration gets court review on narrow grounds only.\n\nAnd here's the reveal I promised. The mistake is preparing for both the same way. One needs a compromise focus, the other needs trial-ready.\n\nWalk a mediation-quality file into an arbitration and it's like showing up to a job interview in your pajamas because the last meeting was on Zoom.\n\n{name}, which column are we in next?\n\nPrep for the table you're actually sitting at.",
   "ask": "{name}, on John Doe's file, which of these two columns is he heading into next, and how do you know?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Game time: Referee or Coach? I describe a person, you shout referee if they decide, coach if they only help.\n\nA judge at trial. A marriage counselor. A soccer ref. {name}, you're the tiebreaker: a mediator? Hold your answer.\n\nTwo names, side by side: Hon. Carla Benton (Ret.) and Laura Whitcomb, Esq.\n\nJudge Benton is our mediator, and she's a coach. She can't decide anything. She can only help the parties agree, and any deal needs John's agreement, through Atty. Thorne.\n\nLaura Whitcomb is the arbitrator, and she's the referee. She decides, and her award stands with almost no appeal.\n\nThese two sessions are three months apart, and here's what I want you to sit with. There's one mistake that happens all the time when a file moves from the first table to the second, and it can quietly sink a hearing.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll know what it is.\n\nA coach helps you win. A referee decides who did.",
   "ask": "{name}, what can Hon. Carla Benton do to John Doe's case that Laura Whitcomb can't, and the other way around?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Mediation is guided compromise: non-binding and confidential under the governing rule. Court-ordered means John Doe must attend, not agree.\n\nBinding arbitration, like Harbor Point's clause, makes the arbitrator the judge, with narrow court review. Here, BI settled at mediation on 03/18/2027, and the UM carriers' fight over the gap went to a decision.\n\nKnow the limits. Damages are reduced first by the $10,000 BI paid. Harbor Point pays above that up to $20,000, with Cardinal State's $10,000 behind it. How an award applies is Atty. Thorne's analysis.\n\nFor mediation, collect 3 to 4 date windows first, book a private room, check the interpreter, and hold his 2-hour buffer. For arbitration, contact the arbitrator's office only as he directs, and confirm the retainer in writing.\n\nAnd the Golden Rule: you never argue, speak on the record or represent John Doe. A question comes at you across the table and the urge is to be helpful. Don't. You defer, {name}, and hand Atty. Thorne the document.\n\nAt either table, you support. You never speak for John.",
   "ask": "{name}, the mediation was court-ordered. In your own words, what did that require of John Doe, and what didn't it?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here are the two forums on John Doe's file, facing each other. {name}, before I describe them, guess which card is heavier, and why.\n\nOn the left, the lighter card: mediation, 03/18/2027, Hon. Carla Benton (Ret.), tagged non-binding, confidential, facilitates.\n\nOn the right, the heavier navy card: binding arbitration, 06/22/2027, Laura Whitcomb, Esq., tagged acts as judge, binding award, appeal limited. Its hard dates sit underneath, 06/02, 06/11 and 06/22/2027. An orange arrow joins them, compromise to trial-ready.\n\nAcross the bottom runs the red-bordered UPL bar. UPL is the unauthorized practice of law, doing a lawyer's job without being one.\n\nWhat you can do: observe, log directives, update the CMS. What you never do: argue, speak on the record, speak for John Doe, negotiate.\n\nThat bar spans both cards on purpose. The line is the same at both tables.\n\nSame line at both tables: observe, log, never argue.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think that UPL bar runs under both cards instead of just the arbitration one?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run the real sequence. The order requires mediation by 03/31/2027. So before you ever contact the mediator's office, you collect 3 to 4 windows from Atty. Thorne, John Doe and opposing counsel in the Calendaring Simulators, and make sure the session lands inside 03/31/2027.\n\nOpposing counsel rejects every window? You log the pushback and escalate the same day for an attorney-to-attorney call.\n\nLater, the arbitrator's fee schedule and retainer request arrive. You confirm the schedule, send the retainer to accounting, send the internal invites, and get payment confirmed in writing before the hearing.\n\nHere's the thing: an unpaid retainer can bump a hearing, and a bumped hearing is months more of John waiting on his money.\n\nWhen a directive comes from the bench, you log it as given in Case Notes, calendar it and memo Atty. Thorne that same day.\n\nAnd yes, someone has to order the mediation-day lunch. Get it right, {name}. A hungry room settles nothing.\n\nGood logistics give counsel room to think.",
   "ask": "{name}, opposing counsel says no to every window you offered. What's your very next move?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Two Dos and three Don'ts, all about the hearing room.\n\nDo treat your hearing notes as Atty. Thorne's roadmap for afterward. Write down every directive the moment it's given. And do confirm the retainer payment for the neutral in writing, so nobody's arguing about it the week of.\n\nNow the Don'ts. Bringing a mediation-quality binder to an arbitration, where the evidence has to hold up. “Helpfully” answering the arbitrator yourself. And logging bench directives the next day from memory, which is really just guessing with confidence.\n\nIf the arbitrator turns to you, the answer is that you'll get that to Atty. Thorne right away. That's it. Logistics feel clerical, but the private room, the two-hour buffer and the interpreter check are what let him and John think clearly.\n\nThe low bar is showing up with a binder and a pen. The high bar is notes he can act on before he's back at his desk.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen in a room? {name}, what would you have done instead?\n\nYour mystery for the binder lessons: one document from the mediation is the most dangerous thing you could carry into the arbitration room.\n\nEach table gets its own binder, built for its own job.",
   "ask": "{name}, the arbitrator turns to you and asks about a bill. Do or Don't answer, and what do you actually say?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the task. In the Action Plan Editor, you'll build the ADR rules matrix with two columns, the 03/18/2027 mediation and the 06/22/2027 arbitration. ADR just means alternative dispute resolution, any way of settling a fight outside a courtroom.\n\nThe rows cover the neutral and her power, binding effect and appeal, confidentiality, the opposing parties, the limits in play, the preparation standard, your hard dates and the logistics.\n\nToday you'll also hard-code the arbitration dates on the CMS Calendar, counting back from the hearing, with alerts.\n\nThen map the UPL boundary for the hearing: what you do, what you never do, and the exact words you'd say if Laura Whitcomb, Esq. asks you about a bill.\n\n{name}, you've got the matrix. {name}, you're writing the boundary map.\n\nYou'll hand in the matrix, the docketed dates and a one-page boundary map, all sent to Atty. Thorne.\n\nKnow the table before you sit at it.",
   "ask": "{name}, on 03/18/2027 the other side was Robert Smith. Who is it on 06/22/2027, and what are they disputing?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Quick game: Same or Different? I name something about the mediation, you tell me if it's the same or different at the arbitration.\n\nWho's on the other side. Whether the decision is binding. Whether you can speak at the table. {name}, that last one, same or different?\n\nIt's the same, and it's the one that matters.\n\nNow fast-forward to 04/05/2027. The 03/18/2027 mediation before Hon. Carla Benton (Ret.) produced Keystone's $10,000 BI tender. BI is used up, the UM consents are in, and Robert Smith is dismissed.\n\nAtty. Thorne writes: “Harbor Point and Cardinal State dispute damages, citing the gap. Harbor Point has invoked its UM arbitration clause and Cardinal State joins: one binding arbitration before Laura Whitcomb, Esq., hearing 06/22/2027.”\n\nHe wants the rules matrix, the exhibit and brief dates docketed today, and a map of what you do at the hearing.\n\nAs set for this file, exhibits are exchanged 20 days before the hearing and the brief is due 10 days before. Keep that 10 in your head. It's coming back.\n\nThe forum changes. Your silence at the table doesn't.",
   "ask": "{name}, what changes operationally when a file moves from mediation to arbitration?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your matrix before you log it. Mediation: Hon. Carla Benton facilitates only, non-binding, held 03/18/2027 inside 03/31/2027.\n\nArbitration: Laura Whitcomb acts as judge, binding award, narrow review, under Harbor Point's clause with Cardinal State joined. Opposing parties went from Robert Smith to the UM carriers, who are now the other side. Limits in play: $20,000 plus $10,000, so $30,000. Exhibits 06/02/2027.\n\nAnd here's the trap I told you to hold onto. 10 days back from the hearing is Saturday 06/12, so the brief moves to Friday 06/11, never Monday 06/14. Backward counts roll earlier.\n\nThat deadline does not care that Friday was your half day.\n\nLogistics and your UPL map are in. Your deferral line goes like this: “I'm the Case Manager providing administrative assistance; Atty. Thorne will address that.”\n\nIf you audited the Riverton Spine & Rehab bill, you're going to want to answer. Don't.\n\nTen days back from a Saturday means Friday, never Monday.\n\nMystery for the Learning Check: {name}, which single date from today could quietly lose the hearing?",
   "ask": "{name}, say your deferral line out loud once: what are the exact words you'll use at the hearing?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-arbcal": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll start in Court E-Filing, the firm's master calendar of court and hearing deadlines. Enter the hearing and re-anchor the exchange and brief entries to the letter, so the letter is their trigger, not the memo from 04/05.\n\nThen work in the Calendaring Simulators and count back the internal dates: the Litigation Desk's draft index, the day binder QC opens, the last day to send the certified-records requests, and their chase.\n\nRecords offices, by the way, run on their own time zone, somewhere between \"soon\" and \"we never got your request.\"\n\nHold John's attendance confirmation, the prep session Atty. Thorne will lead and the technology check on the CMS Calendar. Route the $900.00 to accounting as a case cost.\n\nLast, check it all against the master calendar. The SOL entries stay. The court's dates in a suit that ended with Robert Smith's dismissal go to Atty. Thorne as a question, not into the bin.\n\n{name}, you own the count-back. {name}, you own the questions for Atty. Thorne.\n\nNew trigger, new anchor: count from the letter.",
   "ask": "{name}, the records have to be in hand when binder QC opens. So which date do you count the request deadline back from?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Quick one: would you rather have a hearing date with no deadlines around it, or a hearing date with nine weeks of deadlines hanging off it? {name}, choose wisely.\n\nTrick question. You always get the second one.\n\nIt's Monday, 04/26/2027, and Laura Whitcomb's scheduling letter has just landed. The consolidated UM arbitration against Harbor Point and Cardinal State is set for Tuesday, 06/22/2027, at 9:00 a.m.\n\nArbitration is a private hearing where a neutral decides the dispute. Consolidated means both UM carriers are handled in one hearing.\n\nExhibits and witness lists are exchanged 20 days before, and briefs are due 10 days before. The plaintiff's share of her fee is $900.00.\n\nKeystone's $10,000 went in at the 03/18/2027 mediation and Robert Smith is out of the case. But the master calendar still shows the court's dates from the 12/10/2026 Scheduling Order, and the SOL entries, the deadline to sue.\n\nOne date is fixed now, and everything for the next nine weeks hangs off it, like ornaments off a tree. A few of those dates aren't in the letter at all.\n\nFind them before they find you.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you open anything, name every date that has to count back from 06/22/2027. Which ones are not in the letter at all?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your entries, and {name}, you're reading yours aloud.\n\nHearing 06/22/2027 at 9:00 a.m., exchange 06/02/2027, and the brief on Friday 06/11/2027. Ten days back is a Saturday, and a backward count moves earlier, never to Monday 06/14.\n\nCounting back to a weekend and rolling forward is the calendar version of showing up a day after the party.\n\nBinder QC opens Monday 05/17/2027, 16 days before the exchange, with the draft index due Friday 05/14. Records requests out by Monday 05/03/2027, which is 05/17 less the 14-day custodian window, with a chase tickler on each.\n\nJohn confirmed by 04/21, the prep session proposed for 06/15 and the tech check 06/21. The $900.00 is a case cost.\n\nThe SOL hard date 02/04/2028 and the 11/05/2027 cutoff are untouched, and the court dates are kept, marked and asked about.\n\nBackward counts roll earlier. Always.\n\nAnd here's the mystery. There's no award date anywhere. Every other date in this arbitration is on the calendar. So why not that one?",
   "ask": "{name}, why is there no entry for the award, when every other date in this arbitration is on the calendar?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-a3": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job: route the impasse.\n\nWrite the memo to Atty. Thorne. Put the offers against the limits, Harbor Point's stated reason, and each layer's dispute clause quoted word for word, with Cardinal State's marked as outstanding. Lay out both routes and what each would require operationally.\n\nThen ask him three things in writing: which route governs on each layer, each layer's own suit or arbitration-demand deadline, and whether the two should be heard together.\n\nSet the CMS up to carry his answer, with the ADR entry, the status, and a task per open question. Draft the calendar entries, but don't save them. Then write what you'll say to John when he calls to ask what happens now.\n\n{name}, you're drafting the memo. {name}, you're John on the phone.\n\nA memo that says “offers are low, please advise” just hands him homework. Write one he can decide from without opening the file.\n\nDraft it, hold it, and let his answer set the dates.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do we draft the calendar entries and hold them instead of simply docketing them today?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Quick poll before the file opens. Thumbs up if you think two insurance companies offering fourteen thousand total is good news, thumbs down if not.\n\n{name}, I see a sideways thumb. Fair enough. Let's find out.\n\nIt's the fifth of April twenty twenty-seven, and we're on Track B. The UM demands went out on the twenty-second of March, after Keystone tendered its ten thousand in bodily injury limits at the mediation, with both UM carriers' written consents, a Limited Release and Robert Smith dismissed.\n\nNow the answers are in. Marcus Bell at Harbor Point offers eight thousand against a twenty thousand dollar Host UM limit. He's calling the fourteen-day gap a causation problem, meaning he says the crash didn't cause all of it.\n\nThat's the insurance version of “it's not me, it's you.”\n\nTeresa Okafor at Cardinal State offers six thousand against ten thousand. Harbor Point's policy has a binding arbitration clause. Cardinal State's certified policy was requested and hasn't arrived.\n\nOne of those two facts is about to decide your whole memo.\n\nRead the policy before you plan the fight.",
   "ask": "{name}, fourteen thousand offered against thirty thousand of limits. What's our first move, and what is not?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work against this. Both claims named as first-party claims, separate from the Keystone claim that closed at the mediation in March. The figures exact, with their dates. Exhaustion documented, not assumed.\n\nHarbor Point's clause quoted and called binding, with no interpretation. Cardinal State treated as its own layer, its policy marked outstanding with a chase task, and Harbor Point's clause never read onto it.\n\nBoth routes laid out with what they'd mean operationally. Bad faith raised only as a question, with the handling dates recorded as facts. Three written questions asked.\n\nNothing docketed, and the tort statute of limitations not used as a UM deadline.\n\nThe explanation to John is procedural only: what a Demand for Arbitration is, that a neutral decides instead of a jury, and that Atty. Thorne will go through what it means.\n\nYour job is the questions. His job is the answers.\n\nNow your mystery. When Atty. Thorne answers, three dates will suddenly exist. {name}, before the next activity, where will you count them back from?",
   "ask": "{name}, whose work would you rather hand to Atty. Thorne: the memo with an answer in it, or the one with three questions?"
  }
 },
 "d4m2-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Into the Learning Check, game-show style. Three questions, one for each part of this module, and I'm calling on people for a sixty-second preview of each before anyone types.\n\nQuestion 1 is all the date math in one: the discovery mailed on the first of December, the Sunday statute of limitations, both weekend directions, and what happens to an unanswered Request for Admission. Show me the working, not just the dates. {name}, you're previewing that one.\n\nQuestion 2 is the UM side: the two routes out of an impasse, what decides which one governs, where a bad-faith action sits, and why Margaret's Bluewater policy isn't a route.\n\nQuestion 3 puts mediation and arbitration on the timeline and asks for non-binding and binding in the words you'd use with John.\n\nAnswers come from the file, in your own words. And no, the three-hole punch can't help you here. I've checked.\n\nShow the math, not just the date.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of those three would you least like Atty. Thorne to find wrong in your answer, and why?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now write your Self-Summary: the principles from this module in your own words.\n\nThen take the scenario on the slide. It's 09/04/2026, Keystone has ignored two follow-ups and the 48-hour escalation, and John Doe calls asking why nothing's happening and whether he should just take the $3,500. Write what you say, what you don't say, and what goes to Atty. Thorne today.\n\nHere's a real one for you. I once heard a Case Manager answer that exact question with, “Honestly? I would.” She meant well. It took Atty. Thorne two meetings and a very long lunch to undo it.\n\nA good answer is warm, honest about the process, and silent on the number.\n\nAnd that $3,500 is a nice reminder of how far this file has come: from that offer to a binding hearing on thirty thousand of limits, all because every date was right.\n\n{name}, think about one date on this file you'd always check twice.\n\nWarm on the process, silent on the number.",
   "ask": "{name}, which single date on this file would you check twice, and why?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "To finish, fill in the Skill Scale. Rate each skill from 1 to 5, honestly, and submit it for review.\n\nI'll put your self-rating next to your score. Where there's a wide gap, we'll talk it through.\n\nSame as every day, UPL is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive an answer that speaks for John Doe at the hearing or tells him whether to take a number.\n\nNow your mystery for the last check of the day. The binders you built are about to be put to the test.\n\nEvery skill today comes down to dates and documents you can prove.\n\nSomewhere in one of them is a document that could get excluded from evidence, not because of what it says, but because of how it was printed. {name}, go find it before Laura Whitcomb does.",
   "ask": "{name}, which skill here are you least sure you could perform under pressure at a live hearing?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Think of the Mediation Binder as the attorney's cheat sheet. It holds the case's most critical documents, put together so counsel never fumbles during a fast-moving compromise session.\n\nIt opens with the executive summary and administration, a one-page snapshot of the parties, the order and the schedule. That's the first thing Atty. Thorne sees.\n\nNext come the mediation briefs, both sides' arguments read side by side, so he can prepare comebacks before anyone sits down.\n\nThen the core pleadings, the filed documents that say what this lawsuit is actually about. Those first three sections set the stage.\n\nBy the way, the binder has six sections. And I've watched grown professionals fight over the last good set of numbered tabs in the supply closet like it was the last slice of pizza. Get yours early.\n\n{name}, the other three sections are coming next. Want to guess one?\n\nThe front of the binder sets the stage.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these first three sections have you already started building on John Doe's file without realizing it?"
  },
  "theory2": {
   "say": "Here's the reveal: the back half of the binder is where the case gets proved.\n\nSection four is key evidence and liability, the smoking guns. That's the raw proof of who caused the crash, from reports to video stills to highlighted deposition pages. A deposition, quick reminder, is sworn testimony taken outside court.\n\nSection five is damages, financials and experts. It backs up counsel's dollar figure, with the records, the bills, the wage proof and the expert reports.\n\nAnd section six, settlement history and drafts, closes it out. It's there so Atty. Thorne can finalize a deal before anyone leaves the room.\n\nThink about it. If a deal comes together at four in the afternoon, you don't want to be drafting from scratch while the other side's lawyer checks his watch and the mediator eyes the door.\n\n{name}, why do drafts get built before anybody even agrees?\n\nThe back of the binder proves the case and closes the deal.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do you think settlement drafts belong in the binder before the session even starts?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Here's the six-section binder in its fixed order. {name}, say each section number out loud with me as I go.\n\nSection one holds the snapshot sheet, the mediation order or agreement, and the session schedule. Section two, our confidential statement and the defense's brief, if they exchanged one.\n\nSection three, the operative complaint and the answer with its affirmative defenses, which are the reasons the defense says they shouldn't have to pay.\n\nSection four, the Police Report, photos and video stills, and deposition summaries with the key pages highlighted. Section five, the medical chart and bills ledger, the wage-loss proof and the expert reports.\n\nAnd section six, the Negotiation Log of every demand and offer, plus a draft release.\n\nSame order, every time, so Atty. Thorne's hand goes to the right tab without thinking. Honestly, muscle memory wins mediations.\n\nSix sections, same order, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, on John Doe's file, which of these six sections do you think will take the most work to get right?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Five-second challenge to start. I name an item, you tell me where it is on your desk right now, in five seconds or less.\n\nYour phone charger. Your last pay stub. The pen that actually works. {name}, how'd you do? Most of us fail at least one. I definitely do.\n\nNow picture Atty. Thorne the morning of the mediation, handed a file that's organized like your junk drawer. He loses momentum. He looks unprepared in front of the mediator. And he may give up a point just because he couldn't find the proof in time.\n\nRemember, Keystone's stonewall on the 14-Day Gap is the reason John Doe's file is in suit at all. At the 03/18/2027 mediation, Atty. Thorne will have minutes to answer that argument.\n\nSo every record, every figure, every quote has to sit in a known place.\n\nAnd there's one number on this binder's index that, if it's wrong, Keystone will use to stall the whole day. Stick around.\n\nIf Atty. Thorne can't find it, it doesn't help him.",
   "ask": "{name}, what happens if Atty. Thorne gets an unorganized file the morning of the mediation?"
  },
  "doc": {
   "say": "Before you build a binder around it, let's read the other side's playbook. {name}, if the defense handed you a list of every argument they plan to make, would you want it? Good news: they have to.\n\nIt's the Answer and Affirmative Defenses, the defendant's response to the Complaint. It admits or denies each allegation, then pleads the defenses Robert Smith will rely on.\n\nLegally, it tells the court and Atty. Thorne what Smith disputes, and on which defenses he'll fight the claim. As evidence, it's the roadmap.\n\nAtty. Grant Mercer filed it on 11/12/2026. It pleads comparative negligence, meaning John was partly to blame. Causation, leaning on the gap and a so-called degenerative MRI. And failure to mitigate, meaning he didn't do enough to get better.\n\nEvery one of those is evidence you'll need to pull.\n\nYour part: log it, file it in 01_PLEADINGS, keep Section 3 to the operative pleadings, and map each pleaded defense to an exhibit or a flag. What each defense means is Atty. Thorne's to judge.\n\nDon't just file it and move on. Turn every defense into a to-do list before the other side asks.\n\nEvery pleaded defense needs an exhibit that answers it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the Answer's defenses does the 03/13/2026 MRI help answer, and how?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. The binder is Atty. Thorne's and the mediator's road map, indexed so any document is found in five seconds.\n\nSix numbered sections, fixed order, each tab matching one master index in the CMS, the single source of truth. The binder prints from the index, never the reverse. Exhibit numbers belong to the proceeding; Bates numbers, the stamped page numbers, belong to the page.\n\nSection three holds only the 09/24/2026 complaint and 11/12/2026 answer, which pleads comparative negligence, causation and failure to mitigate. Plainly put: John was partly to blame, the crash didn't cause his injuries, and he didn't do enough to get better, like skipping therapy.\n\nThe mediator's fee split is simply who pays what share of the mediator's bill.\n\nSection five's ledger ties to the certified invoices, with the duplicate removed, $6,290.00 down to $6,105.00, for $16,401.40 total. That's the reveal from earlier, {name}: the one number Keystone will pounce on.\n\nSection six fixes Keystone's $3,500 baseline and holds a draft Limited Release.\n\nYou're the quality checker: text-searchable, statement delivered by 03/11/2027, audited 48 hours out. Arguments, positions and every number are his, and you never discuss value with anyone.\n\nThe binder prints from the index, never the reverse.",
   "ask": "{name}, where do you find the mediator's fee split, and why can't you just assume it's 50/50?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Here's John Doe's binder, open and seen from above. Six navy divider tabs step down the right edge, each with its orange number and section name.\n\nBeside each one is a card listing this file's documents: RPD-26-018842, the EDR, the 03/13/2026 MRI, the $16,401.40 in Specials.\n\nAcross the top runs the master index, one source of truth, with columns for exhibit, Bates, source and status. See those thin orange lines? They run from the index rows down into the tabs.\n\nOn the right there's a stopwatch reading five seconds, and a red warning that drafts and old pleadings never enter the binder.\n\n{name}, which direction do those lines run? Only one way, from index to binder, never back.\n\nBuild the binder first and index it later by squinting, and you'll be squinting forever. Index it first, and the binder basically prints itself.\n\nIndex first. The binder follows.",
   "ask": "{name}, if the printed binder and the CMS index disagree, which one wins?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the flow once the mediation is set for 03/18/2027. You open the master index under Documents and seat each document in exactly one section. Every entry gets its section, exhibit number, Bates range and source.\n\nOnce the pleadings and evidence are indexed, you go to the Pleadings Log and check each defense pleaded in the answer against sections four and five. Every pleaded defense needs an answering exhibit, or a flag to Atty. Thorne.\n\nThen, with the binder assembled, you OCR it, which just makes the PDF text-searchable. Bookmark it, check it against the index and send it for approval. It has to be searchable, approved, and delivered by 03/11/2027.\n\nWhy all the fuss? When the mediator asks “where's the proof on causation,” the attorney who finds it in five seconds sounds confident. And confident sounds expensive to the other side.\n\n{name}, what do you do with a defense that has no answer?\n\nFive seconds to find it, or it isn't ready.",
   "ask": "{name}, a pleaded defense has no answering exhibit in sections four or five. What do you do with it?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Here's the Mediation Binder in two lists.\n\nThe Do side. Start from the 07/20/2026 Demand Package, because most of what the binder needs already lives there. And bookmark to the page Atty. Thorne will quote, not the cover. If he wants Dr. Reyes's causation opinion, he lands on the opinion, not page one.\n\nThe Don'ts. An unorganized file the morning of the 03/18/2027 session. A snapshot sheet with stale opposing-counsel contacts. And the confidential statement sent ten minutes before the session. Ours is due 03/11/2027, seven days out, and it goes to the mediator, not the other side.\n\nThe quick binder is a stack of PDFs in date order. The binder I want has an index that tells you exactly where every fact lives, and when two copies disagree, the index decides.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? {name}, how close to the session was it?\n\nAnd your mystery. Dr. Reyes's causation opinion may need to be disclosed by 03/15/2027. Figure out why that date sits before the mediation, and who gets told today. How it's disclosed is Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nThe index is the product. The binder is just a printout.",
   "ask": "{name}, you bookmark Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter to its cover page. Do or Don't, and what's the rule?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your task. You'll assemble the six-section Mediation Binder in the CMS, working from the master index and starting with the 07/20/2026 Demand Package.\n\nSeat each document in one section with its exhibit number and Bates range, and map every allegation in the 11/12/2026 answer to the exhibit that answers it.\n\nThen run the pre-mediation audit. Rebuild the ledger from the certified bills you have to $16,401.40, and cross-check each provider balance against what PIP and Peach State already paid. PIP, quick reminder, is John's own no-fault medical coverage.\n\nAdd the Negotiation Log, a draft Limited Release and the UM consent requests. That's the release that ends only the claim against Robert Smith and Keystone, and nothing else.\n\n{name}, you've got section five. {name}, you're on the defense map.\n\nYou'll hand in an indexed, searchable binder with an audited ledger, a lien summary and a short memo on both landmines, sent to Atty. Thorne. Nail this ledger and Keystone loses its excuse to stall.\n\nEvery number in section five ties to a certified bill.",
   "ask": "{name}, Atty. Mercer says the ledger is unverified. Is he right, and what in section five proves your answer?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Game to start: Spot the Landmine. I'm putting a draft binder on the board, and somewhere in it are two mistakes big enough to blow up a mediation.\n\nFirst person to spot one gets first pick of the snacks on mediation day. And trust me, the good cookies go fast.\n\nIt's 03/04/2027, and this just came from Atty. Thorne: “Mercer writes that Keystone will not put a real number on the table while our medical ledger is unverified. The draft binder carries Riverton at $6,290.00 and Crescent as an open balance. Rebuild it from the master index and audit it.”\n\nThe statement and binder go to Hon. Carla Benton (Ret.) by 03/11/2027, sent to him first, for the 03/18/2027 mediation.\n\nJohn Doe was deposed 02/18/2027, and the certified transcript's in the file. Riverton Spine & Rehab's Final Itemized Ledger, requested 02/08/2027, followed up 02/22 and red-flagged 03/01, still hasn't arrived.\n\n{name}, see those two red chips? What tipped you off?\n\nAn unverified number is an invitation to stall.",
   "ask": "{name}, who spotted those two landmines in the draft before I pointed them out, and what tipped you off?"
  },
  "grade": {
   "say": "This is what a Grade-A Mediation Binder looks like, before you build one.\n\nThe rule that runs everything: the binder prints from the master index, never the other way round. Any document should be found in five seconds, so every section is tabbed, every PDF bookmarked and searchable. Only current pleadings go in, no drafts.\n\nThe set opens with your cover letter to Hon. Carla Benton, then the master index, then the six sections in their fixed order.\n\nDownload the case documents to your training Drive and build the index first. Rebuild the ledger from the certified bills, write the cover letter and the audit memo, and upload the finished binder to the CMS for the audit.\n\n{name}, here's the difference in one picture. A stack of paper with tabs is just a stack. A Grade-A binder is a map.\n\nAnd if you've ever lost a fight with a three-hole punch that only punches two holes, you already know why we build digital first.\n\nA Grade-A binder is a map, not a pile.",
   "ask": "{name}, what goes wrong if the index is written after the binder is assembled?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "This is the index the Litigation Desk put together on the second of March. Atty. Mercer says Keystone won't put a real number on the table while the medical ledger is unverified, and on this draft, sadly, he's right.\n\nSection five was typed from the providers' original bills. So Riverton Spine and Rehab comes in at six thousand two hundred and ninety on thirty-four lines, and the medical total reads fifteen thousand and two forty, against the fourteen thousand eight hundred and seventeen forty we served in July.\n\nCrescent shows as an open balance Peach State already paid.\n\nCall every section, and tell me what's missing as well as what's wrong. {name}, you take sections one to three. {name}, four to six.\n\nOne file, one total, and it's the one the records prove.\n\nHere's your mystery. There are two medical totals for one file, and only one survives cross-examination. When you hand it in, you'll see the binder as we delivered it on the eleventh of March.",
   "ask": "{name}, two medical totals for one file. Which one would you defend in that room?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log this, run your checks. Section one's snapshot has LSH-2026-MVA-770412, No. 26-CV-04417, Atty. Mercer's verified contacts, and the fee split read from the order. Section three, only the filed complaint and answer.\n\nYour defense map gets graded hard. Five allegations, five answers, each with an exhibit number. “See Medical Records” isn't a map.\n\nOkay, the reveal on the landmines. Landmine one: Riverton audited to 33 visits at $185.00, $6,105.00, duplicate removed, pending final verification, for $16,401.40.\n\nLandmine two: Crescent's $3,400.00 satisfied by Peach State's $1,860.00, with open liens of $7,965.00.\n\nSection six's log runs from the 07/20/2026 demand to suit. The release and consent requests are drafts, so leave every term to Atty. Thorne.\n\nThen swap binders with your partner and check each other's index. {name}, be kind, but be ruthless.\n\nAn exhibit number is a map. “See Medical Records” is a shrug.",
   "ask": "{name}, when you swap with your partner, which exhibit number in their defense map will you check first?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The Arbitration Binder isn't a reference packet. It's a trial-ready evidence record, handed straight to a private judge who decides.\n\nYour role here is operational: timelines, scheduling and documents, run with total precision.\n\nThe arbitration brief deadline is the absolute cutoff for claims, exhibit indexes and witness lists, and it goes to the arbitrator and opposing counsel. The arbitration submissions are the starting paperwork that set the ground rules for the whole proceeding, which is why they lead the binder.\n\nOkay, here's the scary part, and the reveal. A missing page or a grayscale print, black-and-white instead of color, can be excluded.\n\nA color photo of a crash shows damage. A grayscale one shows a blurry gray blob that looks like a potato. And with a binding award, there's no second chance to fix it.\n\n{name}, which of these ideas feels most different from the Mediation Binder?\n\nIf the arbitrator can't read it, it might as well not exist.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these four ideas feels most different from how we built the Mediation Binder?"
  },
  "frame": {
   "say": "Six tabs again, but with a different job. {name}, as I go, raise your hand at the tab you think the carriers will fight over.\n\nTab one, arbitration submissions: the snapshot sheet, the final brief, the governing order or agreement and fee disclosures. Tab two, arbitration pleadings: the current demand and each party's written position, with no old versions.\n\nTab three, liability and biomechanics, the physics of the crash: the certified Police Report, diagrams, and photos and stills in color, never grayscale.\n\nTab four, medical damages: the consolidated ledger, provider records and bills, imaging, the causation opinion.\n\nTab five is the prior medical shield, prior injury records and work history paired with a medical opinion. And tab six is economic losses, wage-loss proof only.\n\nSix tabs, six jobs, and not one of them is “miscellaneous.” Trust me, the miscellaneous tab is where good exhibits go to disappear.\n\nEvery tab has one job, and none of them is miscellaneous.",
   "ask": "{name}, looking at John Doe's file, which tab is going to carry the gap fight that the carriers are pressing?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: Persuade or Prove? I hold up an item, you shout whether it persuades or proves.\n\nA closing argument. A certified medical record. A sticky note that says “trust me.” {name}, I heard you say “persuade” on the sticky note, and honestly, I respect the optimism.\n\nNow I'm holding up the Mediation Binder. This one persuaded. The next one is evidence.\n\nAnd I'm writing a date on the board: 06/02/2027. Everything in this lesson counts back from that exchange.\n\nOn 04/05/2027, Harbor Point and Cardinal State picked up Keystone's gap argument, and Laura Whitcomb, Esq. is going to decide damages, bindingly. The Arbitration Binder is the record her award rests on.\n\nA page that's missing, unreadable or not exchanged by 06/02/2027 may simply not count. No second chances.\n\nAnd remember the mystery from earlier, the document that could get excluded because of how it was printed? You'll get your answer on the next slide.\n\nMediation persuades. Arbitration decides on the record.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the difference between a binder that persuades and a binder that's evidence?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "A binding award rests on the exhibits, with very limited review, so apply each tab's quality check as you sort.\n\nThe threshold leads. UM opens only because BI is used up, so prove it with Keystone's $10,000 tender, the consents, the Limited Release and Robert Smith's dismissal, never the Mediation Statement.\n\nAuthenticate everything, meaning prove it's real, with business-records certifications from each provider, Riverton Fire, Riverton PD and Northgate HR.\n\nKeep video in its original file, with the 02/19/2026 chain of custody, and print stills in color. Chain of custody is the written trail of who had the file, when, and what they did with it, so nobody can claim it was edited.\n\nEven if the forum relaxes evidence rules, build to the strict standard. Admitted isn't the same as persuasive.\n\nNumber exhibits C-1 onward and freeze the set once exchanged. Nothing crosses over from mediation, and the Lien Sheet stays in Atty. Thorne's set. He decides what's argued, exchanged or withheld, and you never argue an exhibit.\n\nSo, {name}, “close enough” is not a phrase we use here. The word is “certified.”\n\nAuthenticate everything, and build to the strict standard.",
   "ask": "{name}, who can say the doorbell video is what we say it is?"
  },
  "model": {
   "say": "Look at the two panels. On the left, small and grayed out, is the Mediation Binder, captioned persuades, non-binding. A heavy orange arrow says the gap dispute carries over.\n\nOn the right, much bigger, are the six arbitration tabs, each with a card stating its quality rule. Over tab one sits a threshold chip: BI exhausted, $10,000 tendered 03/18/2027.\n\nUnder the tabs runs an authentication rail: custodian certification, original file, chain of custody. Bottom right is a red warning: not exchanged by 06/02/2027 means it may not count, with a reminder to confirm the forum's rules.\n\n{name}, why do you think the designer made the right panel so much bigger?\n\nThat size difference is the message. This record is the one that decides.\n\nAnd yes, the label maker finally earns its keep here. C-1 through the end, every tab, no handwriting.\n\nThis is the record the award stands on.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the BI exhaustion proof sit on top of tab one, ahead of everything else?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it unfolds. Arbitration's invoked on 04/05/2027, so you open the claimant exhibit index and put the 06/02/2027 exchange and the 06/11/2027 brief in Court E-Filing that day.\n\nAs the provider records come in, you go through Medical Records Requests and ask each records custodian, the person in charge of a provider's files, for a business-records certification. One per provider, covering every date you're exhibiting.\n\nCustodians are slow. Like, glacier slow. So don't wait.\n\nOnce the exchange set is assembled, you send the set and every flagged item to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate. It has to be approved before 06/02/2027, with no privileged item anywhere in it.\n\nNow connect it to the money. An exhibit that can't be authenticated is an exhibit the arbitrator can ignore, and every ignored bill is money John doesn't get.\n\n{name}, so why the rush on certifications?\n\nNo certification, no exhibit, no money for that bill.",
   "ask": "{name}, why do we request certifications the same week the arbitration is invoked rather than once the binder's built?"
  },
  "dodont": {
   "say": "Arbitration raises the stakes, and these two lists show you how.\n\nOn the Do side, build to the strict evidence standard anyway, even when the forum says it's relaxed. And request certifications the week arbitration is invoked. For us that's the week of 04/05/2027, not the week before the 06/22 hearing.\n\nThe Don'ts. A grayscale print of a crash still, where you can't tell a brake light from a shadow. A draft brief or superseded complaint slipping into the exchanged set. And the Confidential Mediation Statement landing in the Arbitration Binder. Once it's exchanged, it can't be unsent.\n\nRelaxed admission doesn't mean full weight, and the clause may relax nothing at all. The lazy set is whatever printed. The set I want is in color, certified, current, and screened, with anything privileged flagged for Atty. Thorne to decide.\n\n{name}, which of these Don'ts have you seen happen? {name}, which one scares you most?\n\nYour mystery: the defense has one argument it's run since the first offer, and it's about John's past, not the crash. Find it before the next lesson.\n\nRelaxed rules don't mean relaxed proof.",
   "ask": "{name}, the forum relaxes the rules of evidence, so you skip the custodian certification. Do or Don't, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-prior": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The deck puts it bluntly. Defense counsel use prior records to argue the injury was already there, and Tab 5, the Prior Medical Shield, answers that before they raise it.\n\nThe defense makes three moves: it was already there, the imaging shows wear and tear, and the injury is just a continuation of the old one. Often there's a fourth: there's no imaging from before the crash.\n\nOur answer starts with the pre-morbid baseline. That just means John's condition before 02/06/2026, built from records, not memory.\n\nBehind it sits the eggshell idea. Generally, the at-fault driver takes the injured person as he finds him, and making an old problem worse still counts. Picture a cracked vase: knock it over and you still broke it. That's Atty. Thorne's argument, and the rule must be confirmed.\n\nDefense lawyers love an old chiropractic visit the way some of us love a fresh box of binder tabs.\n\nA prior left unshielded, without a medical opinion beside it, is an arbitration landmine. {name}, neck versus low back. What does that answer?",
   "ask": "{name}, John's prior was his low back and his injury is his neck. Which of the defense's moves does that answer, and which does it leave open?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Two truths and a lie, file edition. One: Keystone called John's MRI degenerative. Two: John had chiropractic visits in 2021. Three: John's 2021 visits were for his neck.\n\n{name}, which one's the lie?\n\nIt's number three, and that's the whole lesson in one sentence.\n\nHere's the line the defense has run on John Doe's file from the very first offer: it was already there. Keystone called the MRI degenerative, meaning wear and tear from age, on 08/05/2026.\n\nAtty. Grant Mercer's Answer on 11/12/2026 pleaded causation on that same reading. And at the 02/18/2027 deposition, defense counsel spent time on John's three chiropractic visits back in 2021.\n\nNow Harbor Point and Cardinal State are disputing damages, and Laura Whitcomb, Esq. decides on 06/22/2027, bindingly.\n\nTab 5 of the Arbitration Binder is where we answer the prior from the record. If it isn't answered there, there's nowhere left to answer it after the award.\n\nAn old injury isn't a problem. An unanswered one is.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would we put records the carriers could use against John into our own binder?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "We disclose the prior, never hide it, and we build the shield in five pieces.\n\nFirst, Birch Avenue Chiropractic's complete chart for 05/04, 05/11 and 05/18/2021, certified for every date, never a summary. Second, resolution: three visits, low-back stiffness resolved, released to full activity, in the record's own words.\n\nThird, the body region, side by side: low back then, neck and right arm now. Fourth, the gap in between, proved by records: the release, his Northgate job from 01/12/2026, and every history saying no prior neck complaint.\n\nFifth, Dr. Park's sentence and Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter in the same tab.\n\nThen check every medical history for consistency and tie every claimed harm to a dated record. A mismatch, or a letter silent on 2021, goes to Atty. Thorne. He decides on a declaration.\n\nHiding a prior is like hiding a coffee stain under a mug. Somebody always picks up the mug, usually at the worst moment.\n\nDisclose it, then shield it, {name}. Never the other way round.",
   "ask": "{name}, one provider's intake form disagrees with the rest. Who decides what happens next, and what do you not do?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the calendar. Arbitration is invoked on 04/05/2027. That same week, you send Birch Avenue Chiropractic a records request through Medical Records Requests for the complete 2021 chart, with a business-records certification covering all three dates.\n\nWhen it arrives, you build the side-by-side comparison in the exhibit index, and every cell cites a record and a page.\n\nIf the histories don't line up, or Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter doesn't mention 2021, you escalate to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate, with the records attached, and you change nothing. Anything still open 10 days before the 06/02/2027 exchange gets a red flag.\n\nWaiting for the defense to bring up 2021 means you're already behind. Answer it before they open their mouths, and that's how a “wear and tear” discount comes off the award.\n\nAnswer the prior before they ask.\n\nNow your mystery: {name}, which single document makes the whole shield stand up?",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the prior chart request go out the week arbitration is invoked, not once the binder is built?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-tx": {
  "why": {
   "say": "{name}, quick question. When does a carrier start valuing John Doe's injury? At the demand?\n\nNope. It starts the day he walks into urgent care, because everything the carrier will ever pay gets argued from the treatment record. How he was treated, how steadily, and what the doctors found.\n\nSo a Case Manager who watched the treatment phase with the demand in mind shows up with every visit documented and every gap already answered.\n\nOne who didn't finds out about the gaps from the adjuster's offer letter. Ask me how I know.\n\nThe demand gets written on the first day of treatment, not the last.",
   "ask": "{name}, which stop on John Doe's treatment do you think the adjuster reads first, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how it lands on John's file. MMI came on 06/12/2026, then Dr. Reyes's Causation Letter on 06/19, then the BI demand on 07/20.\n\nThe MRI from 03/13 shows the C5-C6 herniation with no significant degeneration next to it, and that's the line we quote.\n\nThe fourteen-day gap and the April disruption each had their answer on file before Keystone's offer cited them. That's the whole trick: answer first, so their argument lands on a wall.\n\nThe Specials are $16,401.40, billed and audited. And the repeat injections Dr. Reyes mentioned? Narrative, with the note quoted, never in the Specials.\n\n{name}, why do you think future care can't go in the Specials?\n\nBilled goes in the Specials. Everything else goes in the story.",
   "ask": "{name}, the adjuster says it's minor soft tissue. What one line on the file answers that?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Three moments on the job.\n\nWhen MMI is declared, you close the chronology and audit the Specials in the CMS and the Net Sheet, every bill tied to a dated record.\n\nWhen a gap or a missed visit shows up, you pair it with its documented reason the same week, in Case Notes and the Gap Explanation Record. Same week, while everyone still remembers why.\n\nAnd when the demand or a binder is assembled, you map each claim to the record that proves it in the Packet Builder, so no figure or finding goes out without its source.\n\n{name}, predict it for me: what happens to the offer if one bill in the demand has no record behind it?\n\nNo source, no number.",
   "ask": "{name}, what would you check first if a binder's damages tab didn't tie to the Specials?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your task. You'll sort that folder into the six-tab Arbitration Binder using the Exhibit index, Medical Records Requests and the Lien Tracker, applying each tab's quality rule as you go.\n\nLead with the BI exhaustion record, number every exhibit C-1 onward with its Bates range, and log each record's authentication.\n\nThen defuse both landmines. Build tab five's prior medical shield against the 2021 low-back visits defense counsel pressed at the 02/18/2027 deposition and the “degenerative” MRI reading from 08/05/2026. And strip the Lien Sheet to what's actually owed. Flag anything privileged for Atty. Thorne.\n\nYou'll also take a call from Lori Banks at Crescent. Find out what's holding the records up, get a firm delivery date in writing, and log it.\n\n{name}, you're on that call. {name}, you're building tab five.\n\nQuick tip: a records office promising “end of the week” is a weather forecast, not a deadline. Get it in writing.\n\nGet every promise in writing, with a date.",
   "ask": "{name}, Harbor Point is the other side now. Our attorney's-set Lien Sheet says it's owed $5,000.00. What goes wrong?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Countdown game. I say a number of days, you shout what you could get done in that time.\n\nSixteen days. {name}, go. Renovate a kitchen? Plan a wedding?\n\nHere's what landed on 05/17/2027: exhibit exchange in 16 days, and the Arbitration Binder isn't started. Zero of six tabs. Yikes.\n\nThe hard date is 06/02/2027, before Laura Whitcomb, Esq., on HP-26-7741 and CS-26-20355. Robert Smith was dismissed after Keystone tendered its $10,000 at the 03/18/2027 mediation, and the carriers are disputing damages over the gap.\n\nThe unsorted folder holds a pre-filing draft of the Complaint, grayscale doorbell stills, the 09/08/2026 impasse memo, and a Lien Sheet listing Harbor Point PIP at $5,000.00 and Summit Imaging at $2,395.00. Crescent Pain & Spine's certified records, requested 05/05/2027, haven't come in.\n\nI'm putting four red traps on the board, then covering them before anyone sorts.\n\nSixteen days, and the deadline doesn't care that one of them is Memorial Day weekend.\n\nSixteen days is plenty, if you start today.",
   "ask": "{name}, before I cover them, which of those four traps do you think you'd have missed on your own?"
  },
  "grade": {
   "say": "Now the arbitration standard. It's stricter than mediation, because the award rests on these exhibits.\n\nThe threshold leads: BI used up, proved with the signed documents. Every exhibit is authenticated by its custodian's certification, and you log each one as it arrives. Nothing from the confidential mediation crosses over.\n\nClaimant exhibits run C-1 onward, the production Bates numbers stay, and the set freezes once it's exchanged on 06/02/2027. The Lien Sheet stays in Atty. Thorne's set.\n\nBuild your binder from the downloaded documents in your training Drive, send it to Atty. Thorne, and upload it to the CMS for the audit.\n\n{name}, dumping everything in the folder into the binder is easy. The real skill is putting in everything that belongs and flagging everything that doesn't.\n\nA Case Manager I knew once labeled every tab so beautifully with a label maker that nobody noticed tab two was empty. Pretty isn't complete.\n\nEverything that belongs goes in. Everything else gets flagged.",
   "ask": "{name}, which document proves the threshold, and which one must never be used to prove it?"
  },
  "audit": {
   "say": "This is the desk's exhibit index from the fourteenth of May, and remember, Harbor Point is on the other side of this hearing now.\n\nDecide for each exhibit which tab it belongs in, or that it's withheld. Apply the quality check as you sort, not after.\n\nSo, what's wrong? The Confidential Mediation Statement is sitting at C-2 for background. Nobody has asked a single provider for a certification, and the authentication log hasn't been started.\n\nTab one has no exhaustion record at all, so there's nothing proving the bodily injury layer is gone. And the Lien Sheet claims five thousand dollars for Harbor Point MedPay, which gave up any reimbursement right in writing in March.\n\n{name}, that's four problems I've told you about. Here's your mystery: there's at least one more I haven't.\n\nWhen the other side changes, what you send changes too.\n\nFind it before you hand it in, and then you'll see the set we exchanged on the second of June.",
   "ask": "{name}, Harbor Point is the opponent now. What changes about what we send?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check before you log. Tab one has the snapshot and the 06/11/2027 brief slot, never a draft. Threshold exhibits come first: the $10,000 tender, consents, Limited Release, dismissal and both Coverage Letters.\n\nTab two holds current versions only, with the pre-filing draft gone. Tab three has the certified RPD-26-018842 and color stills from the original file. Tab four ties $14,817.40 to certified bills, with certifications requested from each provider.\n\nOn tab five, pull and pair the 2021 records with the MRI read and Causation Letter, but don't argue causation. A supplemental opinion from Dr. Reyes is Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nThe Lien Sheet drops Harbor Point and Summit, leaving $7,965.00. Tab six is wages, $1,584.00.\n\nAnd the impasse memo is the privilege test. It never goes in tab six.\n\n{name}, every one of those checks is a piece of the award. Skip one, and the arbitrator might skip a bill.\n\nIf you're not sure it can go out, Atty. Thorne decides.",
   "ask": "{name}, where did you put the 09/08/2026 impasse memo, and who decides whether it's ever exchanged?"
  }
 },
 "d4m3-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Last Learning Check of Day 4, and we're playing Index Face-Off. Put your two master indexes side by side before you start.\n\nWhen I call your name, you've got ten seconds to find any document I name in your index. {name}, you're up first: find the Police Report.\n\nThis check is about the binders you actually built, not your memory of my slides. Answer from the file, in your own words, typed right under each question.\n\nYou'll name the six mediation sections with a document for each, and explain what the master index records and why it's the source of truth. Then trace the unbelted defense, the argument that John wasn't wearing his seatbelt, to the exhibits that answer it.\n\nIf your index takes longer than ten seconds, that's not a fail. That's your binder trying to tell you something.\n\nAnd if you're reaching for the three-hole punch right now, put it down. It can't help you.\n\nRemember this one: if you can't find it in ten seconds, neither can Atty. Thorne.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why does the index decide when it and the printed binder disagree?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary: write the principles in your own words.\n\nThen take the scenario on the slide, John Doe on 09/04/2026 asking whether he should just take the $3,500. Write what you'd say, what you wouldn't, and what goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nOn question 7, the pressure scenario, a strong answer requests the missing records and a certification covering all 33 visits at once, tells Atty. Thorne that evening, and leaves the exchange to him. Never exchange a ledger its records don't support.\n\nThink about the road this file took. From a $3,500 offer, through a mediation that got Keystone's full $10,000, to a binding hearing on thirty thousand more. Every step was carried by a binder somebody built right.\n\n{name}, I once watched a printer jam on page one of a binder the night before a hearing. The Case Manager had a searchable PDF ready. Nobody even blinked.\n\nThat's the whole lesson: build it so nothing on the night can break it.",
   "ask": "{name}, which document would hurt John Doe most if it were missing from these binders, and which would hurt most if it were included?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last piece for today: the Skill Scale. Rate each skill from 1 to 5, honestly, then submit it for review.\n\nI'll put your rating next to your score, and if they're far apart, we'll talk about why.\n\nAnd remember, UPL is a gate here too. A strong score doesn't survive a privileged memo landing in an exchanged set.\n\nYour takeaway from today fits in one line: a Mediation Binder persuades, an arbitration binder proves.\n\nNow your mystery for tomorrow. Day 5 opens at the litigation fork, the moment a file stops negotiating and goes to court. Everything you built today was for a table where people talk. Tomorrow, some of it has to survive a courtroom.\n\n{name}, tonight, pick one document from your binders and decide: would it hold up in front of a judge, or only a mediator? Bring your answer, and your reason, in the morning.",
   "ask": "{name}, which binder skill would you rate yourself lowest on right now, and what would move it up a point?"
  }
 },
 "d4m4-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "First, last lesson's mystery. {name}, which date makes all five folders useless if it's wrong?\n\nThe Statute of Limitations, 02/06/2028. Get that one wrong and the best-organized file in the building is just a very tidy loss.\n\nNow a game: Would You Rather. Would you rather have one huge, obvious problem on a file, or ten tiny quiet ones? Hands up for huge. Hands up for tiny. {name}, defend your vote.\n\nHere's the twist: the quiet ones are worse. Put two numbers side by side, $10,000 and $40,000. How much of John Doe's coverage did Keystone's silence lock? All of it.\n\nThat's what one unmanaged bottleneck costs. A bottleneck is anything that's stuck and holding the rest of the file up, like one slow car in a single-lane tunnel.\n\nThey rarely look dramatic. They look like a quiet inbox, a record that's “on its way,” a date nobody confirmed.\n\nThere's one on this file that nearly cost us at mediation. I'll show you which.\n\nSo remember: quiet problems are the expensive ones.",
   "ask": "{name}, how much of John Doe's coverage did Keystone's silence lock?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's the definition: a bottleneck is anything that can stop the file reaching a hard date. Log it the day it starts, with the date it threatens, and age it daily.\n\nFollow up a silent adjuster every 5 business days. Keystone left 08/24 and 08/31/2026 unanswered, then the 09/02 escalation.\n\nSilence is a position, so record it as dated facts, never “adjuster busy.” “Waiting on Keystone” tells nobody anything. Say what, since when, and what date it puts at risk.\n\nA record unanswered after 14 days is a bottleneck. The biggest mediation one is an unverified ledger: $16,401.40, Riverton at $6,105.00, Peach State at $1,860.00.\n\nGet verified ledgers to Keystone in time for them to raise their authority, the brief to Hon. Carla Benton by 03/11/2027, and stills printed in color. Shield the 2021 low-back visits as Atty. Thorne directs. Sync calendars before offering dates.\n\nYou chase, document and flag. Legal remedies and value talk are his.\n\nEvery bottleneck gets a start date and a threatened date.",
   "ask": "{name}, when did the Keystone bottleneck start: 08/21, 08/24 or 09/04, and why that one?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run it like a real week. A follow-up goes unanswered, so you log it in the Communications Log with date, time, method and outcome, and set the next follow-up five business days out.\n\nWhen the second one goes unanswered, you open an entry in the Bottleneck Log: the blocker, the start date and the hard date it threatens. It's just the list of everything stuck waiting on someone else, and for how long. For Keystone, the start date is the first unanswered request, 08/24/2026.\n\nWhen a proposed mediation or arbitration date comes in, you run it through the Conflict Resolver, which checks it against every deadline and every party's availability. No mediation date after 03/31/2027 ever reaches Atty. Thorne as available.\n\nWhen something the binder needs is still open 10 days out, you red-flag it, like the 03/01/2027 flag for the 03/11/2027 binder.\n\nThe payoff: a clean, checked binder, and the carrier's best excuse is gone before lunch. A Case Manager I trained called that “winning by paperwork.”\n\nLog it early, and the deadline never sneaks up on you.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the start date go back to the first unanswered request instead of the day it started hurting?"
  }
 },
 "d4m4-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll set up four countermeasure rules in the CMS under Tasks, Countermeasure rules, with the Calendar and Risk Flags.\n\nRule one handles silence: follow-ups every 5 business days, then 48 hours after the second unanswered one it goes to the supervisor, and 48 more to Atty. Thorne.\n\nRule two: a record not received 14 days after request gets a second request and a subpoena flag for him. Rule three: a hard date 10 days out with something still missing gets a red flag.\n\nRule four is the 48-Hour Mandate audit before every hard date, with what it checks and a fallback plan. Two days out, you check everything's ready while there's still time to fix it. If that day's a holiday, you go earlier, never later.\n\nCalibrate rule one on Keystone, then fire all four on the mediation calendar, each with a date, action and owner.\n\n{name}, you're on rules one and two. {name}, three and four.\n\nAnd no, you can't set an alarm to remind IT about your ticket. People have tried.",
   "ask": "{name}, the ledger is still missing at the 03/09/2027 audit. Do you hold the binder, and what do you send instead?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Here's what landed on 02/22/2027. {name}, before I read it, guess how many days late a record has to be before it officially counts as a bottleneck.\n\nThe answer: a record overdue 14 days. Riverton Spine & Rehab's Final Ledger, the full list of their charges, was requested 02/08/2027 and still isn't here.\n\nThe Mediation Binder needs it, and that's due to Hon. Carla Benton (Ret.) on 03/11/2027, ahead of the 03/18/2027 session. Keystone's attendee and their authority aren't confirmed either, and this file has no countermeasure rules yet.\n\nCountermeasure rules are automatic alarms that fire when something goes quiet too long. Think smoke detector, but for silence.\n\nThe demand-stage audit holds Riverton at $6,105.00, 33 visits at $185.00. Peach State's payment statement shows $1,860.00 paid on Crescent's $3,400.00 bill.\n\nBefore we touch live dates, we calibrate. We enter the Keystone dates and watch Rule 1 fire on 09/02 and 09/04.\n\nIf an alarm can't reproduce a known record, we can't trust it on a live one.",
   "ask": "{name}, if Rule 1 doesn't fire on 09/02 and 09/04 during calibration, what would that tell you?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Let's check your dates, because every firing date is arithmetic from the file. {name}, read me your calibration dates.\n\nThey fire 09/02/2026 and 09/04/2026, with the memo on 09/08. Rule two fires 02/22/2027, that's 02/08 plus 14, with a second request that day. Your subpoena flag reads as a question for Atty. Thorne, never as decided.\n\nRule three flags 03/01/2027 for the ledger and 03/08/2027 for Keystone's attendee with authority. That second one is the one people forget. Rule four audits 03/09 and 03/16/2027.\n\nPeach State's $1,860.00 closes Crescent's bill, so the $1,540.00 written off is never a lien.\n\nYour fallback includes the $6,105.00 interim ledger, the running bill so far, 33 visits at $185.00. It stands in until the clinic's Final Ledger arrives.\n\nEvery firing has a date, action and owner, and no rule messages anyone outside on its own.\n\nI once saw an alarm that never fired because the computer had shown “restart required” for three weeks.\n\nAn alarm you haven't tested is just a decoration.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of your firing dates would you double-check first, and why that one?"
  }
 },
 "d4m4-l2": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Mystery from last lesson first. {name}, what got a Keystone person with real authority into that mediation room?\n\nTwo things: verified ledgers sent early enough for Keystone to raise its authority, and a flag to confirm who was coming and what they could pay.\n\nNow a game: Spot the Problem. I read you a real escalation, you shout what's missing. Ready? “Keystone still not responding, please advise.”\n\nGo. {name}, what's missing? {name}, what else?\n\nWhen was the last contact? What deadline is at risk? What's been tried? What does he need to decide?\n\nIt reads like an IT ticket that just says “computer broken.” IT can't fix that, and Atty. Thorne can't decide from it. Without dates, the deadline at risk and a clear ask, he has to do your research before he can act.\n\nThe ladder decides who hears about a problem and when. The report decides whether they can act on it in one read.\n\nAnd by the end, I'll show you the one word on the perfect report that does most of the work.",
   "ask": "{name}, what does Atty. Thorne have to look up before he can act on “Keystone still not responding, please advise”?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Climb one step at a time and log each attempt: the source, then its supervisor with a written Notice of Delay, then Atty. Thorne. That notice states facts only and never accuses anyone of bad faith.\n\nThe 48-Hour Mandate times the climb. Every rung gets 48 hours, and if it stays quiet, the file moves up one rung. Think of it as a kitchen timer for silence.\n\nFor Keystone, the second follow-up was 08/31, the supervisor got it 09/02, silence on 09/04 sent it to Atty. Thorne, and the impasse memo followed on 09/08/2026.\n\nIt also means auditing ledgers, binders and searchable scans 48 hours before every hard date. Searchable means we ran OCR, which turns a picture of a page into words the computer can find.\n\nStep three is where the legal tools live. You can name one as a question, but he decides, signs and sends.\n\nEvery report carries five fields: blocker, start date, hard date, what was tried, and the ask. Zero defaults: by the hard date, file what you have marked pending, or he holds a docketed extension.\n\nReport up weekly, never out. Don't skip, don't sit.",
   "ask": "{name}, who should have heard about the Keystone silence first: Dana Whitfield's supervisor or Atty. Thorne?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's the rhythm on the job. When step one goes unanswered on schedule, you send a written Notice of Delay to the supervisor or office manager from the Email Workspace. Name the item, its start date and the hard date, and log it.\n\nIf step two sits unanswered for 48 hours, you escalate to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate, with all five fields filled in and one ask per blocker.\n\nEvery week, on reporting day, you send him the Bottleneck Log, each item with its age and days to its hard date.\n\nAnd if a hard date arrives with the blocker still open, you file what you have, marked pending, or you confirm his extension is docketed. Zero defaults.\n\nI once watched a Case Manager hold a whole exhibit package for one late record, waiting, waiting, and the deadline passed with nothing filed. The record showed up the next morning. Nice of it.\n\n{name}, the deadline never waits for the last record. So neither do you.",
   "ask": "{name}, a hard date arrives and one record is still missing. What are your only two acceptable options?"
  }
 },
 "d4m4-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll sort all five in the CMS, using the Conflict Resolver, the Bottleneck Log and Tasks, Escalate. That's the calendar checker, the stuck-items list, and the button that sends a task up to Atty. Thorne.\n\nFor each, name the hard date it threatens, the ladder step it's reached and the countermeasure that applies.\n\nThen put them in order: what goes to Atty. Thorne first, what you fix yourself today, and what the 48-hour audit checks and when.\n\nYou hand in one bottleneck report, sent 05/24/2027, with every item that needs his decision, five fields and one ask each.\n\nAnd there's a live call with Teresa Okafor. Ask whether Cardinal State has a lawyer, get the format and exchange method confirmed in writing, and decline any talk about the gap or value. {name}, you're taking the call. {name}, you're writing the report.\n\nIf she asks your opinion on the gap, declining isn't rude. It's the most professional thing anyone says on that call.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these can still hurt John Doe after 06/22/2027?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's Monday, 05/24/2027, nine days to the 06/02/2027 exhibit exchange before Laura Whitcomb, Esq. Five open items. And remember the mystery: one of them is late because of us.\n\nItem one, Crescent Pain & Spine's certified records for the 05/15/2026 injection: requested 05/05, second request 05/19, nothing yet.\n\nTwo, Dr. Reyes's declaration shielding the 2021 low-back visits, and Lakeside says she's away until 06/01/2027.\n\nThree, Cardinal State's Teresa Okafor hasn't confirmed the hearing format or how exhibits get exchanged, and the 05/21 supervisor escalation is unanswered.\n\nFour, Riverton's refreshed ledger shows $6,290.00, the old duplicate charge back again.\n\nFive, the copy set prints the doorbell stills in black and white, and the scans aren't searchable. Nobody ran OCR, so the computer sees pictures of pages, not words. That came off the printer stuck on black and white since somebody's memo about toner costs.\n\nShow of hands: which do you tackle first? {name}, you first. Most people pick the reprint because it's easy.\n\nEasy isn't first. The nearest decision is.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of these five do you tackle first, and what's your reason?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your sorting before you log it. Every item maps to the 06/02/2027 exchange, inside the red-flag window.\n\nItems one, two and three go to Atty. Thorne first, in one report on 05/24/2027, because each needs his decision.\n\nOn item three, check CMS Parties for a lawyer first, and never email the arbitrator. That goes through Atty. Thorne. Its 48 hours ran out on a Sunday, so it's at step three today.\n\nItem four you fix yourself: the set keeps $6,105.00 with the duplicate documented, because $6,290.00 invites the carriers to doubt every number. Item five, reprint in color and make the scans searchable. The audit lands Friday 05/28/2027, ahead of Memorial Day.\n\nAnd the mystery solved: item one's real cause was our own late request.\n\n{name}, that's the lesson. Blaming the clinic is easy. Asking what we could have sent sooner is what makes you better.\n\nFinal challenge: which item could still be fixed without anyone outside the firm?\n\nBefore you blame the source, check your own sent folder.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the 48-hour audit move to Friday 05/28/2027 instead of the following Tuesday?"
  }
 },
 "d4m4-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Learning Check, lightning round. Answer from this file, in your own words, typed under each question.\n\nI'll call on people at random to read one answer aloud, so make them ones you're proud of. {name}, you're warming up the room.\n\nYou'll name the three bottleneck families with this file's examples, walk Rule 1 on the Keystone record with every date, and explain what failed if Keystone's person shows up without authority.\n\nQuestion 5 is the pressure scenario, and it's the whole module in one question. If you'd hold the binder there, you've failed the 48-Hour Mandate.\n\nNaming the rule is half an answer. A full answer names the rule, the date, and what you'd send.\n\nAnd if your laptop picks this exact moment to install updates, that's not a bottleneck. That's a tradition.",
   "ask": "{name}, in question 5, what are the four things you do when the ledger still hasn't arrived?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your Self-Summary, the principles in your own words, plus the scenario on the slide.\n\nBefore you write, think about question 7. Teresa Okafor calls. The right answer takes the message, says Atty. Thorne will respond, and logs it, without touching the gap argument or the offer.\n\nQuestion 6 tests the ladder. 14 days unanswered on 05/19/2027 sends a Notice of Delay to Lakeside's office manager that day, and 48 hours of silence moves it to Atty. Thorne on 05/21/2027.\n\nWhy does it matter so much? The carriers' best weapon in arbitration isn't a clever argument. It's a missing record. Every bottleneck you clear early is one less weapon in their hands.\n\nA Case Manager I trained used to say her job was being “professionally annoying, in writing.” {name}, give me your version of that in one sentence.\n\nClear it early, and they've got nothing to swing.",
   "ask": "{name}, Teresa Okafor calls and wants to talk about the gap. What do you say to her?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Finally, the Skill Scale. Rate each skill from 1 to 5, honestly, then submit it for review.\n\nI'll compare your rating with your score, and where there's a big gap, we'll sit down and talk it through.\n\nOne last reminder: UPL is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive a value conversation with a carrier, no matter how well everything else went.\n\nIf you remember one thing from this module, make it this: a silent file is a file in trouble.\n\nNow the mystery. The file is in suit, the bottlenecks are logged, and every alarm is set. But John Doe still has to sit across a table from Robert Smith's lawyer and answer questions under oath, the deposition we prepped for earlier today.\n\n{name}, think about everything we've logged on this file. Which one fact do you predict the defense will ask him about first, and is he ready for it?",
   "ask": "{name}, which escalation skill from today do you most want to practice again before your next file?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Okay, quick game: true or false, hands up for true. One, an adjuster's deadline can bend if you ask nicely. Two, a court's deadline can bend if you ask nicely.\n\n{name}, I see you, your hand went up for number two. Bold. Honestly, I love the optimism, but no.\n\nHere's where we are. Our 07/20/2026 Time-Limited Demand to Keystone lapsed unaccepted on 08/21/2026, and the 48-Hour Mandate got no answer. So Atty. Thorne made the call, and on 09/24/2026 John Doe's file became Civil Action No. 26-CV-04417.\n\nFrom that day on, a judge sets the pace, not an adjuster.\n\nSo we keep two things. A Pleadings Log, which is just our running list of everything filed and served. And a Discovery Calendar, which is every date the other side owes us something, or we owe them.\n\nHere's the one thing to carry out of this slide: once we file, the judge holds the stopwatch.\n\nOne warning before we go on. There's one kind of paper in this lesson that, if you ignore it, answers itself, and not in our favor. I'll show you which one.",
   "ask": "{name}, now that we're in suit, who decides the very next legal step, and who makes sure it actually happens on time?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "First rule, and it's a big one: litigation isn't just a longer negotiation. From the 09/24/2026 filing, the court sets the pace.\n\nBefore Atty. Thorne signs the Complaint, you check the filing date against the SOL, the deadline to sue, and you confirm the defendant's exact legal name.\n\nService is the proof the defendant knew he was sued, so log every attempt. Still unserved at day 14, that was 10/08/2026, and you escalate for Skip-Trace authority.\n\nA Skip-Trace is a paid professional search for someone's current address, so Atty. Thorne approves it first. Smith was served 10/14/2026.\n\nNow, the paper I warned you about: the RFAs. Leave one unanswered past its deadline and it may be deemed admitted, meaning the court treats it as true. That's the paper that answers itself.\n\nWhen discovery lands, calendar it that day and book a Discovery Intake Meeting. You could mail John Doe all 70 requests with a note saying “fill these out.” Please don't. Sit down with him, one request at a time.\n\nYou track and draft; Atty. Thorne decides and signs. And you never tell John Doe how to answer. {name}, that one's not negotiable.\n\nUnanswered RFAs answer themselves. Calendar them the day they land.",
   "ask": "{name}, an RFA lands on your desk at 4:55 on a Friday. What do you do before you go home?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "So here's how it plays out on the job. Atty. Thorne authorizes suit.\n\nBefore he signs, you confirm Robert Smith's exact legal name from an independent record, like the Declaration Page or DMV records, not the Police Report alone.\n\nI once saw a complaint use the defendant's middle name as his last name, because somebody copied it off a crash report in a hurry. It took weeks to fix. Weeks.\n\nOurs is filed 09/24/2026, well inside our 02/04/2028 internal hard date. It's logged in the Pleadings Log, with the stamped complaint in 01_PLEADINGS the same day.\n\nA service attempt fails? Log it. At day 14, 10/08/2026, you escalate to Atty. Thorne with the attempt log, and run the Skip-Trace once he approves.\n\nWhen the RFAs land, you calendar each response with the math written in, and alert him within the hour, time-stamped.\n\nWhy so strict, {name}? Because one deemed admission can hand the defense the very fact our whole case depends on.\n\nGet the name right, log every attempt, and calendar every RFA within the hour.",
   "ask": "{name}, why doesn't the Police Report alone count as proof of Robert Smith's legal name?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. Repair the Pleadings Log and Discovery Calendar for 26-CV-04417, working in CMS under Litigation, Calendar and Documents.\n\nAdd every missing row, recompute each response date from its trigger and its method of service, and write the math right into the row.\n\nFile each document in its numbered sub-folder with a proper name, and calendar the Scheduling Order's six dates.\n\nThen the graded call. The RFA deadline never had its own row, and it's passed. Prove compliance from that Certificate of Service, record the misses honestly, and report to Atty. Thorne today.\n\nPartway through, Jenna Price, Atty. Mercer's paralegal, will call. Log her words exactly, with the time, take no position, tell her only that Atty. Thorne will respond, and route it to him.\n\n{name}, you're on the Certificate. {name}, you're on the RFA row. And remember, quietly hiding the miss is the easy fix. Showing it, with the proof, is the one that protects us.\n\nFix the date, write the math, and never hide a miss.",
   "ask": "{name}, the log said 12/31/2026, which was too early. Why fix a date that could only have made us early?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 01/05/2027, Track B, the road where the case went to suit. Quick bet before I show you anything.\n\n{name}, how many mistakes can a three-month-old Pleadings Log collect if nobody checks it? Throw out a number.\n\nCivil Action No. 26-CV-04417 is three months old. The docket calendar was audited and corrected on 12/10/2026, but nobody reconciled the CMS Pleadings Log to it, and now the check shows five mismatches.\n\nThe executed Affidavit of Service, filed 10/16/2026, is still sitting in an Allied Process Services email with no row. That's the process server's sworn statement that Smith was handed the lawsuit, our only proof he was served.\n\nThe Interrogatories and RFPs mailed 12/01/2026 share one row that still says due 12/31/2026, while the calendar shows 01/04/2027. And there's no row at all for the 15 RFAs.\n\nThe Scheduling Order is saved as scan_1210.pdf. And a Certificate of Service dated 01/04/2027 sits in Documents, unlogged. That's our signed line saying when and how we sent papers to the other side.\n\nHere's the lesson hiding in this mess: a log nobody reconciles slowly turns into fiction.\n\nIf you've ever named a file scan_1210, don't worry, we've all got one. Okay, ninety seconds. Find what's wrong.",
   "ask": "{name}, in ninety seconds, how many problems can you find in this log as it stands?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log this, let's go row by row, and yes, I'm calling on people.\n\n{name}, is there an affidavit row, personal service 10/14/2026 by Allied Process Services, filed 10/16/2026, saved in 02_SERVICE_DOCS?\n\nDid you correct the Interrogatories and RFPs to 01/04/2027 with the count written out: 12/01 plus 30, plus 3 for mail, Sunday rolled to Monday? The 15 RFAs need their own row with a red deemed-admission flag.\n\nIs the Certificate of Service logged and checked, signed verification attached? Smith's responses go on 01/13/2027, with no extra 3 days for e-service on this file.\n\nE-service just means papers sent electronically, and whether it adds days depends on the court's rules, which Atty. Thorne confirms.\n\nThe Scheduling Order renamed, filed in 01_PLEADINGS, all six dates in. And the late rows recorded as misses, not back-dated. Back-dating is the one thing that turns a mistake into a problem nobody can fix.\n\nA miss you record is a mistake. A miss you back-date is a cover-up.\n\nYour mystery for the next activity: one date on the trial-prep checklist is still from the old calendar. {name}, hunt it down.",
   "ask": "{name}, explain why the e-served set doesn't get three extra mail days, and who confirms that rule?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-l2": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Would you rather: an adjuster's letter with ten deadlines, or one page from a judge with six? {name}, pick one and tell me why.\n\nMy answer? I'll take the adjuster every time, because the judge's page is the one nobody gets to argue with.\n\nThis is the Scheduling Order, entered 12/10/2026, and it now runs John Doe's case to a 09/13/2027 trial calendar. Think of it as the judge's calendar for the case: every big deadline, from experts to trial, in one order nobody can ignore.\n\nEvery date in it is absolute. So how do we keep any of them from sneaking up on us? Two tools.\n\nA trial-prep checklist turns each date into a task somebody owns. And KPIs, which are just measurable scores, prove week by week that each task is ahead of its deadline.\n\nThe order sets the dates, the checklist assigns them, and the numbers show we're winning the race. Oh, and one KPI on this list is something most firms never track. It's the one that saves trials. Stick with me.\n\nRemember it this way: the order sets it, the checklist owns it, the KPI proves it.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does one page signed by a judge outrank every adjuster letter in this file?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The Scheduling Order controls, full stop. Pin it to the front of the file with 30-, 14- and 7-day alerts on every date.\n\nThat's our experts 03/15/2027, theirs 04/15/2027, mediation by 03/31/2027, discovery closing 05/28/2027, pretrial 08/12/2027 and trial calendar 09/13/2027.\n\nEvery extension goes in writing. Once Atty. Thorne agrees, you draft the same-day confirmation for his signature, in his name.\n\nTwo traps to dodge. One is treating court deadlines like adjuster deadlines. The other is letting the client talk to the other side. If Keystone or a defense investigator reaches John Doe, step in and tell Atty. Thorne that day.\n\nFor the 02/18/2027 deposition, you handle the reporter, the link, the tech check and the packet by 02/11/2027. Prepare the process, never the testimony.\n\nAnd here's that KPI I promised. Every KPI gets a formula and a target, because “stay on top of it” isn't a measure. {name}, that's the one most firms skip.\n\nA KPI needs a number and a deadline, or it's just a hope.",
   "ask": "{name}, defense counsel will spend John Doe's deposition on the 14-Day Gap. What's your job before 02/18/2027, and where does it stop?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's do a week in the life. The Scheduling Order is entered 12/10/2026. You pin it to the file, calendar all six dates with three alerts each, that's 18 alerts, and file the order in 01_PLEADINGS.\n\nThen Atty. Mercer's office calls, and an extension gets agreed by phone. It's tempting to hang up, say “great, we're good,” and move on.\n\nInstead, you confirm Atty. Thorne actually approved it, draft the written confirmation for his signature, send it that same day in his name, and recalendar. No Scheduling Order date moves without a court order.\n\nA Case Manager I knew once trusted a handshake extension. Three weeks later, opposing counsel “didn't recall” it. Funny how that works.\n\nThen the deposition is noticed. You book the reporter, the link and the tech check, and send the attorney-approved packet by 02/11/2027, confirming John Doe got it.\n\nAnd every week you run the docket audit, every KPI with its formula, target and dated reading, and every miss gets routed. {name}, that weekly habit is the whole game.\n\nIf it isn't in writing, it didn't get agreed.",
   "ask": "{name}, opposing counsel says “we'll paper the extension later.” What do you send, and when?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-subp": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Two tools, two different jobs. A Subpoena Duces Tecum, which is Latin for “bring it with you,” orders someone outside the lawsuit to produce documents, like a hospital, a phone company or an employer.\n\nA trial subpoena orders a witness to show up and testify: fact witnesses, treating doctors, police officers. One brings paper, the other brings a person.\n\nNow the warning. A request for “all records of any kind” is what the other side calls a fishing expedition, and they can file a Motion to Quash, which asks the court to cancel it.\n\nThe model is narrow: emergency room records, triage notes and imaging, one client, the accident date only. Ask for the ocean and you get nothing. Ask for one fish and it arrives fast.\n\nAnd the order never changes: you draft, you notice opposing counsel, you serve the custodian, and you track compliance. {name}, in your own words, why does notice come first?\n\nNarrow, noticed, then served. Every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, why would a court care whether a subpoena is narrow, even when the records are about our own client?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: Who Has to Answer? I'll name someone, you shout “has to” or “doesn't have to” answer our questions right now.\n\nRobert Smith, the defendant. The hospital's records custodian. Ruth Calloway, the witness. {name}, you take the tiebreaker on Ruth.\n\nHere's the reveal. Robert Smith has to, because he's a party in the lawsuit. The custodian and Ruth Calloway don't, not unless the court commands it. That command is a subpoena.\n\nIt's powerful, and it comes with rules. Serve it out of order, write it too broadly, or let the records come back after discovery closes on 05/28/2027, and Atty. Thorne could walk into trial without the evidence.\n\nAnd trust me, the first time you call a records department, someone will cheerfully tell you they've “never heard of” a patient whose MRI they did. Today you learn to make that call go very differently.\n\nParties have to answer. Everyone else needs the court to ask.",
   "ask": "{name}, who on John Doe's file holds evidence we need but owes us nothing unless the court commands it, and why?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on John Doe's file. The trigger is a refused request, or a record Atty. Thorne needs from its custodian. You ask him whether he wants a records subpoena, and he decides, signs and issues it.\n\nYou draft on the court-approved form with the case style, Civil Action No. 26-CV-04417, the named custodian and a narrow Exhibit A: one subject, exact dates, named record types.\n\nA copy goes to Atty. Grant Mercer first, and only after the notice period does Allied Process Services serve the custodian.\n\nFive days before production you make the compliance call, and you pay the copy invoice promptly, because an unpaid invoice is the most popular reason records go missing. Everything comes back before 05/28/2027.\n\nTrial subpoenas go only to the witnesses Atty. Thorne names, tasked against 08/12/2027 and 09/13/2027. {name}, why do you think we call five days before, not the day of?\n\nHe decides, you draft, notice goes first, and the records come home before discovery closes.",
   "ask": "{name}, the custodian says on the compliance call that the request is too broad. What do you do, and who decides?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's run one through. Atty. Thorne needs the Riverton Fire Run Report from its custodian. You route that question to him through Tasks, and you draft nothing until he decides.\n\nOnce he approves, you build it from the template with a narrow Exhibit A: run RF-26-0206-114, Medic 7, John Doe, 02/06/2026 only.\n\nThe notice to Atty. Mercer is dated before service, every single time. I once audited a file where the notice and the service had the same date, and the defense argued for a month about which happened first. Nobody wins that argument.\n\nThen, while it's open, you review its row in the subpoena log every ten days, call the custodian five days before production, and log each reading with its date. Any miss goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\nWhy? Because a record that arrives after discovery closes may never reach the jury.\n\nSo here's your mystery, {name}: which record on John Doe's file is most likely to need a subpoena next, and why that one?",
   "ask": "{name}, which two dates on a subpoena log row tell you, at a glance, whether notice came first?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's what you'll do. In the CMS, audit the checklist line by line against the 12/10/2026 Scheduling Order. Every date right, every task with an owner, each with 30-, 14- and 7-day alerts.\n\nAdd the 02/18/2027 deposition logistics and the prep document set.\n\nThen build the KPI tracker in CMS under Litigation: all nine KPIs, each with its formula and target, this file's reading with its source, and met, missed or open.\n\nClose with one task each for the three review lenses: coverage, treatment and liability. Think of them as three pairs of glasses. Is there money to pay, is the care documented and on track, and can we prove who caused the crash?\n\n{name}, you're on the dates. {name}, you're on the KPIs. The graded call is choosing which two items go to Atty. Thorne first.\n\nAnd fair warning, e-filing portals have a sixth sense for the moment you're in a hurry. Build your alerts so you're never filing at 11:59 p.m. A checklist with tasks is fine. One with owners, alerts and proof is what we want.\n\nEvery date gets an owner, three alerts and a source.",
   "ask": "{name}, which two items go to Atty. Thorne first, and why those two?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 01/20/2027, and a notice just landed. {name}, before I read it, guess what's being scheduled.\n\nHere it is: John Doe's deposition, 02/18/2027, 10:00 a.m., by videoconference, with Riverton Court Reporting. The link comes from the reporter, and they want us to confirm attendance.\n\nNow the problem. Our trial-prep checklist was drafted from the calendar as first keyed, before the 12/10/2026 audit, and nobody ever reconciled it.\n\nIt shows our expert disclosure on 04/15/2027, lists mediation 03/31/2027 as if that's the session date, plans records subpoenas for June 2027, and sources the final records run from the old Demand Package.\n\nThere are no alerts and no deposition tasks. Smith's responses came in 01/13/2027 and were never docketed, and there's no KPI tracker at all.\n\nHonestly, a wrong checklist is worse than no checklist, because people trust it. So put it beside the Scheduling Order and find the first wrong date.",
   "ask": "{name}, which date on the checklist is wrong first, what should it read, and what does the error risk?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check before you log it. {name}, our expert date should now read 03/15/2027, with 04/15/2027 kept as the defense date. Mediation says completed by 03/31/2027.\n\nRecords subpoenas return before 05/28/2027, and 04/28/2027 is flagged as the last day to e-serve a 30-day request, confirmed with Atty. Thorne. All six dates carry three alerts, 18 in all.\n\nThe deposition notice is docketed by 01/22/2027, the reporter confirmed, calendars aligned and the packet set for 02/11/2027.\n\nYour Gap Record lists the documents: the EMS report, the Northgate attendance, the texts to Margaret, the MRI read, the Causation Letter. And nothing scripted for John Doe. Nothing.\n\nNine KPIs with formula, target, sourced reading and status. And Smith's 01/13/2027 responses logged as a docket miss and routed.\n\nFacts and documents go in the Gap Record. Never a script.\n\nNow your mystery for the Learning Check. John Doe goes quiet before his deposition. {name}, what's your first move, and who hears about it?",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, what's the difference between a Gap Record and a script for John?"
  }
 },
 "d5m1-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Welcome to the Learning Check, game-show edition. Every answer comes from John Doe's file, in your own words, typed right under the question. {name}, you're our first contestant.\n\nQuestion 1 is the gate, so take it slowly. Work the 12/01/2026 response date step by step.\n\nThen explain how the count changes for a set e-served by consent, meaning both sides agreed in writing to trade papers electronically, and tell me why you'd confirm the court's rule.\n\nIf your answer is 12/31/2026 or 01/03/2027, that's the buzzer. Stop and rework the count before you go on.\n\nAfter that, name Atty. Mercer's three defenses and the evidence on file you'd pull against each, and tell me where the affidavit, the RFAs and the Scheduling Order get filed and named.\n\nNo phone-a-friend, but you can phone the calendar. And no, the cousin who's “basically a paralegal” doesn't count either.\n\nThe count is three steps every time: trigger, plus days, then roll the weekend.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you start, what are the three steps in counting from a mailed trigger?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the self-summary, in your own words. But first, picture this: opposing counsel wants two weeks at 4:45 p.m. and says they'll paper it later. Who can say yes?\n\nOnly Atty. Thorne, and only in writing, the same day.\n\nA Case Manager I trained once said “sure, no problem” on a call like that, just to be friendly. Friendly cost us three weeks of arguing.\n\nOkay, your scenario. It's 01/28/2027. John Doe has been unreachable for six days, his deposition is noticed for 02/18/2027, and defense counsel wants to inspect Margaret's Civic.\n\nWrite your next five actions in order, and next to each one, who owns it. Be specific about what's yours and what goes to Atty. Thorne.\n\n“Call John again” isn't an answer, it's a shrug. I want five steps, five owners, and a reason for the order. {name}, I'll read yours first.",
   "ask": "{name}, opposing counsel wants two weeks at 4:45 p.m. and will paper it later. Who can say yes, and what happens if nobody writes it down?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step. Rate yourself on each competency from 1 to 5, honestly, then submit it for review.\n\nI'll compare your rating with your score. So if you gave yourself a 5 on deadlines but missed the count in Question 1, we'll talk about that gap. It's not a gotcha, I promise. It's how we find what to practice next.\n\nAnd remember, UPL, giving legal advice when you're not a lawyer, is a gate. A strong score doesn't survive an ethics breach.\n\nAn honest rating today is a better coaching plan tomorrow.\n\nHere's your mystery for the Property Damage check coming up next. The Civic's screen had a number untouched since April that was wrong in two directions at once.\n\n{name}, can you name it, and both directions, without looking?",
   "ask": "{name}, where do you expect the biggest gap between your self-rating and your score?"
  }
 },
 "d5m2-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Remember the question from Day 1? Does Property Damage touch our case? Well, it's back, and this time there's money on the table.\n\nQuick true or false, hands up for true. Keystone offering Margaret $792.00 for her Civic lowers John Doe's settlement. {name}, true or false?\n\nFalse. Margaret's Loss of Use and Diminished Value are her own claim, on the separate file the firm opened for her, paid from Keystone's $25,000 Property Damage limit, outside John's $40,000 stack. His numbers don't move.\n\nBut here's the catch. Three things from that file can still reach him: a release worded too widely, the photos and estimates already in his severity set, and the rental dates behind his gap.\n\nSo today's job is telling which is which, and routing.\n\nMargaret's car money is her own claim. John's numbers don't move, but watch the release.",
   "ask": "{name}, of those three, which one could hurt John Doe the most if nobody caught it?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Let me paint two Case Managers. The first sees a car offer, figures it has nothing to do with John, and forwards the paperwork to whoever asked.\n\nThe second reads the release before the numbers. Keystone's 12/10/2026 release covers all claims arising from the 02/06/2026 accident and asks Mr. Doe to sign. Uh-oh.\n\nA Property Damage release must never release the injury claim. So you check the claim number, KM-26-440871-PD for the car against KM-26-440871 for John, you hold it, and you route it to Atty. Thorne the same day.\n\nJohn signs nothing. Keep the photos, the estimate and the $2,315.00 supplement identical on both files, and the rental dates too.\n\nAnd never accept, reject, counter or value a car figure. {name}, be the second Case Manager.\n\nRead the release before the numbers, every time.",
   "ask": "{name}, the release says Mr. Doe. Which Mr. Doe could sign it, and why does that worry us?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it plays out on the job. Keystone sends a release: you check its scope and claim number, hold it, and escalate it to Atty. Thorne in Tasks. Nobody gets asked to sign.\n\nKeystone restates its figure: you log it on Margaret's Property Damage file, and John's Net Sheet doesn't change by a penny.\n\nDefense counsel asks for the car documents in discovery: you confirm the severity set and the rental dates match the Property Damage file, page by page.\n\nI once saw a release signed in a parking lot because the client wanted lunch. Nobody wants that story, {name}.\n\nA small thing today, a release held for one afternoon, protects the whole injury case.",
   "ask": "{name}, Keystone moves its offer up. Explain what changes on John Doe's Net Sheet, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d5m2-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Your task has five moves. First, read the release, its scope and its claim number, before you look at a single dollar figure.\n\nSecond, hold it and route it to Atty. Thorne in Tasks, escalated the same day.\n\nThird, sort each outcome: the release, the Diminished Value figure, the Loss of Use figure, the severity set and the rental dates. For each one, does it reach John's settlement or stay on Margaret's file, and why, in one line.\n\nFourth, take Leah Moran's call, and accept nothing, reject nothing, counter nothing.\n\nFifth, write Atty. Thorne a short memo that ends in one line: does this touch John's case, and how? {name}, I'll read yours first.\n\nScope first, dollars second, Atty. Thorne decides.",
   "ask": "{name}, what will you say when Leah asks if Mr. Doe will just sign today?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 12/10/2026, and an email just landed from Leah Moran at Keystone's Property Damage desk. Before I open it, {name}, guess what's attached. A check?\n\nNope. A release. Keystone's final position on Margaret's Civic is $792.00 for Diminished Value and $0 for Loss of Use.\n\nAnd the release covers all claims arising from the 02/06/2026 accident, for Mr. Doe to sign so payment can go out. She's calling this afternoon.\n\nMeanwhile, John's injury suit is in discovery, and Atty. Thorne wants to know what any of this changes for John.\n\nHere's a truth from the job: an adjuster's deadline is always this afternoon, and our answer is always whenever Atty. Thorne decides.\n\nRelease in hand? Read the scope before the dollars.",
   "ask": "{name}, before any math, what's the first thing you check on that release?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log it, check your work, and I'm picking on a few of you.\n\nWas the release flagged and routed the same day, with nobody asked to sign? Were the claim numbers checked? Did Diminished Value and Loss of Use land on Margaret's file?\n\nAre John's liens and Net Sheet unchanged, because Keystone's Property Damage money sits outside his stack? Do the severity set and the rental dates match on both files? Was Leah told only that it's with Atty. Thorne?\n\nOne more thing about the memo. A memo that just lists numbers makes Atty. Thorne do the thinking. A good one leads with the decision he has to make and ends with the answer.\n\nLead with the decision, end with the answer.\n\n{name}, read me your last line.",
   "ask": "{name}, when Leah asked whether he'd take it, what exactly did you say?"
  }
 },
 "d5m2-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Property Damage transition, game-show style. Every answer from John Doe's file, in your own words, right under each question.\n\nFirst: Keystone's release covers all claims. Why must it never reach John, and what do you do with it?\n\nSecond, sort Keystone's final position. What reaches John's settlement, what stays on Margaret's file, and why?\n\nThird, which car documents already serve John's case, and what has to match on both files?\n\n{name}, you're up first. Fair warning, the buzzer is very sensitive today.\n\nCar money stays on Margaret's file. A wide release never reaches John.",
   "ask": "{name}, in one breath: what does $792.00 do to John Doe's Net Sheet?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now the Self-Summary, in your own words. Start with that one line from Day 1, upgraded for settlement: how does Property Damage touch John Doe's case now, and how doesn't it?\n\nThen the pressure scenario. Leah wants an answer today and her release is on your desk. What's your next action, and why that one rather than something else?\n\nLast, name the thing you're least sure of. {name}, I'm going to ask you to read yours, so make it honest.\n\nHold it, route it, and let Atty. Thorne decide.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's your next action with the release, and why that one first?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Finally, rate yourself on each skill, 1 to 5, and submit it for review. I'll sit your rating next to your score.\n\nIf you gave yourself a 5 on a block you scored 60 on, we'll talk. If you gave yourself a 2 on something you aced, we'll talk about that too. Modesty gets a conversation, same as overconfidence.\n\nThe skill I'll look at first today is reading a release for its scope, because that's the one that protects John's whole case. {name}, where'd you land on that one?\n\nHonest self-scores are how we find what to practice next.",
   "ask": "{name}, which skill here did you rate lowest, and what would move it up a point?"
  }
 },
 "d5m3-l1": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "Okay, the reveal. The sentence is “Don't worry, you'll do great.” Sounds kind, right? It's a prediction, and predictions aren't ours to make.\n\nSo let's get the ideas down. A deposition can shape the whole outcome of a litigated case, meaning one that's gone to court. Atty. Thorne owns the legal strategy. You prepare the client for the process and the nerves.\n\nThat starts with a landmine audit, which just means reading the file for anything the defense will pounce on, so counsel sees it first.\n\nWhy bother? Because consistency is credibility. The defense lines up John's testimony with his signed answers and his records, and any mismatch looks like hiding something.\n\nThen there's the TV myth. Clients expect a courtroom drama, somebody yelling “Objection!” and slamming a table. The real thing is a conference room and a laptop.\n\nAnd afterward, the defense lawyer writes a report to the insurance company on how John will come across to a jury. {name}, that report is basically a review of John, and it moves the money.\n\nCalm him with the process, never with a prediction.",
   "ask": "{name}, who reads that defense evaluation report, and why should it worry us?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: true or false, and I want hands. One: when a nervous client calls about his deposition, the first thing you do is calm him down. Two: a deposition happens in front of a judge. Three: the Case Manager tells the client what to say.\n\n{name}, you're my tiebreaker on number one. Here's the twist: they're all false, and the first one's the sneaky one.\n\nLook at the calendar. John resurfaced on 02/03/2027, fifteen days before he testifies on 02/18/2027, by video, from a room the firm doesn't control.\n\nBefore anybody calms anybody, we audit the file for everything the defense might use, so Atty. Thorne sees it first.\n\nA deposition, by the way, is just the other side's lawyer asking John questions under oath, with a court reporter typing every word.\n\nHold onto this split all module: Atty. Thorne prepares the substance, what John's testimony covers. You prepare the person and the setting.\n\nAudit first, calm second. Atty. Thorne owns the substance, you own the setting.\n\nAnd there's one sentence clients hear all the time that can wreck a deposition before it starts. I'll tell you what it is in a minute.",
   "ask": "{name}, John has fifteen days until he testifies. What has to happen before anyone starts calming him down?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Picture the Case Manager who sends John a link and says “good luck.” That's not us.\n\nWe finish the audit before 02/04/2027 and route every landmine to Atty. Thorne: the 14-Day Gap, the 19-day disruption, the three 2021 low-back chiropractic visits, the seatbelt and the degenerative MRI.\n\nYou check John's signed answers and his public social media, and any mismatch goes up the same day. Never tell him to delete a post, because deleting can look worse than the post.\n\nHis account matches his records because he reviewed them with Atty. Thorne, not because he memorised a script.\n\nThen the pillars: calm with the process, pause two seconds, tell the truth, stop talking, business casual, plain about limitations.\n\nTest the setup with him on 02/16/2027, confirm with all three parties on 02/17/2027, and nobody in the room, no notes, no messages, not even to you.\n\nWhy so strict, {name}? One surprise on the record can cost more at settlement than a month of good treatment earned.\n\nEvery landmine goes to Atty. Thorne before the defense finds it.",
   "ask": "{name}, John asks what he should say about the two weeks before urgent care. What do you tell him?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how the two weeks really run. The notice of deposition, the formal paper saying when and where John testifies, is served 01/20/2027.\n\nOne calendar entry that just says “depo” won't cut it. You calendar the deposition, both prep sessions, the 02/11/2027 packet and the day-before checklist, so 02/04, 02/11, 02/16, 02/17 and 02/18 at 10:00 a.m. are on the calendar for Atty. Thorne and John.\n\nWhen the audit's done, you route the landmine list and any discovery mismatch to Atty. Thorne before 02/04/2027, every landmine tied to its source record.\n\nOn 02/16/2027 you run the test call on John's own device, in the room he'll use, and log camera, audio, link, lighting, room and backup number, each pass or fail.\n\nOn 02/17/2027 you confirm with all three and get the exhibit set approved by Atty. Thorne.\n\n{name}, here's why this matters. The defense writes that evaluation report the same week. A calm, prepared John is a bigger number on the other side's spreadsheet.\n\nFive dates on the calendar, one prepared client.",
   "ask": "{name}, on the test call, which item would you check first, and why that one?"
  }
 },
 "d5m3-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. You'll run your segment of John's first prep session in the Call Simulator with AI John Doe.\n\nConfirm his new number, with Margaret as backup. State the logistics exactly and set a 02/16/2027 test call on the device and in the room he'll use.\n\nDeliver the three pillars and the room rules in plain words, and practise the two-second pause on neutral questions only, like “What's your name?”\n\nNow, remember my challenge, the five questions that sound like logistics but aren't? Here they come. AI John will ask what to say about the gap, the missed April therapy, the seatbelt, his 2021 chiropractor visits and the MRI.\n\nRecognise each as substance, answer none, and log each in his words to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate. Have the gap set ready to share.\n\n{name}, you're up first. {name}, you're tracking how many of the five your classmate catches.\n\nYou hand in your segment, a logistics record and John's routed questions.\n\nRecognise it, don't answer it, route it in his words.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of John's questions can you answer, and which ones can't you?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/04/2027 on Track B, the road where John Doe's case is in a lawsuit. Before I tell you what just landed, {name}, guess: after twelve days of silence, how do you think we finally reached John?\n\nHere's the reveal. John Doe went dark on 01/22/2027, and yesterday we reached him through Margaret. He has a new phone number.\n\nHis deposition is noticed for 02/18/2027 at 10:00 a.m., by videoconference with Riverton Court Reporting. And he's anxious. He's expecting a TV cross-examination, somebody pacing in front of a jury box.\n\nAtty. Thorne's note says prep session one is at 1:00 p.m., and you open it: the new number, the logistics, the test call, the three pillars and the room rules. He'll listen, then take the substance, and he wants John's gap set ready to share on screen.\n\nOne thing before we start. What decides your grade is the routing. A polished segment that answers even one substantive question fails.\n\nClients ask the big question in the smallest voice, usually right as you're saying goodbye, with “oh, one quick thing.”\n\nAnswer the process. Route the substance. That's the whole grade.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the very first thing you'll update in the CMS before the session starts?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Before you log your segment, check yourself, and I'm going to pick on a few of you.\n\n{name}, is his new number updated in the CMS under Parties, with Margaret confirmed as backup? Did you state the logistics exactly, 02/18/2027, 10:00 a.m., videoconference, Riverton Court Reporting, and set the 02/16/2027 test call?\n\nDid you explain the setting without predicting any outcome, practise the pause on neutral questions only, and give the dress talk as advice every client gets?\n\nDid you cover the room rules: private room, no one else, no notes, no phone, no messaging? And the big one: all five substantive questions met with routing, not content, each logged in John's words before the segment ended.\n\nSounding helpful is easy. Being helpful and safe is the job. Be careful with the helpful half-answer. “Always tell the truth” is a rule; “just say you had no ride” is a script.\n\nRules are help. Scripts are harm.\n\nAnd here's your mystery for the mock. On 02/16, one trap is going to work on John. Your log decides whether Atty. Thorne can fix it.",
   "ask": "{name}, explain the difference between a rule and a script, using the gap as your example?"
  }
 },
 "d5m3-l2": {
  "theory": {
   "say": "The defense has a simple goal in a deposition: get the client to contradict himself, lose his temper or exaggerate, because each one chips away at his credibility. General rules protect against that. A script can't.\n\nSo let's reset some expectations. Defense counsel may ask very broad questions, and wide questions are normal, not attacks.\n\nThere's a real difference between an estimate and a guess. An estimate rests on what you saw or felt. A guess claims what you couldn't know. Only the estimate is honest testimony.\n\nA deposition is information gathering, not a battle. Nothing gets won there, so the smallest target protects the case. And when Atty. Thorne objects, that's a formal flag on the record protecting the witness, not a sign of trouble.\n\nNow, the over-sharer problem, in one story. A client of ours was asked, “What time did you leave the house?”\n\nHe answered with the time, his breakfast, his feud with the neighbor's dog, and the fact that he'd had “maybe one beer the night before.” Nobody asked about the beer. {name}, which rule would have saved him?\n\nAnswer the question, then stop. Silence never ends up in the transcript.",
   "ask": "{name}, can you give me an honest estimate about John's crash that wouldn't be a guess?"
  },
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play a game called Don't Take the Bait. I'm defense counsel, you're the witness, and you only win by not falling for it. Ready?\n\n“So it was just the neck stiffness. Is that all?” Hands up if you'd say yes. {name}, I saw that hand.\n\nNow look at the 02/20/2026 urgent-care note: hand numbness. That one yes just fenced off a symptom.\n\nAtty. Mercer doesn't need John to lie. A hurried yes to a short list, a guess or an argument does the same damage. His Answer and the records tell us exactly where he'll dig: the gap, the disruption, the 2021 visits and the seatbelt.\n\nAnd the mystery from last lesson, the harmless word? Here it is: degenerative. If John agrees his neck is just “degenerative,” meaning worn down by age, he's handed the defense a reason the crash didn't cause his pain.\n\nSo what protects him from every one of these traps? Seven general rules and Atty. Thorne's objections. A script protects him from none of them.\n\nRules protect John everywhere. A script protects him nowhere.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, what does a hurried yes to “is that all” cost John later?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules on the job. Handing John the seven rules on a sheet with “read this” isn't prep. The Case Manager who's really good knows where the traps sit on this file.\n\nDefense questions come in four categories: personal background, crash mechanics, medical history and daily life. Clients underreport medical history most, and here that's the 2021 low-back visits.\n\nRule one is the truth, and the prior-injury question is the Liar's Trap; counsel usually has the records already. Pause two seconds and answer in words, since the court reporter can't type a nod.\n\nNever guess, answer only what's asked, then hold the silence and don't argue.\n\nThe “Is that all” trap can fence off the hand numbness, and the complete record, not memory, is the full list. Compound questions fold two facts into one yes, and John can say a question's unclear.\n\nOn an objection, John stops, then answers unless he's told not to. The EMS report, MRI and EDR are Atty. Thorne's to use.\n\nYou explain rules; you never coach answers. {name}, that's the line.",
   "ask": "{name}, which category do clients underreport, and where does that sit on John's file?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the job. Atty. Mercer's Answer, the defense's formal reply to our lawsuit, was served 11/12/2026.\n\nYou take each defense it pleads, comparative fault, causation and failure to mitigate, and map it to the questions it predicts in the deposition prep log, so each one ties to at least one trap.\n\nOnce the trap map's done, you check that the record meeting each trap is actually in Atty. Thorne's set: the EMS report, the MRI report of 03/13/2026, the 06/19/2026 Causation Letter, the EDR download and the Northgate Attendance Record.\n\nAnd when John asks you how to answer one of those traps, and trust me, he will, you log his question in his own words and route it to Atty. Thorne the same day. No wording offered, not even a little.\n\nWhy so careful? If the defense can show the Case Manager fed John his answers, his whole testimony looks rehearsed, and a rehearsed witness is worth less at the settlement table.\n\n{name}, you'll feel the pull to help. Help by routing.",
   "ask": "{name}, John asks you, “How should I answer the seatbelt question?” What exactly do you do next?"
  }
 },
 "d5m3-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "While the mock runs in the Call Simulator, you keep the Trap Log in the CMS under Litigation, Deposition prep log.\n\nFor each trap, log the timestamp, the question in AI Mercer's exact words, its category, the trap type, and which Golden Rules John kept or broke.\n\nBeside every mischaracterisation, meaning the lawyer describing a fact the wrong way, put the record fact and its source. Log each objection and whether John stopped talking.\n\nThen route the finished log to Atty. Thorne before his debrief. You coach nothing, not during the mock and not after. The log is for Atty. Thorne alone; nothing in it goes to John.\n\n“He asked about the gap, John did okay” isn't a log entry. It's a vibe. I want what was asked, word for word, at what minute, and what the record actually shows.\n\n{name}, you're logging the first half. {name}, you take the second. Your hand-in is a timestamped Trap Log, scored against the seven rules.\n\nExact words, exact minute, exact record. That's a Trap Log.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does the log need Mercer's exact words instead of your summary of the question?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 02/16/2027, the second prep session. At 2:00 p.m., Atty. Thorne runs a mock cross-examination, which is a practice round where someone plays the other side's lawyer.\n\nQuick poll before I give you your role: hands up if you think you'll get to talk during this one. {name}, put that hand down slowly.\n\nLet me set the roles out loud, because the boundary matters. Atty. Thorne leads and defends. AI defense counsel, playing Atty. Grant Mercer, asks the questions. AI John Doe answers. And you observe.\n\nYour microphone stays muted the whole time. Your job is the Trap Log: every trap with its time, Mercer's exact words, and the record fact beside any mischaracterisation, plus every objection.\n\nAtty. Thorne debriefs John from your log when the mock ends, so it has to reach him before we finish.\n\nMuting yourself sounds easy. It isn't. Every Case Manager I've trained has, at least once, nodded so hard at a good answer the camera caught it.\n\nMuted mic, open log, exact words.",
   "ask": "{name}, who in this mock is allowed to speak to John, and when?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your log before you route it. {name}, are all six traps there, plus the silent stare if Mercer used it, each with a timestamp, his exact words and one of the four categories?\n\nYour gap entry should read 14 days, 02/06/2026 to 02/20/2026, and any disruption question set against 19 days, 04/02/2026 to 04/21/2026.\n\nDegenerative should cite the 03/13/2026 MRI: no significant degenerative change at the adjacent levels. The seatbelt entry cites the EMS Run Report and the EDR download, and the 2021 entry notes three resolved low-back visits and no prior neck complaints.\n\nEach answer scored on the rules, every objection logged. Why so precise? Every mischaracterisation you catch today is one Atty. Thorne can correct before 02/18, and one less line in the defense's evaluation report.\n\nAnd honestly, did you nod, type in the chat, or say anything to John? That's coaching, in a mock or on 02/18/2027.\n\nEvery mischaracterisation you log is one Atty. Thorne can fix.\n\nYour mystery for the Learning Check: one trap is the one clients fall for most. Name it, and prove it from the file.",
   "ask": "{name}, which trap worked on John, and which rule would have protected him?"
  }
 },
 "d5m3-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "This is the Deposition Readiness Audit, and we're playing it like a game show. Look at the readiness strip on screen first. {name}, which chip is hardest to turn green honestly?\n\nMost people say the audit. I'd say the routing, because it's the one that gets tested under pressure, usually by a client who says “I'm not asking for legal advice, I just want to know what you'd say.”\n\nThat's legal advice wearing a fake moustache.\n\nNow answer from John's file, in your own words, under each question. List the landmines your audit must surface before 02/04/2027, with the record behind each. Lay out the day-before checklist for 02/17/2027.\n\nAnd name the four categories of defense questions, the one clients underreport, and where that risk sits here.\n\nThat's the mystery from the mock, by the way: the trap clients fall for most lives in that underreported category. {name}, you're first to share when we review.\n\nRouting under pressure is the skill that keeps John safe.",
   "ask": "{name}, which chip on the readiness strip is hardest to turn green honestly?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "On to your self-summary. Warm-up first, and {name}, you're answering. It's 02/03/2027, and John has just resurfaced with a new phone, fifteen days before he testifies. What can't wait?\n\nHis new number in the CMS, and the audit routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\nSome people would say “great, he's back” and head to lunch. The one I'd hire updates Parties before the call even ends. Keep that urgency in mind.\n\nThen write your scenario: it's 01/28/2027, John's been unreachable for six days, his deposition is noticed for 02/18/2027, and defense counsel wants to inspect Margaret's Civic. Give me your next five actions in order, with an owner beside each.\n\nEvery one of those actions protects the same thing: a deposition that goes forward on time, with a client who shows up.\n\nA no-show deposition doesn't just cost a day. It hands the defense a story about a client who doesn't care about his own case.\n\nWhen a client resurfaces, update first, celebrate later.",
   "ask": "{name}, John just resurfaced fifteen days out. What's the first thing you do before the call ends?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last piece: rate yourself 1 to 5 on each competency, then submit it. I'll compare your rating with your score and talk through any wide gaps with you.\n\n{name}, no pressure, but I've seen people give themselves a five and then spell “deposition” three different ways in one answer.\n\nLet me be straight about Question 5, because it's the UPL gate. UPL means the unauthorized practice of law, a non-lawyer giving legal advice.\n\nAny reply that gives John content about the gap fails the module, however polished it is. The right reply routes his question to Atty. Thorne and logs it in John's words. Rate yourself with that in mind.\n\nRoute the substance, log his words, and you pass the gate.\n\nAnd your mystery for the final check: $20,359.92. That's a real number from John's file, to the cent.\n\nBefore the next Learning Check, work out where it comes from, because you'll be asked to rebuild it deduction by deduction.",
   "ask": "{name}, where would you honestly rate your routing under pressure right now?"
  }
 },
 "d5m4-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Quick game: Whose Fault Is It Anyway? I'll read you three things Robert Smith's Answer says. Shout “John” if it's about John's own conduct, and “Not John” if it isn't.\n\nOne: the Escape entered the all-way stop out of turn. Two: John was unbelted. Three: John failed to mitigate by missing visits. {name}, you call the first one.\n\nHere's the reveal. Number one isn't about John at all. Entering out of turn is about Kevin Alvarez's driving.\n\nAfter suit, the defense went after fault and mitigation. The seatbelt is comparative fault, which means blaming John for part of his own injuries. The missed visits are failure to mitigate, which means saying he didn't do enough to get better.\n\nAnd here's the difference that matters. Fault can reduce or even wipe out recovery. Mitigation only cuts the damages the missed care actually caused.\n\nFunny thing about crashes: I've never once met a driver who thought it was their fault. Ten cars, ten witnesses, ten people sure it was the other guy.\n\nThat's why we answer every allegation with a document, not an opinion.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of Smith's three allegations isn't about John's own conduct at all?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules. Comparative negligence splits fault. Under pure, damages fall by John's share, however large.\n\nUnder modified, they fall, but recovery is barred at 50% in some states or 51% in others. Under contributory, any fault at all bars recovery. Confirm the governing rule with Atty. Thorne.\n\nRecord liability status with its source. Keystone accepted PD liability on 02/17/2026, and the Answer now pleads comparative fault.\n\nThe out-of-turn allegation targets Kevin Alvarez, and whatever the defense says about him, the stack stays $40,000. Unbelted is answered by the EDR and the EMS report. Failure to mitigate trims only avoidable damages, and the A-C-T record answers it.\n\nEvidence counts only if it can come in, meaning it's allowed in court. So record source, witness and custody, meaning who held it and when. Don't rest on the citation alone.\n\nYou gather and index; Atty. Thorne decides fault, admissibility and coverage. {name}, here's the bottom line: every point of fault we can't answer comes straight out of John's check.\n\nEvidence without a witness is just a piece of paper.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a jury finds John 20% at fault, what does he recover under each of the three systems?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Let's see it on the job. The Answer is filed on 11/12/2026, pleading comparative fault, causation and failure to mitigate.\n\nYou log each defense as its own line in the Pleadings Log, dated 11/12/2026, and confirm the filing on the docket, the court's official list of everything filed in the case.\n\nThen you map the file's evidence to each allegation in the counter-package, with its source, witness and custody. Nothing goes in without a source document and a named witness who can vouch for it.\n\nWhen an item lacks foundation, or a holder won't hand it over, you flag it for a Subpoena Duces Tecum, a court order to produce documents, and route it to Atty. Thorne, naming the gap and the record holder.\n\nWith deposition prep set for 02/04 and 02/16/2027, you deliver the counter-package and the Gap Record into his prep set before 02/04/2027.\n\nI once saw a perfect piece of evidence get tossed because nobody could say who'd had the USB stick for six months. It was in a desk drawer. Under a sandwich. {name}, don't be the sandwich drawer.\n\nSource, witness, custody. Three answers for every exhibit.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does every item in the counter-package need a named witness and not just a document?"
  }
 },
 "d5m4-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. You're building the liability counter-package for Atty. Thorne in the CMS under Litigation.\n\nFor each of the three allegations, list the evidence that answers it, its source document, the witness who can vouch for it, its chain of custody and any gap in its foundation.\n\nWrite out the doorbell video's chain from the 02/17/2026 preservation request to the 02/19/2026 copy. Where a record will need a custodian, draft a narrow Exhibit A for a Subpoena Duces Tecum from the firm's template in the CMS, under Documents, for his review.\n\nThen close with the questions only he can answer: the governing fault rule, seatbelt and citation admissibility, and whether fault on Kevin Alvarez touches coverage.\n\n{name}, you take the out-of-turn row first. {name}, you're on the seatbelt.\n\nA list of evidence is a start. What we want is evidence that can actually walk into a courtroom with a witness holding its hand. You hand in the allegation-by-allegation package, the draft Exhibit A and those routed questions.",
   "ask": "{name}, which single document answers two of the defenses?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 11/12/2026 on Track B, and something just got filed. {name}, guess: when the other side answers a lawsuit, do they ever just say “yep, our fault”? Me neither, not once in my career.\n\nHere's what landed. Atty. Grant Mercer of Mercer & Hale LLP has filed Robert Smith's Answer in Civil Action No. 26-CV-04417.\n\nIt denies liability and pleads comparative negligence, saying the Escape entered the all-way stop out of turn and John was unbelted. It pleads causation, the gap and a degenerative MRI, and failure to mitigate.\n\nAtty. Thorne wants, for each allegation, the evidence that answers it, its source, its authenticating witness, meaning the person who can swear the evidence is what we say it is, and its chain of custody. Plus a list of the questions only he can answer.\n\nIn the file you've got report RPD-26-018842, Ruth Calloway's details, the doorbell video, the 10/2026 EDR download, the EMS Run Report and the A-C-T record.\n\nYou'll get three blank rows, one per allegation, and I'll grade row by row.\n\nNo witness, no row. Remember that before you start.",
   "ask": "{name}, predict it: what happens to a strong exhibit at trial if nobody can say who held it and when?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your package against this before you log it. Three separate rows: out of turn, unbelted, failure to mitigate.\n\nOut of turn is answered by RPD-26-018842, Ruth Calloway of 2209 Maple Hollow Rd, the video and the point of impact, with the EDR's 11 mph as corroboration only, because speed alone doesn't prove right-of-way. {name}, does yours say that?\n\nUnbelted is answered by the EDR and the EMS report.\n\nMitigation is answered by the A-C-T record and its clinical cause, with the disruption stated exactly: 19 days, 04/02 to 04/21/2026, four missed visits, crash-related anxiety documented by Dr. Reyes and the counselor.\n\nThe video's chain runs from Margaret Doe's camera through 02/17 and 02/19/2026, native file unaltered. Every item has a witness.\n\nYour Exhibit A covers only John Doe's 02/06/2026 Riverton Fire EMS run and named record types, never all records. And the stack still reads $40,000.\n\nNarrow subpoena, sourced evidence, same $40,000 stack.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does a narrow Exhibit A get records faster than one asking for everything?"
  }
 },
 "d5m4-l2": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Here's the mystery I left you with: two people went missing on this file. Quick game first. I'll describe one of them, you shout “Smith” or “John.”\n\nMoved before he could be served. Went silent 27 days before his deposition. Has a mom who answers the phone. {name}, you get the last one.\n\nRobert Smith moved before he could be served. John Doe went silent 27 days before his deposition.\n\nWhat do they share? Neither one has a working contact point. What's different? One's the opponent and one's our client, and who's allowed to do what follows from that.\n\nThe tools overlap: Skip-Trace, the process server, certified proof of notice. A Skip-Trace is a search of records to find someone's current address. A process server is the person who hands over the court papers.\n\nNow, if you've watched TV, you think a skip-trace means a dark room, six monitors and someone yelling “enhance.” In real life it's a database search, a permission form and a lot of waiting.\n\nEither way, the record we keep is what proves we did our job.",
   "ask": "{name}, Smith and John both went missing. What do they share, and what's different about them?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Now the rules, with the real dates. The clerk issues the Summons, the process server gets the papers, you follow up every five days, and the affidavit is filed: here 09/24 filed, 10/14 served, 10/16/2026 affidavit.\n\nThe Summons, by the way, is the court's official notice telling the defendant he's been sued and how long he has to answer.\n\nThree failed attempts at 118 Larkspur Ln and a neighbor saying Smith moved means a stale address. So Skip-Trace first, permissible purpose only. On 10/09 it found 4410 Quarry Ridge Ct, Millbrook.\n\nService starts his clock, answer due 11/13/2026, and you check the docket weekly. A Subpoena Duces Tecum needs a narrow Exhibit A, the list of exactly which records we want, and opposing counsel gets notice first.\n\nFor John, silent since 01/22/2027, run Day 2's No Contact Protocol, our step-by-step plan for a client who goes quiet, every attempt logged. The Step 4 letter goes certified mail and email over Atty. Thorne's signature, by 01/27/2027.\n\nWithdrawal goes in only if he directs it. And Margaret hears only that the firm needs John to call.\n\n{name}, why so careful with Margaret? She's family, not our client, and every extra word to her is a privacy problem.\n\nOpponent or client, the tools look alike. Who's allowed to use them doesn't.",
   "ask": "{name}, three attempts failed at 118 Larkspur Ln. Why is a fourth at 6:00 a.m. the wrong move?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Back to October, on the job. On 10/08/2026, the third attempt fails and a neighbor says Smith moved.\n\nYou escalate for Skip-Trace authority, log the 10/09/2026 result and re-assign the server to 4410 Quarry Ridge Ct. When the affidavit is filed 10/16/2026, you docket the answer date, 11/13/2026, and set a recurring weekly docket check.\n\nLater, Atty. Thorne approves a Subpoena Duces Tecum. So you send notice to Atty. Grant Mercer first, dated before service, then have the custodian, the person who keeps the records, served, and set a compliance call five days before production.\n\nAnd when John goes unreachable from 01/22/2027 with a deposition noticed, you run Steps 1 to 3, then draft the Step 4 notice for Atty. Thorne's signature, response date before 02/04/2027, and log both the certified mail and the email.\n\nSo what's this got to do with money? A defendant who's never served can't be made to pay, and a client who misses his deposition can sink his own case.\n\nServed defendants pay. Present clients win.\n\n{name}, both lanes end at the settlement table.",
   "ask": "{name}, why does opposing counsel get notice of the subpoena before the custodian is served?"
  }
 },
 "d5m4-a2": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's the job, in two parts. In Part one, escalate for Skip-Trace authority through Tasks, Escalate, then evaluate the 10/09 return.\n\nChoose the address on recency and two corroborating sources, meaning two separate records that agree, and record why. Re-instruct Allied, log service on 10/14 and the affidavit on 10/16, and docket the answer date.\n\nIn Part two, run Steps 1 to 3 of the No Contact Protocol with the litigation impact. Then draft the Step 4 certified notice, the Final Warning Letter that lists what we tried, sets a deadline and warns we may withdraw.\n\nDraft it from the firm's template in the CMS, under Documents, for Atty. Thorne's signature: attempts itemized, a 7 to 14 day response date before 02/04/2027, how to respond. Send it certified mail and email to a confirmed address.\n\nYou'll also call Margaret Doe: verify her identity, say only that the firm needs John to call, and log it. Then assess John's Skip-Trace gate.\n\n{name}, you're on Smith. {name}, you're on John. Anyone can knock again and leave a voicemail. I want a file that can show a judge every step, by date.",
   "ask": "{name}, John and Smith both have no working contact point. Why does Smith get a Skip-Trace and John doesn't?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "Two episodes, one file, and both are disappearing acts.\n\nPart one. It's 10/08/2026, and Allied Process Services reports a third failed attempt at 118 Larkspur Ln, tonight at 6:40 p.m., no answer, no vehicle, after tries on 10/01 and 10/05. A neighbor says Robert Smith moved out in August. The server wants to know how to proceed.\n\n{name}, before I show you the Skip-Trace return, guess how many addresses come back.\n\nThree. The 10/09 return lists 118 Larkspur Ln with utilities closed 08/2026, 4410 Quarry Ridge Ct, Millbrook, with a utility opened 08/2026 that matches his registration, and a 2019 address. Remember the mystery? Only one is real, and you'll prove which.\n\nPart two. It's 01/22/2027, and calls to John go unanswered, two days after his deposition was noticed for 02/18/2027. Prep is set for 02/04 and 02/16/2027. Treat both like a race against a stale address.\n\nNeighbors, by the way, are the best investigators in America. They know who moved, when, and what kind of couch went with them.\n\nStale address, new tool. Never just knock harder.",
   "ask": "{name}, after the 10/08 report, what's the graded move: a fourth knock, or something else?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Time for your self-check. {name}, Part one: did you escalate for Skip-Trace after 10/08/2026, not a fourth knock?\n\nDid you choose 4410 Quarry Ridge Ct on two current sources, reject 118 Larkspur Ln as stale, log service 10/14/2026 and the affidavit 10/16/2026, and docket the answer for 11/13/2026?\n\nPart two. Step 1 names the deposition, 27 days out, the prep dates and the 03/15/2027 expert disclosure. Your ladder is logged: calls, email to john.doe@example.com, text, then Margaret.\n\nNow check every response date against 02/04/2027. A 14-day window from 01/22 lands on 02/05, one day too late, so it's sent by 01/27/2027 for 02/03/2027.\n\nOne day. That's how most deadlines get missed, not by a month, by one calendar square.\n\nCertified mail to 2214 Maple Hollow Rd and email, signed by Atty. Thorne. No withdrawal language from you, anywhere. And the gate isn't met: John was reached 02/03/2027.\n\nCount the days twice. One calendar square is the whole difference.\n\nYour final mystery: the Final Comprehensive Knowledge Check spans all five days. Which number on this file do you think shows up in it most?",
   "ask": "{name}, where does your notice's response date fall against the 02/04/2027 prep session?"
  }
 },
 "d5m4-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "This is the Final Comprehensive Knowledge Check, the last one before Track B's close-out, and it spans all five days. Every answer comes from one file: John Doe's names, dates and figures, not general rules.\n\nBefore we start, a lightning round, hands up as fast as you can. Who remembers the date of loss? Who remembers the stack total? {name}, you were first. Keep that energy.\n\nWrite in your own words under each question. Lay out the Recovery Stack in its order of operations, with each limit and the total, name three coverages that aren't part of it, then the Payer Order for his Medical Bills.\n\nThe Recovery Stack is the order the insurance money gets tapped, layer by layer. The Payer Order is who pays which Medical Bill first.\n\nTell me when the window on the 07/20/2026 BI demand closed and why. The BI demand, remember, is our letter asking the at-fault driver's insurer to pay for John Doe's injuries.\n\nAnd reconcile the Track A net of $20,359.92 from $40,000.00, deduction by deduction. There it is, the number from the mystery. Time to prove where it comes from.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you start, what's the first layer of John's Recovery Stack?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Before you write your self-summary, let's do Question 7 out loud, and {name}, you're answering. John asks you how to explain the gap. What do you say?\n\nThe only right answer listens, tells him Atty. Thorne will go through it with him, escalates at once and logs the call.\n\nThen Question 4, the arithmetic gate: $40,000.00, less the $13,333.33 fee, $486.75 in costs, and the $1,240.00 and $4,580.00 liens, leaves $20,359.92, to the cent.\n\n{name}, double-check me on a calculator. Every client checks it, usually twice, usually while asking why the lawyer gets a third.\n\nThat's the whole week in one number. Every lien you logged, every reduction, every cost receipt shows up right here.\n\nNow your scenario: 01/28/2027, John unreachable for six days, deposition noticed for 02/18/2027, defense counsel asking to inspect the Civic. Your next five actions, in order, with owners.\n\nListen, route, log, and know the arithmetic to the cent.",
   "ask": "{name}, John asks how to explain the gap. What do you say, word for word?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Last step for the final check. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each competency across all five days, then submit for review. I'll compare your ratings with your scores.\n\nPlease be honest with yourself here, because this is what we'll coach from.\n\nI once had a trainee rate herself five out of five on everything, including “accuracy,” on a form where she'd misspelled her own batch number. We still laugh about it. She's a supervisor now.\n\nAnd know that UPL is a gate, not a weight. A 5 on the UPL competency from someone who coached John in Question 7 doesn't pass, however strong the rest of the paper is.\n\nHonesty in the rating is how we coach the real gaps.\n\nHere's your last challenge before close-out, {name}, and everyone. Think back to the very first slide on Day 1. Whose file is it?\n\nYou've got a much better answer now than you did back then. Bring it to the end-of-day discussion.",
   "ask": "{name}, across all five days, which competency surprised you most when you rated it?"
  }
 },
 "d5m5-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Let's play Guess the Net. Same $40,000.00 gross on both tracks. On Track A, where Keystone settled, John Doe netted $20,359.92.\n\nOn Track B, Keystone stonewalled, so we sued, mediated and arbitrated, and Laura Whitcomb's award came in on 07/06/2027. Thumbs up if you think John nets more on Track B, thumbs down if less.\n\n{name}, you're thumbs down? Give me a number, and tell me why you picked it.\n\nHere's the reveal: $14,713.75. Same gross, almost six thousand dollars less in his pocket. Three things moved that number, and I'll reveal them one at a time.\n\nAnd yes, when that award check lands, someone in this office will want a photo holding it like a lottery winner. Take the photo. Then it goes straight into trust.\n\nJohn is going to notice the difference, and he deserves to understand every dollar before he signs anything. That's what the Net Sheet is for.",
   "ask": "{name}, same $40,000.00 gross on both tracks. Predict the three things that pulled John's net down, and why each one moved?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "The award ends the case, not the work. Laura Whitcomb's 07/06/2027 award gives Harbor Point's $20,000.00 and Cardinal State's $10,000.00, and with Keystone's $10,000.00 BI from the 03/18/2027 mediation, the gross is $40,000.00.\n\nThe fee follows the retainer: suit filed 09/24/2026, so 40%, $16,000.00, confirmed by Atty. Thorne.\n\nLitigation costs need receipts: filing $218.00, service $285.00, mediation $1,200.00, arbitration $900.00, reporter $645.50, records $160.00, plus the pre-suit $486.75, totaling $3,895.25.\n\nThe fund pays only what's owed. Peach State's reduction follows the fee charged, $744.00, accepted at $1,116.00, and Riverton Spine & Rehab accepts $4,275.00. Net: $14,713.75.\n\nNothing leaves trust until the checks clear, the Payoff Letters are in and John signs. Copying Track A's numbers is the shortcut that bites. {name}, you rebuild every line from this file.\n\nYou explain the arithmetic. Whether suing was worth it is Atty. Thorne's conversation.",
   "ask": "{name}, Peach State's reduction was $620.00 on Track A. Explain why it's $744.00 now?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how the close-out runs on the job. The award arrives on 07/06/2027. You log it under Litigation and diary each carrier's payment: Harbor Point $20,000.00 and Cardinal State $10,000.00.\n\nOnce the fund is known, you prepare the Reduction Requests, our letters asking each lienholder to take less, with the math and the reason, in the LSH Net Sheet, for Atty. Thorne's approval. They come back as Peach State at $1,116.00 and Riverton Spine & Rehab at $4,275.00.\n\nWhen the last UM check has cleared, you complete the Net Sheet and route the Settlement Statement through Tasks, Escalate, which drops it in Atty. Thorne's queue with a time stamp, showing a net of $14,713.75. All before John signs anything.\n\nWar story. I once saw an office order a sheet cake that said WE WON before the second check cleared. The check came back with the payee name wrong, the cake got eaten anyway, and the client waited three more weeks for his money.\n\n{name}, who decides when the cake gets ordered?\n\nCelebrate when the money clears, not when the envelope arrives.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the one thing that has to be true before you send John's Settlement Statement to Atty. Thorne, and why?"
  }
 },
 "d5m5-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your job. You'll complete John Doe's LSH Net Sheet for the litigated recovery. Section one is the gross by carrier. Section two is the 40% fee and every receipted cost, pre-suit and litigation, each on its own row.\n\nSection three lists every provider and payer with charges, payments, adjustments and balance owed, and each lien's reduced figure with its Payoff Letter. Section four is the net to John.\n\nTie it to the Trust Ledger in CMS, the record of every dollar sitting in the firm's trust account, then route the Settlement Statement to Atty. Thorne through Tasks, Escalate, so it lands in his queue with a record of when it went.\n\n{name}, you'll also take John's call. Walk him through fee, costs, liens and net, show the 40% from his own retainer, and give the reduced liens with their acceptances.\n\nClients always have a friend who kept more. Every single one. Your answer is the arithmetic, never an opinion.\n\nHand in a Net Sheet reaching $14,713.75, every number proved.",
   "ask": "{name}, John says a friend kept way more from his settlement. What do you say, word for word?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 07/22/2027, and something just landed. Before I tell you what, {name}, guess: good news or bad news?\n\nGood news. Laura Whitcomb's 07/06/2027 award gave Harbor Point's $20,000.00 and Cardinal State's $10,000.00. Harbor Point's check came 07/20, and Cardinal State's arrived this morning, joining Keystone's $10,000.00, held in trust since 03/29/2027. That's all $40,000.00 in trust.\n\nPeach State accepted $1,116.00 on 07/14, and Riverton Spine & Rehab accepted $4,275.00 on 07/16, both in writing, and every litigation cost has a receipt.\n\nAtty. Thorne's note reminds us suit was filed, so the fee is the 40% tier under John's retainer, the agreement he signed with the firm on day one: a third before suit, 40% after.\n\nHe wants the Net Sheet and the Settlement Statement for his review before John sees anything. And no, nobody orders the cake yet.\n\nOpen the Net Sheet beside this slide and set it to the Track B stage. Every number you enter needs a paper behind it.",
   "ask": "{name}, before you enter a single number, what fee percentage applies at the Track B stage, and which document proves it?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your Net Sheet before you log it, and I'm calling on people. {name}, the gross: Keystone $10,000.00 from mediation, plus Harbor Point $20,000.00 and Cardinal State $10,000.00 from the award, $40,000.00.\n\nThe fee is the 40% tier, $16,000.00, confirmed from the retainer, not 33⅓% out of habit. Costs come to $3,895.25, each on its row with a receipt.\n\nLiens: Peach State from $1,860.00 down to $1,116.00, and Riverton Spine & Rehab from $6,290.00 to the audited $6,105.00 to $4,275.00, totaling $5,391.00. Zero-balance lines like PIP's $5,000.00 and Crescent's $1,540.00 write-off stay off the payables.\n\nNet $14,713.75, tied to the $40,000.00 in trust. Nothing disbursed until John signs on 07/29/2027, disbursement 07/30/2027. And no lien paid from a phone call.\n\nHere's the test I use: a sheet that matches the total passes today. A sheet that survives an audit passes forever.\n\nYour mystery: one number on somebody's sheet in this room snuck in from Track A. {name}, whose, and which one?",
   "ask": "{name}, does your Trust Ledger tie to the $40,000.00 gross to the cent, and if not, how would you find the gap?"
  }
 },
 "d5-drive": {
  "task": {
   "say": "Here's your last backup job. File the litigation and ADR documents from Work From so each lands in Litigation and ADR on your drive.\n\nThen the final audit. Open My Backup Drive, run Check my drive, and confirm the count backed up equals what's filed in the CMS, across all nine folders, with every superseded version labeled and every draft marked.\n\nThen the closing checklist in Case Notes for Atty. Thorne: every folder complete, every Payoff Letter and the Settlement Statement on the drive, the closing letter backed up, the last check clean.\n\nAnd the step after sign-off: the whole folder moving under Closed Files with the closing date logged. Don't move it in your training drive; the course checks it where it is. {name}, you're first to report your count.\n\nMoney out doesn't mean done. The file closes on sign-off.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, why does the folder move only after Atty. Thorne signs off?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "It's 07/30/2027, three in the afternoon, and John Doe's case is won. The award went out today, every lien paid on its Payoff Letter.\n\nThe litigation file is in the CMS: the Complaint, the service papers, the Answer, the Scheduling Order, the mediation term sheet and both binders.\n\nOne job is left before the file closes, and honestly, it's the one people skip. Think of it like packing up after a move: if the boxes aren't labeled, nobody finds the kettle.\n\nPicture someone opening this file five years from now, looking for the Answer. {name}, what do they need to find it in seconds?",
   "ask": "{name}, the money's out. Is the file closed? Who decides, and why them?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Last check of the course, so let's make it count. Are the Complaint, the service papers, the Answer, the Scheduling Order and the term sheet in Litigation and ADR?\n\nDoes backed up equal filed? Is every draft marked and every old version labeled? Did Check my drive come back clean? And does your closing checklist end with sign-off and the move to Closed Files?\n\nNow the real test. {name}, the HIPAA authorization from Day 1. Go. {name}, the Answer from November. Go. I'm timing you.\n\nAny document, intake to award, in seconds. That's a systematized backup.",
   "ask": "{name}, a lien refund check arrives a month after the close. Where does its paper go, and what does that tell you about the timing of the move?"
  }
 },
 "d5m5-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Final round of the game show, and the jackpot question is the whole case. John Doe's file is now closed, from intake all the way to the award. Take a second to appreciate that: you carried one file through every phase.\n\nAnswer from the file, in your own words, typed under each question. Take the $40,000.00 award down to John's $14,713.75, naming every deduction with its figure and source.\n\nExplain why the fee is 40% on this file, which document says so and who confirms it. And show why Peach State accepted $1,240.00 on Track A but $1,116.00 on Track B.\n\n{name}, you're my lightning-round contestant: I'll ask you one of these out loud when time's up. And {name}, you're the judge. If you hear a number without a source, you buzz.\n\nA number without a source is just a rumor.",
   "ask": "{name}, quick-fire: which document tells us the fee on this file is 40%, and why does that document win?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your self-summary, in your own words. Keep Question 5 in mind as you write, because it's the UPL gate, the line between explaining facts and giving legal advice.\n\nArithmetic, yes; opinion, no. If you write that the lawsuit was or wasn't worth it, you've crossed the line, however good the rest is.\n\nThen your scenario: it's 01/28/2027, John's been unreachable for six days, his deposition is noticed for 02/18/2027, and defense counsel wants to inspect Margaret's Civic. Give me your next five actions, in order, and who owns each one.\n\nWhy bring back an old moment? The best Case Managers I've worked with can tell you a file's whole story from memory, like a favorite movie. The ones who struggle remember only the last scene.\n\n{name}, which kind are you going to be?",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, where exactly is the line between explaining John's net and judging whether suing was worth it?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "Here's your Skill Scale for the award and the Net Sheet. Score yourself 1 to 5 on each competency, then submit it for review.\n\nI'll sit down with your rating beside your score, and if there's a wide gap either way, we'll talk it through together. Be as honest about yourself as you've been with John's numbers.\n\n{name}, no all-fives just because the case is won. The award check doesn't grade your work. The Net Sheet does.\n\nAnd keep your pen out, because we've got one more file to close, and it's not a winner. Denise Harlow's case is dropped.\n\nHere's the mystery to carry into that check: when a case ends without a check, what's left in the file that proves the firm did its job?",
   "ask": "{name}, looking back across John Doe's whole file, which skill do you feel strongest in now, and what proves it?"
  }
 },
 "d5m6-l1": {
  "why": {
   "say": "Would you rather, quick round. Would you rather tell a client their case is being dropped in a letter, or in a phone call? Letter hands up. Phone hands up.\n\n{name}, you picked the letter. Defend it.\n\nOkay, confession: trick question. Neither of those is your first move. Atty. Thorne talks to the client first, and the letter is never the client's first news.\n\nA Dropped Case is a claim the firm stops pursuing before it resolves. John Doe's file never gets here, because earlier today the Skip-Trace found the people his file needed. But some cases do.\n\nAnd a Withdrawal Letter is basically the politest breakup letter ever written: it's not you, it's the case.\n\nDone carelessly, a drop leaves a client confused and the firm exposed. Done right, the file proves everyone was told.\n\nThe client hears it from the attorney first. The letter only confirms it.\n\nThere's one sentence in the client's letter that must never appear in the carrier's. I'll tell you which one shortly.",
   "ask": "{name}, if a Dropped Case just went quiet in the CMS with no letters, who could get hurt by that?"
  },
  "rules": {
   "say": "Six steps, always in this order. First, the gate: the status reads Pending Drop, and there's a note from Atty. Thorne or the Case Manager that the client was told. If either is missing, nothing goes out.\n\nSecond, prepare a Withdrawal Letter for every party on the file, over Atty. Thorne's signature, saved in Word and PDF. Third, send each one by its method.\n\nFourth, annotate each send with party, method and date, and save the sent letter. Fifth, follow up on receipt. Sixth, switch the status to Dropped and keep the proof.\n\nIt's so tempting to just flip the status to Dropped and move on. Don't. The file has to prove every step.\n\nWar story. A Case Manager I trained got a call two years after a drop from a client swearing nobody ever told him. She pulled up the note, the logged text, and the signed green Certified Mail card. The call ended in about ninety seconds.\n\nAn unresponsive client only gets here through the No Contact Protocol, our step-by-step plan for a silent client, and this is where it ends. {name}, got it?\n\nGate, letters, send, log, follow up, then Dropped. Never skip ahead.",
   "ask": "{name}, the status says Pending Drop but there's no note. What do you do, and who do you tell?"
  },
  "job": {
   "say": "Here's how it runs on the job. The trigger is the status Pending Drop, with Atty. Thorne's note that the client was told.\n\nYou open the CMS, under Documents, where the firm's letter templates live, and prepare each party's Withdrawal Letter from the template, for his signature. Save each one in Word and PDF.\n\nOnce the letters are signed, you send each by its method, email, text, fax or Certified Mail, and you log the party, the method and the date in the Communications Log.\n\nWhen the receipts are followed up, you switch the status to Dropped and file the proof in Documents: the receipts, the logs and the attorney's note.\n\nWhy all the fuss? Because a dropped file can still come back years later as a complaint, and the only thing standing between the firm and trouble is that proof.\n\nEvery file ends up as the last box in storage someday. Make sure yours is the box nobody has to worry about. {name}, you're up.",
   "ask": "{name}, where in the CMS would you look first to prove a client was told about the drop?"
  }
 },
 "d5m6-a1": {
  "task": {
   "say": "You'll work in the CMS, under Documents, with the firm's approved letter templates, so you never start from a blank page. Record the gate you checked first.\n\nThen draft three Withdrawal Letters for Atty. Thorne's signature. Denise's carries the date, her name, the date of loss, the reason in his note's words, and the next step he approves: speak to another attorney promptly, because time limits apply.\n\nGranite Shield's and Brookside's carry the date, the client, the date of loss and the claim number, and say only that the firm no longer represents her. Name each send method and why.\n\nThen write the close-out log: Word and PDF saved, each send annotated, the follow-up, the switch to Dropped and the proof kept.\n\n{name}, you're drafting Denise's letter. {name}, you're on Granite Shield's. And be ready for Denise's call, because she'll ask whether her case was worthless.\n\nHere's a test from experience: the carrier letter is the shortest letter you'll ever write. If yours runs past one paragraph, you've said too much.",
   "ask": "{name}, Denise asks whether her case was worthless. What will you say, and what will you do right after the call?"
  },
  "case": {
   "say": "This one's a practice matter, not John Doe's file, so don't open it or create it in the CMS. It's 12/09/2026. Meet Denise Harlow.\n\nShe was rear-ended at a red light on 09/19/2026. Granite Shield Insurance has the BI claim, GS-26-48213, and the CRM, our contact records, says its adjuster, Paul Ostrander, takes email only.\n\nShe treated at Brookside Physical Therapy from 09/24 to 10/29/2026, then stopped. {name}, before I tell you why, predict it: why is Atty. Thorne dropping this case?\n\nHere's the reveal. She has no Health Plan to pay for care, and Brookside declined a Letter of Protection, the firm's written promise to pay the provider out of any settlement. Think of it as an IOU the provider wouldn't take.\n\nSo the status is Pending Drop. His 12/08/2026 note says he called Denise, she understood, her address at 87 Alder Creek Dr is confirmed, and she prefers text and email.\n\nShe heard it by phone first. The letter only confirms what she already knows.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, which two things on this file do you check before drafting a word, and where do you find each?"
  },
  "check": {
   "say": "Check your work before you log it. {name}, the gate is recorded: Pending Drop and Atty. Thorne's 12/08/2026 note, both confirmed before anything was prepared.\n\nDenise's letter is dated 12/09/2026, names the 09/19/2026 date of loss, quotes his reason and gives the approved next step. Granite Shield's shows claim GS-26-48213 and the date of loss, Brookside's asks them to update their records, and neither says why.\n\nEvery letter is saved in Word and PDF. Denise gets text, email and Certified Mail to 87 Alder Creek Dr, Granite Shield gets email, and Brookside gets a fax.\n\nEach send is logged, the follow-up is on 12/16/2026, and the status moves to Dropped only after that, with the proof kept.\n\nI once found a file marked Dropped where the only proof was a sticky note that said sent, probably. Probably isn't proof.\n\nSending the letters isn't the finish line. Proving they arrived is. So, {name}, predict it: what if 12/16/2026 comes and the receipt isn't back?",
   "ask": "{name}, if any letter except Denise's mentions treatment or insurance, what would you fix, and why does it matter?"
  }
 },
 "d5m6-kc": {
  "questions": {
   "say": "Last game of the course: Prove It. I ask, you answer, and every answer needs a name, a date or a method from the file, or it doesn't count.\n\nThree questions, answered from the practice matter in your own words, under each one. First, name the two things you confirm before any Withdrawal Letter goes out, and why both matter.\n\nSecond, what goes in Denise Harlow's letter that doesn't go in Granite Shield's or Brookside's, and why do we keep it that way?\n\nThird, Denise's letters went out on 12/09/2026. What has to be true before her status becomes Dropped, and what proof stays in the file?\n\n{name}, you're first on question one. {name}, you've got question two.\n\nNames, dates and methods, please. A general rule without the file behind it is just a guess in a nice outfit.",
   "ask": "{name}, which of the three letters is the only one that explains why, and what's the reason the others don't?"
  },
  "summary": {
   "say": "Now your self-summary. In your own words, what did you learn closing a Dropped Case? I want the principles behind the close-out, not the clicks.\n\nThen your strategy. Denise's letters are out, and on 12/16/2026 her return receipt still hasn't come back. What's your next action, and why is it the right one rather than something else?\n\nBe honest about the one thing in this module you're least sure of. That's where we'll practice.\n\nKeep the line in mind as you write. If your answer explains the drop to Denise, or tells her what to do next beyond what Atty. Thorne approved, it has crossed into his territory.\n\nAnd remember, the polite breakup letter only works if it actually arrives. {name}, read me your next action out loud when you're done.",
   "ask": "{name}, in your own words, whose job is it to tell Denise why her case was dropped, and what's yours?"
  },
  "scale": {
   "say": "And here we are, the very last rating of the course. Rate yourself 1 to 5 on each skill: confirming the Pending Drop gate, drafting a Withdrawal Letter for each party, choosing and logging each send method, and closing the file with proof and without advice.\n\nSubmit it, and I'll compare your rating with your answers. A wide gap is a conversation, not a grade.\n\nThink back to Day 1. I told you John Doe's file had a detail that could cost him money, and it turned out to be where he was sitting in the car. That's the job.\n\nSo here's your final challenge, for your very first real file. Within the first hour, find one detail nobody has flagged yet, the one that could cost that client money.\n\nWrite it down, back it with a document, and bring it to your attorney the same day. {name}, you're up first.\n\nFind it, prove it, route it. Do that every time, and your files will be the boxes in storage nobody ever has to open.",
   "ask": "{name}, what's the first thing you'll check on your first real file, and why that one?"
  }
 },
 "_days": {
  "1": {
   "agenda": {
    "say": "Good morning, and welcome to Case Management. Before a single lesson, a quick game: two truths and a lie, Case Manager edition. I say three things about the job, you vote with your hands.\n\nOne: a Case Manager can tell a client what their case is worth. Two: if it isn't in the CMS, it didn't happen. Three: the Conflict Check comes before the matter is even opened. {name}, which one's the lie? Number one, and by lunch you'll know exactly why.\n\nHere's how today runs. First the lessons: John Doe's case, then your role, the line between sharing facts and giving legal advice, reading the raw file, the Case Plan and his treatment, then the car side of things, the do's and don'ts, the repair, the rental.\n\nThen the Practice Lab, seven Skill Activities on his real file, including the firm's backup drive. Then the Knowledge Check.\n\nBy the end of today his matter is open the right way, and his car problem has a plan. Now the teaser. Somewhere on John Doe's file there's a damaged car, and nobody has worked out yet whose it is.\n\nThat one detail decides who gets a rental. I'm keeping the answer until this afternoon. And a confession: my first week, I thought CMS stood for Can't Miss Saturday. It doesn't, but you'll still never miss a deadline in it.",
    "ask": "{name}, of everything on today's agenda, which part do you think a brand-new Case Manager gets wrong most often?"
   },
   "lab": {
    "say": "Quick show of hands: who's ever built flat-pack furniture without reading the instructions? Hands up if there were screws left over. {name}, I see you.\n\nThis is the Practice Lab, and today there are no leftover screws. Open the tools on this slide now, the CMS first, beside the slides.\n\nWe'll run seven Skill Activities on John Doe's real file, in order. First, you open his case in the CMS, with the Conflict Check before anything else. Second, you link your Google Drive and build the firm's backup folders.\n\nThird, a voicemail: the other side's adjuster wants to record John's story. You plan it and deliver his Direction Packet. Fourth, you assign those directions in the system. Fifth, the Civic investigation: whose rental is it, really?\n\nNow, you could click around until the screen turns green. Please don't. I want every field matching a document, every correction with its source, every step logged.\n\nWhy so fussy? Months from now an adjuster reads this file, and what you type today is page one of the story they believe.\n\n{name}, you're my CMS navigator. {name}, you're my fact checker. Stuck on one step for more than ten minutes? Log what you have and move on; we'll fix it in the debrief.\n\nAnd one little mystery: a helpful colleague has already left a mistake waiting for you in activity one.",
    "ask": "{name}, before you click anything, which document will you keep open beside the CMS, and why that one?"
   },
   "wrap": {
    "say": "Okay, before the Knowledge Check, let's step back. Quick game first: finish this sentence, one breath each. On a brand-new file, the one thing I will never do is… {name}, go. {name}, you're next. Love it.\n\nNow the real question for the room. Robert Smith's insurance company has already called John Doe, and he needs a car to get to work. What do you tell him today, in what order, and what has to be on the file before tomorrow's claim set-up?\n\nThe easy answer is \"don't talk to them, we'll handle it, bye.\" What I actually want is the do's and don'ts in plain words, the rental path laid out, every step logged the same day, and anything legal routed to Atty. Thorne.\n\nI once had a client who, trying to be helpful, told the other side's adjuster his whole life story, including the part about his old football knee. That knee showed up in every offer for a year.\n\nWhat John says this week lives in the adjuster's notes for the life of the case. So, two or three answers, each tied to a moment on his file from today.\n\nThen your mystery for tonight: tomorrow we send the letters that tell every insurance company we represent him. How many companies is that, and which one would John never have guessed is involved?",
    "ask": "{name}, what's the very first thing you'd tell John Doe, and why that first?"
   }
  },
  "2": {
   "agenda": {
    "say": "Welcome back, everyone. Let's settle last night's mysteries before we even sit down.\n\nQuick game: guess the number. Somewhere on John Doe's file there's a number of days that changes everything. Shout your guesses. {name}? {name}?\n\nIt's fourteen. Fourteen days between the crash and his first real visit to a doctor, and today you'll see why an adjuster circles that in red.\n\nSecond mystery: how many insurance companies get our letters today? Three. The other driver's insurer for the bodily injury claim, the insurer on the car he was riding in, and one he'd never have guessed, a policy he's covered by through his family.\n\nThat's the stack, forty thousand dollars of coverage, and we'll prove every layer in writing.\n\nSo here's today. Claim set-up first: the stack, the letters of representation, which are just the letters that say talk to us, not our client, and the Case Plan to Atty. Thorne.\n\nThen treatment: keeping John reachable, getting every bill paid by the right payer, and keeping his care moving when life gets in the way. Then a big Practice Lab and the Knowledge Check.\n\nOne teaser. Before we're done, one of today's insurance companies is going to try to get John on a recorded call. Some Case Managers only find out afterward. You'll see it coming.",
    "ask": "{name}, of the three insurance companies, which one do you think will be the hardest to pin down in writing?"
   },
   "lab": {
    "say": "Practice Lab time, and honestly, today it's a marathon, so stretch. Ten Skill Activities on John Doe's file.\n\nWould you rather chase one adjuster for a week, or three for one day? {name}? Good news: today you get both.\n\nOpen the CMS first and keep it beside the slides. You'll build and verify his Policy Stack, send the letters to all three insurers, audit the Coverage Matrix, which is our one-page map of who covers what, flag the fourteen-day gap, and route the Case Plan.\n\nThen the treatment side: outreach when John goes quiet, the warning letter, and the letters of protection that let a clinic treat now and get paid from the settlement.\n\nAfter that, the Health Plan's payback right, the A-C-T crisis steps when his care gets disrupted, and a referral he won't attend. Then the backup drive: records and bills kept apart.\n\nSame rhythm every time: Your Case, Your Task, Check Your Work.\n\nHere's the thing about notes. \"Called client\" tells nobody anything. Who, when, which channel, what was said and what happens next: that's a note. Every one of those becomes proof we did our job when the adjuster says we didn't.\n\n{name}, you're tracking our time. Stuck ten minutes? Log it and move on.\n\nMystery for you: if one of today's letters went to the wrong address, which one would cost John the most money?",
    "ask": "{name}, which of today's activities do you think protects the most money down the road?"
   },
   "wrap": {
    "say": "Before the Knowledge Check, let's step back. Quick lightning round: one word for today. {name}? {name}? I heard \"paperwork\" twice, and honestly, I'll allow it.\n\nNow the real question. It's 04/08/2026. Keystone confirmed its limit in writing on 03/20, Riverton Spine & Rehab reports John Doe has missed two visits, and Dana Whitfield has asked John for a Recorded Statement. What do you do first, second and third, and what does each one protect?\n\n\"Call John back sometime this week\" won't cut it. Shut the recorded call down through the firm the same day, reach John on every approved channel and log it, and get the missed visits to Atty. Thorne with the facts and a plan.\n\nI once had a client who skipped therapy because the clinic's parking lot \"had bad vibes.\" Turned out the bus had stopped running. So ask why before you assume.\n\nEach missed visit is a gap the adjuster will quote back to us, which means every step today protects the settlement. Let's take two or three answers and tie each to a moment on his file from today.\n\nThen tonight's mystery: tomorrow, John explains his fourteen-day gap on a recorded call with you. What's the one question you must never ask him?",
    "ask": "{name}, which of those three moves would you make first, and what does it protect?"
   }
  },
  "3": {
   "agenda": {
    "say": "Good morning! Let's close yesterday's mystery first: what could we have done the day John's ride ended? {name}? Yes: asked the clinic about transportation help, logged it, and told Atty. Thorne that same day.\n\nAnd the one question you never ask him about the gap? The one that hands him an answer, like \"it was because of work, right?\" His facts, his words. Hang on to that, because today the file gets judged.\n\nWarm-up game: hot or cold. I name something, you shout hot if it helps a demand, cold if it hurts. A dated doctor's note. Hot. An unexplained gap. Cold. A client who told the adjuster he's fine. Ice cold. {name}, you're scorekeeper.\n\nHere's today. First, John explains his fourteen-day gap on a recorded call with you, facts only, no legal advice.\n\nThen the pre-demand audit: every bill checked against the records, and the Specials, which are just his out-of-pocket damages like Medical Bills, totaled to the cent.\n\nThen a strategy session with Atty. Thorne, and the demand itself: a Time-Limited Demand, which is an offer with a deadline. Then the negotiation, and the Net Sheets that show where every dollar goes.\n\nA pile of paper isn't a demand. A story with proof on every page is.\n\nTeaser: by this afternoon we'll have a number with a comma in it, and a puzzle about where it all goes.",
    "ask": "{name}, what's one thing that would make you nervous about putting a deadline on our own demand?"
   },
   "lab": {
    "say": "Practice Lab. Fourteen Skill Activities, and three of them talk back.\n\nQuick show of hands: who's ever practiced a hard conversation in the shower first? {name}, very confident, I like it.\n\nToday you'll practice on AI versions of the real people: John Doe on his gap, Atty. Thorne in the strategy session, and the adjuster in the negotiation.\n\nOpen the CMS first. You'll build the Gap Explanation Record from John's own words, audit the Specials, and audit the backup drive against the CMS.\n\nThen you'll serve the bodily injury demand with its deadline, and quality-check the two UM demands behind it. UM is the coverage that pays when the other driver's insurance runs out.\n\nThen you negotiate, close the forty thousand, and build both Net Sheets, the page that shows the client every dollar in and out. Same rhythm: Your Case, Your Task, Check Your Work.\n\nPlease don't just read the script back to the AI. Listen, take notes, and ask the next question. A sloppy number in the demand can give the insurer an excuse to say it never had a fair chance.\n\n{name}, you're on the calculator. {name}, you check their math. Stuck ten minutes? Log it and move on.\n\nMystery: one figure on the Net Sheet is one you'd never read to John. Spot it.",
    "ask": "{name}, when the AI adjuster brings up the gap, what's your first move?"
   },
   "wrap": {
    "say": "Before the Knowledge Check, let's step back. Quick game: rate today's stress level on the adjuster's hold-music scale, one to five. {name}? {name}? Fair enough.\n\nNow the real question. It's 07/20/2026. The Specials are audited and Atty. Thorne has approved a policy-limits demand to Keystone. What must be true before the Time-Limited Demand goes out, and what would let Keystone say it never had a fair chance to settle?\n\n\"Send it and hope\" is not a plan. Every record and bill included, every number matching the audit, the deadline clear and reasonable, proof it was received, and Atty. Thorne's sign-off on all of it.\n\nI once watched a demand go out with a page of someone else's case stapled in the middle. The adjuster sent it back with a sticky smile and a lot less urgency.\n\nOne missing page can turn a strong demand into an argument about fairness, and that argument costs the client time and money. Let's take two or three answers and tie each to something you did today.\n\nThen tonight's mystery: we collected forty thousand gross. Tomorrow, the bills come for their share. How much do you think ends up in John's pocket, and who decides how it gets split?",
    "ask": "{name}, what one thing would you check twice before the demand leaves the building?"
   }
  },
  "4": {
   "agenda": {
    "say": "Good morning. Last night I asked how much of the forty thousand ends up in John's pocket. So let's play price is right: closest guess without going over wins. {name}? {name}?\n\nWrite your numbers down. I'll reveal the real one this afternoon, to the penny.\n\nAnd who decides the split? Not us. Atty. Thorne and the client do, from the numbers we prove.\n\nHere's today. First the money side of Track A, the file that settled. We audit the release, which is the paper John signs to end the claim, so it doesn't accidentally let anyone else off the hook.\n\nThen the liens, the bills that get paid out of the settlement, and the provider balances: we ask for reductions, in writing, with reasons. Then the final Net Sheet and the disbursement, meaning the money actually goes out.\n\nThen Track B, the version where the case doesn't settle. Mediation, where a neutral person helps both sides talk, and arbitration, where a neutral person decides. Hard dates, the UPL line at hearings, and two binders built tab by tab.\n\nSome Case Managers take the first lien number and move on. Not you. You ask, you document, and you ask again, because every dollar saved goes to the client.\n\nTeaser: there's one sentence in Keystone's release that could cost John far more than any lien. Find it.",
    "ask": "{name}, before we start, what's your guess: which bill on John's file will be the hardest to reduce?"
   },
   "lab": {
    "say": "Practice Lab. Thirteen Skill Activities, two sides of the same file.\n\nQuick game first: true or false? A lien holder will always reduce a bill if you ask nicely. {name}? False. You need reasons, records and math. Asking nicely just gets you a nice no.\n\nOpen the CMS first. On the settled side, you'll run the Health Plan and statutory lien reductions, negotiate provider balances, and finish the final Net Sheet with both tabs.\n\nThen you audit the release, trigger the disbursement, and back up the lien file, once you find a missing folder.\n\nOn the unsettled side, you'll route the UM impasse to arbitration or court, audit the court's hard rules and set the calendar cutoffs, and calendar the mediation and the arbitration with every date that hangs off them.\n\nThen you map the ADR rules and the UPL line, and build both binders. Same rhythm: Your Case, Your Task, Check Your Work.\n\nEverything printed in any order isn't a binder. I want six tabs where the attorney finds any page in ten seconds. Why care? The mediator reads the first few pages and forms an opinion before lunch.\n\n{name}, you're our calendar captain. {name}, you're checking the math to the penny. Stuck ten minutes? Log it and move on.\n\nChallenge: check every date against its source letter. A date that doesn't add up never gets on our calendar.",
    "ask": "{name}, which number on the Net Sheet will John look at first, and how will you explain it?"
   },
   "wrap": {
    "say": "Before the Knowledge Check, let's step back. Quick reveal first: John's net is $20,359.92. {name}, how close was your guess? Nearest without going over gets bragging rights and first pick of the good pen.\n\nNow the real question. Keystone's draft release arrives on 08/14/2026 releasing “all other persons, firms, corporations and insurers.” Harbor Point hasn't answered the consent request, and Atty. Thorne is out of the office until Monday. What do you do, and what must not happen before he's back?\n\nIt's tempting to say it's just a form and send it to John to sign. Don't. Flag that language, because it could release the UM insurers too. Chase Harbor Point's consent in writing, log everything, and make sure nothing gets signed until Atty. Thorne reviews it.\n\nI once saw a client sign a release in a parking lot because he was so excited to be done. It took months to fix.\n\nThat one sentence could cost John the rest of his recovery, so it waits for the attorney. Let's take two or three answers and tie each to something on today's file.\n\nThen tonight's mystery: tomorrow is the litigation fork. Pick one document from your binders. Would it hold up in front of a judge, or only a mediator?",
    "ask": "{name}, what's the one thing that must not happen before Atty. Thorne is back, and why?"
   }
  },
  "5": {
   "agenda": {
    "say": "Good morning, and welcome to our last day. Last night's question: would your binder document hold up in front of a judge? {name}? {name}?\n\nThe honest answer for most of you: only if we can prove where it came from and that nobody changed it. Hold that thought, because today you build a file of your own.\n\nHere's today, and it's quicker on the lessons than any other day. First, a fast refresher on litigation: the fork where a file stops negotiating and goes to court, filing and service, discovery, John's deposition, the award, and closing a case we drop.\n\nThen the big one. You get two and a half hours to build your companion case from scratch, the case I assigned you, the way you worked John Doe's file all week.\n\nRead it, open it in the CMS with the Conflict Check first, build its folder, set up the claims, plan the care, and bring Atty. Thorne the decision he needs.\n\nTeaser: one of you will find a problem on your case in the first ten minutes that John Doe's file never had. Guess what it is.",
    "ask": "{name}, of everything you did on John Doe's file this week, which step are you most likely to skip when you're rushed?"
   },
   "lab": {
    "say": "Practice Lab, the last and biggest one: sixteen Skill Activities.\n\nQuick would-you-rather: would you rather e-file at four in the afternoon or at 11:59 at night? {name}? Correct answer: four. Always four.\n\nOpen the CMS first. You'll trigger the litigation fork and move the file to litigation status, e-file the complaint and calendar service, fire the CMS countermeasures when work gets stuck, and clear a bottleneck. Then the logs: pleadings and discovery, and the trial-prep checklist.\n\nThen the car: Repair Estimates against what the car was worth, Loss of Use, and Diminished Value, the drop in a car's value just because it's been in a crash.\n\nThen John's deposition prep and a mock cross-examination, the comparative liability package, and skip-tracing a client who's vanished.\n\nAnd to finish, the Litigation Net Sheet for the award, the final backup audit that gets John's file ready to close, and closing a Dropped Case. Same rhythm: Your Case, Your Task, Check Your Work.\n\nDoing the task isn't enough today. Do the task and calendar everything it starts. In litigation, a missed date isn't an awkward email, it can end the claim.\n\n{name}, you're our deadline hawk. {name}, you're verifying every exhibit. Stuck ten minutes? Log it and move on.\n\nChallenge: for every deadline you set today, name the date it counts from and the document that proves it.",
    "ask": "{name}, when a deadline depends on another date, which one do you calendar first, and why?"
   },
   "wrap": {
    "say": "Before the last Knowledge Check, let's step back one more time. Quick game: one sentence, what will you do differently on your first real file because of this week? {name}? {name}? Hold onto those.\n\nNow the real question. It's 01/05/2027. Opposing counsel's paralegal says your client's discovery responses are late, John Doe has changed his phone number, and the deposition is set for 02/18/2027. What do you do today, in what order, and what's Atty. Thorne's call, not yours?\n\n\"Email John and wait\" isn't going to save this one. Confirm the real discovery deadline on the calendar, tell Atty. Thorne today, start the outreach on every approved channel and log it, and if John stays silent, begin the skip-trace steps, all while protecting the deposition date.\n\nWhether to ask for more time, or what to tell the other side, is Atty. Thorne's call.\n\nI once found a client who'd changed his number because he \"needed a fresh start.\" His new voicemail was full by Tuesday.\n\nLate discovery can cost the client evidence and money, so today matters. Let's take two or three answers and tie each one to something from this week.\n\nThen your last mystery: on your first real file, which of these three problems shows up first?",
    "ask": "{name}, of those three problems, which one would you tackle first, and why?"
   },
   "build": {
    "say": "Okay, this is the part I've been looking forward to all week. You've worked John Doe's file for four days. Now you build one yourself.\n\nYou've got two and a half hours, and the slide splits it up for you. Ten minutes to read your file, really read it, because the facts decide everything after. Then half an hour in the CMS, and the Conflict Check comes first, on every party's full legal name.\n\nThen the folder, by the SOP: your full name, a dash, the case name, the nine subfolders, connected. Then the claims, every carrier in the stack, in writing. Then the care plan, with the 14-day rule and who pays first.\n\nLast, the note to Atty. Thorne. Decision first, then the deadline it hangs on.\n\nI'll call time at each step so nobody spends the whole build in the CMS. {name}, you're my timekeeper for the first one.\n\nHere's the thing: nobody's going to hand you the answer today. Your case has its own trap, and finding it is the job.",
    "ask": "{name}, before you open anything: what's the first fact on your case you'd want to confirm, and where on the file would you look for it?"
   },
   "extra": {
    "say": "Before the Knowledge Check, one quick note. Behind this slide are John Doe's own Day 5 activities: the litigation fork, the complaint and service, the deposition prep, the logs and the Dropped Case.\n\nThey're optional today. Your practical was your companion case, and that's the work I'm grading you on.\n\nBut here's my honest advice. Come back to these this week, while it's fresh. The deposition prep and the dropped-case close-out are the two that catch new Case Managers out on a real file.\n\nFor now, we skip straight to the Knowledge Check. {name}, how did your build go, in one sentence?",
    "ask": "{name}, which of John Doe's Day 5 activities would you do first after today, and why that one?"
   }
  }
 }
};
